INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (1,'toronto','Toronto','City of Toronto',1,43.6532000,-79.3832000,'M4C, M5A, M5V, M6H, M4K','The Annex\nCabbagetown\nRiverdale\nLeslieville\nParkdale\nLittle Italy\nTrinity Bellwoods\nRoncesvalles\nThe Beaches\nYorkville\nLiberty Village\nCorso Italia\nHigh Park North\nDanforth Village','CN Tower\nSt. Lawrence Market\nHigh Park\nKensington Market\nQueen Street West\nUnion Station\nCasa Loma\nYonge-Dundas Square','downtown-toronto,midtown-toronto,east-york,york,north-york,etobicoke','Handyman Toronto - Repairs & Assembly','Handyman services in Toronto for repairs, TV mounting, flat-pack assembly, drywall, doors, plumbing fixtures and painting. Ask Sercan for a free quote today.','Handyman Services in Toronto, Ontario','Looking for a handyman in Toronto usually starts with a short list of jobs that have been waiting a while. A shelf that never went up. A door that sticks in July. A tap that drips. I am Sercan, and I do this work myself across the Greater Toronto Area, so the person who quotes your job is the person who shows up to do it.\n\nToronto is not one kind of house. In a single afternoon I can go from a century semi with plaster walls near the Danforth to a 2015 condo tower with concrete ceilings. Each one needs a different anchor, a different tool and a different plan. I quote free, agree the price before I start, and leave the space clean.','<h2>The housing stock changes block by block</h2><p>Downtown and the old city are full of Victorian and Edwardian semis built between 1890 and 1920. The walls are plaster and lath over wood strapping, not drywall. A screw that would hold fine in a modern wall will crumble plaster and spin free. On those houses I drill differently, find the strapping or the joist behind, and patch with a proper base coat before finishing. Doors in these homes have usually been rehung two or three times, so the frames are rarely square.</p><p>Move north or west and the picture changes to post-war bungalows, 1960s apartment blocks and streets of newer infill. Then there are the concrete towers built since 2005 along the Toronto waterfront, on King West and up Yonge. Concrete ceilings mean no timber to screw into, so anything overhead needs a masonry anchor and a hammer drill. Knowing which of these I am walking into is half of a good quote.</p><h2>Jobs I get called about most in the old city</h2><p>TV mounting is the steady one. Brick chimney breasts, plaster walls, concrete columns and stud walls that are sixteen inches apart on paper but not in practice. Flat-pack assembly is the other constant, usually in a condo or a small second bedroom where the box will not fit through the door once it is built.</p><p>After that it is the maintenance list that piles up week after week:</p><ul><li>Sticking interior doors in older semis and rowhouses</li><li>Faucet, shower head and toilet swaps in dated bathrooms</li><li>Light fixture, dimmer and smart switch replacements</li><li>Drywall and plaster patching after a move-out</li><li>Closet shelving and floating shelves on plaster walls</li><li>Weather stripping and lock adjustments on old front doors</li><li>Backsplash tile and fresh caulking in tired kitchens</li></ul><p>If a job needs new circuits, a panel change, gas or a structural change, I say so and refer you to a licensed electrician or plumber. Swapping a fixture that is already there is my work.</p><h2>Parking, elevators and the practical side</h2><p>Working in Toronto means planning the arrival, not just the job. Narrow one-way streets, permit parking and rear laneways all matter. In condo buildings the service elevator has to be booked, and most towers want the contractor cleared with the property manager first. Give me the building name with your quote request and I will check what is needed ahead of time.</p><p>Small jobs are worth batching. If you have five little things, one visit costs you less than five, and it saves the trip and the parking hunt each time. Photos help as well. A picture of the wall, the fixture or the box on the floor tells me more than a paragraph.</p><h2>How the quote and the visit work</h2><p>Quotes are free and there is no obligation. You describe the job, I ask a couple of questions, and you get either a fixed price or an hourly rate agreed before I start. Nothing changes partway through without you saying yes first.</p><p>Materials come with receipts if I supply them, or you buy your own if you would rather choose the finish. When I am done the area is swept and the offcuts leave with me. If something is not right afterwards I come back and put it right. Most quote requests get a reply within one business day, and I work Monday to Saturday, 8:00 AM to 8:00 PM, with Sunday by appointment.</p>','Every part of the city has its own vintage. On one street you can find a house with plaster walls, a light box that predates the current wiring rules and a basement apartment added in the 1970s, sitting next to a rebuild finished last year. Add permit parking, laneway access and condo front desks with their own rules, and planning a Toronto job matters as much as doing it. That is why I ask questions before quoting.','Plaster wall patching in century semis\nTV mounting on downtown concrete condo walls\nSticking door adjustments in Victorian rowhouses\nFlat-pack assembly in small condo bedrooms\nFaucet and toilet swaps in older bathrooms\nShelving anchored into plaster and lath walls\nWeather stripping on original front doors\nVideo doorbell setup on semi-detached porches','[{\"q\":\"Can you work in a condo that needs the service elevator booked?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Most downtown towers want the service elevator reserved and the contractor cleared with management before the day. Tell me the building when you ask for a quote and I will find out what your property manager needs, then book a slot that works around your schedule. It is worth starting early, because elevator slots in busy buildings can be a week or more out.\"},{\"q\":\"My walls are plaster, not drywall. Can you still hang a heavy mirror?\",\"a\":\"Yes, though it takes a different approach. Plaster over lath will not hold a standard drywall anchor under load. I find the strapping or the stud behind it, drill slowly so the face does not blow out, and fix into solid wood. If there is nothing usable behind the exact spot you want, I will tell you and suggest a position a few centimetres over.\"},{\"q\":\"I have a list of small jobs around the house. Is that worth one visit?\",\"a\":\"That is usually the best way to do it. Write the list out, even the tiny items, and send photos with it. I price the whole list at once, bring what I need for all of it, and work through it in order. One visit for six jobs costs you less than six separate trips, and it saves hunting for parking each time.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you take on electrical work in older houses?\",\"a\":\"I swap what is already there. Light fixtures, ceiling fans, dimmers and smart switches on an existing circuit are all fine. New circuits, panel work or anything that means opening up wiring inside a wall goes to a licensed electrician, and I say that straight away rather than start and stop. Old houses do sometimes surprise you once the original fixture comes off.\"}]','',1,1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (2,'north-york','North York','City of Toronto',1,43.7615000,-79.4111000,'M2M, M2N, M2R, M3A, M3H, M6A','Willowdale\nBayview Village\nNewtonbrook\nLansing\nBathurst Manor\nArmour Heights\nYork Mills\nHoggs Hollow\nDon Mills\nHenry Farm\nDownsview\nFlemingdon Park\nHumber Summit\nClanton Park','Yorkdale Shopping Centre\nFairview Mall\nMel Lastman Square\nEdwards Gardens\nDownsview Park\nBayview Village Shopping Centre\nSheppard-Yonge Station\nOntario Science Centre','willowdale,don-mills,york,midtown-toronto,thornhill,toronto','Handyman North York - Home Repairs','Handyman in North York for home repairs, TV mounting, furniture assembly, drywall patching, doors, light fixtures and plumbing swaps. Ask Sercan for a quote.','Handyman Services Across North York','Most handyman work in North York happens in one of two places: a post-war house on a quiet street, or a condo somewhere along the Yonge line. I am Sercan, and I am the one who turns up. Free quote first, a fixed price or hourly rate agreed before anything starts, then the job gets done and the space gets swept.\n\nBungalows and side-splits around Willowdale, Bathurst Manor and Downsview went up mostly between 1950 and 1970. They have generous lots, finished basements added later and hardware that has been painted over a few times. Towers along Sheppard and Yonge are a different job entirely, with concrete walls and a front desk to get past. Both are ordinary days for me.','<h2>Basements, rec rooms and the space under the stairs</h2><p>A lot of North York houses were built with a rough basement that somebody finished later, often in the seventies or eighties. Panelling on strapping, a suspended tile ceiling, a bar in the corner and a bathroom squeezed under the stairs. The framing behind that panelling is rarely where you would expect it, so hanging a television or a heavy shelf down there starts with working out what is actually behind the surface. I check first and drill second.</p><p>Those same rooms are where small jobs collect. A basement door that never closed properly after the floor was levelled. A vanity from the eighties with a leaking trap. Pot light trims that no longer match, a handrail that has worked loose, laminate that lifted at a seam. I would rather take a list of eight of those in one visit than come back eight times.</p><h2>Condos along the Yonge and Sheppard lines</h2><p>Towers around Sheppard-Yonge, Bayview Village and North York Centre bring their own rules. Concrete ceilings and shear walls need masonry anchors and a hammer drill, not plastic toggles. Balcony doors drift out of alignment and start dragging on the sill. Curtain tracks and blinds want fixing into the header above the window rather than the drywall return, which is thinner than it looks.</p><p>Buildings also want the service elevator booked and the contractor cleared with the property manager first. Give me the address when you ask for a quote and I will check what is required before the day, so the visit is not spent standing at a front desk.</p><h2>Seasonal work in this part of the city</h2><p>Winter is hard on doors and weather stripping across North York. Frames swell and shrink through the season, latches stop lining up, and cold air finds the bottom corner of the front door. Spring brings decks and fences: boards lifted by frost, posts leaning, gates that no longer meet the latch. Summer is the season for ceiling fans, patio doors and screens.</p><p>Fall is the one worth planning ahead for. Once the temperature drops, exterior caulking and paint stop curing properly, so outside work is better booked while the weather still cooperates. Anything on a ladder outside is a much easier job in September than in November.</p><h2>What to have ready before I arrive</h2><p>Preparation shortens the visit and makes the price easier to pin down. Before I come it helps to have:</p><ul><li>Photos of the wall, fixture or flat-pack box sent ahead</li><li>A clear path to the work area, with furniture pulled back</li><li>Any part you bought yourself already on site</li><li>Parking sorted, or the visitor space number for a condo</li><li>Pets settled in another room</li><li>Your full list, including the jobs you think are too small</li><li>The building rules for contractors if you are in a tower</li></ul><p>Materials come with receipts if I supply them. Quotes are free and carry no obligation, the price is agreed before the first screw goes in, and most requests get a reply within one business day. I work Monday to Saturday from 8:00 AM to 8:00 PM, and Sunday by appointment. Anything needing a new circuit, a panel, gas or structural work goes to a licensed trade, and I will tell you that before any work begins.</p>','North York grew fast after the war, so whole streets went up in the same few years with the same layouts. That is useful. Once I have seen one side-split on a street, I have a fair idea what sits behind the walls of the next one. What varies is everything added since: a basement finished in 1982, a kitchen redone in 2004, and a bathroom nobody has touched since the house was built.','TV mounting on finished basement panelling\nBalcony door adjustments in Yonge corridor condos\nVanity and faucet swaps in 1960s bathrooms\nFence and gate repair on wide lots\nInterior door hinges on split-level landings\nPot light and dimmer replacements in rec rooms\nFlat-pack wardrobe assembly in Willowdale condos\nWeather stripping on front doors before winter','[{\"q\":\"Can you mount a television on the panelled wall in a basement rec room?\",\"a\":\"Usually yes, but it takes a look first. Panelling is often nailed to thin strapping with a gap behind, so the fixing has to reach the framing or the block wall past it. I sound the wall, find something solid, and set the bracket into that. If the only solid point is off to one side, I will show you before anything gets drilled.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you take on the older bungalows around Bathurst Manor and Downsview?\",\"a\":\"Often. Those houses are straightforward once you know their habits: doors that have swollen, hinges buried under layers of paint, original trim worth saving and a basement finished by a previous owner. Repairs, fixture swaps, flooring, shelving and painting all suit them well. Send photos of what needs doing and I can usually price the list without coming out first.\"},{\"q\":\"How far ahead should I book deck and fence repairs?\",\"a\":\"Earlier than most people think. Frost lifts posts over the winter, so damage shows up in April and everybody calls at once. Spring and early autumn are the sensible windows. Exterior wood repair, staining and caulking all want temperatures that hold, so a September booking beats a November one. Tell me roughly what needs doing and I will fit it into a suitable stretch of weather.\"},{\"q\":\"The hinges and handles on my old doors are painted over. Fix or replace?\",\"a\":\"It depends on what is underneath. Solid brass hardware from a fifties or sixties house is often worth stripping and reusing, and it looks better than a modern replacement. Hardware that is worn, bent or seized is quicker to swap out. I will tell you which you have, and if you want new handles or locks, I fit those and adjust the strike plates so everything closes cleanly.\"}]','',1,2,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (3,'scarborough','Scarborough','City of Toronto',1,43.7764000,-79.2318000,'M1B, M1E, M1K, M1P, M1T, M1W','Agincourt\nGuildwood\nWest Hill\nMalvern\nWoburn\nBirch Cliff\nCliffside\nBendale\nHighland Creek\nDorset Park\nWexford\nMilliken\nPort Union\nRouge','Scarborough Bluffs\nScarborough Town Centre\nRouge National Urban Park\nGuild Park and Gardens\nBluffer\'s Park\nMorningside Park\nToronto Zoo\nKennedy Station','agincourt,east-york,north-york,pickering,markham,toronto','Scarborough Handyman - Home Repairs','Handyman services in Scarborough for door repairs, TV mounting, assembly, drywall, flooring, decks and fences. Free quote, no obligation. Send me your list.','Scarborough Handyman for Home Repairs','The handyman jobs I get called for in Scarborough start with doors more often than anything else. A screen door that will not latch, a patio slider that takes two hands, a front door that lets a draft in at the corner. Houses here are mostly sixty years old now, and doors are the first thing to go out of true when a house settles.\n\nI am Sercan and I work on my own. Quotes are free, the price is agreed before I start, and if something is not right after I leave I come back. Beyond doors I do the usual list: mounting, assembly, drywall, plumbing fixtures, flooring and trim. Send a couple of photos with your request and the quote comes back faster.','<h2>Sixty-year-old houses and the doors that came with them</h2><p>Most of Scarborough was built between 1955 and 1975: bungalows, backsplits and sidesplits on wide lots, with brick fronts and aluminum storm doors. Those houses sit on clay. Clay moves with the wet and the dry, and after six decades of small movement the frames are no longer square. That is why the latch misses the strike plate, and why the bottom corner of the door drags on the sill.</p><p>Fixing it is rarely a new door. Usually it is shimming a hinge, moving a strike plate a few millimetres, easing an edge and putting fresh weather stripping in. Sliding patio doors are the same story with different parts: worn rollers, a track full of grit and a lock that no longer lines up. Both are an afternoon of work, not a project.</p><h2>Decks, fences and the outdoor list</h2><p>Wide lots mean fences, and fences here take a beating. Frost lifts posts over the winter, gates drop and stop latching, and a few boards go soft every year. Deck work is similar: lifted boards, loose railings and stairs that have gone springy underfoot. I repair what can be repaired rather than push a rebuild, and I say plainly when a structure is past the point where a repair makes sense.</p><p>Outdoor work is worth booking before the weather turns. Paint and exterior caulking need reasonable temperatures to cure, so September is an easier month than November for anything on the outside of the house.</p><h2>Apartments, towers and townhouse complexes</h2><p>Scarborough has a lot of rental towers from the sixties and seventies along Kingston Road, Markham Road and Victoria Park, plus newer condo blocks near Scarborough Town Centre. Concrete walls need masonry anchors. Units in older buildings often have doors and cabinets that have been painted so many times the hardware barely moves, and a careful strip and adjustment brings them back. Townhouse complexes usually have their own rules about exterior work, so it is worth checking with the corporation before I quote.</p><h2>Small jobs, long lists and bigger projects</h2><p>A typical week\'s list looks something like this:</p><ul><li>Patio slider rollers and track cleaning</li><li>Front door weather stripping and lock alignment</li><li>TV mounting on brick and concrete walls</li><li>Flat-pack wardrobes and bed frames built</li><li>Toilet, faucet and shower head replacements</li><li>Laminate flooring and baseboards in basements</li><li>Backsplash tiling and kitchen cabinet hardware</li></ul><p>Batching pays off. Five small jobs in one visit beats five separate visits, and I would rather price the whole list at once than one item at a time. Bigger work runs over several days: a bathroom refresh, a basement floor, a full round of doors through a house.</p><p>Anything that needs a new circuit, a panel, gas or a structural change goes to a licensed trade, and I will tell you that up front. Replacing an existing fixture, switch, faucet or toilet is my work. I reply to most requests within one business day and work Monday to Saturday, 8:00 AM to 8:00 PM, with Sunday by appointment.</p>','This is a suburb that grew in one big push, so the houses share a vintage and share the same complaints. Clay soil underneath, doors out of square, aluminum windows at the end of their life, basements finished twenty years after the house was built. The Bluffs side gets wind straight off the lake, which is hard on exterior paint and fence boards. None of it is complicated work, but it rewards knowing what to expect.','Patio slider roller and track repairs\nFront door adjustments in 1960s bungalows\nFence post and gate repair after frost\nBasement laminate flooring and baseboard fitting\nTV mounting on concrete rental tower walls\nBathroom vanity and toilet replacements\nAluminum storm door and screen repairs\nCabinet hardware and backsplash in dated kitchens','[{\"q\":\"Why do so many doors in Scarborough houses stop closing properly?\",\"a\":\"Ground movement, mostly. The clay under these neighbourhoods swells when it is wet and shrinks when it is dry, and sixty years of that leaves frames slightly out of square. Humidity does the rest, which is why a door sticks in August and rattles in February. The fix is usually adjusting hinges and the strike plate, not buying a new door.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you repair my sliding patio door rather than replace the whole thing?\",\"a\":\"In most cases, yes. The common faults are worn rollers, a dirty or dented track, and a latch that has drifted out of line. New rollers, a cleaned track and an adjustment will bring back a door that slides with one finger. If the frame itself has failed or the sealed unit is fogged, I will tell you honestly that replacement is the better spend.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you cover the east end, out toward West Hill, Highland Creek and Rouge?\",\"a\":\"Yes, that end is part of my normal run. If you are out that way it helps to batch the work, so send me everything on your list rather than one item. I will price it all together and set aside enough time in a single visit. Photos with your request speed the whole thing up, and I usually reply within one business day.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you put new flooring and baseboards into a backsplit basement?\",\"a\":\"That is a job I do regularly. Laminate and vinyl plank suit basements well, and the tricky parts are the transitions at the split-level stairs and getting the trim to sit right against uneven old walls. I scribe where I have to. Concrete floors need checking for level and moisture first, and I will tell you what I find before starting.\"}]','',1,3,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (4,'etobicoke','Etobicoke','City of Toronto',1,43.6205000,-79.5132000,'M8V, M8W, M8Y, M9A, M9B, M9C','Mimico\nNew Toronto\nLong Branch\nAlderwood\nHumber Bay Shores\nIslington Village\nThe Kingsway\nSunnylea\nMarkland Wood\nEatonville\nPrincess Gardens\nRichview\nThistletown\nRexdale','Sherway Gardens\nHumber Bay Park\nColonel Samuel Smith Park\nCentennial Park\nMarie Curtis Park\nKipling Station\nIslington Station\nBloor Street West','mississauga,high-park,the-junction,bloor-west-village,york,toronto','Handyman Etobicoke - Repairs & Mounting','Handyman in Etobicoke and along the lakeshore for TV mounting, assembly, doors, drywall and fixture swaps. Free quote from Sercan, usually within a day.','Handyman in Etobicoke and the Lakeshore','A handyman day in Etobicoke often has two halves: a morning in a lakeshore condo tower and an afternoon in a post-war bungalow ten minutes north. I am Sercan and I do both myself. The tower needs a booked service elevator, a hammer drill and masonry anchors. The bungalow needs a stud finder, a bit of patience with old trim and a door that has not shut properly since 1998.\n\nFree quotes, a fixed price or hourly rate agreed before the work starts, materials with receipts if I supply them, and the area left clean. That is the whole arrangement. Send photos with your request and I can usually price it without a site visit first, which saves you a step.','<h2>Working in the lakeshore towers</h2><p>The Etobicoke condo strip along Humber Bay Shores and Mimico has grown quickly since 2005, and those buildings all have a process. The service elevator gets booked, management wants the contractor cleared before the day, and loading happens through a specific door at a specific time. None of that is difficult, but it has to be arranged in advance or the visit turns into an hour of waiting in a lobby.</p><p>Inside the units the work is fairly consistent. Concrete ceilings and shear walls take masonry anchors rather than toggles. Balcony doors on the lake side get a lot of wind, and their rollers wear faster than you would expect. Curtain tracks want fixing into the header above the window. The television wall is often the one drywall partition in the room, so where the studs fall really does matter.</p><h2>North of the Queensway</h2><p>Away from the water the housing changes. Alderwood, Long Branch and New Toronto are full of small post-war houses on narrow lots. Up through Islington Village, Princess Gardens and Markland Wood you find 1950s and 1960s bungalows and sidesplits on bigger lots, and around The Kingsway there are older brick homes with plaster walls and original trim worth keeping. Each of those asks for a different way of fixing something to a wall.</p><p>Basements are the other Etobicoke feature. Many were finished long after the house was built, so what looks like a solid wall turns out to be panelling on strapping with a gap behind it. I check before I drill rather than after.</p><h2>The calls that come in most often</h2><p>Across Etobicoke the same jobs repeat, whatever the building:</p><ul><li>TV mounting on concrete condo walls</li><li>Balcony and patio door adjustments</li><li>Flat-pack furniture built in tight condo bedrooms</li><li>Faucet, shower head and toilet replacements</li><li>Light fixtures, ceiling fans, dimmers and smart switches</li><li>Drywall patching and painting after a move</li><li>Baseboards, trim and laminate flooring</li><li>Video doorbells and thermostats set up and connected</li></ul><p>Several of those in one visit is normal, and usually the sensible way to do it. I would rather quote a list than a single item, and it keeps the number of trips down.</p><h2>Where I stop and call in a licensed trade</h2><p>Swapping something that already exists is my work: a light fixture for a new light fixture, a dead dimmer for a smart switch, an old faucet for a new one, a tired toilet for a modern one. That covers most of what people ask for around here.</p><p>New circuits, panel changes, gas appliances, structural alterations, roofing and heating systems are not. Those belong to a licensed electrician, plumber or the relevant trade, and I will say so before going any further instead of starting something I should not finish. Quotes are free with no obligation either way. I usually reply within one business day, and I work Monday to Saturday from 8:00 AM to 8:00 PM, with Sunday by appointment.</p>','Etobicoke splits neatly in two for this kind of work. Down at the lake it is towers, booked elevators and concrete that wants a hammer drill. North of the Queensway it is post-war houses with old trim, basements finished decades later and hardware painted over three times. The drive between the two is short, so a morning in a condo and an afternoon in a bungalow is an ordinary day for me.','TV mounting on concrete condo walls\nBalcony door roller and alignment repairs\nFlat-pack assembly in Humber Bay condos\nShelving fitted to plaster walls in Kingsway homes\nBasement panelling repairs in post-war bungalows\nVideo doorbell and thermostat setup\nFence and gate repair on narrow Long Branch lots\nBathroom recaulking and shower head swaps','[{\"q\":\"Can you mount a television on a concrete wall in a lakeshore condo?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Concrete is actually a strong surface to fix into once you have the right bit and anchors. It takes a hammer drill and dust control, which I bring. The part to sort in advance is the building side: the elevator booking and whatever your property manager asks for from a contractor. Give me the address with your quote request and I will check.\"},{\"q\":\"My Kingsway house has original trim. Will a repair match what is there?\",\"a\":\"That is always the aim. Older profiles are not sold off the shelf, so I either source the closest match and adjust it, or repair the existing piece where the damage is limited. Splicing in a short section and filling carefully often looks better than replacing a whole run. I will show you both options and what each involves before deciding.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you cover the north end, around Richview, Thistletown and Rexdale?\",\"a\":\"Yes, the whole area is within my normal run, and so is the rest of the Greater Toronto Area. Jobs up there tend to be houses rather than condos, which usually makes scheduling simpler since there is no elevator to book. Send your list and a few photos and I will come back with a price, normally inside one business day.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you fit a video doorbell if my house has no doorbell wiring?\",\"a\":\"A battery model is the straightforward answer, and I fit those on brick, siding or a wooden frame, then get the app and chime working before I leave. A hardwired unit needs existing doorbell wiring and a suitable transformer. If yours has none, running new wiring is a job for a licensed electrician, and I will tell you rather than improvise.\"}]','',1,4,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (5,'east-york','East York','City of Toronto',2,43.6905000,-79.3300000,'M4B, M4C, M4G, M4H, M4J','Leaside\nThorncliffe Park\nFlemingdon Park\nWoodbine Heights\nPape Village\nBroadview North\nCrescent Town\nO\'Connor-Parkview\nTopham Park\nEast Danforth\nGovernor\'s Bridge\nBermondsey','Michael Garron Hospital\nEast York Civic Centre\nTaylor Creek Park\nStan Wadlow Park\nCoxwell subway station\nOverlea Boulevard\nEast York Town Centre\nTodmorden Mills','leaside,greektown-danforth,leslieville,don-mills,midtown-toronto,scarborough','East York Handyman & Home Repairs','Handyman in East York for plaster repairs, TV mounting, doors, flooring, faucets and flat-pack assembly. Free quotes, price agreed before I start. Call today.','Handyman Services in East York, Toronto','Most of the handyman work I do in East York happens in houses built between the 1930s and the 1950s. Solid brick, small rooms, and walls that were plastered long before drywall showed up. Those homes have aged well, but they hold surprises. Studs land where they feel like it, plaster crumbles if you rush a screw into it, and door frames have shifted a little over eighty-odd years.\n\nPlenty of my week also goes to the apartment towers around Thorncliffe Park and Crescent Town, where the walls are concrete and the job is usually a light fixture, a leaking faucet or a TV that needs to go up properly. I work Monday to Saturday, quote before I start, and clean up after myself. Sunday visits happen by appointment.','<h2>What East York houses are actually built from</h2><p>Walk almost any street south of O\'Connor Drive and you get the same story: brick, one and a half storeys, built for young families in the forties and fifties. Behind the paint there is usually plaster and lath rather than drywall, and that matters more than people expect. Plaster will not hold a cheap anchor. A shelf that felt solid on the drill pulls away from the wall a month later. I sound out the wall first, find the real framing, and pick fixings that suit what is actually behind them.</p><p>Frames move as well. A door that closed cleanly ten years ago catches on the strike plate now, because the house has settled on clay soil and the opening is no longer quite square. Most of the time that is a hinge shim and a plate moved, not a new door.</p><p>Basements are their own world. Many were finished in the seventies with panelling and a low ceiling, and anything mounted down there needs a plan for the concrete block sitting behind the studs.</p><h2>The calls that come in most often</h2><p>There is a rhythm to the work in East York. Spring brings deck boards and fence posts lifted by frost. Autumn brings weather stripping, sticking doors and the last of the outdoor painting. The rest of the year runs on small indoor jobs, and it is common for one address to have five or six of them waiting.</p><p>These are the jobs I am asked for week after week.</p><ul><li>Plaster patching and repainting in post-war brick houses</li><li>TV mounting on concrete walls in high-rise units</li><li>Sticking doors, worn hinges and drifted strike plates</li><li>Faucet, shower head and toilet replacements</li><li>Flat-pack wardrobes built in narrow upstairs bedrooms</li><li>Baseboards and trim fitted after new flooring</li><li>Ceiling fans, dimmers and light fixtures swapped over</li></ul><h2>Apartments, towers and condo units</h2><p>The East York towers at Thorncliffe Park and Crescent Town, along with the buildings on Overlea Boulevard and St Clair Avenue East, are concrete from floor to ceiling. Anchors made for a stud wall are no use there, so I carry masonry bits and fixings rated for concrete. Some buildings ask for the service elevator to be booked ahead and want documentation from anyone coming in to work on a unit. Tell me what your management office requires and when the elevator is free, and I will build the visit around it.</p><p>Space is tight in those units, so I bring what the job needs and nothing more, and I take the packaging away with me.</p><h2>How I price, and where I stop</h2><p>Quotes are free and carry no obligation. I look at the job, give you a fixed price or an hourly rate, and it is agreed before a tool comes out of the bag. Materials come with receipts if I supply them, or you buy your own and I fit them. The area is left clean, and if something is not right afterwards I come back and put it right.</p><p>One line I do not cross. Swapping an existing light fixture, switch, dimmer, faucet or toilet is ordinary handyman work. New circuits, panel changes, gas appliances and anything structural belong to a licensed electrician or plumber. I will say so plainly and point you the right way rather than take on work that is not mine to do.</p>','East York packs two very different kinds of building into a small area. South of Eglinton you have street after street of solid post-war brick houses with plaster walls, shallow closets and original trim worth saving. North and east of them sit some of the tallest apartment slabs in the city, where everything is concrete and every drill bit has to suit it. Knowing which one I am walking into changes the tools I load in the van.','Plaster wall patching in post-war brick houses\nTV mounting on concrete Thorncliffe Park walls\nSticking door and hinge adjustments in bungalows\nFaucet and shower head swaps in older bathrooms\nFlat-pack wardrobes built in narrow upstairs rooms\nBaseboard and trim replacement after new flooring\nCeiling fan and dimmer switch replacements\nFence post repair after winter frost heave','[{\"q\":\"Do you work in the apartment towers at Thorncliffe Park and Crescent Town?\",\"a\":\"Yes, a good part of my week is spent in those buildings. The walls and ceilings are concrete, so I bring masonry bits and fixings rated for it rather than the anchors that come in the box. If your building needs the service elevator booked, or wants paperwork from anyone working in a unit, let me know early and I will work around it.\"},{\"q\":\"My house has plaster walls. Can you still hang a heavy mirror securely?\",\"a\":\"Plaster and lath will hold plenty if the fixing is right. I find the framing behind it first, and where there is no stud where you want the mirror, I use anchors sized for plaster instead of drywall. I drill slowly so the surface does not blow out, then patch and touch up any test holes before I leave.\"},{\"q\":\"The doors in my 1950s bungalow stick. Do they need replacing?\",\"a\":\"Almost never. Sticking usually comes from the house settling, worn hinges or a strike plate that has drifted out of line. I plane where planing is needed, shim or replace hinges, move the plate and check the frame is still square. That is roughly an hour of work on each door, and the door you already have keeps working.\"},{\"q\":\"How quickly can you get to a job in East York?\",\"a\":\"I reply to most quote requests within one business day. Scheduling depends on what is already booked, and I will give you a real date rather than a vague promise. I work Monday to Saturday, eight in the morning until eight at night, and Sundays by appointment when that is the only time that suits you.\"}]','',1,5,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (6,'york','York','City of Toronto',2,43.6896000,-79.4800000,'M6C, M6E, M6M, M6N, M9N','Weston\nMount Dennis\nSilverthorn\nKeelesdale\nRockcliffe-Smythe\nCaledonia-Fairbank\nOakwood Village\nHumewood-Cedarvale\nBriar Hill-Belgravia\nLambton Baby Point\nEglinton West\nBeechborough-Greenbrook','Eglinton Flats\nSmythe Park\nCedarvale Park\nWeston GO station\nStockyards Village\nYork Civic Centre\nKeelesdale Park\nHumber River','forest-hill,the-junction,north-york,midtown-toronto,etobicoke','York Toronto Handyman & Home Repairs','Handyman services in York, Toronto: door and window fixes, plaster patching, flooring, fixtures and assembly. Free quote, no obligation. Get in touch today.','Your Local Handyman in York, Toronto','The single most common handyman call I get in York is a door that will not close properly. These are old houses, many of them built between the 1920s and the 1950s, and the ground under them has shifted. Frames rack, hinges sag, and a basement entrance that worked for years starts catching on the sill.\n\nDoors are only the start of it. This part of the city covers a lot of ground, from Weston and Mount Dennis down through Silverthorn and Fairbank to Humewood, and the houses vary street by street. I handle repairs, painting, flooring, fixtures, assembly and the jobs nobody else wants to bother with. Quotes are free, the price is agreed before I begin, and I reply to most messages within one business day.','<h2>Doors, windows and a house that has settled</h2><p>Most of York sits on clay, and clay moves with the seasons. After eighty or ninety years a house has moved with it. That is why so many doors here bind at the top corner, why old sash windows stop sliding, and why a deadbolt suddenly needs a shoulder behind it to throw. The fix is nearly always adjustment rather than replacement. Hinges get shimmed, strike plates get moved, worn weather stripping gets renewed, and the door closes with one finger again.</p><p>Basement entrances take the worst of it. Side doors sitting below grade get frost, standing water and road salt every winter, so frames rot from the bottom up and the sill goes soft. I repair what can be repaired, replace thresholds and stripping, and tell you straight when a door is past saving.</p><h2>The York housing stock, street by street</h2><p>Weston has genuinely old homes, some of them from well before the war. Mount Dennis, Silverthorn and Keelesdale run to small brick detached houses and semis from the twenties through the fifties. Humewood and the streets near Cedarvale have larger semis with high plaster ceilings and deep original trim. Newer infill has gone up on the wider lots, and a fair number of older houses now have a second suite downstairs.</p><p>Each of those wants a different approach. The biggest difference is what sits behind the wall: lath and plaster, drywall over strapping, or concrete block once you are in the basement. I check before I drill, every time.</p><h2>Small jobs, and the bigger ones</h2><p>A lot of my work here runs an hour or two. Those are worth batching, and I would rather do six of them in one visit than come back six times. This is the sort of list I get handed at the door.</p><ul><li>Doors eased, rehung and locks replaced</li><li>Plaster cracks filled and rooms repainted</li><li>TV brackets fitted on plaster and block walls</li><li>Kitchen faucets, shower heads and running toilets</li><li>Ceiling fans and dimmers swapped like for like</li><li>Flat-pack furniture assembled and levelled</li><li>Baseboards, quarter round and door casings</li></ul><p>Bigger projects run over several days: a room stripped and repainted, laminate laid through a main floor, a bathroom refreshed with a new vanity, tiled backsplash and fresh caulking, or a deck repaired board by board. Those get quoted the same way, with the price settled before the first day and materials receipted.</p><h2>What to have ready</h2><p>Photos help more than anything else. A picture of the door, the leak or the wall you want a TV on lets me price the job properly before I arrive, and it often saves a wasted trip. If you are supplying materials, have them on site. If you would rather I bring them, I will, and you get the receipts.</p><p>Parking is worth a thought too. Plenty of York streets are narrow, with permit parking and mutual drives, so tell me where the van can go and whether the driveway is shared. Access to an outdoor tap and a power outlet helps for anything that involves cutting or mixing.</p>','York is a patchwork. One block gives you a 1920s brick semi with plaster ceilings and a cold cellar dug by hand, the next gives you a fifties bungalow with a side entrance and a basement suite. Lots are narrow, driveways are shared or mutual, and parking is tight almost everywhere. The Humber and Black Creek ravines cut through it, so grades are uneven and fences rarely run level for long.','Below-grade side door frame repairs\nSticking sash window and door adjustments\nPlaster ceiling crack repairs in older semis\nBasement suite lock and handle replacements\nFence post resetting on sloping ravine lots\nLaminate flooring laid over uneven old subfloors\nBathroom vanity and faucet replacements\nDeck board repairs after winter frost','[{\"q\":\"Can you sort out the side entrance to a basement apartment in Weston or Mount Dennis?\",\"a\":\"That is one of my regular jobs. Below-grade doors take frost, water and salt every winter, so the frame rots at the bottom and the door swells shut. I repair the frame where it is sound, replace weather stripping and the threshold, adjust the hinges and fit a proper lock. If the door is beyond repair I will say so and hang a new one.\"},{\"q\":\"My house has old wiring. Will you still change a light fixture?\",\"a\":\"If the existing wiring is sound and the fixture is a like-for-like swap, yes, that is ordinary handyman work. If I open the box and find something that needs new wiring, a new circuit or panel work, I stop and refer you to a licensed electrician. I would rather lose the job than leave you with something unsafe.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you cover both sides of the Humber River?\",\"a\":\"I do. Weston, Mount Dennis and Rockcliffe-Smythe on the west side, Fairbank, Silverthorn, Oakwood and Humewood over to the east, and everything in between. The Greater Toronto Area is my patch generally, so crossing the river is no trouble. Tell me the nearest cross streets and where I can leave the van, and I will plan around it.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you patch plaster and repaint just one room?\",\"a\":\"Yes, single rooms are common work for me. I fill and skim the cracks, sand back, prime the repairs so they do not flash through the finish, then paint. Old plaster keeps cracking along the same lines, so the preparation matters more than the paint does. Everything gets sheeted, and the room is cleaned up before I leave.\"}]','',1,6,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (7,'downtown-toronto','Downtown Toronto','City of Toronto',2,43.6500000,-79.3830000,'M5A, M5B, M5E, M5H, M5J, M5V','Financial District\nEntertainment District\nKing West\nQueen West\nSt. Lawrence\nDistillery District\nCorktown\nChurch-Wellesley\nCityPlace\nHarbourfront\nKensington Market\nChinatown\nCabbagetown\nGarden District','CN Tower\nUnion Station\nSt. Lawrence Market\nNathan Phillips Square\nEaton Centre\nRogers Centre\nHarbourfront Centre\nOsgoode Hall','liberty-village,yorkville,the-annex,leslieville,midtown-toronto,toronto','Downtown Toronto Handyman Services','Downtown Toronto handyman for condo and house jobs: TV mounting, assembly, blinds, fixtures and doors. Free quote, price agreed first. Message me today.','Handyman in Downtown Toronto, Condos and Houses','Being a handyman in Downtown Toronto is as much about getting into the building as it is about the work itself. Loading docks, booked service elevators, a fifteen minute window at the front of a King Street tower, and no parking anywhere near it. I plan around all of that. Tell me the building rules when you call and the visit runs smoothly.\n\nThe work itself splits two ways down here. In the condo towers it is mounting, assembly, fixtures, blinds and the hundred small things a new unit needs. In the old rows of Cabbagetown, Corktown and Kensington Market it is plaster, sticking doors, and trim on walls that have not been square since the 1880s. I quote free, agree the price first, and clean up before I leave.','<h2>Getting into a Downtown Toronto building</h2><p>Towers here have rules, and they are not all the same. A few want the service elevator reserved several days in advance. Others ask for documentation from anyone doing work in a unit before they will release a fob. A good number only allow noisy work between nine and five on weekdays. None of that is a problem if I know about it early, so tell me what your property manager asks for when you first get in touch.</p><p>Street access is the other half of it. King, Queen and Adelaide are hard to stop on, the laneways behind Kensington Market and Corktown are tight, and a booked loading bay beats circling the block three times. I pack for the job so I can carry it up in one trip.</p><h2>What actually holds in a condo wall</h2><p>Walls in a Downtown Toronto tower are nothing like walls in a house. Demising walls are often concrete or block. Interior partitions are usually steel stud, which is thinner and hollower than wood and needs a toggle rated for the load rather than the screws in the bracket box. Ceilings are frequently post-tension slab, so anything going overhead gets careful treatment or a different plan altogether.</p><p>Most of my condo work is the same short list, done properly the first time.</p><ul><li>TV mounting on steel stud and concrete walls</li><li>Blinds, curtain tracks and blackout rails fitted</li><li>Flat-pack wardrobes and beds built in small units</li><li>Faucet, shower head and toilet replacements</li><li>Light fixtures and dimmers swapped like for like</li><li>Balcony door adjustments and worn weather stripping</li><li>Shelving, mirrors and artwork hung level</li></ul><h2>The old rows east and west of the core</h2><p>Cabbagetown, Corktown and the streets around Kensington Market and the Garden District hold Victorian and Edwardian houses that predate almost everything else in Downtown Toronto. Floors slope. Nothing is plumb. Plaster sits on wood lath and cracks along the same old lines every few years. Trim is often original and worth working around rather than ripping out. I patch, repaint, ease doors and scribe shelving to walls that lean, which takes longer than it sounds and is worth doing properly.</p><p>Plenty of those houses were divided into flats decades ago, which means two of everything and doors that have been rehung more than once. Fitting new hardware to a hundred-year-old door is fiddly work, and it is usually the better answer than tearing out a solid door.</p><h2>Quotes, prices and honest limits</h2><p>Pricing here is straightforward. A free quote with no obligation, then a fixed price or an hourly rate settled before I start, materials with receipts if I supply them, and the unit left clean. If anything is not right afterwards, tell me and I will come back for it.</p><p>Some work is not mine to do. New wiring, panel changes, gas appliances and anything structural in a high-rise go to a licensed electrician, a licensed plumber or the building engineer. I will say so on the spot rather than waste your time or take a chance with your home.</p>','Downtown Toronto is the one part of the city where the building itself sets the schedule. Elevator bookings, loading bay windows, quiet-hours rules and fob access all shape when a job can happen and how long it takes. Add concrete ceilings, steel stud partitions and Victorian rows with nothing square in them, and the same task can need three different approaches within four blocks.','TV mounting on concrete condo walls\nBlinds and curtain tracks in new units\nBalcony door adjustments in waterfront towers\nFlat-pack assembly in small studio condos\nPlaster repairs in Cabbagetown row houses\nToilet and faucet swaps in older flats\nDoor hardware upgrades on century-old doors\nShelving fitted to walls that are not plumb','[{\"q\":\"Can you mount a TV on a concrete or steel stud wall in a condo?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and those two walls need completely different fixings. Concrete takes a hammer drill and sleeve anchors. Steel stud takes toggles rated for the weight, because the screws packed with most brackets are made for wood framing. I check what is behind the drywall before I commit to a spot, keep the cables tidy and leave the unit clean.\"},{\"q\":\"My building wants the service elevator booked. What do you need from me?\",\"a\":\"Ask the concierge or property manager for the next available window and for anything they require from someone coming in to work. Send me those details once the booking is made. I will match my arrival to the window, bring everything up in one trip, and plan the job so the noisy part falls inside the hours your building allows.\"},{\"q\":\"There is nowhere to park on my street. Is that a problem?\",\"a\":\"It is manageable as long as I know in advance. Tell me if the building has a visitor spot, a loading bay or a laneway I can use, or point me at the nearest Green P. I pack for the job rather than hauling the whole van in, so a short walk from a garage is fine. Unloading goes quicker when someone can hold the door.\"},{\"q\":\"I live in an old row house near Cabbagetown. Are the walls different?\",\"a\":\"Very. Those houses are usually plaster on wood lath over rough framing that was never on modern spacing, and after a century nothing is square. I locate real timber before drilling, use anchors suited to plaster where there is no stud, and scribe shelves and trim to fit walls that lean. It takes longer, and it looks right.\"}]','',1,7,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (8,'midtown-toronto','Midtown Toronto','City of Toronto',2,43.7050000,-79.3980000,'M4N, M4P, M4R, M4S, M4T, M4V','Davisville Village\nYonge and Eglinton\nChaplin Estates\nMoore Park\nDeer Park\nSummerhill\nLawrence Park\nLytton Park\nAllenby\nNorth Toronto\nMount Pleasant West\nYonge-St Clair','Yonge-Eglinton Centre\nMount Pleasant Cemetery\nKay Gardner Beltline Trail\nEglinton Park\nSherwood Park\nJune Rowlands Park\nDavisville station\nEglinton station','forest-hill,rosedale,yorkville,leaside,the-annex,north-york','Midtown Toronto Handyman & Repairs','Midtown Toronto handyman for repairs, TV mounting, assembly, painting, flooring, decks and fixtures. Free quotes, price set before I start. Send a photo.','Handyman Services Across Midtown Toronto','Handyman work in Midtown Toronto follows the seasons more than people realize. March and April fill up with deck boards lifted by frost and fence posts leaning after a hard winter. By June it is patios, screens and outdoor lights. Autumn is weather stripping, sticking doors and the last of the exterior painting before the cold sets in.\n\nIndoors it runs all year. Most of these houses went up between the 1910s and the 1940s, and they were built well but built small. Closets are shallow, hallways are tight, and the plaster does not forgive a lazy anchor. I take on repairs, mounting, assembly, fixtures, flooring and painting across the streets between Yonge and the ravines, with a free quote and a price agreed before I start.','<h2>Decks, fences and the freeze-thaw cycle</h2><p>Backyards in Midtown Toronto are mostly small, fenced and heavily used, and they take a beating from November through March. Frost lifts deck posts, water gets into end grain, and a board that felt fine in September has gone soft by spring. Cedar fences lean where a post has rotted at ground level. I replace boards, reset and brace posts, rehang gates that drag and reseal what is worth sealing.</p><p>Ravine lots make it more interesting. Properties backing onto the Beltline or the slopes near Mount Pleasant Cemetery sit on ground that shifts, and a fence on a grade has to be stepped rather than run straight, or it will be leaning again next year.</p><h2>Inside the older Midtown Toronto houses</h2><p>Houses around Davisville, Chaplin Estates, Allenby and Lytton Park were built for a different way of living. Small kitchens, one bathroom, narrow back stairs and plaster over lath on almost every wall. Owners have added to them for a century, so it is normal to find three eras of wiring, two kinds of wall and a hallway that has been moved at some point.</p><p>Practical upshot: I check before I drill and keep the patching kit close. Hanging a heavy mirror or a floating shelf on old plaster needs anchors chosen for what is behind the surface, not whatever came in the box with the bracket.</p><h2>The condo towers along Yonge Street</h2><p>North and south of Eglinton station the Midtown Toronto towers keep going up, and a new unit always needs the same handful of jobs before it feels finished. Concrete ceilings and steel stud partitions mean the fixings are different from a house, and I bring both kinds so nothing holds the job up.</p><p>Access is the thing to sort out early. Buildings here normally expect the service elevator to be reserved, and a few want paperwork filed with management before anyone works inside a unit. Give me the window and I will carry everything up in one trip.</p><ul><li>TV mounting on concrete and steel stud walls</li><li>Blinds and curtain rails fitted in new units</li><li>Flat-pack furniture assembled inside small condos</li><li>Light fixture and dimmer replacements</li><li>Faucet and shower head swaps</li><li>Mirrors, shelving and artwork hung level</li></ul><h2>Getting several jobs done in one visit</h2><p>Small jobs add up. A wobbly towel bar, a dead dimmer, a door that will not latch, a bookcase still in its box and a dripping shut-off under the sink are each ten minutes to an hour. Booked one at a time they cost you five separate afternoons at home.</p><p>Send me a list, with photos where you can, and I will price the lot as one visit. Photos of the actual fixture, wall or leak let me arrive with the right parts and usually shorten the job. Anything that turns out to need a licensed electrician or plumber, such as a new circuit or a gas line, I will flag rather than attempt.</p>','Midtown Toronto sits on a ridge of pre-war housing with condo towers pushed up against it along Yonge Street. That mix means one visit can be plaster and original trim in a 1928 semi and the next can be steel stud and concrete twenty floors up. The ravines and the old Beltline corridor add sloping yards, uneven gates and fences that need stepping. Access ranges from a private drive to a booked service elevator.','Deck board replacement after frost heave\nCedar fence post resetting on sloping lots\nPlaster repairs in 1920s Davisville semis\nBacksplash tiling and cabinet hardware in small kitchens\nScreen door and window screen repairs\nBathroom vanity swaps in single-bathroom houses\nBlinds and curtain rails in Yonge Street condos\nGate hinges and latches adjusted after winter','[{\"q\":\"Do you repair decks and fences in Davisville and Chaplin Estates?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and spring is the busy stretch for it. Frost lifts posts over the winter, gates start dragging and the odd board goes soft underfoot. I replace boards, reset or brace posts, rehang gates and refit the hardware. On sloping lots near the ravines a fence has to be stepped rather than run straight, or it will lean again next year.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you do several small jobs in one visit near Yonge and Eglinton?\",\"a\":\"That is the way I prefer to work. Write the list out, add photos where you can, and I will price the whole visit up front. Doing a dozen ten-minute jobs together is far better use of the time than booking them one by one, and you only have to be home once instead of five times.\"},{\"q\":\"Will a wardrobe or sofa fit up the stairs in a 1930s Midtown Toronto house?\",\"a\":\"Often it will not, and it is worth checking before delivery day. Back stairs in these houses are narrow, and the turn at the top is usually the pinch point. Flat-pack is the easy answer, because I can carry the boxes up and build the unit in the room. For anything pre-assembled, measure the tightest turn first and send me the numbers.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you work in the newer towers near Eglinton station?\",\"a\":\"I do. New units tend to need the same short list: blinds up, a TV mounted, wardrobes built, a few fixtures swapped and the odd shelf hung level. Concrete and steel stud need different anchors, so I bring both. Check with your concierge about elevator booking and what the building asks of anyone doing work inside a unit.\"}]','',1,8,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (9,'the-beaches','The Beaches','City of Toronto',2,43.6710000,-79.2960000,'M4E, M4L, M4M','Kew Beach\nBalmy Beach\nWoodbine Beach\nThe Upper Beach\nFallingbrook\nBeach Triangle\nWoodbine Corridor\nBirch Cliff\nEast Toronto','Kew Gardens\nThe Beaches boardwalk\nWoodbine Beach Park\nAshbridges Bay Park\nQueen Street East\nGlen Stewart Ravine\nR.C. Harris Water Treatment Plant\nWoodbine Park','leslieville,greektown-danforth,east-york,scarborough,toronto','Handyman in The Beaches, Toronto','Handyman services in The Beaches, Toronto. Repairs, TV mounting, drywall and painting, doors, decks and fixture swaps. Free quotes, get in touch today.','Your Handyman in The Beaches, Toronto','I am Sercan, the handyman most people in The Beaches call for jobs that are too small for a contractor and too fiddly for a Saturday afternoon. Houses down here were built close to the lake, many of them a century ago as summer cottages that got winterised later. That history shows up in the walls, the floors and the porches. Ten years and more than 500 projects in, I know how these homes are put together.\n\nQuotes are free and you get a fixed price or an hourly rate before I start. The east end is regular ground for me, so a small job on the streets around Queen Street East is usually easy to fit into the calendar.','<h2>Century cottages, narrow lots and what that means</h2><p>Most of the housing between Queen Street East and the boardwalk went up between roughly 1900 and 1930, and plenty of it started life as a summer cottage that somebody winterised later. Framing is irregular. Studs turn up at odd spacings, joists run where you do not expect them, and behind many of the older walls there is lath and plaster rather than drywall. Plaster crumbles if you drive a screw straight into it, so a pilot hole and the right anchor do the job instead.</p><p>None of that is a problem, it only changes the method. I find the real framing before anything goes on a wall, and if a heavy mirror or a large television has nothing solid behind it, I say so and fit a backing board rather than trust a wall plug.</p><h2>The jobs I am called for most in The Beaches</h2><p>Weather off Lake Ontario is hard on anything outdoors. Porch railings, gate latches, fence boards and deck surfaces on the streets south of Queen take a beating through every freeze and thaw, so spring fills up with that kind of repair. Indoors the list is steadier: a door that has stopped latching, a dripping shower head, a light fixture or dimmer swapped for a new one, shelving fitted into a third-storey room with a sloped ceiling.</p><p>Bigger pieces of work come up as well. Flooring through a main floor, baseboards and trim, a bathroom updated with a new vanity and fresh caulking, a kitchen backsplash tiled. Those run over a few days and get quoted the same way as a two-hour job.</p><ul><li>Sticking doors and hinge adjustments in century homes</li><li>Porch railing and step repairs near the boardwalk</li><li>Plaster patching and repainting after a leak</li><li>TV mounting on walls with irregular stud spacing</li><li>Faucet, shower head and toilet replacements</li><li>Deck board and gate repairs after winter</li><li>Flat-pack wardrobes built in tight upstairs rooms</li></ul><h2>How a visit actually goes</h2><p>Send a message describing the work and add a photo if you can. Photos let me price accurately and turn up with the right parts, which matters in The Beaches because side-street parking is permit only in places and a trip out to a store costs everybody time. I usually reply within one business day with a fixed price or an hourly rate, and nothing begins until you have agreed to it.</p><p>Several small jobs can go into a single visit. People often save up a list: a curtain rod, a wobbly banister, a cabinet door that will not sit square, a smoke alarm bracket that has come loose. Batching them costs you less than four separate call-outs, and I would rather work that way.</p><h2>Where I stop and call in a trade</h2><p>Swapping an existing light fixture, dimmer, switch, faucet or toilet is ordinary work for me. Adding a new circuit, opening a panel, touching a gas line or altering anything structural is not, and in houses this old those questions do come up. When a job crosses that line I say so on the spot and point you to a licensed electrician or plumber instead of improvising.</p>','Working in The Beaches means planning around the lake. Salt-damp air and hard winter wind chew through outdoor wood faster than they do further inland, so railings, gates and deck boards need attention more often. Lots are narrow, laneways are tight and much of the side-street parking is permit only, so I arrive with the parts already sorted. Inside, plaster walls and odd stud spacing decide how anything heavy gets fixed to a wall.','Rebuilding wooden porch steps on Kew Beach streets\nPatching plaster walls in 1920s Beaches homes\nAdjusting doors that swell in lake humidity\nMounting TVs above narrow living room fireplaces\nReplacing weather stripping on old wooden doors\nRepairing and refastening back deck boards\nFitting shelving under sloped attic ceilings\nSwapping bathroom faucets in tight upstairs bathrooms','[{\"q\":\"My Beaches house has plaster walls. Can you still mount a heavy TV?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Plaster and lath is common in the older homes here and it will not hold a TV bracket on its own. I locate the studs behind it, sometimes with a probe rather than a stud finder because plaster confuses electronics. If the spacing does not line up with the bracket, I fit a painted backing board across two studs and mount to that. It ends up stronger than a drywall install.\"},{\"q\":\"Parking is tight on my street near the boardwalk. How do you handle that?\",\"a\":\"I ask about it before the visit. Tell me if your street is permit only, whether there is a spot in the lane or the driveway, or if the nearest legal parking is a couple of blocks away. Knowing in advance means I carry in what I need in one trip instead of walking back and forth. It also means the quote holds, because I am not losing an hour to a parking hunt.\"},{\"q\":\"My back deck took a beating over the winter. Is that a repair or a rebuild?\",\"a\":\"Usually a repair. Rot tends to start at the ends of boards, at the base of posts and anywhere water sits, so I probe a few spots and see how far it has gone. Swapping tired boards, refastening loose railings and tightening the frame gets most decks in The Beaches through several more seasons. If the structure underneath is gone, I will tell you plainly that it needs replacing.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you work in the Upper Beach and Fallingbrook as well?\",\"a\":\"Yes. I cover the whole stretch from Coxwell across to Victoria Park and up past Kingston Road, so the Upper Beach, Fallingbrook and Birch Cliff are all normal ground for me. The houses change as you go north of Queen, with more post-war builds mixed in among the older ones, but the work is much the same. Send a photo and I will price it.\"}]','',1,9,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (10,'leslieville','Leslieville','City of Toronto',2,43.6635000,-79.3320000,'M4M, M4L, M4J','Riverside\nSouth Riverdale\nStudio District\nGreenwood-Coxwell\nBlake-Jones\nGerrard India Bazaar\nWoodbine Corridor\nQueen Street East corridor','Jimmie Simpson Park\nGreenwood Park\nLeslie Grove Park\nAshbridge Estate\nThe Broadview Hotel\nQueen Street East\nEastern Avenue','the-beaches,greektown-danforth,downtown-toronto,east-york,toronto','Leslieville Handyman for Home Repairs','Leslieville handyman for door and window fixes, drywall repair, flooring, shelving, plumbing fixtures and flat-pack assembly. Free quotes, get in touch today.','Handyman Services in Leslieville, Toronto','Half the handyman calls I get in Leslieville start the same way: a door that has stopped closing properly. Rows of narrow Victorian and Edwardian houses along the streets off Queen have been settling on clay soil for well over a century, and frames go out of square by a few millimetres at a time. That is a morning of work, not a renovation. My name is Sercan and I do the work myself, which is why the person who quotes your job is the person who shows up to it.\n\nTen years and more than 500 projects in, I have learned that most of what people here need is small, specific and long overdue. Free quote, agreed price, then it gets done.','<h2>Small jobs, long lists and bigger projects</h2><p>A lot of what I do in Leslieville is a list rather than a single job. Someone moves into a semi off Dundas or Carlaw, lives with it for six months, then writes down eleven things: a towel bar, a stuck sash window, a hallway light that flickers on its old dimmer, two flat-pack wardrobes, a toilet that runs. Those go into one visit and one price. It works out better than calling four different people.</p><p>Bigger work happens too. Laminate or vinyl plank through a main floor, baseboards and trim after a paint job, a bathroom brought up to date with a new vanity, fresh caulking and a tiled kitchen backsplash. Those run over a few days and get quoted the same way, agreed before I start.</p><h2>Doors, windows and a house that has settled</h2><p>The workers\' cottages and bay-and-gable semis around here were put up on clay, and clay moves. Floors slope, door frames rack, and a latch that lined up in 1995 no longer meets the strike plate. Shimming a hinge, moving a strike or planing an edge fixes most of it in under an hour. Windows tell the same story, especially painted-shut sashes in houses that have been redecorated a dozen times.</p><p>Walls need care as well. Behind the plaster there is often no stud where a bracket wants one, and old renovations left patches of drywall over lath. I check before drilling and pick anchors to suit whatever is actually there.</p><h2>Lofts and mid-rise buildings along Queen and Eastern</h2><p>Plenty of people in Leslieville live in a converted industrial loft or one of the newer mid-rise buildings near Eastern Avenue. Those come with their own rules. Most want the service elevator booked ahead, some want contractors signed in at the desk, and a few restrict the hours when drilling can happen. Tell me what your building asks for when you request the quote and I will work around it.</p><p>Concrete ceilings and steel-stud partitions change the hardware, not the job. A television on a concrete wall needs masonry anchors and a bit of patience. On a hollow steel-stud wall it needs proper toggles. Either way it ends up level and solid.</p><h2>What to have ready before I arrive</h2><p>Clear access to the work area helps more than anything else. Beyond that:</p><ul><li>The parts you already bought, still boxed, in the room they belong in</li><li>Somewhere legal to park, or a note about the laneway</li><li>Paint tins or offcuts left over from the last decorator</li><li>Keys for the back gate, garage or storage locker</li><li>A quick word about pets and where they will be</li><li>Your full list, including the jobs you think are too small to mention</li></ul><p>One more thing worth saying plainly. Replacing an existing light, switch, dimmer, faucet or toilet is work I do every week. New wiring, a panel, gas or anything structural is not mine to touch, and I will hand you over to a licensed electrician or plumber when a job goes that way.</p>','Leslieville packs a lot into small footprints. Row houses sit inches apart, so ladders go up inside more often than out, and there is rarely a driveway to unload into. Most of the stock predates the First World War and has been renovated at least twice, which means the wall you drill into might be plaster, drywall or an old patch of both. Laneway garages and back gates are their own small repair category here.','Freeing painted-shut sash windows in Victorian semis\nRealigning door latches in settled row houses\nMounting TVs on concrete walls in Eastern Avenue lofts\nRebuilding back gates onto laneway fences\nLaying vinyl plank through narrow main floors\nReplacing vanities in small second-floor bathrooms\nAssembling flat-pack storage for tight bedrooms\nCaulking and refreshing older kitchen backsplashes','[{\"q\":\"My floors slope and nothing sits level. Can shelves still look right?\",\"a\":\"They can. In these old houses I set shelving to level rather than to the floor or the ceiling, because the eye reads a shelf against the horizon, not against a sagging joist. Where the gap at one end would look odd, I scribe the back edge or add a slim filler strip. It takes a few extra minutes and the result stops looking crooked.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you get to a Leslieville job through the laneway?\",\"a\":\"Usually, yes. A lot of the properties off Queen back onto a lane, and if the gate is wide enough that is the easiest way to bring in flooring, doors or a deck load. Let me know the gate width and whether the lane is paved or gravel. If it is blocked or too narrow, I plan for a longer carry and factor it into the quote.\"},{\"q\":\"My building near Carlaw wants paperwork before any contractor starts. What now?\",\"a\":\"Send me the building contractor rules along with your quote request. Most of what property managers ask for is straightforward: booking the service elevator, signing in at the desk, sticking to set working hours, laying protection in the corridor. I read through the list before a date is agreed, so nothing gets turned away at the door. If your building asks for documents I cannot provide, I will tell you that up front rather than waste your time.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you take on a whole list in one visit, or one job at a time?\",\"a\":\"One visit is usually better. If you send the full list with photos, I can work out how long it will take and price the lot, then bring everything needed in one go. Ten small jobs in an afternoon costs you less than ten separate visits. Anything that turns out bigger than it looked gets flagged before I touch it.\"}]','',1,10,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (11,'liberty-village','Liberty Village','City of Toronto',2,43.6378000,-79.4200000,'M6K, M5V, M6J','Niagara\nFort York\nKing West\nQueen West\nParkdale\nExhibition Place\nTrinity Bellwoods\nOrdnance Triangle','Liberty Market Building\nLamport Stadium\nExhibition Place\nBMO Field\nExhibition GO Station\nKing Street West\nCoronation Park','downtown-toronto,toronto,roncesvalles,high-park,the-junction','Liberty Village Handyman Services','Handyman in Liberty Village for TV mounting on concrete, shelving, faucet swaps, patching and flat-pack work in condos and lofts. Ask for a free quote today.','Handyman in Liberty Village, Toronto','Getting a handyman into a Liberty Village condo takes a bit of planning, and I have done it enough times to make it painless. Most buildings here want the service elevator booked, a sign-in at the concierge desk and drilling kept to set hours. Tell me your building rules when you ask for a quote and I will fit the visit around them.\n\nOnce I am through the door the work is straightforward. I am Sercan, I do every job myself, and after ten years and more than 500 projects I know what holds in a concrete wall and what does not. Quotes are free, the price is agreed first, and the suite is left clean.','<h2>Booking a visit in a tower or a loft</h2><p>Almost every building in Liberty Village has a process. The service elevator gets reserved for a window of time, the concierge needs a name at the desk, and some corridors need floor protection down before anything moves through them. Send me whatever your property manager requires along with your quote request and I will build the schedule around the slot you can get.</p><p>Parking is the other half of it. Streets around Hanna Avenue and Atlantic Avenue fill early and visitor spots go fast, so knowing whether there is a loading bay or an underground space changes how I plan the load-in. Small details, but they are the difference between a two-hour job and a four-hour one.</p><h2>Concrete, steel studs and what actually holds</h2><p>Suites in the newer towers are built around a poured concrete structure, with interior walls framed in steel or made of hollow partition board. There is no timber to screw into. A television bracket goes into concrete with masonry anchors, or into a steel-stud wall with heavy-duty toggles rated for the load. Floating shelves, wall cabinets and heavy mirrors get the same treatment.</p><p>Drilling into a ceiling slab is where I stop and check with the building first, because post-tensioned slabs are common and nobody wants to find a cable the hard way. Where a fixing is not safe, I say so and suggest another way to get the same result.</p><h2>What people in these suites call about</h2><p>Space is at a premium, so much of the work is about making a small floor plan behave.</p><ul><li>Television mounting on concrete and steel-stud walls</li><li>Flat-pack wardrobes and closet systems built in place</li><li>Floating shelves fitted over sofas and desks</li><li>Balcony and patio door adjustments that stop the dragging</li><li>Kitchen faucet and shower head replacements</li><li>Cabinet handles, soft-close hinges and drawer runners</li><li>Smart thermostats, video doorbells and cameras set up</li><li>Drywall patching and touch-up paint before a move-out</li></ul><p>Swapping an existing light fitting, dimmer, faucet or toilet is normal work. Anything that means new wiring or opening up a panel goes to a licensed electrician, and a plumbing fault behind the wall goes to a licensed plumber. In a shared building that is not a corner worth cutting.</p><h2>The brick-and-beam side of Liberty Village</h2><p>Not everything around here is glass. Old factory buildings off East Liberty Street and Jefferson Avenue were converted into hard lofts with exposed brick, timber posts and ceilings well over three metres. Those are a different job again. Brick needs the right bit and a slow speed or it blows out, timber beams are usually solid but not always where you expect, and anything above head height means bringing a taller ladder up in a narrow lift.</p><p>Older converted spaces also carry older fittings. A tap that has been in service since the conversion may have a seized shut-off valve behind it, so I check that before pulling anything apart. If the valve will not close, that becomes a plumber job before mine, and I would rather find it at the quote stage than halfway through.</p>','Liberty Village is dense, and that shapes the work more than the jobs themselves. Almost every visit begins with an elevator booking and a sign-in, materials come up in a lift rather than through a front door, and there is nowhere to spread out. Walls are concrete or steel stud, so ordinary wall plugs are useless. Working quietly inside the hours a building allows matters as much as doing the job well.','Mounting televisions on poured concrete condo walls\nAdjusting balcony doors that drag on the track\nAssembling closet systems in narrow condo bedrooms\nHanging heavy mirrors on steel-stud partition walls\nFitting shelving into brick-and-beam loft walls\nPatching drywall before a condo move-out inspection\nSetting up smart thermostats and video doorbells\nSwapping kitchen faucets in compact condo kitchens','[{\"q\":\"Will you deal with my concierge and the elevator booking?\",\"a\":\"I will work with whatever the building needs, but the booking itself usually has to come from you as the resident. Property managers rarely take a request from a contractor directly. Send me the available windows and I will pick one that fits the job, then arrive early enough to sign in at the desk and get the load up in one trip.\"},{\"q\":\"Can a TV be mounted on a Liberty Village concrete wall without wrecking it?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Concrete takes a hammer drill, a masonry bit and sleeve anchors, and the holes are small and tidy. The bracket ends up far stronger than one in a timber wall. If you move out later, the holes fill and paint like any others. What I avoid is drilling into a ceiling slab without checking, because many of these buildings use post-tensioned concrete.\"},{\"q\":\"I am moving out and need the suite patched and touched up. Do you do that?\",\"a\":\"That is a regular request here. Anchor holes, scuffed corners and dents from furniture all get filled, sanded and painted back to match. Bring me the original paint if you still have it, or the code from the building finishes list, because condo whites are not all the same. Give it a couple of days before the final walk-through so the paint has time to dry properly.\"},{\"q\":\"My building only allows drilling at certain hours. Does that work with your schedule?\",\"a\":\"It does. Most buildings allow noisy work through the middle of the day on weekdays and a shorter window on Saturday, which sits inside the hours I work anyway. I plan the quiet parts of the job around the edges: unpacking, layout, assembly, cleaning up. Tell me the rules when you book and the day gets arranged so nothing stalls.\"}]','',1,11,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (12,'yorkville','Yorkville','City of Toronto',2,43.6710000,-79.3930000,'M5R, M4W, M4Y, M5S','Bloor-Yorkville\nThe Annex\nRosedale\nBay Street Corridor\nChurch-Wellesley Village\nSummerhill\nUniversity-Rosedale\nYonge and Bloor','Royal Ontario Museum\nYorkville Village\nVillage of Yorkville Park\nRamsden Park\nBay Station\nBloor-Yonge Station\nManulife Centre\nCumberland Street','the-annex,rosedale,downtown-toronto,midtown-toronto,forest-hill','Yorkville Handyman for Homes and Condos','Handyman in Yorkville for art and mirror hanging, door fixes, cabinet hardware, caulking and flat-pack work in houses and condos. Ask for a free quote today.','Handyman Work in Yorkville, Toronto','A handyman working in Yorkville spends as much time protecting the room as fixing the thing in it. Floors are finished, walls are papered or freshly painted, and art is already hanging. So I bring drop sheets, take my shoes off, contain the dust and clean up before I leave. That is not a sales line, it is just how work gets done in a finished home.\n\nMy name is Sercan. I run this on my own, so the person who quotes the job is the one holding the drill. Free quote, agreed price before anything starts, and if something is not right afterwards I come back and sort it.','<h2>Two kinds of home on the same block</h2><p>Step off Bloor onto Hazelton or Scollard and you are among brick Victorian houses from the 1870s and 1880s, most of them altered several times over. Move a hundred metres and you are in a tower, some of them from the 1980s, some finished within the last few years. The jobs asked for are similar. The walls behind them are not.</p><p>Old houses in Yorkville hide lath and plaster, timber that has been drying out for 140 years, and stud spacing that follows no rule. Condo suites give you concrete and steel studs instead. I check what is there before the first hole goes in, because guessing costs a repair on top of the original job.</p><h2>Hanging art, mirrors and screens so they stay put</h2><p>Much of my work here is putting things on walls, and the value of what gets hung leaves no room for a fixing that might hold. Heavy framed pieces go onto proper picture hooks or into solid backing. Gallery arrangements get laid out on the floor first, measured, then marked so the spacing reads evenly across the wall. Mirrors get bearing support underneath, not just screws at the top.</p><p>Televisions are the same principle with more weight. On plaster I locate the framing and, if the spacing is wrong for the bracket, fit a discreet backing board. On concrete I use masonry anchors. Cables get run out of sight wherever the wall construction allows it.</p><h2>The small jobs that keep getting put off</h2><p>Most calls in Yorkville are for things that have been annoying somebody for months.</p><ul><li>Doors that rub the frame or will not stay shut</li><li>Cabinet hinges and handles replaced in a fitted kitchen</li><li>Dimmers and smart switches swapped for existing ones</li><li>Dripping taps and running toilets put right</li><li>Wardrobe and shelving systems assembled and secured to the wall</li><li>Fresh caulking around baths, sinks and shower trays</li><li>Loose handrails and stair spindles made solid again</li></ul><p>Several of those in one visit is the sensible way to do it. Write the list, send photos, and I will price the whole thing before I set out.</p><h2>Bigger work, and where I hand it over</h2><p>Larger projects run here as well. A bathroom refreshed with a new vanity and fresh caulking, a backsplash tiled, flooring and trim replaced through a suite, a room repainted after drywall repairs. Those take several days and get quoted as one price agreed in advance, with materials supplied and receipts handed over, or bought by you if you prefer.</p><p>There are limits I keep to. Replacing an existing light fitting, switch, dimmer, faucet or toilet is my work. New circuits, panel changes, gas appliances, anything structural, and any job on a designated heritage property that needs a permit are not. When a job reaches that point I say so and you get a licensed electrician or plumber in.</p>','Yorkville puts century houses and glass towers within a block of each other, and the approach changes depending on which door I walk through. Access is the constant. Loading zones on Cumberland and Yorkville Avenue are short-term, most towers want the service lift booked, and there is rarely a spot to leave anything on the street. Finishes are good here too, so protection goes down before the tools come out.','Hanging framed art on old plaster walls\nLevelling gallery walls in condo living rooms\nAdjusting heavy Victorian doors on worn hinges\nReplacing cabinet hardware in fitted kitchens\nSecuring tall wardrobes to concrete suite walls\nRecaulking bathrooms in older Bloor Street towers\nFixing loose stair handrails in narrow townhouses\nSwapping dimmers and smart switches for existing ones','[{\"q\":\"Can you protect the floors and furniture while you work?\",\"a\":\"That is standard. Drop sheets go down on the route in and around the work area, furniture gets covered or moved back, and I use dust extraction on the tools that make a mess. Sanding filler or cutting drywall gets sealed off with plastic where it needs to be. At the end I vacuum and wipe down, so the room is usable the same evening.\"},{\"q\":\"I work downtown and can only be home in the evening. Does that work?\",\"a\":\"It can. I work Monday to Saturday from eight in the morning until eight at night, so a start after five is possible for smaller jobs. In a condo it depends on your building, since noisy work is often restricted to daytime hours. Quiet jobs like assembly, hanging pictures or fitting cabinet hardware are usually fine in the evening. Sunday visits are by appointment.\"},{\"q\":\"The vanity I want comes from a showroom on Davenport. Can you fit something I supply?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Plenty of people here buy their own fittings, and I am happy to install what you supply. Send me the model or a link before the date so I can check the rough-in dimensions and the waste position against what is already there. If it will not fit without moving plumbing inside the wall, I will tell you before you buy rather than after.\"},{\"q\":\"My unit is in an older Yorkville tower from the eighties. Anything different about those?\",\"a\":\"A few things. Interior walls in that era are often solid block or plaster over concrete rather than hollow drywall, so fixings need masonry anchors and the dust is heavier. Bathroom and kitchen fittings from the original build have usually been changed once already, and the connections underneath can be tired. I check the shut-off valve first and tell you if it needs replacing before a tap swap goes ahead.\"}]','',1,12,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (13,'high-park','High Park','City of Toronto',2,43.6465000,-79.4637000,'M6P, M6R, M6S','High Park North\nHigh Park-Swansea\nSwansea\nRunnymede\nWindermere\nSunnyside\nBloor West Village\nThe Junction Triangle\nRoncesvalles Village\nParkdale\nBaby Point\nLambton Mills','High Park\nGrenadier Pond\nColborne Lodge\nHigh Park Zoo\nBloor Street West\nHigh Park Station\nSunnyside Bathing Pavilion\nRennie Park','roncesvalles,bloor-west-village,the-junction,liberty-village,toronto','Handyman in High Park, Toronto','Handyman services in High Park, Toronto: repairs, TV mounting on plaster walls, drywall, painting, flooring and fixture swaps. Ask for a free quote.','Handyman Services in High Park, Toronto','I am a handyman working in High Park, and most of the homes I get called to were built long before anyone thought about hanging a flat-screen on a wall. Behind the plaster there is lath, or brick, or nothing where you expect a stud. I find out what I am fixing into before a hole gets drilled.\n\nMy name is Sercan and I do the work myself, so the person who prices the job is the person who shows up with the drill. One visit can cover several small things: shelves, a sticking door, a leaking faucet, a light fixture swap. Send a few photos with your quote request and I will usually have pricing back to you within one business day.','<h2>What is behind the walls in a High Park house</h2><p>Plenty of the housing here went up between 1900 and the late 1920s, brick detached homes and semis with wood trim, tall baseboards and plaster over lath. Plaster is not drywall. It cracks in lines rather than dents, it chips out when a screw goes in carelessly, and a magnet stud finder will lie to you because the lath nails are everywhere.</p><p>So I probe before I mount. For a heavy television or a floating shelf I want to be into solid framing, or into masonry with the right anchor, not into a hollow pocket that lets go six months later. Those extra ten minutes at the start are the difference between a job that holds and a repair I have to come back for.</p><h2>Older apartments and the newer buildings near the park</h2><p>The mid-century apartment blocks along High Park Avenue and Quebec Avenue are mostly concrete and block. That changes the tools and it changes the fixings: hammer drill, masonry bits and sleeve anchors instead of drywall toggles. Balcony doors in those buildings drag after decades of settling, and usually it is the roller or the strike plate at fault rather than the door itself.</p><p>Newer condo buildings near Bloor Street West run to their own routine. Most want the service elevator booked ahead and paperwork filed with the property manager before a trade carries anything through the lobby. Tell me what your building asks for when you request the quote and I will say straight away whether I can meet it.</p><h2>Common calls in this part of the west end</h2><p>Some weeks it is all shelving. Other weeks it is doors and door hardware. A short list of what gets asked for around here most often:</p><ul><li>TV mounting on plaster and lath living room walls</li><li>Hairline crack repair and repainting in older bedrooms</li><li>Interior doors planed and rehung after seasonal swelling</li><li>Faucet, shower head and toilet replacements</li><li>Ceiling fans, dimmers and smart switches swapped for existing ones</li><li>Flat-pack wardrobes built in narrow third-floor rooms</li><li>Baseboard and quarter round after vinyl plank flooring</li><li>Deck boards and fence pickets replaced after winter</li></ul><h2>How I price and how I work</h2><p>Quotes are free and carry no obligation. Before anything starts you get either a fixed price for the job or an hourly rate, agreed up front, so there is no surprise at the end. Materials can come from me with the receipts handed over, or you buy your own and I fit what you have chosen.</p><p>Anything needing a licensed trade gets said out loud rather than fudged. Replacing a light fixture, a dimmer, a faucet or a toilet is my work. New circuits, panel work, gas lines or moving a drain is not, and I will point you to a licensed electrician or plumber instead of taking the job on.</p><p>Dust sheets go down and the offcuts leave with me. Ten years and more than five hundred projects have taught me that people remember a clean floor as clearly as they remember the finished shelf. If something is not right after I go, tell me and I will come back to it.</p>','Work in High Park is shaped by the age of the housing and by the park itself. Lots back onto ravine land, so decks and fences sit on ground that moves and gates need re-hanging most springs. Inside, plaster walls, painted-over hinges and doors trimmed for a floor that has since been replaced are all normal. Streets are narrow and permit parking is tight, so I load in once and stay put.','TV mounting on plaster and lath walls\nMasonry anchors for shelving in concrete apartments\nBalcony door rollers adjusted in mid-century blocks\nBack gates re-hung on ravine-side fences\nBaseboard replaced around old radiator pipes\nStorm and screen door hardware repairs\nAttic bedroom shelving under sloped ceilings\nKitchen backsplash tiling in 1920s semis','[{\"q\":\"Can you mount a TV on a plaster wall in an old High Park house?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and it is one of my most common calls here. Plaster over lath will not hold a bracket on its own, so I find the real framing behind it, or I drill and anchor into the brick where the wall is solid masonry. Any test holes get patched and touched up before I leave, and I tidy the cables rather than leaving them hanging.\"},{\"q\":\"My building near Bloor Street West makes trades book the service elevator. How do you handle that?\",\"a\":\"Send me the building rules along with your quote request. Most places want the elevator booked for a window of time and some paperwork filed with management first. I will read what your property manager requires and tell you honestly whether I can meet it before anything is booked, so nobody wastes a morning being turned away at the desk.\"},{\"q\":\"The doors upstairs stick every summer and rattle every winter. Can that be sorted properly?\",\"a\":\"Usually, yes. Solid wood doors in older homes take on moisture in humid months and shrink back once the heating runs. I plane the binding edge just enough, seal the bare wood so it stops soaking up damp, then reset hinges and move the strike plate a few millimetres if the latch is missing. That normally holds through both seasons.\"},{\"q\":\"The back yard slopes toward the ravine and the deck steps feel spongy. Is that something you take on?\",\"a\":\"I do deck and step repairs, so long as it is repair rather than a rebuild that needs an engineer. Soft boards, loose stringers, a wobbly railing and posts that have shifted are all fair game. I will look at what is causing it, replace the boards and hardware that have gone, and tell you plainly if the frame underneath is past saving.\"}]','',1,13,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (14,'roncesvalles','Roncesvalles','City of Toronto',2,43.6470000,-79.4494000,'M6R, M6K, M6P','Roncesvalles Village\nSorauren\nParkdale\nSouth Parkdale\nSunnyside\nHoward Park\nBrockton Village\nLittle Portugal\nDundas West\nHigh Park-Swansea\nLiberty Village\nThe Junction Triangle','Roncesvalles Avenue\nSorauren Avenue Park\nRevue Cinema\nSt. Josephs Health Centre\nDundas West Station\nSunnyside Bathing Pavilion\nHigh Park','high-park,liberty-village,the-junction,bloor-west-village,toronto','Handyman in Roncesvalles, Toronto','Handyman services in Roncesvalles for repairs, flat-pack assembly, plaster patching, painting, porches and fixture swaps in older semis. Get a free quote.','Roncesvalles Handyman for Home Repairs','Being a handyman in Roncesvalles means half the job is getting in the door. The houses are narrow semis and row houses, the staircases turn twice on the way to the third floor, and parking out front is permit only for most of the day. I plan the load-in before I plan the work: what fits up the stairs, what has to be built in the room, and where the van can sit while I do it.\n\nI am Sercan and I run this on my own, so you deal with one person from the quote to the last screw. Send photos with your request and I will usually price it inside one business day.','<h2>Narrow houses and the stairs that come with them</h2><p>Most of the housing between Queen and Dundas went up as Victorian and Edwardian semis and row houses on lots barely five metres wide. Rooms are small, hallways are tight, and the staircase to the top floor was built for people carrying a good deal less than a boxed wardrobe. A fair amount of my work here is measured before anything is bought.</p><p>If a bed frame or a wardrobe will not go up in one piece, I build it in the room. If a sheet of drywall will not turn the corner at the landing, I cut it down on the porch first. Tell me the room and the floor when you ask for a quote and I will flag anything likely to cause trouble before the delivery van arrives.</p><h2>Plaster, old framing and walls people want to hang things on</h2><p>Century semis around here are plaster over lath on original studs, and those studs sit where the carpenter felt like putting them rather than where a modern bracket expects. Party walls between two halves of a semi are often solid brick. Neither is a problem, but each takes a different fixing, and guessing is how a mirror ends up on the floor.</p><p>Shelving in the alcoves beside a chimney breast is a standing request across Roncesvalles, along with television brackets, heavy artwork and closet systems in bedrooms with sloped attic ceilings. I check what is behind the surface first, then fix into something that will still be holding in ten years.</p><h2>What people on and around Roncesvalles Avenue call about</h2><p>Much of it is small and specific rather than big and dramatic. Grouping several items into one booking usually costs less than three separate visits, so save the list up and hand me the whole thing.</p><ul><li>Flat-pack furniture built in third-floor bedrooms</li><li>Front porch railings, treads and porch light swaps</li><li>Sticking basement apartment doors planed and re-hung</li><li>Plaster crack patching and repainting in hallways</li><li>Cabinet hardware and backsplash tiling in galley kitchens</li><li>Under-sink leaks, faucets and toilet replacements</li><li>Laneway garage doors, gates and fence panels repaired</li></ul><h2>Pricing, materials and the limits</h2><p>Quotes cost nothing and commit you to nothing. Before a tool comes out you have a fixed price for the job or an hourly rate, agreed first, and I hold to it. Materials can come from me with receipts passed over, or I will fit exactly what you have already bought.</p><p>There is work in these houses I will not take on, and I would rather say so on the phone than halfway through the afternoon. Replacing an existing light fixture, dimmer, faucet or toilet is fine. New wiring, a panel, a gas line or anything structural in a house this age belongs with a licensed electrician or plumber, and I will tell you that plainly.</p><p>Everything gets swept up and the offcuts leave in my van, which matters more than usual in a house where the living room is also the hallway. If something is not sitting right a week later, call me and I will come back and put it right.</p>','Roncesvalles sits between the lake and the rail corridor, and that shows up in the work. Houses stand close together, so noise and access matter and I keep to sensible hours. Back laneways mean garage doors, gates and fence panels take a beating from ice every winter. Front porches are original on many homes and need boards, paint and railings looked after. Parking is permit controlled, so I unload once and leave the van put.','Flat-pack wardrobes assembled in third-floor bedrooms\nAlcove shelving beside original chimney breasts\nFront porch boards and railing repairs\nLaneway garage door and gate adjustments\nPlaster hallway cracks patched and repainted\nBasement apartment doors planed and re-hung\nBacksplash tiling in narrow galley kitchens\nScreen and storm door hardware replaced','[{\"q\":\"My street is permit parking only. Where are you going to park the van?\",\"a\":\"Give me the block when you book and I will work it out beforehand. Normally I pull up long enough to unload, then move to whatever legal space is nearby and walk the last stretch. If the street is genuinely full at that hour I will suggest a quieter time slot instead, or build the extra carrying into the quote so nothing changes later.\"},{\"q\":\"The wardrobe I ordered will not fit up my staircase. Can it still be assembled upstairs?\",\"a\":\"Almost always. Flat-pack furniture arrives in panels, so the boxes go up the stairs and the unit gets built in the room where it will live. That is the usual answer in these houses. What I need to know first is whether the finished piece can stand upright in a room with a low ceiling, so measure the height and send it over.\"},{\"q\":\"I rent out the basement unit. Can you handle the repairs between tenants?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and turnovers suit a single booking well. Filling picture holes, patching plaster, repainting, adjusting a door that no longer latches, swapping a tired light fitting and replacing a dripping faucet all fit inside one visit if I know the full list ahead of time. Send photos of the unit and I will price the whole lot together.\"},{\"q\":\"The front porch on my century home has soft boards and a loose railing. Do you repair those?\",\"a\":\"I do. Porch work in Roncesvalles is mostly replacing the boards that have gone, resetting or replacing railing posts, tightening spindles and repainting so the new wood matches. If the structure underneath has rotted through to the joists I will tell you honestly, because at that point it is a rebuild rather than a repair and you deserve to know early.\"}]','',1,14,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (15,'the-annex','The Annex','City of Toronto',2,43.6700000,-79.4050000,'M5R, M5S, M6G','Seaton Village\nHarbord Village\nHuron-Sussex\nSussex-Ulster\nMirvish Village\nPalmerston\nSouth Annex\nYorkville\nCasa Loma\nDupont\nChristie Pits','Casa Loma\nSpadina Museum\nJean Sibelius Square\nChristie Pits Park\nBathurst Station\nDupont Station\nBloor Street West\nRoyal Ontario Museum','yorkville,downtown-toronto,forest-hill,rosedale,toronto','Handyman in The Annex, Toronto','Handyman services in The Annex for sticking doors, old windows, plaster repair, painting, fixtures and assembly in century homes. Free quote, no obligation.','Handyman Services for The Annex, Toronto','A handyman in The Annex spends a lot of time on doors and windows that no longer sit where they were built to sit. Houses here mostly date from the 1880s to the early 1900s: tall brick and sandstone, wide staircases, plaster walls, solid wood doors painted a dozen times over. After more than a century of settling, the frame is no longer square and the door has never been told.\n\nI am Sercan and I do the work myself rather than sending someone else. That suits old houses, because the person who looked at the problem is the person who fixes it. Photos with your quote request help me price faster, and a reply usually reaches you within one business day.','<h2>Doors, windows and a century of settling</h2><p>The single most common call I get in The Annex is a door that binds, drops or refuses to latch. Sometimes the hinge screws have chewed out the old softwood and want longer screws driven into solid framing. Sometimes the strike plate needs moving a few millimetres. Sometimes the door itself has swollen and needs planing along one edge, then priming so it stops drinking moisture every July.</p><p>Original wood windows tell the same story. Sashes painted shut, cords gone, weather stripping perished, a storm window rattling all winter. I free them off, replace the hardware and reseal where the draught is actually coming from, which is far more often the frame than the glass.</p><h2>Big houses cut into apartments</h2><p>Plenty of the larger homes here were divided into flats decades ago, and a good share of my work follows the turnover between tenants. That means patching plaster, filling picture holes, repainting a hallway, swapping a tired light fitting for something current, adjusting a unit door so it closes properly and dealing with the slow leak under a bathroom sink.</p><p>Landlords usually want the whole list handled in one booking at a price agreed first. Tenants usually want shelves, a television bracket and a wardrobe built without marking anything they will be charged for on the way out. Both suit me. I just need to know which walls I am allowed to put a hole in.</p><h2>Regular requests around Bathurst and Bloor</h2><p>Some of it is finishing work and some of it is putting right what a previous owner improvised on a Sunday. A rough sample of recent work in The Annex:</p><ul><li>Interior doors planed, re-hinged and re-latched</li><li>Painted-shut sash windows freed and weather stripped</li><li>Plaster patching and repainting between tenants</li><li>Bookshelves anchored to solid framing in high-ceilinged rooms</li><li>Ceiling light fixtures and dimmers swapped like for like</li><li>Bathroom vanity, faucet and toilet replacements</li><li>Stair spindles, handrails and squeaking treads tightened</li></ul><h2>Quoting, materials and where I stop</h2><p>Quotes are free and asking for one commits you to nothing. Before any tool comes out you have a fixed price or an hourly rate in plain terms. I can bring the materials and pass you the receipts, or simply fit what you have already picked out yourself.</p><p>Old houses hide things, so I raise the awkward part early rather than late. Knob-and-tube behind a ceiling rose, a drain that has been reworked three times by three different people, a wall that turns out to be carrying load: those go to a licensed electrician, a licensed plumber or the right specialist. Replacing an existing fixture, switch, dimmer, faucet or toilet sits squarely inside my work.</p><p>Dust sheets go down before I start, offcuts leave with me and the floor gets swept properly. Anything not right afterwards, tell me and I will be back to sort it. That has been the arrangement across ten years and more than five hundred projects.</p>','Houses in The Annex are tall, old and rarely square, which explains most of the repair work. Plaster over lath, original wood windows, staircases running up to a third floor and rooms that have been divided and re-divided since the 1970s. Street parking is metered or permit only and private driveways are rare, so I unload once and carry. Many buildings hold several households, so I keep the noise down and work sensible hours.','Painted-shut sash windows freed and re-corded\nDoors planed and re-hung in settled frames\nPlaster patching between tenants in divided houses\nHeavy bookshelves anchored in high-ceilinged rooms\nSqueaking stair treads and loose handrails tightened\nTrim and baseboard repairs beside old radiators\nVanity swaps in narrow upstairs bathrooms\nDraught proofing around original front doors','[{\"q\":\"My bedroom door will not latch any more. Is one small job like that worth booking?\",\"a\":\"It is, though you will get better value if you walk the house first and write down everything else that has been annoying you. A latch on its own takes minutes. A latch, two loose handrail spindles, a dripping faucet and a light fitting come to one visit at one price. Most people are surprised how much fits into a single booking.\"},{\"q\":\"The house is split into three units. Can you work while tenants are home?\",\"a\":\"Usually yes, and I do it often in The Annex. Give me notice of which units are occupied and I will keep noisy work inside reasonable hours, cover floors in shared hallways and clear my tools out of the stairwell between trips. If a tenant needs to be present for access, arrange that with them and tell me the time window.\"},{\"q\":\"The original windows have been painted shut for years. Can they be made to open again?\",\"a\":\"Often they can. I score and free the paint seal, ease the sash, sort out the hardware and add weather stripping so it seals when shut. Older paint layers need care, so I keep sanding light and contained rather than opening it all up. If a sash is rotted through or the frame has failed, I will say so instead of forcing it.\"},{\"q\":\"Parts of the wiring here are knob-and-tube. Will you still change a light fixture?\",\"a\":\"Not on a circuit that is still knob-and-tube. I will take the fitting down, look at what is actually there and tell you what I find. Old wiring on that kind of circuit is a licensed electrician\'s job, and I would be doing you no favours pretending otherwise. On modern circuits, a like-for-like fixture, dimmer or switch swap is straightforward.\"}]','',1,15,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (16,'leaside','Leaside','City of Toronto',2,43.7050000,-79.3670000,'M4G, M4H, M4P','North Leaside\nSouth Leaside\nBennington Heights\nGovernors Bridge\nThorncliffe Park\nMoore Park\nDavisville Village\nSunnybrook\nEast York','Bayview Avenue\nLaird Drive\nLeaside Memorial Community Gardens\nTrace Manes Park\nSunnybrook Park\nSerena Gundy Park\nSunnybrook Health Sciences Centre\nEglinton Avenue East','east-york,don-mills,midtown-toronto,north-york,toronto','Handyman in Leaside, Toronto','Handyman services in Leaside for repairs, TV mounting, bathroom and kitchen updates, decks, fences and fixture swaps. Ask me for a free quote today.','Handyman Services in Leaside and Bennington Heights','Most of the calls I take as a handyman in Leaside come from houses built between the 1930s and the early 1950s, when the whole area was laid out street by street. Solid brick, two storeys, plaster walls, small original bathrooms, a single garage at the end of a long side drive. They were built well. They were also built before anyone owned a television the size of a dining table.\n\nSercan is my name and I do every job myself, from the quote through to the cleanup. Repairs, mounting, drywall and paint, fixtures, flooring, trim and assembly are all day-to-day work here, and several small items can go into one booking. Send photos with your request and I will usually reply within one business day.','<h2>A planned suburb, and what that means for repairs</h2><p>Leaside was drawn up as a whole community rather than filled in lot by lot, so the houses run to a handful of patterns. That helps. Once you have worked in a few, you know roughly where the framing sits, how thick the plaster is, and that the upstairs bathroom will be about two metres square with a window in exactly the wrong place for the vanity somebody has bought.</p><p>Original doors are solid wood and heavy, which is good news for the hardware and hard on hinges that have carried them for eighty years. Floors are usually strip oak under whatever went down on top later. Additions and second-storey rebuilds are everywhere, so half a house can be lath and plaster and the other half modern drywall, with a small step where the two meet.</p><h2>The jobs that stack up in a Leaside house</h2><p>Families around here tend to keep a running list and call once it is long enough to justify a visit. That approach works well, because batching costs you less than booking each item on its own.</p><ul><li>TV brackets fixed into solid framing above brick fireplaces</li><li>Cabinet hardware and cupboard doors realigned in original kitchens</li><li>Vanity, faucet and toilet swaps in small upstairs bathrooms</li><li>Fitted closet shelving and hanging rails in bedrooms</li><li>Basement rec room drywall repairs and repainting</li><li>Ceiling fans, dimmers and smart switches replaced like for like</li><li>Baseboards, quarter round and casing after flooring work</li><li>Garage side doors and locks adjusted on long side drives</li></ul><h2>Apartments and condos on the edges</h2><p>The high-rise blocks around Thorncliffe Park and the newer buildings near Bayview and Eglinton are a different animal. Concrete and block walls want a hammer drill and proper anchors. Hollow partition walls want the right toggle rather than a plastic plug pushed in and hoped for. Getting that wrong is how a shelf full of books ends up on the carpet.</p><p>Buildings also have rules. Most ask for the service elevator to be booked in advance and paperwork filed with the property manager before a trade carries anything through the lobby. Let me know what yours requires when you ask for the quote and I will tell you right away whether I can meet it.</p><h2>Outside work, and the jobs I hand to a licensed trade</h2><p>Back yards here sit on clay, and clay moves. Deck boards cup, gate posts lean, fence panels work loose and a gate that swung fine in October scrapes the path by April. Spring and early summer are when most of that gets booked, along with railing repairs and swapping out a few boards rather than tearing off the whole deck.</p><p>Some things are not mine to do and I say so on the first call. Changing out an existing light fitting, dimmer, faucet or toilet is mine to do. New circuits, panel work, gas appliances, anything structural or a roof goes to a licensed electrician, plumber or the right specialist, and I would rather send you there than take your money for it.</p><p>Quotes are free, the price is settled before I start, and the work area is left clean. If something is not right afterwards, tell me and I will come back to it.</p>','Two things shape the work in Leaside: the age of the original houses and how many of them have been added to. A 1940s brick two-storey with a recent back extension needs two different approaches under one roof, and the join between them is where most patching happens. Bathrooms and closets are small by current standards, so careful measuring matters more than muscle. Driveways run long and narrow, which makes parking easy and manoeuvring less so.','TV mounted above a 1940s brick fireplace\nOriginal solid wood doors re-hinged and adjusted\nSmall upstairs bathroom vanity replacements\nDeck boards replaced after a wet spring\nFence gates re-hung on shifting clay ground\nBasement rec room drywall patched and painted\nAnchors set into concrete condo walls\nTrim matched between original rooms and additions','[{\"q\":\"Can you mount a television above the brick fireplace in a Leaside living room?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Brick takes a proper masonry anchor and holds very well once it is drilled correctly, so the bracket is rarely the hard part. Cables are the question. I can run them inside the wall where the cavity allows it, or hide them in a slim surface channel painted to match. A new power outlet behind the screen is an electrician\'s job, not mine.\"},{\"q\":\"Half the house is plaster and the addition is drywall. Can a patch be made to match?\",\"a\":\"It can, with a bit of patience. Plaster sits at a different thickness and has a different surface texture, so I build the repair up in coats rather than filling it once, then feather it wide and match the texture before priming. Paint on old walls has usually shifted in colour too, so I would rather repaint a full wall than leave an obvious square.\"},{\"q\":\"The fence posts and gate shift every year. Is there a fix that lasts on this clay soil?\",\"a\":\"Ground movement is normal here, so the honest answer is that nothing is permanent. What helps is resetting the post properly with drainage below it, re-hanging the gate with heavier hinges, and adding an adjustable brace so small seasonal changes can be taken out with a spanner rather than a fresh dig. That usually buys you several quiet years.\"},{\"q\":\"New flooring is going down next month. Do you do the trim and door work afterwards?\",\"a\":\"Finishing work after a floor goes down is a regular booking for me. Once the floor is in I fit baseboards and quarter round, adapt or replace door casing where the height has changed, undercut doors that now catch, add transition strips at doorways and refit any vents. Book me for the week after the flooring crew leaves and it can all be done in one go.\"}]','',1,16,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (17,'don-mills','Don Mills','City of Toronto',2,43.7350000,-79.3460000,'M3A, M3B, M3C, M2J','Banbury\nParkwoods\nDonalda\nGraydon Hall\nFlemingdon Park\nHenry Farm\nVictoria Village\nYork Mills\nThree Valleys\nThe Peanut\nWynford Heights','The Shops at Don Mills\nEdwards Gardens\nToronto Botanical Garden\nE.T. Seton Park\nWilket Creek Park\nOntario Science Centre\nDonalda Club\nLawrence Avenue East','north-york,leaside,willowdale,east-york,scarborough','Don Mills Handyman Services in Toronto','Handyman in Don Mills, Toronto for repairs, furniture assembly, TV mounting, drywall patching and fixture swaps in houses and condo towers. Get a free quote.','Handyman Services in Don Mills, Toronto','I am the handyman most people in Don Mills call once the list on the fridge gets too long. My name is Sercan, and I run Worldwide Handyman on my own, so the person who gives you the price is the person who turns up and does the work. Ten years in the trade and more than 500 finished projects means very little in these houses surprises me any more.\n\nMost homes around here went up in one push in the 1950s and 1960s, and they wear in similar ways. Doors drop out of square, trim splits, taps drip, and walls argue when you try to hang something heavy on them. I sort that out, sweep up after myself, and get out of your way.','<h2>What sixty years of the same housing stock means</h2><p>Don Mills was laid out as a planned community after the war, and the street pattern still shows it. Bungalows, side-splits and back-splits sit on wide lots, with low-rise apartment blocks set behind them. The framing in these houses is generally sound. It is the finishes that have moved.</p><p>Wall surfaces are not consistent from room to room. Some are early drywall, some are plaster over lath, and some carry panelling that a previous owner nailed straight over the top. That matters the moment a heavy mirror or a floating shelf is going up. So I check what is actually behind the surface, choose the anchor to suit, and never assume a stud is sitting exactly where the tape says it should be.</p><p>Split levels bring their own puzzle. Half flights of stairs create short awkward walls, tight landings, and railings that have worked loose under decades of hands.</p><h2>Working in the apartment blocks and condo towers</h2><p>Two very different kinds of building share this area. Concrete rental slabs from the 1960s stand along the main roads, and newer towers have gone up beside the shops at Don Mills and Lawrence. Both ask for a different approach than a house does.</p><p>Ceilings and dividing walls are usually poured concrete or block, so a television bracket needs a masonry bit and proper sleeve anchors rather than the plastic plugs that come in the box. Drilling takes longer than most people expect and makes more dust. Buildings here also tend to want the service elevator booked ahead and the paperwork sorted with the property manager before anything is carried through the lobby. Send me the building rules with your enquiry and the visit gets planned around them.</p><h2>The jobs these homes call for most</h2><p>Some requests come round again and again in this part of Toronto:</p><ul><li>Rehanging interior doors that have dropped and catch on the frame</li><li><strong>TV mounting</strong> on concrete in apartments and on plaster in older houses</li><li>Patching and repainting hallway walls after years of traffic</li><li>Swapping tired faucets, shower heads and toilets for new ones</li><li>Changing light fixtures, ceiling fans and dimmers like for like</li><li>Assembling flat-pack furniture, then anchoring it to the wall</li><li>Fitting closet doors, sliding tracks, shelving and coat hooks</li><li>Sorting sticking screens, handles and weather stripping on side doors</li></ul><p>Anything small enough to feel silly booking on its own is worth adding to the list. Batching five little jobs into a single visit costs you less than five separate trips, and it makes better use of my day as well.</p><h2>How a visit actually works</h2><p>Nothing is charged for a quote, and asking for one puts you under no obligation. Send a short description with a photo or two, because pictures let me price properly instead of guessing, and I usually reply within one business day. Before any tools come out, you and I agree either a fixed price for the job or an hourly rate, so the number does not move once I am standing in your hallway.</p><p>Materials can go either way. I supply them and hand over the receipts, or you buy exactly what you want and I fit it. The work area is left clean before I go, and anything that is not right afterwards gets a return visit.</p><p>There are jobs I will not take on, and I would rather say so early. New wiring circuits, panel work, gas appliances, structural changes and roofing belong with a licensed electrician, plumber or contractor. Replacing an existing light fixture, dimmer, faucet or toilet is ordinary work, and I do it most weeks.</p>','Whole streets in Don Mills went up quickly and to a single plan, so they share the same construction and the same faults. Learn one house and you have learned twenty. The other half of the work is vertical: concrete apartment blocks from the sixties and newer towers beside the shops, where drilling is slower, elevators have to be booked, and material comes up in one careful trip rather than five.','Tightening railings on split-level stairs\nTV mounting on concrete condo walls\nPatching plaster and early drywall walls\nRehanging sagging interior doors in bungalows\nSwapping kitchen faucets and shower heads\nAssembling flat-pack furniture in apartments\nFitting closet doors and sliding tracks\nWeather stripping on old side doors','[{\"q\":\"Can you mount a television on a concrete wall in a Don Mills apartment?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Most of the older buildings along the main roads here are poured concrete or block, so the bracket goes in with a masonry bit and sleeve anchors rated for the weight. It takes longer than a stud wall and makes more dust, and I plan for both. Send me the model of the television and a photo of the wall and I can price it properly.\"},{\"q\":\"My bungalow near Lawrence Avenue East has plaster walls. Will a patch blend in?\",\"a\":\"It should. Plaster repairs need building up in thin coats, sanding back and priming before paint, which is why they take a day or two rather than an hour. Matching an existing colour is easiest when you still have the tin or the paint code. If the wall has aged, carrying the paint out to the nearest corner hides the join.\"},{\"q\":\"Can a list of small jobs around a Parkwoods bungalow be done in one visit?\",\"a\":\"Usually, yes. Write the whole list out, add a few photos, and I will price it as a single trip. A dripping tap, a wobbly railing, two shelves and a set of new handles often fit into the same afternoon. Batching also means you are not paying for travel time more than once, which is the part people forget.\"},{\"q\":\"Which parts of Don Mills do you cover?\",\"a\":\"All of it, plus the pockets sitting right on its edges. That includes Banbury, Parkwoods, Donalda, Graydon Hall, Flemingdon Park and Henry Farm, and I am on those streets most weeks. If you live close by and are not certain your address falls inside the area, send a message or call and I will tell you straight away.\"}]','',1,17,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (18,'willowdale','Willowdale','City of Toronto',2,43.7702000,-79.4118000,'M2M, M2N, M2R, M2K','Willowdale East\nWillowdale West\nLansing\nNewtonbrook\nAvondale\nBayview Village\nNorth York Centre\nBayview Woods-Steeles\nHillcrest Village','Mel Lastman Square\nNorth York Centre subway station\nEmpress Walk\nToronto Centre for the Arts\nGibson House Museum\nBayview Village Shopping Centre\nFinch Bus Terminal\nEdithvale Park','north-york,thornhill,don-mills,markham,richmond-hill','Willowdale Handyman Services, North York','Handyman in Willowdale, North York for condo furniture assembly, TV mounting, door repairs, painting and fixture swaps. Get a free quote from Sercan today.','Handyman Services in Willowdale, North York','If you are looking for a handyman in Willowdale, you are probably in one of two places: a condo somewhere between Sheppard and Finch, or a house on a quiet street just off Yonge. I work in both. My name is Sercan, I run Worldwide Handyman myself, and you deal with one person from the first message to the last screw.\n\nTen years in the trade and over 500 finished projects have taught me that the hard part here is rarely the job itself. It is getting to the job. Loading bays, elevator bookings, permit parking and long stair runs decide how a day goes, so I plan around them before I arrive.','<h2>Getting into your building is half the job</h2><p>Willowdale\'s condo corridor runs the length of Yonge from Sheppard up past Finch, and every tower has its own way of doing things. One building wants the service elevator reserved days ahead. The next has a loading bay with a strict time limit. A third has neither, and everything travels in through the front doors and along a carpeted hallway.</p><p>None of that causes trouble as long as I know about it early. Send the concierge number or the building rules with your request, tell me the window you can get the elevator, and the visit gets built around it. Furniture and long boards especially need that planning, because a wardrobe that will not fit in a passenger lift has to travel up in pieces.</p><h2>Houses off Yonge: old bones and new builds side by side</h2><p>Step two streets east or west and the towers stop. Willowdale side streets still hold post-war bungalows on wide lots, and standing between them are large custom houses built on the same lots in the last twenty or thirty years. They ask for different things.</p><p>The older bungalows have settled. Doors bind at the top corner, basement stairs creak, kitchen taps are on their third washer, and closet doors have jumped the track. Newer builds are square but not always solid: hollow-core doors, engineered trim, and drywall with steel corner bead that dents the moment a chair backs into it. Both are fixable in an afternoon once you know which one you are looking at.</p><h2>Smart home and tech setup</h2><p>Plenty of calls in this area now start with a box sitting on the kitchen counter. Video doorbells, cameras, smart thermostats and smart switches all get bought faster than they get installed. Fitting one properly means checking the existing wiring suits the device, mounting the bracket straight and level, and running through the app with you before I leave so you know it works.</p><p>Where a device needs a new circuit, or anything more than swapping like for like, I will say so and hand it to a licensed electrician. Replacing a switch, a dimmer or an existing light fitting is straightforward and I do it regularly.</p><h2>What to have ready before I arrive</h2><p>A little preparation saves real time, and time is what you are paying for:</p><ul><li>Photos of the job sent along with your quote request</li><li>The parts or fixtures you have already bought, unboxed if possible</li><li>Your elevator booking window and parking instructions</li><li>Clear floor space around the wall, sink or fixture in question</li><li>Paint tins or colour codes if a patch needs touching in</li><li>A note of any <strong>other small jobs</strong> to look at while I am there</li></ul><p>Quotes cost nothing and carry no obligation. You and I settle on a fixed price or an hourly rate before I start, materials come with receipts unless you would rather supply your own, and messages usually get an answer within one business day. Everything is tidied up before I go, and if a repair is not sitting right, tell me and I will come back and put it straight.</p>','Two quite different jobs share one Willowdale postal code. Along Yonge it is towers, concierges, booked elevators and concrete walls that need masonry anchors. A block away it is post-war bungalows and big rebuilt houses on the same street, where a door that has dropped three millimetres is the entire complaint. Sorting out parking and access before the day matters here almost as much as the repair itself.','Furniture assembly in Yonge corridor condos\nBalcony door adjustments in high-rise units\nHanging mirrors and artwork in condo hallways\nShelving built into custom home closets\nSmart thermostat and video doorbell setup\nUnder-sink leak repairs in rental units\nBaseboard and trim touch-ups after moving\nToilet and faucet replacement in older bathrooms','[{\"q\":\"Parking is tight around my condo near Yonge and Finch. How do you handle that?\",\"a\":\"By sorting it out before the day. Most towers on that stretch have a loading bay or a visitor spot that has to be booked through the concierge, and street parking nearby is either metered or permit only. Give me the building\'s parking instructions with your request. If nothing is available, I bring only what I can carry in one trip and time the visit to suit.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you assemble furniture in a new condo near Sheppard before I move in?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and an empty unit is the easiest place to work. Have the boxes delivered inside the unit rather than left in the lobby, and book the elevator for the window agreed. Beds, wardrobes, desks and shelving all go together faster with clear floor space, and the packaging leaves with me so you are not left with a mountain of cardboard.\"},{\"q\":\"My house near Bayview Village was rebuilt about fifteen years ago. Does that change how you hang things?\",\"a\":\"It does. Newer builds often have engineered framing, steel corner bead and hollow-core doors, so the fixings differ from a post-war bungalow three doors down. I locate the framing, confirm it before drilling, and use anchors rated well above the weight going up. Anything heavy, like a large television or a floating vanity, gets fixed into solid framing rather than board alone.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you install video doorbells and smart thermostats in Willowdale houses?\",\"a\":\"Regularly. A doorbell or thermostat that replaces an existing one is straightforward: I check the wiring suits it, mount it level, connect it, and test it with you on your phone before leaving. If the device needs a new circuit or work inside the panel, that belongs to a licensed electrician and I will tell you so rather than improvise.\"}]','',1,18,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (19,'agincourt','Agincourt','City of Toronto',2,43.7854000,-79.2785000,'M1S, M1T, M1V, M1W','Agincourt North\nAgincourt South\nMalvern West\nTam O\'Shanter\nSullivan\nMilliken\nL\'Amoreaux\nSteeles\nDorset Park\nWexford','Agincourt Mall\nAgincourt GO Station\nL\'Amoreaux Park\nMilliken Park\nGoldhawk Park\nAgincourt Recreation Centre\nSheppard Avenue East\nKennedy Road','scarborough,markham,unionville,north-york,don-mills','Handyman in Agincourt, Scarborough','Handyman in Agincourt, Scarborough for sliding door repairs, fences and decks, drywall patching, mounting and fixture swaps. Get a free quote today.','Handyman Services Across Agincourt, Scarborough','The most common call I get as a handyman in Agincourt is about a door that will not behave. Patio sliders that stick, screens that have torn, a garage side door dropped out of square. My name is Sercan, I run Worldwide Handyman on my own, and I have been fixing this sort of thing for more than ten years and over 500 projects.\n\nMost of the houses here went up between the 1960s and the 1980s, and they were built well. What they have now is wear. Hardware that has done fifty winters, fences pushed around by frost, decks that need a few boards rather than a rebuild. Sensible repairs, done once and done properly.','<h2>Winter always leaves a list behind</h2><p>Freeze and thaw is hard on the outside of a house, and Agincourt has plenty of exposed backyard fencing and decking from the subdivision era. Posts heave, boards cup, gate latches stop lining up, and one section of fence starts leaning towards the neighbours. Most of it is repairable. Replacing six boards and resetting a gate costs far less than a new fence and buys years.</p><p>Cold months also find every draught indoors. Weather stripping flattens, door sweeps wear through, and the side door from the garage turns into the coldest spot in the house. Sorting those out in spring or early autumn beats living with them for another season.</p><h2>Big family homes, long lists</h2><p>Homes here are often shared by more than one generation, and that shapes the work. Second kitchens, extra bathrooms, bedrooms downstairs and a lot of daily traffic through the same hallways. Things loosen faster than they do in a quiet house.</p><p>Grab bars beside the tub, a brighter fixture over the stairs, a handrail that no longer wobbles, lever handles in place of round knobs: small changes that make a shared home easier to live in. None of them take long on their own, which is exactly why they are best done together.</p><h2>The repairs Agincourt asks for most</h2><p>Over a typical month in this part of Scarborough, the same requests come round:</p><ul><li>Freeing and adjusting sliding patio doors so they roll again</li><li>Rescreening window and door screens torn over the summer</li><li>Fence board, post and gate repairs after the frost</li><li>Deck board replacement and railing tightening</li><li><strong>Drywall patching</strong> and repainting in busy hallways and stairwells</li><li>Swapping ceiling fans, light fittings and dimmers</li><li>Recaulking tubs and tiling a kitchen backsplash</li></ul><p>Bigger projects fit in too. Laminate or vinyl plank through a basement, a full run of new baseboards and trim, a bathroom vanity swap, or cabinet hardware and painting right through a kitchen are all multi-day jobs I take on as one project.</p><h2>Pricing, timing and what I will not touch</h2><p>Quotes are free. Send photos with the details, since a picture of a sagging gate tells me more than three paragraphs of description, and expect an answer usually within one business day. Before work starts, you and I agree a fixed price or an hourly rate, so nothing shifts on you halfway through.</p><p>My hours are Monday to Saturday, eight in the morning to eight at night, with Sunday by appointment. Materials come with receipts when I buy them, or you pick up your own and I fit what you have chosen. Everything is swept up at the end, and I stand behind the work: if a repair does not hold, I come back and put it right.</p><p>Work that needs a licensed trade in Ontario goes to one. New circuits, panel changes, gas lines, structural alterations and roofing are not my work, and I will say so plainly rather than take the job. Changing an existing fixture, faucet, dimmer or toilet is another matter, and that happens week in, week out.</p>','Agincourt was built out in large subdivision phases, so a whole street can share the same patio door, the same fence line and the same garage side entry. I usually recognise the problem before I am out of the van. The other constant is the backyard: long fence runs, older decks and gates that take a beating every freeze and thaw, and are almost always worth repairing rather than replacing.','Freeing stuck sliding patio doors\nRescreening torn window and patio door screens\nFence board and post repairs after winter\nRealigning garage side entry doors\nGrab bar installation in family bathrooms\nBacksplash and caulking in 1970s kitchens\nDeck board replacement on backyard decks\nCeiling fan swaps in bedrooms and dens','[{\"q\":\"The sliding patio door on my back-split near Sheppard and Kennedy sticks. Can it be fixed instead of replaced?\",\"a\":\"Usually it can. Sticking sliders in these houses nearly always come down to worn rollers, a track full of grit, or a door that has dropped slightly in its frame. Cleaning the track, fitting new rollers and adjusting the height brings it back most of the time. If the frame itself has failed, I will say so and you can put the money towards a replacement.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you repair a shared backyard fence after a Scarborough winter?\",\"a\":\"I can, and it is worth speaking to your neighbour first, since a shared fence usually means a shared cost. Rotten boards, a leaning post or a gate that drags along the ground can all be fixed section by section. I look at the whole run and tell you what genuinely needs replacing now and what still has a few seasons left in it.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you work in the townhouse complexes around Agincourt North and Milliken?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Townhouses come with their own quirks: narrow stairs, shared walls, and a board that may want notice before work starts. Let me know if your complex has rules about noise hours or contractors signing in, and the visit gets planned around them. Inside the unit, the jobs are much the same as any house on the street.\"},{\"q\":\"My parents moved in with me. Can you make the house safer for them?\",\"a\":\"That is regular work for me. Grab bars beside the tub and toilet, a sturdier stair handrail, brighter fixtures on landings, lever handles instead of round knobs, and a taller toilet are all straightforward. Grab bars need fixing into solid framing or heavy-duty anchors rather than plain drywall plugs, and that is the part people most often get wrong.\"}]','',1,19,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (20,'the-junction','The Junction','City of Toronto',2,43.6656000,-79.4665000,'M6P, M6N, M6S, M6R','Junction Triangle\nHigh Park North\nRunnymede\nBaby Point\nCarleton Village\nLambton\nRockcliffe-Smythe\nWeston-Pellam Park\nBloor West Village','Dundas Street West\nKeele Street\nWest Toronto Railpath\nStockyards Village\nHigh Park\nAnnette Street\nBaird Park\nRunnymede subway station','high-park,roncesvalles,bloor-west-village,york,the-annex','Handyman in The Junction, Toronto','Handyman in The Junction, Toronto for plaster repairs, TV mounting on old walls, painting, doors and fixture swaps. Message Sercan for a free quote.','Handyman Services in The Junction, Toronto','Old houses are the whole story here, and a handyman in The Junction spends most of the week working around plaster, lath and brick. My name is Sercan. I run Worldwide Handyman on my own, and I have spent more than ten years and 500 projects learning where a hundred-year-old wall will hold a fixing and where it crumbles in your hand.\n\nNothing in these houses is standard. Studs land where they feel like landing, floors slope, frames sit out of square, and the wall you want your television on may be brick with a thin skin of plaster over strapping. That is fine once you expect it. It only gets expensive when someone drills first and thinks afterwards.','<h2>Plaster, lath and brick behind every wall</h2><p>Most of The Junction went up between the 1890s and the 1920s for railway and stockyard workers, which means semis, row houses and small detached homes on narrow lots. Interior walls are usually plaster on wood lath. Exterior walls are often brick with wood strapping and plaster over that, so there is a hollow void exactly where you expect solid backing.</p><p>Hanging anything heavy on that takes care. A stud detector on its own is not enough over lath, so I probe, find the real framing or the strapping, and use fixings suited to what is genuinely there. Done properly, a television or a loaded bookshelf sits on a century-old wall without a crack showing. Done in a hurry, you get a fist-sized hole and a plaster repair on top of the original job.</p><p>Cracks are the other constant. Old plaster moves with the seasons and along old repairs, and patching it well means raking the crack out, filling in layers, and blending the texture rather than smearing filler across the surface.</p><h2>Narrow lots, laneways and staircases</h2><p>Access shapes how long a job takes on these streets. Many houses have no driveway, permit parking on the road and a rear lane that is tight for anything wider than a car. Front doors open onto steep porch steps, and the staircase inside is often narrow with a turn at the top.</p><p>Furniture is where that bites hardest. A wardrobe that suits the bedroom may not make the turn on the stairs, so it is worth assembling upstairs rather than down, or checking the box dimensions before delivery day. Describe the layout when you ask for a quote and I will flag anything that looks like trouble.</p><h2>What I get called for around The Junction</h2><p>The usual list on these streets looks something like this:</p><ul><li>Mounting televisions and shelving on plaster and brick walls</li><li>Patching cracked plaster and repainting whole rooms</li><li>Easing doors that swell and stick in old frames</li><li>Replacing worn faucets, shower heads and toilets</li><li>Swapping light fittings, dimmers and smart switches like for like</li><li>Assembling and anchoring furniture in tight upstairs rooms</li><li><strong>Baseboards, trim and vinyl plank flooring</strong> in rooms that are not square</li></ul><p>Larger work happens too. A kitchen backsplash, a bathroom refresh, a full run of new trim, or several days of patching and painting are all things I take on as one project rather than a string of short visits.</p><h2>Straight answers before the work starts</h2><p>Pricing gets settled before a single tool comes out of the bag, and asking for a quote costs you nothing. Photographs help more in old houses than anywhere else, because a picture of a cracked wall or an original door tells me what I am dealing with. A reply normally lands with you inside one business day. The figure is fixed or hourly, agreed up front, and it does not shift once I am on site.</p><p>Every job gets cleaned up before I leave, which counts for more in a house where plaster dust travels. If I buy the materials you get the receipts, and if you would rather choose your own, I will fit whatever you bring home. Should a repair not hold afterwards, say so and it goes straight back on my list.</p><p>Some jobs in century homes belong to a licensed trade. Knob-and-tube rewiring, new circuits, panel work, gas lines and structural changes go to a licensed electrician, plumber or contractor, and I will tell you that on the spot rather than take the money. Changing an existing fixture, switch or tap is everyday work.</p>','Almost everything in The Junction is over a century old, and that decides the work. Plaster over lath inside, brick and strapping outside, floors that slope, doors hung by hand, and no two rooms exactly square. Add narrow lots with no driveway and a staircase that turns at the top, and the planning matters as much as the fixing. Rushing an old wall is how small jobs turn into big ones.','TV mounting on plaster and lath walls\nPatching cracked plaster in century semis\nEasing swollen doors in old frames\nAssembling furniture in narrow row houses\nShelving fixed into brick and strapping\nReplacing tired bathroom faucets and shower heads\nStraightening loose stair railings in walk-ups\nWeather stripping on original front doors','[{\"q\":\"There is no driveway at my Junction semi. Where do you park?\",\"a\":\"On the street in the permit zone, which normally allows a short stay, or in the rear lane if there is room to pull in. It rarely causes a problem as long as I know in advance. Tell me which side of the house is easiest to unload from and whether the lane is passable, and materials come in on one trip rather than blocking the road.\"},{\"q\":\"Can a television be mounted on plaster and lath walls in a century-old semi?\",\"a\":\"Yes, though not with the fixings that come in the box. Interior walls are plaster on lath and outside walls are often brick behind strapping, so I check what is really there before drilling and pick anchors to match. A properly fixed bracket holds fine. The failures I get called out to repair are almost always plastic plugs pushed into soft plaster.\"},{\"q\":\"Will a wardrobe fit up the narrow staircase in a row house here?\",\"a\":\"Sometimes it will not, and that is worth knowing before delivery day. Flat-pack is the safe answer, since the boxes go up in pieces and get built in the room. If the piece is already assembled, measure the narrowest point of the turn and the doorway at the top, send me the figures, and I will tell you honestly whether it stands a chance.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you take on work in the converted loft buildings near the rail lines?\",\"a\":\"I do. Old industrial conversions usually mean concrete or block walls, exposed ceilings and building rules about noise hours and elevator use. Drilling takes longer and makes more dust than in a house, and I plan for both. Check the booking times with your property manager and pass the details on with your request so the day runs smoothly.\"}]','',1,20,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (21,'greektown-danforth','The Danforth','City of Toronto',2,43.6790000,-79.3480000,'M4J, M4K, M4C, M4L','Greektown\nRiverdale\nNorth Riverdale\nPlayter Estates\nPape Village\nDanforth Village\nEast Danforth\nChester\nBlake-Jones\nBroadview North\nOld East York\nWoodbine Corridor','Withrow Park\nRiverdale Park East\nDanforth Music Hall\nPape Station\nChester Station\nGreenwood Park\nMonarch Park','leslieville,the-beaches,east-york,downtown-toronto,leaside','Handyman on The Danforth, Toronto','Handyman on The Danforth for repairs, plaster patching, TV mounting, flat pack assembly, doors and light fixtures in Riverdale and Greektown. Free quote.','Handyman on The Danforth, Greektown and Riverdale','If you are after a handyman on The Danforth, the odds are your house was built before the Second World War and has been patched a few times since. I am Sercan. I work on my own, and after more than ten years and over five hundred jobs I know what these old brick semis do to a simple task.\n\nQuotes are free and there is nothing to sign. You get a fixed price or an hourly rate agreed before I start, materials come with receipts if I supply them, and several small jobs can be stacked into one visit so you only have to be home once.','<h2>Old brick semis and where the studs actually are</h2><p>Between Broadview and Woodbine, most of the housing went up between roughly 1905 and 1930. Two storey brick semis, front porches, narrow staircases, and behind the paint, plaster on lath instead of drywall. That single fact changes a lot of jobs. A plastic anchor pushed into plaster crumbles the coat behind it and lets go the first time weight goes on the fixing.</p><p>Framing in these houses is rarely at a tidy sixteen inches. I use a magnet as well as a scanner, because the old cut nails give a more honest answer than the electronics do. Where there is nothing solid to hit, I open a small patch, put in a timber backer, close it up and sand it flush, so paint is all that is left to do.</p><h2>What people around The Danforth call about</h2><p>Riverdale, Playter Estates and Pape Village houses share a fairly short list of complaints. Doors move with the seasons. Floors slope enough that a new washer rocks. Attic rooms were converted decades ago and the trim was never quite finished. This is the run of work I do most in the area:</p><ul><li>Interior doors planed and re-hung after seasonal swelling</li><li>Plaster cracks above door frames filled and repainted</li><li>TVs mounted on chimney breasts and shared party walls</li><li>Flat pack wardrobes built in tight converted attic rooms</li><li>Faucets, shower heads and toilets swapped like for like</li><li>Ceiling fixtures, dimmers and smart switches changed on existing wiring</li><li>Baseboard and quarter round refitted after new flooring</li><li>Back deck boards, gate hinges and fence pickets repaired</li></ul><h2>Flats above the shops</h2><p>A fair share of my calls are for the apartments over the storefronts on Danforth Avenue. Those come with their own conditions. Stairs are steep and turn hard at the landing, so I measure the opening before anybody orders a wardrobe or a sofa bed. Walls are sometimes masonry rather than stud, which means different fixings for a television bracket.</p><p>Those units also sit on a street with metered and permit parking and no room to double park. Tell me how loading works at your address when you get in touch, and I will arrive with everything in one trip instead of blocking traffic twice.</p><h2>How a visit is arranged</h2><p>Send a couple of photos with your message and I can usually price the job without coming out first. A reply normally lands within one business day. You will know the number before I pick up a tool, and you can buy your own materials if you would rather choose them yourself.</p><p>Hours are Monday to Saturday, eight in the morning to eight at night, with Sunday by appointment. Anything that needs a new circuit, a panel change or gas work goes to a licensed electrician or plumber, and I will say so at the quote rather than waste a trip.</p><p>When the job is done the area gets swept and the offcuts leave with me. If something is not sitting right a week later, tell me and I will come back and put it straight.</p>','The Danforth is a walking street with tight residential blocks behind it, and that shapes the work. Lots are narrow, drives are shared or missing, and most wall surfaces are plaster over lath rather than drywall. Everything comes in by hand through a front door or up a back staircase. Because so many houses were split into flats years ago, a lot of my time goes on doors, latches and unfinished trim.','Plaster and lath crack repair in Riverdale semis\nDoors re-hung in settled century houses\nTV mounting on narrow shared party walls\nFlat pack assembly in converted attic bedrooms\nUnder sink leaks in Playter Estates kitchens\nCeiling fans and dimmers swapped on existing wiring\nBaseboards and trim after new laminate flooring\nBack gate and fence repairs off the laneway','[{\"q\":\"My attic was converted years ago and the trim was never finished. Can you complete it?\",\"a\":\"Yes, that is a common one in these houses. Unfinished attic conversions usually need casing around the door, baseboard scribed to a floor that is not level, and a few plaster patches where the old work simply stopped. None of it is difficult, it just takes patience and careful scribing. I can price it from photos or after a quick look.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you work in a flat above a shop on Danforth Avenue?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and those jobs go smoothly if I know two things in advance. First, how I get in, since many of the entrances are a side door off the street with a keypad. Second, where I can stop for ten minutes to unload. The staircases are steep and turn at the landing, so I measure before anything large is ordered.\"},{\"q\":\"I am in a semi. Will the neighbours hear the drilling?\",\"a\":\"Some of it, yes. A shared party wall carries drilling noise well, so I keep the loud part short and do it in the middle of the day rather than first thing in the morning. If you want to warn next door, tell me the day before. Quiet tasks like assembly and door adjustments get done around the noisy bits.\"},{\"q\":\"I have five small jobs saved up. Can they go into one visit?\",\"a\":\"That is how I prefer to work. A sticking door, a picture rail, a running toilet, a dimmer swap and a wobbly towel bar make a comfortable morning together instead of five separate appointments. Write the list out, add photos where you can, and I will price the whole lot up front. If one turns out bigger than it looked, I will flag it first.\"}]','',1,21,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (22,'bloor-west-village','Bloor West Village','City of Toronto',2,43.6500000,-79.4845000,'M6S, M6P, M6R, M8Y','Swansea\nBaby Point\nRunnymede\nOld Mill\nLambton Baby Point\nHigh Park North\nWarren Park\nHumbercrest\nThe Kingsway','High Park\nRunnymede Station\nJane Station\nEtienne Brule Park\nSwansea Town Hall\nOld Mill Bridge\nHumber River\nRennie Park','high-park,roncesvalles,the-junction,etobicoke,york','Handyman in Bloor West Village','Handyman in Bloor West Village for door and window repairs, decks and gates, TV mounting, plaster patching and small home fixes. Ask for a free quote.','Handyman Services in Bloor West Village','Looking for a handyman in Bloor West Village often starts with something outside. A gate that has dropped on its hinges, deck boards lifted by the winter, a fence panel leaning where the ground slopes off toward the Humber. My name is Sercan and I work by myself. Ten years of this and more than five hundred jobs finished, most of them across the Greater Toronto Area.\n\nIndoors it is the interwar houses that keep me busy. Solid old doors, small odd-shaped rooms, and plaster walls that do not take a screw the way you might hope. Quotes are free, the price is agreed before I start, and several small jobs can go into one visit.','<h2>Ground that moves, and the work it makes</h2><p>Yards in Swansea and around Baby Point rarely sit level. They fall away toward the Humber or step down in terraces, and the clay underneath swells and shrinks through the year. A gate that shut cleanly in September catches on the latch by March. Deck posts lift, a stair stringer drops a few millimetres, and a handrail that felt solid last summer starts to give.</p><p>Most of that gets sorted without pulling anything out. Hinges are shimmed and the gate re-hung on its high side, loose boards refastened or swapped, railings drawn back tight and blocked properly. I do repairs rather than rebuilds. If a deck frame has gone past saving I will tell you straight instead of covering it over.</p><h2>Houses from between the wars</h2><p>Houses in Bloor West Village mostly filled in through the 1920s and 1930s. Brick detached and semi-detached, some with timbering across the gable, short steep driveways, and plaster inside. The doors are heavy and properly made, which is a pleasure until one starts dragging on the jamb and the old latch stops catching.</p><p>Windows are the other regular. Painted-shut wood sashes, casements that bind, tired weather stripping and torn screens all turn up every spring. Those I can service. Where a window is genuinely finished I will say so and leave the replacing to a window company, rather than sell you a repair that will not last.</p><h2>The list I usually get handed</h2><p>People here tend to save jobs up until there are enough to fill a morning, which suits the way I work. A typical visit covers something like this:</p><ul><li>Sticking interior doors eased, planed and re-hung</li><li>Curtain rods and blinds fitted into plaster walls</li><li>TVs mounted around awkward 1930s chimney breasts</li><li>Vanities, faucets and shower heads replaced upstairs</li><li>Torn window screens re-meshed and refitted</li><li>Backsplash tile and fresh caulking around tubs and sinks</li><li>Gate hardware, fence pickets and deck boards repaired</li></ul><h2>Booking a visit</h2><p>Message me with a rough description and a photo or two, and you will normally hear back inside one business day. Quotes cost nothing and commit you to nothing. The price, fixed or hourly, is settled before I start, and you are free to buy your own materials or let me bring them with the receipts.</p><p>Parking matters more here than people expect. Drives are narrow, often shared, and the streets near Runnymede and Jane stations fill early. Say where you would like the van left when you message me. If you are above the shops on Bloor Street West or in one of the low-rise buildings near Old Mill, mention the stairs and the entry code so everything comes up in one load.</p><p>I am out Monday to Saturday, eight until eight, with Sunday by appointment. Electrical past swapping an existing fixture, switch or dimmer, and plumbing past fixtures and traps, belongs to a licensed trade and I pass it over. The rest I finish, sweep up after, and come back to if it is not right.</p>','Bloor West Village sits on the lip of the Humber valley, so nearly every street has a grade to it. That grade turns up in the work: gates that bind, front steps that settle, fences and decks shifting with the clay through freeze and thaw. The houses themselves are interwar brick with plaster inside and heavy solid doors, and the shopping strip has flats above it with narrow stairs to climb.','Gate re-hanging on settled Swansea fence posts\nDeck board and railing repairs above the Humber\nPainted-shut wood sash windows freed and serviced\nHeavy solid doors planed and latches adjusted\nTV mounting around 1930s brick chimney breasts\nWindow screen re-meshing before the summer\nBathroom vanity and faucet swaps in older homes\nBacksplash tile and caulking in Baby Point kitchens','[{\"q\":\"My back gate binds every spring. Can that be fixed without a new fence?\",\"a\":\"Usually yes. Frost and clay lift the hinge post a little each winter, so the gate swings on a different arc and misses the latch. The repair is normally packing or resetting that post, shimming the hinges and moving the keeper across. Rebuilding a whole fence for it is rarely necessary, and I will tell you honestly if it genuinely is.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you free up wood windows that have been painted shut in a 1920s house?\",\"a\":\"Often, yes. The sashes get cut free at the paint line, the runs cleaned out, and the hardware freed and lubricated so the window opens and locks properly again. Weather stripping can be renewed at the same time and torn screens re-meshed. If a sash is rotten rather than simply stuck, that is a different job and I will say so.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you do outdoor work in Bloor West Village through the winter, or is it better to wait?\",\"a\":\"Small outdoor repairs are fine in the cold. Gate hardware, a loose railing, a couple of deck boards, all of that can be done in January without any trouble. Anything that means digging a post or working with wet products waits for the thaw, because it will not last otherwise. I will tell you at the quote which category your job falls into.\"},{\"q\":\"I am putting new flooring in one room. Will you handle the baseboards as well?\",\"a\":\"Yes, that is part of the same job for me. Laminate and vinyl plank need the old baseboard off or the trim scribed in, and doors often need trimming at the bottom to clear the new height. I lay the flooring, refit or replace the baseboard and quarter round, then caulk the tops so the room is ready for paint.\"}]','',1,22,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (23,'forest-hill','Forest Hill','City of Toronto',2,43.6940000,-79.4140000,'M5P, M5N, M4V, M6C','Forest Hill Village\nForest Hill South\nForest Hill North\nUpper Village\nHumewood-Cedarvale\nCedarvale\nChaplin Estates\nSouth Hill\nDeer Park\nOakwood Village','Cedarvale Park\nCedarvale Ravine\nSpadina Road\nSt Clair Avenue West\nEglinton West Station\nUpper Canada College\nBishop Strachan School\nKay Gardner Beltline Trail','midtown-toronto,the-annex,yorkville,york,rosedale','Handyman in Forest Hill, Toronto','Handyman in Forest Hill for picture and mirror hanging, door and hardware repairs, plaster patching, flooring and fixture swaps. Ask me for a free quote.','Handyman Services in Forest Hill, Toronto','Most calls for a handyman in Forest Hill start with a wall. A row of pictures that has to hang dead level, a heavy mirror over a mantel, floating shelves in a study where the plaster is close to a century old. Sercan here, working alone as I always have, with ten years and better than five hundred jobs to my name.\n\nThe rest is the ordinary run of a large older house. Doors that drag, hardware gone loose, a tap that drips, a fixture waiting to be changed. A free quote comes first, the price is settled before anything starts, and small items get grouped into a single visit.','<h2>Plaster, picture rails and hanging things that stay up</h2><p>Walls in Forest Hill are plaster on lath, sometimes with a newer skim over the top, and they behave nothing like drywall. A plastic plug bites into the soft coat and releases the moment somebody knocks the frame. So I locate the framing, or I fit a backer where there is none, and everything of any weight goes into wood.</p><p>Many of these houses still have their original picture rails. Where a rail is sound it is worth using, because it keeps weight off the plaster and lets a piece be moved later without another hole. For a gallery wall I lay the whole arrangement out on the floor first, mark from a laser line, then hang from the middle outwards so the spacing holds true.</p><h2>The rest of what a big old house needs</h2><p>Houses on these streets largely went up between about 1910 and 1935, and they age in predictable ways. Solid doors swell and drop on their hinges. Mortise latches wear until a handle spins in your hand. Deep baseboards pull away from the wall as a building settles. Third floor rooms get converted and the trim never gets finished. The regular list runs like this:</p><ul><li>Heavy interior doors re-hung and mortise hardware adjusted</li><li>Plaster cracks and old anchor holes filled and painted out</li><li>Baseboard, casing and crown repairs after settling</li><li>Faucets, shower heads and toilets replaced like for like</li><li>Light fixtures, ceiling fans and dimmers swapped on existing wiring</li><li>Closet systems and flat pack furniture built in awkward rooms</li><li>Video doorbells, cameras and smart thermostats fitted and set up</li></ul><h2>Suites along Bathurst, Eglinton and St Clair</h2><p>Not everyone in Forest Hill is in a detached house. The edges of the neighbourhood carry post-war brick apartment buildings and newer condo suites, and that work runs to a different rhythm. Concrete and block walls want proper masonry fixings rather than the toggle that came free in the bracket box. Service elevators need booking ahead, and most buildings set quiet hours.</p><p>Tell me the building when you write, and check with your concierge or property manager about what they ask of anyone doing work in a suite. Sorting that out ahead of the visit saves a wasted trip on both sides.</p><h2>Short lists, longer projects and where I stop</h2><p>A morning of small fixes is welcome and so is a project that runs several days. Longer work I take on includes a bathroom freshened up with a new vanity and fresh caulking, a run of laminate or vinyl plank with new baseboards, or a kitchen tidied with backsplash tile and new cabinet hardware.</p><p>What I do not touch is anything requiring a licence in Ontario. New circuits, panel work, gas and structural changes go to a licensed electrician, plumber or contractor, and you will hear that at the quote rather than halfway through. Otherwise it is free quotes, a price settled before the first tool comes out, receipts for anything I supply, floors swept at the end, and a return visit if something is not right.</p>','Forest Hill is a big-house area on curving, narrow streets, and both halves of that matter. Indoors the walls are plaster and the doors are solid, so fixings and hinges need real treatment rather than a quick anchor. Outdoors there is often nowhere obvious to leave a van and the drives are short and steep. I plan the load before arriving so I am not walking back and forth past the neighbours.','Gallery walls hung on original plaster and lath\nMortise latches and heavy oak doors adjusted\nDeep baseboard and casing repairs after settling\nMirrors mounted over mantels in older living rooms\nTV brackets fixed to concrete condo walls\nBathroom vanity and faucet replacement upstairs\nCloset organizers built into third floor rooms\nSmart thermostats and video doorbells installed','[{\"q\":\"Can you hang a heavy piece of art on these old plaster walls?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Plaster on lath will not hold weight on a plastic plug, so I never use one for anything substantial. I find the studs and screw into them, and where there is no stud in the right place I fit a backer behind the plaster and patch it in. If your room still has its picture rail, that is often the neater answer.\"},{\"q\":\"The handle on my bedroom door spins and will not open it. Is that a new door?\",\"a\":\"Almost never. Those old mortise latches wear at the spindle, and the usual fix is a replacement latch set into the same mortise, plus a hinge adjustment so the door meets the keeper squarely. The original door and its trim stay exactly where they are. If a matching latch is hard to source, I will tell you before ordering anything.\"},{\"q\":\"I am in a condo suite near Eglinton West. What should I sort out before you come?\",\"a\":\"Book the service elevator if anything bulky is coming in, and ask the concierge what the building expects of anyone working in a suite. Let me know the quiet hours as well. After that I only need the parking arrangement and a lift code. Concrete walls are no trouble, they just need masonry fixings, which I bring with me.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you take on a project that runs several days rather than one repair?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Multi-day work suits me because I am the same person from start to finish. A bathroom refresh, flooring through a couple of rooms with new baseboards, or a kitchen brought up to date with backsplash and hardware are all normal jobs. I give you a schedule with the quote so you know which days I am in the house.\"}]','',1,23,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (24,'rosedale','Rosedale','City of Toronto',2,43.6790000,-79.3820000,'M4W, M4T, M4X, M5R','South Rosedale\nNorth Rosedale\nMoore Park\nSummerhill\nGovernor\'s Bridge\nRathnelly\nSouth Hill\nYorkville\nCabbagetown\nSt James Town','Craigleigh Gardens\nChorley Park\nRamsden Park\nRosedale Station\nSummerhill Station\nRosedale Valley Road\nEvergreen Brick Works\nMount Pleasant Road','yorkville,the-annex,downtown-toronto,midtown-toronto,leaside','Handyman in Rosedale, Toronto','Handyman in Rosedale for repairs in century homes: doors, plaster, fixtures, fences and gates, furniture assembly and small projects. Ask for a free quote.','Handyman Services for Rosedale Homes','Getting a handyman into Rosedale is half about the work and half about reaching the door. Streets bend and dead-end against the ravines, most of them are permit parking, and a good number of the houses are a hundred years old and have been looked after every year since. I am Sercan, a one man operation with more than ten years and five hundred jobs behind me.\n\nThe jobs themselves are what you would expect from houses of that age. Doors that have dropped, plaster that has cracked, hardware worn smooth, fixtures ready to be changed. A free quote comes first, the price is agreed before I start, and a list of small things can be cleared in one visit.','<h2>Getting to the door</h2><p>Streets in Rosedale were laid out to follow the ravines, so they bend, split and stop without much warning. Many are permit parking, and a few run one way in a direction the map does not prepare you for. None of that is a problem, but it does change how I plan a visit. I bring everything in one load and I do not leave a van sitting across a neighbour\'s drive.</p><p>When you write, tell me the nearest cross street, whether there is room on the drive, and which door you want me to use. Front, side, or in from the lane through the garage, it makes no difference as long as I know beforehand.</p><h2>Houses built before the First World War</h2><p>South Rosedale largely went up between the 1890s and the 1920s, with North Rosedale filling in a little later. Detached brick and stone, Georgian and Tudor revival fronts, sash windows, back stairs, and a great deal of original woodwork worth keeping. Much of the area now sits inside a heritage conservation district, so anything visible from the street is treated as repair first and replacement last.</p><p>Inside, plaster on lath is the norm and the framing is old growth timber, which is hard on drill bits and unforgiving of guesswork. I drill small, check what is behind, and fix into solid wood instead of trusting an anchor to hold.</p><h2>What I am usually asked to fix</h2><p>Owners around here keep a running list and call once it is long enough to fill a morning. Most of it looks like this:</p><ul><li>Original doors eased, re-hung and their old latches adjusted</li><li>Cracked plaster and redundant hook holes made good before painting</li><li>Artwork, mirrors and shelving fixed into real framing</li><li>Faucets, shower heads and running toilets replaced</li><li>Light fittings, dimmers and smart switches changed over</li><li>Cabinet hardware, drawer runners and soft close hinges sorted out</li><li>Flat pack and delivered furniture assembled in upstairs rooms</li></ul><p>Jobs that call for new wiring, a panel, gas or a structural change belong to a licensed electrician, plumber or contractor. Changing a fixture that is already there is my work; running a new circuit is not, and I will point you in the right direction.</p><h2>Ravine lots and what the weather does</h2><p>Back gardens that drop into the valley take a beating. Frost lifts fence posts, freeze and thaw splits deck boards and loosens railings, and a gate on sloping ground stops meeting its latch by the end of winter. Wooden side gates are the single most common outdoor call I get in this corner of the city.</p><p>I repair where the timber still has life in it rather than pushing a rebuild. Photographs attached to a quote request speed the pricing up, and an answer usually comes back within one business day. Free quotes, no obligation, a fixed price or hourly rate agreed before anything starts, Monday to Saturday from eight to eight with Sunday by appointment, and the area swept before I leave.</p>','Rosedale is a century-old neighbourhood built on ravine land, and those two facts run together. Streets curve and dead-end, parking is tight and mostly by permit, and back gardens fall away into the valley where frost shifts fence posts every winter. Indoors the walls are plaster and the framing is old growth timber that fights a drill bit. Most of the work here is careful repair of original material rather than tearing anything out.','Original doors re-hung in century detached homes\nFence posts reset after frost on ravine lots\nPlaster repairs before repainting older bedrooms\nHeavy mirrors fixed into old growth framing\nGarden and side gates repaired and re-latched\nRunning toilets and dripping faucets replaced\nCabinet hardware and drawer runners renewed\nFurniture carried up narrow back staircases','[{\"q\":\"My street is permit parking only. Does that make things awkward?\",\"a\":\"Not really, as long as I know in advance. I plan to carry everything in from wherever I can legally stop, so I bring the right load the first time rather than walking back and forth all morning. If there is space on your drive I will happily use it. Just tell me which it is at the time of booking.\"},{\"q\":\"My house sits in a heritage conservation district. Does that limit what you can do?\",\"a\":\"It shapes the approach more than it limits it. On the exterior I repair original material wherever it can be saved, and I match profiles and finishes instead of dropping in something modern. Interior work is not affected at all. If a job would need a permit or heritage approval, you will hear that from me before the quote, not after.\"},{\"q\":\"The framing in my house is old and very hard to drill. Can a television still go over the fireplace?\",\"a\":\"Usually. Old growth timber is dense but it grips a screw better than anything sold today, so once I find it the bracket sits rock solid. What I check first is what the chimney breast is built from and whether power and cabling can reach without cutting into anything structural. If it cannot be done neatly, I will say so before starting.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you get a large piece of furniture up the back stairs in these houses?\",\"a\":\"Often, but measuring first saves everyone a bad afternoon. Back staircases in these houses are narrow and turn tight at the landing, and upstairs doorways can be slimmer than the delivery box. Send me the model or the carton dimensions and I will check the route before anything is ordered. If it will not go, a flat pack version usually will.\"}]','',1,24,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (25,'mississauga','Mississauga','Peel Region',1,43.5890000,-79.6441000,'L4W, L4Z, L5A, L5B, L5M, L5N','Cooksville\nApplewood\nDixie\nMalton\nErin Mills\nCentral Erin Mills\nMeadowvale\nChurchill Meadows\nLisgar\nClarkson\nLorne Park\nMineola\nRathwood\nSheridan','Square One Shopping Centre\nMississauga Celebration Square\nErin Mills Town Centre\nCredit River\nLiving Arts Centre\nCooksville GO Station\nRiverwood Conservancy','port-credit,streetsville,brampton,etobicoke,oakville,milton','Handyman Mississauga - Repairs & Mounting','Handyman services in Mississauga: repairs, furniture assembly, TV mounting, drywall, taps, lights and flooring, done by Sercan himself. Ask for a free quote.','Handyman Services Across Mississauga','Finding a handyman in Mississauga who will take on the small stuff is harder than it should be. My name is Sercan and I am the one holding the tools. No crew, no subcontractors, no sales visit before anything gets done. Ten years and 500-plus projects have taught me that a shelf hung in a 1960s Cooksville wall and a shelf hung in a condo by Square One are two different jobs.\n\nMost calls are a short list rather than one item. A dripping shower head, a wobbly stair rail, a door that will not latch, three flat-pack cabinets still in their boxes. I price all of it in one visit, at a fixed price or an hourly rate you agree before I start. Quotes are free.','<h2>One city, five decades of houses</h2>\n<p>Malton and parts of Cooksville went up not long after the war, so you get small bungalows with lath in places and framing that sits wherever the carpenter felt like putting it. Applewood and Dixie came next with side-splits and back-splits. Erin Mills and Meadowvale are 1970s and 1980s subdivisions full of drywall, hollow-core doors that have dropped on their hinges, and windows already replaced once. Churchill Meadows and Lisgar are newer again, and the towers around Square One are newer still.</p>\n<p>That range matters because the fix changes with the house. A bracket in a 2015 condo goes into a partition wall or straight into concrete, and those are different anchors and different tools. The same television in a Mineola home from the fifties might mean plaster over strapping instead. Checking comes before drilling, every time.</p>\n<h2>What people call me about</h2>\n<p>Across Mississauga the list barely changes from one month to the next.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Flat-pack wardrobes and bed frames built in the room</li>\n<li>Kitchen taps, shower heads and running toilets replaced</li>\n<li>Interior doors adjusted until they latch again</li>\n<li>Dents and cracks in drywall patched, sanded and painted</li>\n<li>Light fittings, dimmers and ceiling fans swapped over</li>\n<li>Baseboards, quarter round and closet shelving fitted</li>\n<li>Backsplash tile laid and tired caulking cut out</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Anything past swapping a fitting on an existing circuit belongs to a licensed electrician, and a drain buried behind a wall belongs to a licensed plumber. You will hear that from me on the phone, not after I have opened something up.</p>\n<h2>Working in the condo towers</h2>\n<p>Condo buildings near Square One, along the Hurontario corridor and down by the Mississauga lakeshore all run to the same rhythm. The concierge wants notice, the service elevator has to be booked, and management has its own paperwork before anything comes up in it. Send me the property manager\'s contact when you book and I will find out what the building expects ahead of the day, so nobody is standing in the lobby sorting it out.</p>\n<p>Upstairs it is mostly anchors and dust control. Concrete takes a hammer drill and a sleeve anchor. Drywall partitions get toggle bolts. Everything takes a drop sheet, because a condo has nowhere to make mess.</p>\n<h2>Booking and how the day goes</h2>\n<p>Photos help more than anything else. A picture of the wall, the fitting or the flat-pack box usually gets you a price without a site visit first. You will hear back from me inside one business day, usually sooner. Hours are Monday to Saturday, 8 in the morning to 8 at night, and Sunday by appointment.</p>\n<p>Several small jobs are best saved up. Six ten-minute tasks in one visit costs less than six separate trips across Mississauga. I supply materials and hand over the receipts, or I fit what you have already bought. When the work is done the offcuts and dust leave with me, and if something is not sitting right a week later I come back and put it right.</p>','Mississauga is really a dozen small towns joined together, and the repair changes with the postal code. An anchor that holds fine in a Meadowvale bedroom will pull straight out of a concrete wall in a tower by the highway, and a post-war bungalow in Malton hides its framing in places no scanner expects. Parking is easy across most of the city, so I can carry tools in once and start rather than shuttling from a lot.','Mounting televisions on concrete condo walls\nFlat-pack wardrobe assembly in Erin Mills bedrooms\nDrywall patching in 1980s Meadowvale hallways\nKitchen tap swaps in Cooksville bungalows\nCeiling fan replacement in Churchill Meadows homes\nAdjusting sagging interior doors in back-splits\nBacksplash tile and fresh kitchen caulking\nBaseboard and quarter round after new flooring','[{\"q\":\"Do you cover the far ends of Mississauga, like Malton and Meadowvale?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Malton, Meadowvale, Clarkson, Lorne Park, Cooksville, Erin Mills and everything in between are a normal drive for me. I work right across the Greater Toronto Area, so no corner of Mississauga counts as out of the way. Give me your cross streets when you get in touch and I will come back with a couple of dates that suit you.\"},{\"q\":\"Can a television go on the concrete wall in my condo?\",\"a\":\"Usually yes. Concrete needs a hammer drill and proper sleeve anchors rather than the plugs that come in the box, and once it is in, that bracket is not moving. Cables cannot be buried in a concrete wall, so I run them in a slim channel or down to the outlet neatly. Check your building rules on drilling before the date is set.\"},{\"q\":\"I have a list of little jobs, none of them urgent. Is that worth a booking?\",\"a\":\"That is my favourite kind of booking. Small tasks are expensive when they are done one at a time, because most of the cost is turning up. Write down everything that has been annoying you, send the list with a few photos, and I will price the whole lot as one visit. Most lists get finished in a single morning or afternoon.\"},{\"q\":\"My older home has plaster walls, not drywall. Does that change the work?\",\"a\":\"It changes the method. Plaster over lath or strapping cracks if you treat it like drywall, so I drill slowly, use fixings made for it, and add solid backing when something heavy has to hang there. Patching takes an extra step and a bit of drying time as well. I allow for that in the quote so the price does not move later.\"}]','',1,25,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (26,'brampton','Brampton','Peel Region',1,43.6832000,-79.7629000,'L6P, L6S, L6V, L6X, L6Y, L7A','Downtown Brampton\nBramalea\nHeart Lake\nSpringdale\nFletcher\'s Meadow\nFletcher\'s Creek\nMount Pleasant\nCredit Valley\nCastlemore\nBram East\nBram West\nSnelgrove\nNorthwood Park\nHuttonville','Gage Park\nBramalea City Centre\nChinguacousy Park\nRose Theatre\nHeart Lake Conservation Area\nBrampton GO Station\nProfessor\'s Lake\nMount Pleasant Village','mississauga,streetsville,caledon,bolton,vaughan,georgetown','Brampton Handyman - Repairs & Assembly','Looking for a handyman in Brampton? I fix, mount, assemble, patch and install across the city, with the price agreed before I start. Ask for a free quote.','Handyman in Brampton for Homes of Every Age','Most of the handyman work I do in Brampton is in houses somewhere between ten and thirty years old, and it shows in what breaks. Builder-grade taps start weeping at the base. Hollow doors sag and stop latching. Cabinet hinges give up one by one. None of it is dramatic, and none of it is worth a trade call-out on its own.\n\nTen years in and past 500 projects, I have learned that people are usually sitting on a list rather than a single job. I am Sercan, I do the work myself, and I would rather see the whole list. Send it over with a couple of photos and I will price the lot before I arrive, either fixed or hourly, agreed before anything starts.','<h2>Bramalea, downtown and the new north end</h2>\n<p>Brampton is three or four building eras sitting side by side. The sections of Bramalea went up in the 1960s as a planned community, so the bungalows and back-splits there share the same framing habits and the same tired hardware. Around Gage Park and the older downtown streets you find brick homes with plaster walls, stone foundations and window frames painted over more times than anyone can count.</p>\n<p>North and west it changes completely. Springdale, Fletcher\'s Meadow, Mount Pleasant and Credit Valley are subdivisions from the late 1990s onward. Tall front hallways, open stairwells, narrow lots and drywall everywhere. Good houses to work in, but the ceilings are high and half the useful jobs need a proper ladder rather than a step stool.</p>\n<h2>Doors, drywall and the things that move</h2>\n<p>The calls repeat, whichever end of the city you are in.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interior doors eased and re-hung so they latch</li>\n<li>Nail pops and cracked corners filled before painting</li>\n<li>Cabinet doors realigned and new handles fitted</li>\n<li>Vanities, taps and constantly running toilets replaced</li>\n<li>Curtain poles and heavy mirrors anchored properly</li>\n<li>Closet organisers and garage shelving put up</li>\n<li>Video doorbells, cameras and smart thermostats set up</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Anything that needs a new circuit, a panel change or work on the incoming supply is not mine to touch, and I will say so on the first call. That goes to a licensed electrician. Same with a structural change or a drain problem past the fixture, which belongs to a licensed plumber.</p>\n<h2>Bigger projects over several days</h2>\n<p>Not every job is an hour\'s work. Basement doors and trim, a room repainted after real drywall repairs, vinyl plank laid through a main floor, a bathroom refreshed with a new vanity, backsplash and clean caulking. Those run over a few days and I book them as a block, with the same price agreed before the first cut.</p>\n<p>Materials go either way. I buy them and hand you the receipts, or you buy exactly what you want and I fit it. In the newer subdivisions people usually want trim and door hardware to match what the builder used, so it is worth checking the garage for leftovers before anything new gets ordered.</p>\n<h2>What to have ready before I arrive</h2>\n<p>Clear a path to the work and move anything breakable out of the way. If parts have already been bought, leave them in the room with the instructions. For flat-pack, keep the boxes taped shut until I get there, because the hardware bags disappear the moment a box is opened. Jot down the small annoyances you have stopped noticing too, since those are usually the quickest wins of the day.</p>\n<p>My week runs Monday to Saturday, 8 a.m. until 8 p.m., with Sunday by appointment. I answer messages within one business day. Quotes are free and nothing is owed after one. Once the work in your Brampton home is finished the floor gets swept, the packaging leaves with me, and if something is not right I come back and sort it.</p>','Two things shape the work in Brampton. The subdivisions in the north went up fast and all at once, so the taps, hinges, doors and fittings in a whole house reach the end of their life in the same couple of years, and one visit usually covers six repairs. The older Bramalea and downtown streets are the opposite, solid but full of surprises behind the plaster. Driveways here are long, which means I park close and carry tools in once.','Nail pop repairs in tall Springdale stairwells\nRe-hanging sagging doors in Bramalea back-splits\nVanity and tap swaps in 2000s builds\nCurtain poles on high Mount Pleasant windows\nGarage shelving and closet organisers installed\nFence board and gate repairs after winter\nBasement trim, doors and baseboard fitting\nVideo doorbell and smart thermostat setup','[{\"q\":\"My Springdale house is barely fifteen years old. Why does so much need fixing?\",\"a\":\"Because everything in it was installed in the same week. Builder fittings are chosen to a budget, and taps, hinges, seals and door hardware all wear out on roughly the same timetable. Add a bit of settling and you get nail pops and doors that stick. Each item is small on its own, which is why they pile up until someone books a morning to deal with them together.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you also work on the older brick homes near Gage Park?\",\"a\":\"Often. Those houses have plaster, older framing and openings that were never quite square, so nothing is a straight swap. I check what is behind a wall before I fix anything to it, and I use fixings that suit plaster instead of drywall plugs. The work runs slower than in a new build and I price it that way from the start, not afterwards.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you take on something bigger, like a basement fit-out over several days?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Doors, trim, baseboards, flooring, patching and painting are all things I do, and a multi-day project gets booked as one block of time so I am not in and out of your house for weeks. The scope and the price get settled first. If part of it needs a licensed electrician or plumber, I tell you that before you commit.\"},{\"q\":\"The fence and deck took a beating over winter. Can you look at them in spring?\",\"a\":\"Spring is busy with exactly that. Split deck boards get replaced, loose railings tightened, gates re-hung and dropped hardware reset so a gate closes on its own again. Ground movement over a hard freeze throws posts and gates out of line more than people expect. If a post or frame has rotted through, I will tell you honestly that it needs rebuilding rather than patching.\"}]','',1,26,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (27,'port-credit','Port Credit','Peel Region',2,43.5545000,-79.5872000,'L5G, L5E, L5H','Old Port Credit Village\nCranberry Cove\nMineola East\nMineola West\nLakeview\nLorne Park\nClarkson\nCooksville\nKenollie\nLakeshore Road East corridor','Port Credit Lighthouse\nJ.C. Saddington Park\nPort Credit GO Station\nCredit River\nLakeshore Road East\nMemorial Park\nWaterfront Trail','mississauga,streetsville,oakville,etobicoke,burlington','Handyman in Port Credit - Home Repairs','Handyman in Port Credit for sticking doors, screens, mounting, assembly, drywall, taps and deck repairs. Price agreed before I start. Send a photo today.','Handyman Work in Port Credit and Mineola','A handyman working in Port Credit thinks about parking almost as much as about the job itself. The village streets are tight, driveways hold one car on a good day, and plenty of these houses were built before anyone owned two. I am Sercan. I load for the whole day, park once, and get on with the work instead of running back and forth to a vehicle three blocks away.\n\nMost of my Port Credit work sits in the older homes south of Lakeshore Road and in the low-rise condo buildings near the water. Repairs, furniture assembly, mounting, drywall, taps and fixtures, doors that have quietly stopped closing. Quote first, price agreed, then the work.','<h2>Cottage bones under newer paint</h2>\n<p>Plenty of houses in the old village started life as summer places and were winterised piece by piece over eighty years. That history turns up the moment you open a wall. Framing at odd spacing, a stud missing where every rule says it should be, three layers of flooring stacked on each other, and a back addition built to a different standard than the room it joins. Mineola is another story again, with 1950s ranch bungalows on deep treed lots, many renovated hard or replaced outright.</p>\n<p>None of that is a problem, it just means I look before I fix. I scan, I tap, and where a bracket has to carry a heavy mirror or a television I would rather find the real framing than trust one reading off a stud finder.</p>\n<h2>Wind, damp and the seasons</h2>\n<p>Living a few streets from the lake is good for most things and hard on a few. Exterior doors swell in August and rattle in February. Weather stripping flattens. Screens tear. Gate hardware seizes. Deck boards near the water go soft at the ends long before the rest of the deck is finished.</p>\n<p>Jobs that come around every year in Port Credit:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Front and back doors adjusted so they close and seal</li>\n<li>Weather stripping and door sweeps renewed</li>\n<li>Window and porch screens re-meshed or re-framed</li>\n<li>Deck boards swapped and loose railings tightened</li>\n<li>Fence pickets and gates re-hung after a hard freeze</li>\n<li>Bathroom caulking cut out and laid again properly</li>\n<li>Ceiling fans and light fittings changed over</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Inside the Port Credit condo buildings</h2>\n<p>The mid-rise blocks along the harbour and the newer streets going up on the old refinery lands all work to the same routine. Book the service elevator, sign in at the desk, keep the dust inside the unit, and be out of the loading area before the next person needs it. Give me the property manager\'s contact when you book and I will find out what the building asks for ahead of the day.</p>\n<p>Inside the unit it is anchors and patience. Concrete walls want a hammer drill and sleeve anchors. Partition walls take toggles. Balcony doors on this stretch of the shoreline take a beating from the wind, and most of them need their rollers and latch adjusted rather than replaced.</p>\n<h2>Getting a visit booked</h2>\n<p>Message me with photos and rough measurements and I can usually price the work without coming out first. A reply normally reaches you within one business day. Monday through Saturday I am working from 8 a.m. to 8 p.m., and Sunday visits happen by appointment for people who cannot manage a weekday.</p>\n<p>Tell me where to park when you confirm the date. On the streets off Lakeshore Road East that single detail decides whether the first twenty minutes are useful. Save your small items up and I will handle them in one trip. Materials come from me with receipts or you supply your own, and anything that genuinely needs a licensed electrician or plumber gets handed to one instead of bodged.</p>','Port Credit is a village that never quite stopped being one. Streets are narrow, driveways are short, and the older houses were pieced together over decades rather than built in one go, so measuring twice matters more here than in most places. Then there is the lake air, which works away at doors, screens, fences and deck boards year after year. The same handful of repairs comes back around like clockwork, and I plan the visit accordingly.','Swollen exterior doors adjusted and re-sealed\nPorch and window screen repairs near the lake\nDeck board replacement on older waterfront lots\nBalcony door roller and latch adjustments\nShelving anchored in cottage-era village walls\nFence gates re-hung after winter heaving\nCaulking renewed in small village bathrooms\nFlat-pack furniture built in tight upstairs rooms','[{\"q\":\"Parking is impossible on my street. Where are you going to leave the van?\",\"a\":\"If your driveway has room I will use it, and if not I find legal street parking nearby and carry the tools in. Tell me which side of the street is restricted and whether there are permit rules when you book. It is one line in a message and it saves twenty minutes of driving in circles on the day.\"},{\"q\":\"My front door sticks all summer and rattles all winter. Can that be sorted?\",\"a\":\"Usually yes, and it is a common one this close to the water. The fix is normally hinges, the strike plate, a new sweep and fresh weather stripping, plus easing the edge of the door where it binds. If the frame itself has dropped or the sill has gone soft, that is a bigger job and I will show you what I am seeing before going further.\"},{\"q\":\"I am in one of the buildings near the harbour. What does the property manager need?\",\"a\":\"Every building runs slightly differently, so I ask for their contact details early. Most want the service elevator booked, a sign-in at the desk, work kept inside set hours and no mess in the common areas. I get that arranged before the date. Inside your unit, drop sheets go down and everything I bring in leaves with me.\"},{\"q\":\"My old house does not have studs where they should be. Can you still hang things?\",\"a\":\"Yes, it just takes a bit more care. I scan, drill a small test hole where it will be hidden, and pick fixings that match whatever is actually behind the surface. For anything heavy I would rather open a small area and put solid backing in, then patch and paint, than rely on an anchor that is fighting old plaster.\"}]','',1,27,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (28,'streetsville','Streetsville','Peel Region',2,43.5860000,-79.7130000,'L5M, L5N','Streetsville village core\nVista Heights\nMeadowvale Village\nMeadowvale\nEast Credit\nCentral Erin Mills\nChurchill Meadows\nLevi Creek\nBritannia\nCredit Valley','Queen Street South\nStreetsville Memorial Park\nStreetsville GO Station\nCredit River\nVic Johnston Community Centre\nBread and Honey Festival','mississauga,brampton,port-credit,milton,georgetown,oakville','Streetsville Handyman - Repairs & Trim','Handyman services in Streetsville for century village homes and newer subdivisions alike: repairs, mounting, trim, taps, doors and assembly. Free quote first.','Handyman Services in Streetsville, Mississauga','Streetsville needs a handyman who slows down, because a good part of the village was built before power tools existed. The brick and frame houses off Queen Street have plaster on lath, old stone foundations, and door openings that have shifted in every direction since. I am Sercan. I work on my own, I find out what is behind a surface before I fix anything to it, and I clean up before I leave.\n\nNewer streets ring the old Streetsville core, and the work there is a different animal. Drywall, hollow doors and builder fittings all wearing out at once. Both get the same deal: a free quote, a price agreed before I start, and a straight answer if a licensed trade is needed instead.','<h2>Village houses and what sits behind the wall</h2>\n<p>Anything close to Queen Street South in Streetsville is likely a century old or more. Plaster on lath will not hold a drywall anchor, and it cracks in a wide arc if you drive a screw into it without thinking. Floors slope. Door openings are narrower than the doors sold today. Ceilings in the original part of a house sit lower than the addition tacked on behind it. It all works fine, it just needs patience and the right fixings.</p>\n<p>Repairs I do most in these houses are patching plaster properly rather than smearing filler across it, easing and re-hanging doors that have not shut since the last damp spell, and putting solid backing in before anything heavy goes on a wall.</p>\n<h2>Matching what is already there</h2>\n<p>Old houses are unforgiving about mismatched parts. Baseboard profiles from the 1890s are not on a shelf at the big-box store, and modern casing beside original trim looks wrong from across the room. Where I can, I take an offcut with me and find something close, or build the profile up out of two pieces so it reads right once it is painted.</p>\n<p>Hardware is the same story. Old hinge sizes, sticking sash windows and painted-shut catches usually have a fix that keeps the character rather than throwing it out. If you would rather go modern, that is your call, just tell me before anything is ordered.</p>\n<h2>The newer streets ringing Streetsville</h2>\n<p>Vista Heights, East Credit and the Meadowvale Village side are a different job entirely. Drywall, predictable stud spacing, and houses of an age where the original fittings are giving up together. In those homes the list usually looks like this:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kitchen and bathroom taps replaced</li>\n<li>Toilets that run constantly repaired or swapped</li>\n<li>Flat-pack furniture and closet systems assembled</li>\n<li>Televisions and heavy mirrors mounted securely</li>\n<li>Light fittings, dimmers and ceiling fans changed</li>\n<li>Vinyl plank laid with fresh baseboards after it</li>\n<li>Damaged drywall patched, primed and painted</li>\n</ul>\n<p>New wiring, a panel, or anything past swapping a fitting on a circuit that already exists is a licensed electrician\'s work. Same with drains past the fixture, which is a licensed plumber\'s. I say so before you book rather than halfway through a Saturday.</p>\n<h2>How a visit runs</h2>\n<p>Send a message with photos and I can usually price the work without coming out first. You should have an answer back within one business day. I am working Monday to Saturday, 8 a.m. to 8 p.m., and Sunday by appointment. Parking along Queen Street South fills through the middle of the day, so mornings tend to suit jobs in the village better.</p>\n<p>Keep your small tasks together and I will do them in a single visit, which costs less than calling someone out three separate times. Materials come from me with the receipts, or you buy what you want and I fit it. Dust sheets go down, the offcuts leave with me, and if something is not sitting right after I have gone, I come back and put it straight.</p>','The old core of Streetsville still reads as a village inside a city. Two short blocks of nineteenth-century brick and frame houses, subdivisions from the 1970s onward pressing in from every side, and the Credit River running through the middle of it. One morning I am chasing solid backing through plaster and lath, and the afternoon job is ordinary drywall and a stud finder. Knowing which of the two I am walking into matters before I quote a price.','Plaster and lath patching in village homes\nMatching baseboard profiles in century houses\nEasing and re-hanging old interior doors\nSolid backing added before heavy wall mounting\nVanity and tap replacement in 1980s bathrooms\nVinyl plank flooring with new trim installed\nCloset systems assembled in Vista Heights homes\nCeiling fan swaps in East Credit houses','[{\"q\":\"Can a heavy mirror go on the plaster wall of an old village house?\",\"a\":\"It can, but not on a plug pushed into plaster. I find the framing first, and if there is nothing usable where you want the mirror, I open a small area, fix solid backing across the studs, then patch and paint over it. Done that way it holds properly for good. It takes a little longer than a drywall job, and the quote reflects that.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you match the trim in my house instead of replacing all of it?\",\"a\":\"That is usually the better route in an older Streetsville home. Give me a spare offcut or let me take a profile from a hidden corner and I will find the nearest stock or build the shape from two pieces. Once it is primed and painted, the join disappears. If nothing close exists, I will tell you honestly rather than fitting something that looks wrong.\"},{\"q\":\"I am at work all day. Can the job happen without me at home?\",\"a\":\"I like to walk the job through with someone at the start, even for ten minutes, so the finish you want is clear to me. After that I am happy to carry on alone if you arrange access. I lock up when I leave and send photos of the finished work before you get home, so nothing is a surprise.\"},{\"q\":\"Do jobs in the old part of the village take longer than in the newer subdivisions?\",\"a\":\"Usually, yes. Nothing is square, wall build-ups vary from room to room, and parking on the main street eats time. I allow for that when I quote instead of adding it to the bill afterwards. On the newer streets the same task is faster and priced lower, which is why I ask which part of town you are in early on.\"}]','',1,28,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (29,'caledon','Caledon','Peel Region',2,43.8667000,-79.9333000,'L7C, L7E, L7K','Caledon East\nCaledon Village\nSouthfields Village\nMayfield West\nInglewood\nAlton\nCheltenham\nBelfountain\nTerra Cotta\nPalgrave\nValleywood\nMono Mills','Albion Hills Conservation Area\nForks of the Credit Provincial Park\nCheltenham Badlands\nBelfountain Conservation Area\nAlton Mill Arts Centre\nCaledon Trailway\nHighway 10\nAirport Road','bolton,brampton,georgetown,king-city,vaughan','Handyman in Caledon - Home Repairs','Handyman in Caledon for repairs, furniture assembly, TV mounting, drywall, fixtures, flooring and deck work. Free quotes on your whole list, so get in touch.','Handyman in Caledon and the Surrounding Villages','Finding a handyman who will actually drive out to Caledon is half the battle. I do, and I have been doing this work for more than ten years across the Greater Toronto Area. Caledon is spread out, so I plan the day around it. If you have a sticking door, a light fixture to swap and a fence board to replace, tell me all of it at once and I will bring what I need for the lot in one trip.\n\nMost of what I see out here is ordinary wear on houses that work hard. Wind, gravel dust and long winters take a toll on decks, gates, doors and trim. I quote before I start and leave the space clean.','<h2>No two Caledon houses are built the same way</h2><p>Drive from Cheltenham across to Southfields Village and you pass through a hundred and fifty years of building. There are fieldstone and red brick farmhouses with plaster over lath, board sheathing behind it, and framing that ignores every rule about studs sitting sixteen inches apart. There are village homes in Inglewood, Alton and Terra Cotta that have been added on to twice. There are estate houses from the 1980s and 1990s sitting on wide lots, and there are new streets near Mayfield Road where everything is square and predictable.</p><p>That mix matters, because the same job is a different job in each of them. Hanging a heavy mirror on modern drywall takes minutes. Hanging one on old plaster means finding solid timber first, drilling slowly so the face does not blow out, and using fixings made for the material. I would rather spend ten extra minutes getting that right than come back in a month.</p><h2>Making one trip count</h2><p>Caledon is a big place and the driving is real, so I plan the day around a list rather than a single task. Most people out here save things up, which suits me well. Send a few photos with your quote request and the whole lot can be priced properly before I load the van.</p><ul><li>Doors that stick, hinges that sag and deadbolts that no longer line up</li><li>Light fixtures, ceiling fans, dimmers and smart switches swapped for what is already there</li><li>Faucets, shower heads, toilets and the slow leak under the kitchen sink</li><li>Drywall patched and painted where a bracket or a doorknob went through</li><li>Flat-pack furniture, television mounts, shelving, mirrors and artwork</li><li>Deck boards, railings, gate latches and loose fence panels</li><li>Baseboards, trim and laminate or vinyl plank flooring</li></ul><h2>Winter always leaves a list behind</h2><p>Frost, wind and meltwater do most of the damage out here. A gate that closed cleanly in October drags by April. Fence posts lift. Deck boards cup, and the odd one splits along a screw line. Exterior doors swell and then shrink again, so the weather stripping stops sealing and the latch misses the strike plate. None of it is dramatic and all of it is easier to sort early.</p><p>Spring and early autumn are the sensible windows for that kind of work. Screens go back in, storm doors get adjusted, and caulking around tubs, sinks and windows gets renewed before the weather turns.</p><h2>Where I stop and hand it over</h2><p>I swap what already exists: a light for a light, a switch for a dimmer, a tired faucet for a new one, a toilet for a toilet. I do not run new circuits, open a panel, touch gas or make structural changes. That work belongs to a licensed electrician, a licensed plumber or the right contractor, and I will tell you straight away if a job crosses that line rather than discovering it halfway through.</p><p>Everything else gets a free quote with no obligation. You get a fixed price or an hourly rate agreed before the first tool comes out. I supply materials with receipts, or you buy your own, whichever suits. The work area is left clean, and if something is not right afterwards I come back and put it right.</p>','Distance is the first thing that shapes the work here. Properties sit far apart, many run on wells and septic, and plenty of them are a century older than the newest streets in town. Materials are not five minutes away, so I plan ahead and carry more. Open fields also mean wind and frost punish gates, decks and exterior doors far harder than they do on a sheltered city street.','Deck board and railing repairs on rural lots\nFence post resets after winter frost heave\nPatching plaster walls in century farmhouses\nReplacing dated light fixtures in older farmhouses\nAdjusting exterior doors that swell and stick\nMounting televisions on brick chimney breast walls\nAssembling flat-pack furniture in new Southfields homes\nRe-caulking tubs and vanities in village bathrooms\nReplacing window screens and weather stripping before winter','[{\"q\":\"Do you come out to the rural parts of Caledon or only the newer subdivisions?\",\"a\":\"Both. I cover the whole town, from the new streets off Mayfield Road to the villages and the concession roads out past Belfountain and Palgrave. The only thing I ask is that you give me the full list when you first get in touch. A long drive for one small job is hard to justify, so I would rather bring the whole list into a single visit.\"},{\"q\":\"My farmhouse walls are plaster and lath. Can you still hang heavy shelves or a television?\",\"a\":\"Usually yes, though it takes longer than drywall. I find the solid timber behind the plaster first, drill slowly with the right bit so the face does not crumble, and fix into wood wherever possible. Where there is no timber in the spot you want, I use anchors rated for plaster and I will tell you honestly what weight that mounting will carry.\"},{\"q\":\"Is there an extra charge for the drive out to Caledon Village or Alton?\",\"a\":\"You get one number before I start, and that is the number. Travel is worked into the quote rather than added to the bill afterwards, so there is nothing waiting for you at the end. Quotes are free and carry no obligation. Send photos with your request and I can usually price the job without an extra trip out to look at it.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you fix a gate that has dropped since the frost came out of the ground?\",\"a\":\"That is one of my regular spring jobs out this way. Often the post has heaved or the hinges have worked loose, and it comes down to resetting the hardware, squaring the gate and adding a brace or a better latch. If the post itself has failed, I will show you why, and I can replace that section rather than patch it twice.\"}]','',1,29,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (30,'bolton','Bolton','Peel Region',2,43.8807000,-79.7376000,'L7E, L7C','Old Bolton\nNorth Hill\nSouth Hill\nColeraine\nMacville\nPalgrave\nAlbion\nSandhill\nNobleton\nKleinburg\nCaledon East','Queen Street South\nDick\'s Dam Park\nHumber River valley\nAlbion Hills Conservation Area\nHighway 50\nBolton Camp','caledon,woodbridge,brampton,king-city,vaughan','Bolton Handyman for Home Repairs','Bolton handyman for door adjustments, drywall patching, TV mounting, fixture swaps, flooring and deck repairs. Free quotes and tidy work, so send a list.','Your Local Handyman in Bolton, Ontario','Bolton sits on both sides of the Humber valley, and a handyman here spends a fair part of the day on stairs. The older subdivisions on the North Hill and the South Hill were built into the slope, so backsplits, sidesplits and raised bungalows with walkouts are everywhere. Half-flights, split levels, decks perched above a drop. That changes how a vanity gets carried in and where a ladder can safely stand.\n\nTen years and more than 500 projects in, I know how these houses behave. Give me the list, I price it before anything starts, and you know the number ahead of time. One small repair, a room of new trim, or a few days of work all suit me equally.','<h2>Bolton is built on a slope, and the houses remember it</h2><p>The older streets climb away from the river on both sides of town. Homes built into a grade settle unevenly over thirty or forty years, and it shows up first at the doors. A bedroom door that has to be lifted before it will latch, a basement door scraping its frame, a patio slider that needs a shoulder behind it. Nine times out of ten that is hinges, a strike plate and a little patience rather than a new door.</p><p>Stairs are the other constant in Bolton. Handrails that have gone loose, a spindle that has worked free, scuffed and dented wall corners where furniture has travelled up and down for years. All of it is quick work once somebody is standing there with the right screws and a bit of filler.</p><h2>What people here call me for</h2><p>Very little of it is exotic. Street to street, the list looks much the same:</p><ul><li>Interior doors shimmed, planed and re-hung so they latch again</li><li>Television brackets fitted on family room walls, including above a fireplace</li><li>Vanities, faucets, shower heads and running toilets replaced</li><li>Ceiling fans and light fixtures changed for something that suits the room</li><li>Drywall repaired and painted where a doorknob or a bracket went through</li><li>Deck boards, railings and gate hardware sorted before the season starts</li><li>Shelving, closet rods, mirrors and artwork hung on proper fixings</li></ul><h2>The old core down by Queen Street</h2><p>Around Queen Street and the mill flats sit the oldest buildings in Bolton, and they behave differently from the hill subdivisions. Plaster walls, tight staircases, doorways that were never sized for a modern vanity or a wide sofa, and the occasional partition that turns out to be solid brick behind the finish. I measure the route in before promising that anything will fit, because getting a bathroom vanity up a Victorian staircase is a planning job as much as a lifting job.</p><p>Older houses also hide their fixings. I use a detector, then a small test hole somewhere that will be covered anyway, rather than guessing and leaving you a row of holes to fill in later.</p><h2>How a visit actually runs</h2><p>You send a list, ideally with a few photos, and I usually reply within one business day. The quote is free, either a fixed price for the job or an hourly rate, and nothing starts until you have agreed the number. If I am supplying materials you get the receipts; if you would rather buy your own, that is completely fine.</p><p>Most Bolton visits cover several small jobs at once rather than three separate trips, which is why I ask for the whole list up front. Offcuts and packaging leave with me, the floor gets swept, and the room goes back roughly the way it was. If something is not right afterwards, say so and I will come back and put it right.</p>','Almost every older street in town runs up or down a grade, and that shapes the housing: splits, raised bungalows and walkouts, with retaining walls and stepped decks out the back. Doors and staircases take the brunt of the settling that follows. The village core near the river is a different world again, with plaster finishes, narrow stairs and rooms that were never sized for modern fittings or modern furniture.','Adjusting interior doors in settled backsplit homes\nRebuilding deck stairs on sloped back yards\nMounting televisions above split-level family room fireplaces\nPatching drywall along staircase walls and corners\nReplacing bathroom faucets in 1980s subdivision ensuites\nFixing gate latches and fence panels after winter\nInstalling closet shelving in older village bedrooms\nSwapping hallway light fixtures for dimmers and fans','[{\"q\":\"The doors in my backsplit will not latch unless I lift them. Can that be fixed without buying new doors?\",\"a\":\"Almost always. A house built into a slope settles, and the frames go slightly out of square along with it. I reset the hinges, add shims, move the strike plate, and plane the edge if the door needs it. It ends up latching properly again on the original hardware. New doors are a last resort and I will say so plainly if that is where it ends up.\"},{\"q\":\"Will a bathroom vanity make it up the stairs in one of the older houses near Queen Street?\",\"a\":\"Sometimes, and it is worth checking before you buy one. Send me a photo of the staircase, the landing and the doorway, with a rough width measurement, and I will tell you what will make the turn. If the vanity you like will not fit through, a narrower cabinet or a top fitted separately once it is upstairs normally solves the problem.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you take work on the newer streets at the north and east edges of Bolton as well as the old part?\",\"a\":\"Yes, all of it. Newer homes tend to need mounting, assembly, closet shelving and fixture swaps, while the older part of town needs patching, door work and general repair. Either way it is the same visit and the same approach. If you have jobs waiting at two addresses in town, tell me about both and I will plan a sensible run between them.\"},{\"q\":\"My deck sits on the slope at the back and the boards and railing have gone soft. Can you repair it?\",\"a\":\"Deck repair is regular work for me. Replacing boards, tightening or rebuilding a railing, sorting the stairs, swapping rusted fasteners. What I do not take on is structural rebuilding. If the posts or the frame underneath have gone, I will tell you plainly that it needs a deck builder rather than patch over something that is not safe.\"}]','',1,30,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (31,'vaughan','Vaughan','York Region',1,43.8361000,-79.4983000,'L4H, L4J, L4K, L4L, L6A','Woodbridge\nMaple\nConcord\nKleinburg\nThornhill\nVellore Village\nPatterson\nSonoma Heights\nIslington Woods\nPine Valley\nCarrville\nNashville\nVaughan Metropolitan Centre','Canada\'s Wonderland\nVaughan Mills\nVaughan Metropolitan Centre subway station\nMcMichael Canadian Art Collection\nKortright Centre for Conservation\nBoyd Conservation Area\nCortellucci Vaughan Hospital\nHighway 7','woodbridge,maple,concord,thornhill,richmond-hill,north-york','Handyman in Vaughan, York Region','Handyman services in Vaughan for TV mounting, assembly, shelving, fixture swaps, tiling and flooring in houses and condos. Free quotes, so send me your list.','Handyman Services Across Vaughan, Ontario','A lot of my handyman work in Vaughan happens in houses that are not old at all. Vellore Village, Patterson and Sonoma Heights went up over the last twenty-five years, and the framing is straight and predictable. What those homes need is everything the builder never finished: the mud room with no hooks, the walk-in closet with a single rod, the garage stacked on the floor, the big television still sitting on a stand.\n\nThen there is the other side of the city. Older Woodbridge streets, Kleinburg village, Concord bungalows and the new towers by the subway station. Different buildings, different rules, same approach. I look at the job, give you a price before starting, and stick to it.','<h2>New houses, unfinished lists</h2><p>Vellore Village, Patterson, Sonoma Heights and the streets around Cortellucci Vaughan Hospital are mostly detached homes and towns from the last twenty-five years. They are well framed, so hanging things is straightforward and I can usually find a stud without hunting for it. What these houses need is the finishing that never happened after moving day, and it tends to sit on the list for years.</p><p>Height is the local quirk. Two-storey foyers, open stairwells and great rooms with ceilings well above three metres are normal in this part of York Region, and they change how a simple job gets done. A mirror over a stairwell, or a fixture at the top of a foyer, needs the right ladder and a safe footing rather than a chair and optimism.</p><ul><li>Televisions mounted on tall feature walls with the cables tucked away</li><li>Closet shelving, hanging rods and garage storage fixed to solid timber</li><li>Builder light fixtures swapped for pendants, flush mounts or a ceiling fan</li><li>Switches changed for dimmers or smart switches in the existing box</li><li>Flat-pack furniture assembled, levelled and anchored back to the wall</li><li>Backsplash tile, cabinet handles and fresh caulking in kitchens and bathrooms</li><li>Laminate or vinyl plank flooring finished off with baseboards and trim</li></ul><h2>Condos around the subway station</h2><p>The towers at the Vaughan Metropolitan Centre and along Highway 7 come with their own rules. Most buildings want the service elevator booked in advance, some ask trades for paperwork at the concierge desk, and a few limit the hours when noisy work is allowed. Ask your property manager what the building expects and pass it on to me before the day, so nothing stalls at the front desk.</p><p>Inside a unit the work is usually mounting and assembly, a balcony door that has dropped on its rollers, a bathroom that needs re-caulking, or a video doorbell and thermostat set up and talking to your phone.</p><h2>Woodbridge, Kleinburg and the older streets</h2><p>Not all of Vaughan is new. Woodbridge has plenty of 1970s and 1980s homes where the original bathrooms and kitchens are still in daily service, and Kleinburg village has houses far older than that, with the plaster and the odd framing to match. Those get the same approach as everywhere else, only with more time spent working out what is behind the wall before anything goes into it.</p><h2>Bigger jobs, planned properly</h2><p>Some of what I do runs across several days: a room of new flooring with trim to match, a bathroom refresh with a vanity, new taps and re-tiled splashes, or a long punch list before a house goes on the market. Those get walked through, written down and priced before the first day, so there is no drifting scope and no surprise at the end.</p><p>If part of the plan needs a licensed electrician or plumber, say new circuits or moving a drain, I flag it early and you can bring that trade in. Everything I take on gets a free quote, an agreed price, receipts for any materials I supply, and a proper clean-up at the end. If something is not right, I come back.</p>','Two things define the work here. First, scale. The houses built since 2000 are large, with tall ceilings and double-height foyers, so half the job is often reaching the spot safely. Second, contrast. A Kleinburg village home and a glass tower at the subway station sit inside the same city, and the rules for working in each are nothing alike. Condo boards in particular want notice, elevator bookings and paperwork.','Mounting large televisions on two-storey great room walls\nAssembling flat-pack furniture in Vellore Village homes\nBuilding out closet shelving in Patterson houses\nHanging mirrors and artwork in tall stairwells\nSwapping builder light fixtures for new ones\nAdjusting balcony doors in Vaughan Metropolitan Centre condos\nRe-caulking showers in older Woodbridge bathrooms\nRepairing fence boards along Concord back lots','[{\"q\":\"Can you work in a condo at the Vaughan Metropolitan Centre?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and a little planning makes it painless. Most towers want the service elevator booked, and some ask trades to hand paperwork to the concierge before work can start. Ring your property manager, find out exactly what your building requires, and send it over ahead of the day. Then the visit is spent on your job instead of on the lobby.\"},{\"q\":\"The great room wall is two storeys high. Is it safe to mount a television that far up?\",\"a\":\"It can be, and it is a job I do often in the newer parts of the city. I locate the studs, use a bracket rated well above the weight of the set, and fix into timber rather than trusting drywall anchors. I will also talk through the viewing height first, because higher is not always better once you are sitting on the sofa.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you put in pot lights or add a new outlet?\",\"a\":\"No, and I would rather tell you that before you book. New wiring, new circuits and anything at the panel belong to a licensed electrician in Ontario. What I do is like for like: swapping a light fixture, fitting a ceiling fan where a fixture already hangs, or changing a switch for a dimmer or a smart switch in the existing box.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you cover Woodbridge and Kleinburg, or only the newer subdivisions?\",\"a\":\"All of it. Woodbridge, Kleinburg, Concord, Maple, Thornhill and the towers by the subway are part of a normal week for me. The buildings differ but the method does not: look at the job, quote it free with no obligation, agree the price before starting, work tidy, and clear up properly at the end.\"}]','',1,31,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (32,'markham','Markham','York Region',1,43.8561000,-79.3370000,'L3P, L3R, L3S, L6B, L6E','Unionville\nMarkham Village\nMilliken Mills\nThornhill\nCornell\nWismer Commons\nBerczy Village\nGreensborough\nBox Grove\nCachet\nAngus Glen\nArmadale\nButtonville\nVictoria Square','Main Street Unionville\nMarkville Mall\nToogood Pond\nPacific Mall\nMilne Dam Conservation Park\nMarkham Civic Centre\nFlato Markham Theatre\nHighway 7','unionville,richmond-hill,thornhill,scarborough,stouffville,agincourt','Markham Handyman for Repairs and Mounting','Markham handyman for furniture assembly, TV mounting, taps and toilets, doors, drywall and trim work. Free quotes and a price agreed first, so get in touch.','Handyman in Markham for Repairs Big and Small','The most common handyman call I get in Markham is not a repair at all. It is a box. Flat-pack wardrobes, desks, bed frames and shelving units that arrived in the back of a car and now have to become furniture, usually up a flight of stairs in a Wismer or Greensborough town home. After that comes television mounting, then curtain rods, mirrors and artwork.\n\nOlder parts of the city keep me busy in a different way. Milliken Mills and Markham Village homes from the 1970s and 1980s are on their second or third set of taps, hinges and light fittings by now. I quote first, agree the number with you, and clean up before I leave.','<h2>The repairs that come up again and again</h2><p>Markham grew in waves, and each wave has its own list. Houses in Milliken Mills, Armadale and Buttonville from the 1970s and 1980s are on their second or third set of taps, toilets, hinges and light fittings. Nothing has failed dramatically. It has all simply worn out at roughly the same time, which is why one visit often covers five jobs. Wismer, Greensborough, Cornell and Box Grove are the opposite: those homes are barely broken in, and the calls are about finishing them off.</p><p>Sinks and showers sit in the middle of both groups. A dripping tap, a shower head with no pressure left in it, a toilet that runs quietly all night, a slow leak in the cabinet under the kitchen sink. Those are like-for-like swaps and they are some of the most satisfying half hours in the job.</p><h2>Town homes, stairs and laneway garages</h2><p>Cornell and the newer town home pockets are built tall and narrow, with living space on the middle floor and bedrooms above. Furniture has to travel up two flights, sometimes around a landing that will not take a long flat box, so I check the route before anything gets opened. Rear laneway garages bring their own small jobs: an entry door that has dropped on its hinges, a torn weather seal, an exterior light or a video doorbell to fit by the back gate.</p><h2>Older Markham and Unionville</h2><p>Around Main Street Unionville and the older end of Markham Village the buildings are frame and plaster, and some are well over a century old. Walls are rarely flat, studs are rarely where a detector first suggests, and a floor can run downhill by a few millimetres across one room. None of that is a problem. It just means scribing trim to the wall, packing shelves level and choosing fixings that suit what is actually behind the surface.</p><ul><li>Flat-pack wardrobes, beds and desks assembled and anchored safely</li><li>Televisions and floating shelves mounted on solid fixings</li><li>Kitchen and bathroom taps, shower heads and toilets replaced</li><li>Doors adjusted, with new handles, hinges or weather stripping</li><li>Drywall dents, cracks and screw holes patched and painted</li><li>Video doorbells, cameras and thermostats fitted and set up</li><li>Cabinet hardware, backsplash tile and fresh caulking</li><li>Laminate and vinyl plank flooring with baseboards to finish</li></ul><h2>What to have ready before I arrive</h2><p>Very little, honestly. Clear a path to the work and move anything breakable out of the way. If you have already bought the fitting, the flooring or the furniture, leave it in the room it belongs in. If you would rather I supplied the parts, say so when you ask for the quote and the receipts come with the bill. Parking is easy on most streets around here, but in a town home block with permit-only visitor spaces, let me know how it works there.</p><p>Photos help more than anything else. A picture of the tap, the wall, the ceiling fitting or the box sitting on the floor lets me price the work properly and turn up with the right parts on the first visit, rather than adding a second trip to the hardware store.</p>','Few places in the region pack this much variety into one municipality. Century frame houses near Main Street Unionville, wide 1980s subdivisions, tall narrow freehold towns with rear laneway garages, and condo blocks along Highway 7. The one thing they share is that access decides how a job goes. Stairs, landings, elevators and where the van can sit usually matter more to the day than the repair itself does.','Flat-pack furniture assembly in Wismer town homes\nTelevision mounting in Cornell freehold towns\nReplacing worn taps in 1980s Milliken bathrooms\nAdjusting laneway garage entry doors and seals\nPatching drywall after moving day in Greensborough\nHanging mirrors and artwork in Unionville century homes\nFitting baseboards and trim after new flooring\nInstalling video doorbells on brick town home porches','[{\"q\":\"The furniture I bought has to go up to a third floor bedroom in a town home. Is that a problem?\",\"a\":\"Not usually, but send me a photo of the stairs and any landing before the day. Tall narrow homes in Cornell and Greensborough often have a turn that a long flat box simply will not make. If it will not go up boxed, I assemble in stages on the landing or in the bedroom itself and carry the parts through separately.\"},{\"q\":\"Are the walls in newer Wismer and Berczy Village homes strong enough for a heavy television?\",\"a\":\"The studs are, and studs are what I fix into. Framing in these houses is regular and easy to read, so the bracket lands on solid timber instead of hollow board. On the narrow walls between windows there is sometimes only one stud to work with, and I will tell you honestly if the mount needs to shift over a little.\"},{\"q\":\"My house near Markham Village is from the seventies and nothing seems to be a standard size. Can you still work with it?\",\"a\":\"That is a large part of what I do. Older homes have taps with unusual spreads, doors that are no longer square and trim profiles discontinued decades ago. I measure first, source parts that genuinely fit, and scribe trim to the wall instead of forcing it flat. When a part is truly unavailable, I show you the closest sensible option before ordering anything.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you take on work in the older houses around Main Street Unionville?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Those homes want a gentler touch: plaster instead of drywall, timber that has moved over a century, and finishes worth protecting. I work slowly, test before drilling and keep dust under control. Should a job risk damaging something original, or need a heritage specialist, I will tell you that rather than press on and hope for the best.\"}]','',1,32,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (33,'richmond-hill','Richmond Hill','York Region',1,43.8828000,-79.4403000,'L4B, L4C, L4E, L4S','Mill Pond\nOak Ridges\nLake Wilcox\nBayview Hill\nRichvale\nNorth Richvale\nJefferson\nRouge Woods\nCrosby\nBeverley Acres\nDevonsleigh\nObservatory\nHeadford\nElgin Mills','Mill Pond Park\nDavid Dunlap Observatory\nHillcrest Mall\nRichmond Green Sports Centre and Park\nLake Wilcox Park\nYonge Street\nRichmond Hill Centre for the Performing Arts\nBathurst Street','thornhill,maple,markham,unionville,aurora,vaughan','Handyman in Richmond Hill, York Region','Handyman services in Richmond Hill for repairs, assembly, mounting, drywall, painting and fixture swaps. Free quotes, price agreed first. Send me your list.','Handyman Services in Richmond Hill','I am Sercan, the handyman a lot of people in Richmond Hill call when the list on the fridge gets too long. I work on my own, I have been doing repairs and small improvements for more than ten years, and I have finished over five hundred projects. Richmond Hill runs a long way north along Yonge Street, so the houses I walk into are rarely built the same way twice.\n\nThat suits me. One morning it is a plaster patch in a post-war bungalow near the Mill Pond, the afternoon is a television going up in a new build with engineered studs. Either way you get a free quote first, a price agreed before I start, and a clean floor when I leave.','<h2>Richmond Hill was not built in one go</h2><p>Drive Yonge Street from Crosby up to Lake Wilcox and you pass through about seventy years of building. Around the Mill Pond and Beverley Acres there are post-war bungalows and side-splits with plaster walls, solid wood trim and studs sitting wherever the builder felt like putting them. Bayview Hill and North Richvale went up through the 1980s in brick. Jefferson, Rouge Woods and much of Oak Ridges are newer again, and a fair number of homes beside Lake Wilcox started life as summer cottages before being rebuilt for year-round living.</p><p>Those differences matter more than they sound. A shelf anchored into plaster and lath needs a different approach from one going into 2010 drywall over engineered framing. I check what I am drilling into before I drill, which is why a mirror I hang stays hung.</p><h2>What people here call me about</h2><p>Most weeks in Richmond Hill are a mix of small things. A door that stopped latching once the frame settled, a kitchen tap that drips, flat-pack furniture that arrived in five boxes, a bathroom that needs fresh caulking before water gets behind the tile.</p><ul><li>Mounting televisions over fireplaces in newer Jefferson homes</li><li>Patching and repainting plaster around older basement stairwells</li><li>Swapping builder-grade light fixtures for something better</li><li>Adjusting and rehanging interior doors that stick in summer</li><li>Replacing toilets, shower heads and leaking taps under sinks</li><li>Repairing gates and fence boards after a hard freeze</li><li>Assembling closet systems, bed frames and office desks</li></ul><p>If a job needs a licence, meaning a new circuit, a panel change or anything involving gas, I say so and point you to a licensed electrician or plumber. I would rather lose the work than do it wrong.</p><h2>Condo and apartment work near the Yonge corridor</h2><p>The towers around Richmond Hill Centre, and the rental buildings closer to Major Mackenzie Drive, come with their own rules. Most want the service elevator booked ahead and paperwork filed with property management. Tell me the building name when you get in touch and I will ask what is needed before the day arrives, so nobody is standing in a lobby sorting it out.</p><p>Inside a unit the work is usually mounting, blinds, shelving, closet fittings and fixture swaps. Concrete ceilings and walls mean proper anchors and a bit of dust, so I bring a vacuum and cover the floor before anything gets drilled.</p><h2>How a visit actually works</h2><p>Send me a short list with photos and I will usually reply within one business day with a fixed price or an hourly rate, whichever fits the job better. You agree to it before I start. If you have five small tasks waiting, put them into one visit rather than calling me back five times. It costs you less and it is a better use of the trip.</p><p>Materials come with receipts if I supply them, or you buy your own where you have a particular colour or model in mind. The work area gets cleaned up before I go. If something is not sitting right a week later, tell me and I come back and sort it out.</p>','Building eras change quickly here. Older homes near the Mill Pond and Crosby hide plaster, odd stud spacing and original trim worth keeping. Bayview Hill and Richvale are 1980s brick. Jefferson, Rouge Woods and Oak Ridges are newer, with tall great-room walls and builder fixtures people want changed, and a number of Lake Wilcox houses began as cottages. Add the condo towers near Highway 7 with their elevator bookings and no two days match.','TV mounting on tall Jefferson great-room walls\nPlaster patching in Mill Pond area bungalows\nDoor adjustments in settling Oak Ridges homes\nTap and toilet swaps in Bayview Hill bathrooms\nFence and gate repair after winter freeze\nShelving anchored into concrete condo walls\nFlat-pack assembly in Rouge Woods basements\nBacksplash and caulking refresh in dated kitchens','[{\"q\":\"Do you cover Oak Ridges and Lake Wilcox as well as the south end of town?\",\"a\":\"Yes. I work the whole of Richmond Hill, from the older streets near Crosby and the Mill Pond right up past Bloomington Road to Lake Wilcox. The north end is a longer drive, so I try to group jobs up there into a single visit. If you have a few tasks waiting, list them all and I will price them together.\"},{\"q\":\"My house near the Mill Pond has plaster walls. Can you still hang a heavy mirror?\",\"a\":\"Usually, yes. Plaster and lath does not behave like drywall, so I find the framing first rather than trusting a stud finder on its own. For heavy pieces I fix into solid wood where I can and use anchors rated well above the weight where I cannot. It takes a little longer and the wall stays intact.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you work in the condo towers near Yonge Street and Highway 7?\",\"a\":\"Condo work like that is fine. Those buildings normally want the service elevator booked ahead and paperwork filed with property management before tools come in. Give me the building name when you ask for a quote and I will check with the concierge what they require, so nothing holds the job up on the day itself.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to decks and fences here after a hard winter?\",\"a\":\"Frost heave and ice do most of the damage. I usually find loose gate hinges, boards that have cupped, and posts that shifted enough to make a gate drag on the ground. I can reset hardware, replace boards and rails, and get gates swinging properly again. Anything needing a post pulled and reset takes longer, so I say so before starting.\"}]','',1,33,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (34,'thornhill','Thornhill','York Region',2,43.8161000,-79.4245000,'L3T, L4J','Thornhill Village\nRoyal Orchard\nGrandview\nBayview Glen\nGerman Mills\nBrownridge\nBeverley Glen\nThornhill Woods\nUplands\nCrestwood\nLangstaff\nDoncrest','Promenade Mall\nThornhill Community Centre\nPomona Mills Park\nGerman Mills Settlers Park\nYonge Street\nCentre Street\nSteeles Avenue','richmond-hill,markham,concord,maple,willowdale,north-york','Thornhill Handyman for Home Repairs','Handyman services in Thornhill for sticking doors, drywall, painting, fixtures and furniture assembly. Free quotes and a price agreed first. Call me.','Handyman Services in Thornhill, Ontario','The job I get called for most as a handyman in Thornhill is a door that will not close properly. Plenty of the houses here went up between 1960 and 1980, the frames have settled since, and a strike plate that lined up perfectly in 1974 does not line up now. I am Sercan. I work on my own, ten years in and past five hundred finished projects.\n\nDoors are only the start of it. The same houses want drywall and plaster patched, taps replaced, ceiling fans hung and kitchens tidied up short of a full renovation. This area straddles Markham and Vaughan, which confuses maps more than it confuses me. Free quote, agreed price before I start, tidy space at the end.','<h2>Houses in Thornhill are at the age where things need attention</h2><p>Royal Orchard, Grandview, Bayview Glen and the streets off Centre Street are full of ranch bungalows, back-splits and two-storey homes from the 1960s and 1970s. They were built well. They are also fifty-odd years old, which shows up as original hollow-core doors, sliding patio doors running on tired rollers, stipple ceilings, and bathrooms where the caulking gave up a while ago.</p><p>Old Thornhill village near Yonge and Centre is older again, with heritage homes that have plaster walls, thick baseboards and floors that are not quite level. Work there is slower and more careful. Newer pockets like Beverley Glen and Brownridge behave more like a modern subdivision, with drywall, nail pops and builder fixtures people are ready to upgrade.</p><h2>Put the small jobs together</h2><p>Most people have a list rather than a single task. Batching them into one visit is cheaper for you and simpler for me, so write everything down before you get in touch, including the things that feel too minor to mention.</p><ul><li>Easing and rehanging doors that stick or will not latch</li><li>Replacing patio door rollers and adjusting sliding screens</li><li>Patching stipple ceilings and repainting after a small leak</li><li>Fitting new vanity taps, shower heads and toilet seats</li><li>Hanging heavy mirrors and artwork on plaster walls</li><li>Swapping tired light fixtures for dimmers and smart switches</li><li>Recaulking tubs, showers and kitchen backsplashes</li></ul><p>Light fixtures, dimmers and switches replacing what is already there are fine. A new circuit, a panel change or anything gas-related is not my work, and you get pointed to a licensed electrician or plumber instead.</p><h2>Apartments and condos near Promenade and Steeles</h2><p>Between Bathurst, Steeles Avenue and the Promenade there are plenty of apartment and condo units, and many are rentals or homes people have downsized into. Buildings there usually want the service elevator booked ahead and paperwork filed with property management, so name the building when you ask for a quote and I will find out what is required.</p><p>In-suite jobs are mostly mounting televisions on concrete walls, putting up blinds and curtain rods, assembling furniture that came up in the lift in boxes, and changing out fixtures. I bring a vacuum. Concrete makes dust and nobody wants it left behind.</p><h2>Seasons change what the phone rings about</h2><p>Spring brings fence boards and gates that shifted over winter, deck rails that worked loose, and screens that tore. Summer humidity swells doors so they bind at the top corner. Autumn is weather stripping, door sweeps, and getting exterior lights and video doorbells sorted before it goes dark at five. Winter is mostly indoor work: shelving, drywall, painting, flooring and the jobs put off all year.</p><p>Whatever the season, the routine is the same. Free quote, a fixed price or hourly rate agreed first, materials with receipts if I supply them, and a clean space when I go. If something is not right afterwards, I come back and put it right.</p>','Thornhill sits across two municipalities and about a century of building, from the heritage homes at Yonge and Centre to the 1970s streets in Royal Orchard and the newer houses west of Bathurst. Most of my work here is maintenance on homes old enough to need it: doors that bind, patio rollers, stipple ceilings, tired caulking and fixtures nobody has touched since they went in. The condo buildings near Steeles add elevator bookings to the plan.','Patio door roller replacement in 1970s homes\nStipple ceiling patching after a small leak\nSticking interior doors eased and rehung\nHeavy mirrors anchored to old plaster walls\nVanity tap and shower head replacement\nTV mounting on concrete condo walls near Promenade\nWeather stripping and door sweeps before winter\nGate and fence board repair after frost heave','[{\"q\":\"This area is split between Markham and Vaughan. Does that change anything for you?\",\"a\":\"Not for the work itself. I cover both sides, east and west of Yonge Street, and the drive is much the same either way. The only place it matters is permits, and the jobs I do, meaning repairs, fixtures, assembly, patching and painting, do not need them. Anything that would need a permit or a licensed trade gets handed on.\"},{\"q\":\"My sliding patio door is heavy to open. Is that something you can fix?\",\"a\":\"Usually it is the rollers, the track, or both. After forty or fifty years the wheels flatten and the track collects grit. I lift the door out, clean the track, fit new rollers and adjust the height so it latches properly again. If the frame itself has failed, I will tell you honestly that a replacement door is the better spend.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you match the stipple ceiling in my back-split?\",\"a\":\"I can get close on a patch. Older stipple varies from house to house, so I feather the repair, texture it to blend and paint the whole ceiling where that gives a better result than a visible square. On large areas it is sometimes tidier to take the texture off and finish flat, and I talk you through both before starting.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you take on bigger projects in the older Thornhill houses, or only small repairs?\",\"a\":\"Both. Plenty of my visits are a two-hour list of small things, but I also take multi-day work: a bathroom refresh, laminate through a main floor, trim and baseboards, or a basement tidied up room by room. Same approach either way, with a price agreed before I start and one person doing the work from beginning to end.\"}]','',1,34,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (35,'woodbridge','Woodbridge','York Region',2,43.7758000,-79.5960000,'L4H, L4L','Woodbridge Village\nMarket Lane\nWest Woodbridge\nEast Woodbridge\nIslington Woods\nSonoma Heights\nWeston Downs\nPine Valley\nVellore Village\nKleinburg','Market Lane Shopping Centre\nWoodbridge Fairgrounds\nBoyd Conservation Area\nHumber River\nIslington Avenue\nHighway 7\nPine Valley Drive\nKortright Centre for Conservation','maple,concord,vaughan,brampton,etobicoke,thornhill','Handyman in Woodbridge, Vaughan','Handyman services in Woodbridge for TV mounting, deck and fence repair, drywall, fixtures and furniture assembly. Free quotes, price agreed first. Message me.','Handyman Services in Woodbridge, Vaughan','A good share of what I do as a handyman in Woodbridge happens at the top of a ladder. Houses built here through the 1990s and 2000s have two-storey foyers, great rooms with high walls and open stairwells that most people would rather not work over. I am Sercan, and I have been doing this on my own for more than ten years across the Greater Toronto Area.\n\nNot everything is that dramatic. Half my visits are taps, doors, shelving and flat-pack furniture. Woodbridge also has an older core around Market Lane and the fairgrounds, where the houses are much smaller and much older, and the work there is a different kind of careful. Quotes are free and the price is agreed before I start.','<h2>Tall walls in newer Woodbridge homes</h2><p>Homes in Sonoma Heights, Weston Downs and Islington Woods tend to be generous inside. Two-storey foyers, open stairwells, and great rooms with a fireplace running up a wall nobody can reach. Mounting a television above that fireplace, hanging a large piece of art, changing a bulb in a foyer fixture or fitting a ceiling fan under a fourteen-foot ceiling all take proper ladders and a plan for getting them in without marking the walls.</p><p>Chimney chases and fireplace walls hide surprises too. Sometimes there is solid framing behind the stone, sometimes there is not, and sometimes the cable and outlet were never brought up. I check first, tell you what is actually in there, and price the job from that instead of a guess.</p><h2>Ravine lots and the Humber valley</h2><p>Plenty of Woodbridge streets back onto the Humber River valley or the land near Boyd Conservation Area. Those lots are lovely and hard on outdoor woodwork. Damp, shade and frost work on deck boards, railings, gates and fence posts faster than they do on an open lot, and the ground moves enough that a gate closing fine in October drags by April.</p><p>Repairs I take on include replacing boards and rails, resetting hinges and latches, tightening railings, and swapping fence panels that have gone soft. Structural rebuilds and anything altering the deck framing are not my line of work.</p><h2>The everyday Woodbridge list</h2><p>Most calls are ordinary and that suits me. If several jobs are waiting, save them up and I will get through them in one booking rather than several.</p><ul><li>Flat-pack wardrobes, beds and office furniture built properly</li><li>Fixture swaps in basement kitchens and second laundry rooms</li><li>Drywall patching and touch-up painting in busy hallways</li><li>Garage shelving and overhead storage put up securely</li><li>Video doorbells, thermostats and smart switches set up</li><li>Interior door handles, locks and hinges sorted out</li><li>Backsplash tiling and fresh caulking around sinks and tubs</li></ul><p>Replacing a fixture, switch, dimmer, tap or toilet already in place is straightforward. New wiring, a panel, gas or structural changes are not, and those go to a licensed electrician or plumber.</p><h2>What to have ready</h2><p>Photos are the fastest route to a price. Send a picture of the wall, the fixture or the broken thing, mention the ceiling height if it is high, and say whether there is room to park on the driveway. That usually gets a fixed price or an hourly rate back within one business day, before anybody commits to anything.</p><p>On the day, clear a path to the work and move anything breakable out of the way. I supply materials with receipts, or you buy your own if you already have a colour or a model in mind. When I finish, the space gets swept and the boxes go out. If something is not sitting right a week later, tell me and I come back.</p>','Woodbridge covers two very different kinds of house. The subdivisions built from the 1990s onward are large, with tall foyers, great-room walls and finished basements that often include a second kitchen, so mounting and fixture work needs long ladders and proper anchors. The old village near Market Lane is small-scale and far older. Add the ravine lots backing onto the Humber, where decks, gates and fences take a beating, and the job list changes street by street.','TV mounting above two-storey great-room fireplaces\nFoyer light fixture changes from tall ladders\nDeck board and railing repair on ravine lots\nGate hinges reset after ground movement\nSecond-kitchen tap and fixture replacement downstairs\nGarage shelving and overhead storage installation\nDrywall repair in high open stairwells\nSmart thermostat and video doorbell setup','[{\"q\":\"My great room has a fourteen-foot wall. Can you mount a television that high?\",\"a\":\"Yes, that is a regular job for me. I bring ladders that reach safely, find the framing behind the finish, and use a mount rated well over the weight of the set. If the outlet and cable were never run up the wall, I will tell you what can be tidied away inside the wall and what needs a licensed electrician instead.\"},{\"q\":\"My deck backs onto the Humber valley and the boards feel soft. What can you do?\",\"a\":\"Soft boards usually come from shade and damp rather than age alone. I replace the bad boards, resecure the rest, tighten railings and reset gate hardware so everything closes properly again. If the framing underneath has gone, that is a bigger rebuild than I take on, and I will say so plainly rather than covering it up.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you finish a few jobs in the basement apartment while you are here?\",\"a\":\"That is a common request in Woodbridge, where a lot of homes have a full second kitchen and bathroom downstairs. Taps, toilets, light fixtures, door adjustments, shelving and patching all get done the same as upstairs. Group them with the jobs on the main floor and it becomes one visit and one agreed price.\"},{\"q\":\"How much notice do you need for work in the older village streets?\",\"a\":\"Two or three days is usually enough. The older streets near the fairgrounds have narrow driveways and tighter parking, so I plan what I need to carry in before I arrive. Tell me if there is no room on the driveway and I will bring less in one go and make an extra trip rather than blocking the road.\"}]','',1,35,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (36,'maple','Maple','York Region',2,43.8563000,-79.5085000,'L6A, L4K','Old Maple\nVellore Village\nPatterson\nCarrville\nRutherford\nDufferin Hill\nConcord\nKleinburg','Canada\'s Wonderland\nVaughan Mills\nMaple Community Centre\nMaple GO Station\nKeele Street\nMajor Mackenzie Drive\nNorth Maple Regional Park\nEagles Nest Golf Club','woodbridge,concord,vaughan,richmond-hill,thornhill,king-city','Handyman in Maple, Vaughan, Ontario','Handyman services in Maple for nail pops, flooring, fixtures, garage storage, mounting and basement finishing. Free quotes, agreed price first. Get in touch.','Handyman Services in Maple, Vaughan','Most of the handyman work I do in Maple is on houses that are not old, but are old enough. Subdivisions that went up around Vellore Village, Patterson and the Rutherford corridor in the 1990s and 2000s have reached the age where builder fixtures look dated, caulking has shrunk and nail pops have shown up along the ceilings. I am Sercan, I work alone, and I have been at this more than ten years.\n\nThere is an older side too, the village along Keele Street, where the houses are small and considerably older. Both keep me busy. You get a free quote, a price agreed before I start, and no pressure either way.','<h2>Maple subdivision houses at the twenty-year mark</h2><p>A house built in 2003 is not a problem house, but it does start asking for things. Nail pops along the ceiling line, hairline cracks above door frames from settling, bathroom caulking gone grey, cabinet hinges sagging, taps that drip, and light fittings that were the cheapest option on the plan. None of it is urgent. All of it looks better done.</p><p>Streets in Vellore Village and Patterson also share a habit: the same builder, the same layouts, the same stud spacing. After a few jobs on a street I generally know what sits behind the wall before I open it, which keeps the mess down and the quote honest.</p><h2>Townhouses and neighbours through the wall</h2><p>Freehold townhouses make up a big share of Maple, particularly around Patterson and the newer streets off Rutherford Road. They are narrow, they are tall, and somebody lives on the other side of at least one wall. Drilling gets planned for daytime hours, anchors go into the right places, and a television on a party wall is mounted so the fixings land where they should.</p><p>Stairs are the other townhouse feature. Three storeys means carrying furniture and materials up, so I break assembly work down and build in the room instead of in the hallway.</p><h2>Basements, garages and postponed jobs</h2><p>Unfinished basements and cluttered garages are where the long-put-off list lives. I do the parts that do not need a licensed trade, and there is usually plenty of it.</p><ul><li>Shelving, workbenches and overhead garage storage</li><li>Laminate and vinyl plank flooring in basement rec rooms</li><li>Baseboards, trim and door casings fitted and painted</li><li>Nail pop repair and repainting through main floors</li><li>Cabinet hardware, soft-close hinges and drawer fixes</li><li>Bathroom vanity swaps and fresh caulking</li><li>Ceiling fans and dimmers replacing existing fittings</li></ul><p>Where a job needs new circuits, a panel, gas or a structural change, it goes to a licensed electrician or plumber. Swapping out what is already in place is my end of it.</p><h2>Booking and pricing</h2><p>Send a list and a few photos. I reply usually within one business day with either a fixed price or an hourly rate, whichever suits the work, and you agree to it before anything starts. The quote is free and there is no obligation to book.</p><p>Batching helps here more than in most places, because the jobs in a Maple house tend to be many and small. Ten minor items in one booking cost less than ten separate call-outs. I supply materials with receipts or fit what you have already bought. Floors get covered, the mess gets vacuumed, and if something is not right when you look at it later, I come back and put it right.</p>','Maple is newer than it looks on a map. Vellore Village, Patterson and the Rutherford corridor are subdivisions and townhouse rows from the 1990s onward, so the work is upgrades and settling repairs rather than restoration: nail pops, caulking, builder fixtures, basement finishing and garage storage. The older village along Keele Street is the exception, with small early homes that want a gentler hand. Townhouse jobs also mean thinking about the neighbour through the wall.','Nail pop repair in twenty-year-old subdivision homes\nBuilder light fixtures swapped for better ones\nTV mounting on freehold townhouse party walls\nVinyl plank flooring in basement rec rooms\nGarage shelving and overhead storage racks\nBathroom vanity replacement and fresh caulking\nBaseboard and trim work on three-storey stairs\nCabinet hardware and soft-close hinge upgrades','[{\"q\":\"Small bumps keep popping through the paint on my ceiling. What causes that?\",\"a\":\"Those are nail pops, and they turn up in houses of that age as the framing dries and moves. I set the fastener below the surface, add a screw beside it where needed, fill, sand and touch up the paint. Across a whole ceiling it is faster and neater to do them all at once and repaint the room afterwards.\"},{\"q\":\"I live in a freehold townhouse. Will the drilling bother my neighbours?\",\"a\":\"Some noise cannot be avoided, but it can be kept short. I do the noisy part in a single stretch during normal daytime hours rather than dragging it across the day, and I plan the mount position so fixings land in framing on your side of the wall. Let the neighbour know the morning I am coming and it is rarely an issue.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you finish a basement rec room, or is that too big for one person?\",\"a\":\"That size of work is fine. Flooring, baseboards, trim, doors, painting and swapping in light fixtures are all things I do, and larger projects run over several days. Framing, new circuits and plumbing rough-ins need licensed trades, so on a full basement build I handle the finishing side and you bring the rest in.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you get out this way often, or is it a special trip?\",\"a\":\"I am through Vaughan most weeks, so Maple sits on my regular run rather than being a special trip. Keele Street and Major Mackenzie Drive make it easy to reach the newer streets and the old village alike. Book a couple of days ahead where you can, and a list of small items will be fitted into one visit.\"}]','',1,36,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (37,'concord','Concord','York Region',2,43.8020000,-79.4800000,'L4K, L4J','Concord West\nDufferin Hill\nGlen Shields\nVaughan Metropolitan Centre\nBeverley Glen\nBrownridge\nSpringfarm\nThornhill Woods\nPatterson\nMaple\nWoodbridge','Vaughan Metropolitan Centre subway station\nHighway 7\nJane Street\nKeele Street\nDufferin Street\nMacMillan Yard\nHighway 400 and Highway 407 interchange','vaughan,thornhill,maple,woodbridge,richmond-hill,north-york','Handyman Services in Concord, Vaughan','Handyman in Concord, Vaughan for TV mounting, furniture assembly, drywall repair, taps and light fixtures in older houses and condo towers. Ask for a quote.','Handyman in Concord: Bungalows to New Towers','Handyman jobs in Concord tend to come from two very different addresses. One morning I am in a 1960s bungalow off Keele Street where the trim is solid wood and nothing is quite square. That afternoon I am on the fifteenth floor of a tower beside the Vaughan Metropolitan Centre station, where the walls are poured concrete and the building wants notice before a drill comes up the service elevator.\n\nBoth of those are ten minutes apart, and both get the same approach. I look at the job first, agree a price with you before anything starts, and keep to it. If you have four small things saved up, that is a better visit than one thing on its own.','<h2>Getting to you and getting parked</h2><p>Concord is laid out unlike anywhere else in York Region. Long blocks of warehouses and shops sit between the streets where people live, so the houses come in pockets rather than one long subdivision. For me that is good news. Driveways are wide, the side streets are quiet by mid-morning, and tools come to the door instead of down the block on a cart.</p><p>Highway 7 is the part worth planning around. Between Jane Street and Dufferin Street it crawls at both ends of the day, and the ramps at Highway 400 and Highway 407 back up with it. I book the first call early and the rest after the rush, which keeps arrival times honest.</p><h2>Concrete walls in the towers by the subway</h2><p>The buildings around the Vaughan Metropolitan Centre station are new, and new here means poured concrete. A television bracket, a floating shelf or a curtain track on a concrete wall needs a masonry bit and proper sleeve anchors, not the plastic plugs that came in the box. Fixed properly, it will outlast the furniture. Rushed, it chews a crater in the wall and holds nothing.</p><p>Each building also has its own rules, and they are worth sorting out before the day. Most want the service elevator booked. Some ask for paperwork before a booking is confirmed, and many limit noisy work to weekday hours. Tell me the address and the time you would like, and I will check with the concierge first.</p><h2>Bungalows, back-splits and the older pockets</h2><p>Streets around Concord West, Glen Shields and Dufferin Hill were built between the 1950s and the 1970s, and they behave like houses of that age. Studs turn up where the builder felt like putting them. Basement walls are often strapping over block, so a shelf that feels solid can be holding on to a thin strip of wood and nothing else. I find out what is behind the surface before I load it.</p><p>Wear is the other theme on those streets. Taps drip, toilets run through the night, hinges sag and door handles work loose. None of it is dramatic, and all of it is quick once somebody is standing there with the right parts in the bag.</p><ul><li>Televisions mounted on concrete condo walls with the cables hidden</li><li>Shelving and closet rods fixed into something that will hold</li><li>Kitchen and bathroom taps swapped like for like</li><li>Toilets repaired or replaced when they run all night</li><li>Light fixtures, dimmers and smart switches changed in the existing box</li><li>Interior doors eased and their hinges reset</li><li>Drywall patched and painted after a move or an old bracket</li><li>Flat-pack furniture built, levelled and anchored to the wall</li></ul><h2>What happens when you call</h2><p>Quotes are free and there is no obligation. Photos sent with your message make pricing quicker, because half of my questions are answered before I reply. You will have a fixed price or an hourly rate agreed before I start, materials come with receipts, or you buy your own if you would rather. The area gets cleaned up before I leave, and if something is not right afterwards I come back to it.</p><p>Some work belongs to a licensed trade and I will say so plainly. New circuits, a panel, gas or anything structural goes to a licensed electrician or plumber. Swapping a fixture, a switch, a tap or a toilet that is already there is ordinary handyman work, and that I am glad to do.</p>','Nowhere else in the region do I move between such different buildings inside one day. A poured-concrete tower by the subway station in the morning, a 1960s bungalow with a block basement after lunch, and a drive between them past truck yards rather than houses. It changes the tools I carry and the anchors I bring, and it is why bookings in Concord work best when a few jobs are stacked into one trip.','Mounting televisions on concrete condo walls near the subway\nAnchoring shelves in 1960s Concord West bungalows\nReplacing worn tap sets in older bungalow bathrooms\nPatching drywall after brackets and shelves come down\nFitting dimmers and smart switches in existing boxes\nAssembling flat-pack wardrobes in condo bedrooms\nRe-hanging interior doors that no longer latch\nBuilding storage shelving in bungalow basements','[{\"q\":\"My condo near the subway station has concrete walls. Can a heavy television still go up?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and concrete is one of the better surfaces to mount on. I drill with a masonry bit and use sleeve anchors rated for the bracket, then load test it before the television goes anywhere near the wall. Dust is the only nuisance, so I catch it at the hole with a vacuum. Once that bracket is in, it is not going anywhere.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you come out to the industrial part of Concord, or only the houses?\",\"a\":\"Most of my work here is homes and condos, but I do take small jobs in offices and units around the employment lands: desks and shelving assembled, a screen mounted in a meeting room, a wall patched and painted, a door handle changed. Anything that needs a licensed trade or a building permit is not mine, and I will tell you that on the phone.\"},{\"q\":\"The basement walls in my Concord West bungalow feel hollow. Can shelves still go up?\",\"a\":\"Usually yes. Older basements around here are often finished with strapping over concrete block, so a screw either finds a thin strip of wood or nothing at all. I check what sits behind the panel, then either hit the strapping where it runs or go straight through into the block with masonry anchors. For heavy shelving the block is the stronger choice.\"},{\"q\":\"Is Highway 7 traffic going to affect what time you arrive?\",\"a\":\"It can, so I plan around it. This part of Vaughan sits between the 400 and the 407, and Highway 7 backs up hard at both ends of the day. I book the first job of the morning early and give later calls a window rather than a single minute, then message you when I am on the way. If I am running behind, you hear it from me.\"}]','',1,37,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (38,'unionville','Unionville','York Region',2,43.8650000,-79.3105000,'L3R, L6G, L6C','Old Unionville\nMain Street Unionville\nBridle Trail\nVillage Parkway\nAngus Glen\nCachet\nMarkham Centre\nDowntown Markham\nBerczy Village\nWismer\nButtonville','Main Street Unionville\nToogood Pond\nVarley Art Gallery of Markham\nMillennium Bandstand\nUnionville GO Station\nMarkham Civic Centre\nAngus Glen Golf Club','markham,thornhill,richmond-hill,agincourt,stouffville','Unionville Handyman for Old and New Homes','Handyman in Unionville for plaster repair, sticking doors, mounting, assembly and fixture swaps, in century homes and Downtown Markham condos. Send your list.','Handyman in Unionville, from Main Street to Downtown Markham','Most handyman work in Unionville happens in houses built long before anyone standardised a stud spacing. The old frame homes along and behind Main Street date to the 1800s, and plenty of what came after them, through the 1970s and 1980s, has settled into a shape of its own. Doors stick in July and swing free in January. Plaster hides where the timber really is.\n\nNewer parts of Unionville are a different job again. Downtown Markham and the condos off Highway 7 are concrete and drywall on a grid, and they come with elevator bookings and set hours for noise. I quote first either way, and the price is agreed before I pick up a tool.','<h2>Old walls do not behave like new ones</h2><p>Plaster and lath is the first thing I expect in the century houses around Main Street. It is harder than drywall and far more brittle, so a screw driven in without care cracks a patch the size of your hand. I drill a pilot hole, keep the pressure light and use fixings meant for old walls. Where a room was strapped and boarded over at some point, there can be an inch of dead air behind the surface before anything solid shows up.</p><p>Movement is the other habit of these houses. Frames sit out of square, floors lean towards the middle of the room, and door tops rub through the humid months. Planing the door is usually the wrong answer, because by January it fits again. Shifting a hinge, packing a shim behind a plate or moving the strike is what actually solves it.</p><h2>The jobs people here call about</h2><p>Some of the list is the age of the building and some of it is simply life going on. Kitchens and bathrooms in the 1980s streets off Village Parkway are on their second set of taps by now. Family houses in Angus Glen, Cachet and Bridle Trail want mounting, shelving and assembly more than repair. A single visit in Unionville often mixes the two.</p><ul><li>Plaster patched, sanded and painted so the repair disappears</li><li>Heavy mirrors and artwork hung on old walls without cracking them</li><li>Interior doors adjusted so they close in every season</li><li>Taps, shower heads and toilets swapped like for like</li><li>Televisions mounted with the cables run out of sight</li><li>Flat-pack wardrobes and desks built and anchored back to the wall</li><li>Backsplash tile, caulking and cabinet handles freshened up</li></ul><h2>Condos and the newer streets</h2><p>Downtown Markham, the towers off Highway 7 and the newer town homes bring the opposite problem. Studs land where you expect them and the walls take a fixing without argument. Work there is about finishing what the builder left behind: the television still on the floor since moving day, the closet with a single rod in it, the mirror wrapped up in the garage.</p><p>Buildings with a concierge usually want the service elevator booked and a slot agreed, and several allow drilling only during set hours. Give me the address when you call and that gets arranged ahead of the day rather than at the front desk.</p><h2>Pricing, and where I stop</h2><p>Quotes are free and carry no obligation. You will have a fixed price or an hourly rate agreed before anything starts. Materials come with receipts, or you buy your own if you prefer a particular brand. Photos with your first message speed everything up, especially in the old houses, where the wall itself is the real question.</p><p>Outside work on the heritage streets is a special case. Changing windows, doors or cladding in the conservation district can need approval from the Town first, so I keep to like-for-like repairs there and say plainly when a job needs that conversation. Anything belonging to a licensed electrician or plumber, such as new wiring or a moved drain, goes to one of them.</p>','Few places in the Greater Toronto Area mix building eras this closely. A frame house from the 1870s, a 1980s subdivision behind it, and a glass tower ten minutes down Highway 7. The old buildings are the interesting ones to work on, because so little of what is in them matches what the hardware store expects, and patience with a plaster wall saves a second repair later. I carry fixings for both worlds rather than guessing.','Patching plaster walls in century Main Street homes\nAdjusting sticking doors in older Unionville houses\nHanging mirrors and shelves on plaster and lath\nMounting televisions in Downtown Markham condo units\nReplacing worn hinges, handles and strike plates\nRe-caulking bathrooms in 1980s Village Parkway homes\nFitting closet rods and shelving in Angus Glen\nRepairing screen doors and window hardware','[{\"q\":\"My house is on the heritage stretch of Main Street. Is there anything you will not touch?\",\"a\":\"Inside the house I work the same as anywhere, just more gently, because the walls are plaster. Outside is different. Altering windows, doors or cladding in the conservation district can need approval from the Town before it happens, so I stick to like-for-like repairs on the exterior and tell you when a job needs that step first.\"},{\"q\":\"The walls are plaster. Will a large mirror or a television actually hold?\",\"a\":\"It will, with the right approach. I find the timber first rather than trusting an anchor in plaster alone, and for anything heavy the fixings go into solid wood. On the odd wall with nothing usable behind it, I would rather fit a discreet cleat or backing board than watch a bracket slowly pull out of a hundred-year-old wall.\"},{\"q\":\"Every interior door in the house sticks by mid-summer. Can that be sorted for good?\",\"a\":\"Mostly yes. Old timber takes on moisture in a humid Ontario summer and gives it back in winter, so a door planed in August rattles in February. I set the hinges, reposition the strike plate and ease only the spot that binds. That combination usually holds through the whole year instead of one season.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you take small jobs in the Downtown Markham condos, or is there a minimum?\",\"a\":\"No list is too short. That said, a trip into a building means booking the service elevator and signing in, so a few jobs in one visit makes far better use of the time than a single curtain rod. Save them up, send me a photo of each, and I will price the lot together.\"}]','',1,38,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (39,'aurora','Aurora','York Region',2,44.0001000,-79.4663000,'L4G, L3Y','Aurora Village\nAurora Heights\nRegency Acres\nAurora Highlands\nBayview Wellington\nHills of St Andrew\nAurora Estates\nTown Park\nRural Aurora\nBayview Northeast','Yonge Street\nWellington Street\nAurora GO Station\nTown Park\nSheppard\'s Bush Conservation Area\nSt Andrew\'s College\nAurora Public Library','newmarket,richmond-hill,king-city,stouffville,maple','Aurora Handyman for Doors, Repairs, Mounting','Handyman in Aurora for sticking doors, deck and fence repair, mounting, flooring, drywall and light fixtures. Free quotes, price agreed first. Get in touch.','Handyman in Aurora: Doors, Drywall and Fixtures','The handyman job I get asked about most in Aurora is a door. Older houses along the Yonge Street corridor sit on ground that moves with the seasons, and frames go out of square by a few millimetres. That is all it takes for a door to rub at the top, a latch to miss the strike, or a patio slider to need a shoulder behind it. Small fixes, and people live with them for years.\n\nEverything else follows the same idea: little problems that never reach the top of the list. I quote before I start, agree the price with you and clean up after. If you have five of these saved up, one visit usually clears the lot.','<h2>Doors, windows and a town that gets four seasons</h2><p>Aurora sees a proper freeze and a humid August, and doors keep score. Timber swells, the frame shifts a fraction, and suddenly the top corner drags or the latch will not catch without a shove. Most of the time the answer is not a new door. It is a hinge moved a couple of millimetres, a strike plate repositioned, and a screw that has lost its bite replaced with a longer one into solid timber.</p><p>Weather stripping is the companion job. It goes hard and flattens after a few winters, and then the draught around the frame gets blamed on the door itself. Renewing the seal and the sweep along the bottom takes very little time, and you notice it on the first cold morning.</p><h2>Four generations of houses in one town</h2><p>Regency Acres and Aurora Heights went up in the 1950s and 1960s: bungalows, back-splits and side-splits on generous lots, with trees now taller than the roofs. Framing in those is solid, though the taps, fittings and light fixtures have often been in place since long before the current owner. Bayview Wellington filled in through the 1990s, and the subdivisions east of Bayview and north of Wellington Street are newer again.</p><p>Near Town Park and the older stretch of Yonge Street sit brick and frame houses that predate all of that, with the quirks age brings. Knowing which of the four I am walking into decides the fixings I bring and how long a job honestly takes.</p><ul><li>Sticking doors adjusted rather than replaced</li><li>Draughty door seals and bottom sweeps replaced</li><li>Deck boards, railings and gate hardware repaired</li><li>Dated light fixtures and ceiling fans swapped out</li><li>Televisions, shelving and artwork mounted securely</li><li>Laminate and vinyl plank flooring, with trim to finish</li><li>Bathroom caulking, taps and cabinet handles refreshed</li></ul><h2>What winter does to a deck and a fence</h2><p>Lots up here are bigger than in the city, and most come with a deck, a gate or a long run of fence. Winter is hard on all three. Boards soften where water sits and freezes, railings loosen, gates drop until they scrape the paving, and a windy night takes out a panel. I repair rather than replace wherever the structure underneath is still sound, and I say so straight when it is not.</p><h2>Small jobs, and larger ones</h2><p>Plenty of what I do here takes under an hour: a curtain rod, a dimmer, a running toilet, a wobbly handrail bracket. Several of those in one visit is the sensible way to use me, and I would rather batch them than drive out four separate times.</p><p>Larger work is welcome too. Laminate or vinyl plank through a main floor with baseboards and trim to finish it, a bathroom brought back to life with fresh caulking, a vanity and new cabinet hardware, or a kitchen backsplash. Anything needing new wiring, gas, a structural change or a roof goes to a licensed trade, and I will point you the right way rather than have a go at it.</p>','Ground movement is the theme in the older parts of town, and the houses show it. Doors that fit in April bind in August. Fence posts and deck footings lift a little each winter and never quite settle back. Add mature trees, deep lots and a lot of houses now on their second set of fixtures, and the work in Aurora leans towards adjustment and repair far more than new installation.','Adjusting exterior doors that swell each summer\nReplacing weather stripping on older Aurora entry doors\nRepairing soft deck boards and loose railings\nRe-hanging sagging back gates and fence panels\nSwapping dated light fixtures in 1960s bungalows\nMounting televisions in Bayview Wellington family rooms\nRefitting baseboards after new laminate goes down\nPatching and painting drywall in finished basements','[{\"q\":\"My back door swells shut every summer and rattles all winter. What is going on?\",\"a\":\"That is timber and weather doing what they always do. The door takes on moisture in August and shrinks back by January, while the frame moves a little as the ground shifts. I adjust the hinges, reset the strike and ease only the edge that binds, then renew the weather stripping so the winter gap closes. Both complaints usually go away together.\"},{\"q\":\"The deck on my Aurora Heights house has a few soft boards. Repair or rebuild?\",\"a\":\"Often just a repair. If the posts, beams and joists are still sound, replacing the surface boards and tightening the railings gets you years more use out of it. I check underneath before quoting. If the structure itself has gone, I will say so rather than screw new boards onto something that should not carry them.\"},{\"q\":\"I am in one of the newer subdivisions east of Bayview. What do people there tend to call about?\",\"a\":\"Finishing, mostly. Those houses are sound but they arrive bare: no shelving in the closets, a builder fixture in every ceiling, nothing on the garage walls, and a television waiting on the floor. Add curtain rods, mirrors and a stack of flat-pack furniture from moving week, and that is a full visit without a single thing being broken.\"},{\"q\":\"Is Aurora too far north for a single small job?\",\"a\":\"Aurora is well inside the area I cover, and I am up there regularly. For one very small item I will usually suggest a day when I am already nearby, or ask if there is anything else on the list worth adding. Send the list and photos, and you will normally have an answer within one business day.\"}]','',1,39,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (40,'newmarket','Newmarket','York Region',2,44.0592000,-79.4613000,'L3X, L3Y, L9N','Central Newmarket\nGlenway\nStonehaven\nSummerhill Estates\nBristol-London\nHuron Heights\nArmitage\nQuaker Hill\nWoodland Hill\nCopper Hills\nGorham-College Manor','Main Street South\nUpper Canada Mall\nFairy Lake\nRiverwalk Commons\nSouthlake Regional Health Centre\nNewmarket GO Station\nTom Taylor Trail','aurora,king-city,stouffville,richmond-hill,vaughan','Newmarket Handyman for Your Whole List','Handyman in Newmarket for repairs, mounting, assembly, taps, doors and light fixtures, several jobs in one visit. Free quotes, no obligation. Send your list.','Handyman in Newmarket for One Visit, Several Jobs','Booking a handyman in Newmarket works best when you save a few jobs up. I am one person, and the drive up Yonge Street or the 404 is part of the day, so a visit covering six small things makes more sense than six separate trips. Most people already have the list anyway. It is on the fridge, or it lives in their head: the door, the tap, the shelf, the light in the hallway.\n\nSend that list with a few photos and you will usually have a reply within one business day, with the price agreed before I start. Nothing gets added to the number partway through the job.','<h2>One visit, a list, a price up front</h2><p>Most jobs in Newmarket get priced before I arrive, from your list and a few photographs. A picture of the tap under the sink, or of the wall where the television is going, answers the questions I would otherwise have to ask twice. You will usually hear back within one business day with a fixed price or an hourly rate to agree.</p><p>On the day itself, the number is the number. Materials I supply come with the receipts, or you can buy your own if there is a brand you like. The area gets cleaned before I leave, and if something is not sitting right afterwards, tell me and I will come back to it.</p><h2>Houses from every decade the town has had</h2><p>Behind Main Street South there are brick and frame houses old enough to have lived through several owners and their ideas of an improvement. Glenway, Bristol-London and Huron Heights are largely 1970s and 1980s, and the fittings in those are well past their first life. Stonehaven, Summerhill Estates, Woodland Hill and Copper Hills came later, and those calls are about finishing rather than fixing.</p><p>Newer apartment buildings along the Davis Drive corridor add a third type, with concierge desks, service elevators to book and set hours for anything noisy. Give me the building when you call and I sort that out ahead of time.</p><h2>What the list usually has on it</h2><p>Every house is different, but the pattern repeats. Something is loose, something drips, something never got hung, and something arrived in a flat box and stayed in it.</p><ul><li>Several small repairs cleared in a single booking</li><li>Original 1980s taps and shower heads replaced</li><li>Televisions mounted and the cables tidied away</li><li>Flat-pack furniture built and anchored to the wall</li><li>Light fixtures, dimmers and ceiling fans changed over</li><li>Drywall dents and scuffs patched and painted out</li><li>Tubs and showers re-caulked before water gets behind them</li></ul><h2>Bigger projects, and the line I do not cross</h2><p>Not everything is a half-hour job. I take on multi-day work as well: flooring through a main floor with the baseboards and trim to finish it, a bathroom refreshed with a new vanity, fresh caulking and new cabinet hardware, or a basement wall properly patched and painted after a leak has been dealt with.</p><p>There is a line, though, and I am straight about where it sits. New wiring, a panel, anything gas, a structural change or a roof is licensed trade work, and I will tell you that instead of having a go. Changing a light fitting, a switch, a dimmer, a tap or a toilet that is already there is ordinary handyman work, and that fills most of my weeks.</p>','Distance shapes the work up here more than the buildings do. Newmarket sits far enough from the middle of the region that a trip should earn its keep, so most of my visits are a list rather than one item. The town also spans a lot of building years, from the brick and frame houses behind Main Street South to subdivisions still settling in, which means the fix that works on one street is not the fix on the next.','Batching several small repairs into one visit\nReplacing original taps in 1980s Glenway homes\nMounting televisions and floating shelves in family rooms\nAssembling flat-pack wardrobes, desks and bed frames\nRepairing drywall dents in busy hallways and stairwells\nSwapping hallway and bathroom light fixtures\nRe-caulking tubs and showers before they leak\nInstalling video doorbells and smart thermostats','[{\"q\":\"I have jobs at my house in Stonehaven and at my mother\'s place in Central Newmarket. Can you do both?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and that is a sensible way to book me. Two addresses in one town on a single trip means far less travelling than two separate visits, and the pricing reflects that. Send both lists together with photos so I can quote them as one job, and let me know if somebody needs to be home to let me in at the second stop.\"},{\"q\":\"My Glenway house is from the eighties and the taps and fittings are all original. Worth replacing them one at a time?\",\"a\":\"I would do them together. Fittings of that age tend to fail within a year or two of each other, and once I am under one sink with the water off and the tools out, the second and third go quickly. Buy the taps you want, or I will supply them with receipts, and the lot gets sorted in one visit.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you take on a multi-day project this far north, or only short jobs?\",\"a\":\"Multi-day work is fine. A floor with all the trim, a bathroom tidied up, a run of built-in shelving: those come with a start date and an agreed price before anything begins. Longer projects are actually easier at this distance, because I am on site properly rather than fitting a half hour in between other calls.\"},{\"q\":\"I am in one of the newer buildings along the Davis Drive corridor. Do you work in apartments too?\",\"a\":\"I do. Apartments and condos need a bit more planning: the service elevator usually has to be booked, there is a sign-in at the desk, and some buildings only allow noisy work at certain hours. Tell me the address when you get in touch and I will check what your building expects before a time is set.\"}]','',1,40,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (41,'king-city','King City','York Region',2,43.9264000,-79.5285000,'L7B, L0G','Nobleton\nSchomberg\nKettleby\nAnsnorveldt\nPottageville\nLaskay\nSnowball\nStrange\nLloydtown\nTemperanceville\nKingscross Estates\nOak Ridges','Keele Street\nKing Road\nKing City GO Station\nSeneca College King Campus\nCold Creek Conservation Area\nHappy Valley Forest\nMarylake','aurora,richmond-hill,maple,vaughan,newmarket','Handyman in King City, York Region','Handyman in King City and King Township for repairs, mounting, assembly, doors, decks and fixture swaps. Free quotes and a price agreed first. Send your list.','Handyman in King City and King Township','Most handyman work in King City happens on properties where the house sits a long way back from the road. Long driveways, a detached garage, a shed or two, a gate at the end of the lane and a fence line that runs further than most city back gardens. That changes how a job is planned more than it changes the job itself, so I bring what a big property needs and keep the trips to a minimum.\n\nJobs get batched here. If you have a sticking door, a dead light fitting, a wobbly railing and a bathroom tap that drips, they go into one visit rather than four. You get a fixed price or an hourly rate agreed before I start. I clean up after myself.','<h2>Getting there, and getting the whole list done</h2><p>King City is spread out. A house can sit a couple of hundred metres off the road behind a gate, and the nearest hardware shop is a drive rather than a walk. That is the biggest practical difference between working here and working in the city, and it is why I ask for the full list up front, with photos where you can send them. One longer visit that clears everything beats three short ones.</p><p>Send the jobs that feel too small to mention as well. A curtain rod, a bracket for a smoke alarm, a cupboard door that will not sit flush, a bathroom fan cover that keeps dropping. Those take minutes once I am already on site, and they are usually the ones that have annoyed somebody for years.</p><h2>The village core and the estate streets are two different jobs</h2><p>Around the four corners at Keele Street and King Road there are older village houses, some of them well past a century old. Plaster over lath, framing that never followed a tidy spacing, floors with a bit of a run on them. Fixings there need thought. A stud finder gets a second opinion from a small pilot hole, and anything heavy goes into solid timber or a proper toggle rather than a plug pushed into old plaster.</p><p>Out on the estate streets and the country subdivisions the issue is scale instead. Ceilings run high, doors are taller than standard, mirrors and artwork are oversized, and the fitting over the stairwell is a long way up. Working on my own means planning for that: the right ladder, the right fixings for the weight, and a straight answer if a job honestly needs two people.</p><h2>Garages, sheds, gates and fence lines</h2><p>Detached garages, workshops and garden sheds come with most properties here, and they age faster than the house because nobody looks at them until something stops working. Side doors drop on their hinges, latches seize, weather stripping curls away from the frame, screens tear. Small jobs, every one of them, and they sit undone for a whole season.</p><p>Fences and gates are the other outdoor regular. Long runs mean long exposure, and after a winter of freezing and thawing a gate that used to swing will scrape the ground or refuse to latch. Rehanging it on longer screws into sound timber, or bracing a frame that has racked, takes far less time than replacing a section.</p><ul><li>Doors adjusted, rehung and fitted with new hinges, handles or weather stripping</li><li>Televisions, oversized mirrors and artwork mounted into what is actually behind the wall</li><li>Light fittings, ceiling fans, dimmers and smart switches swapped for what is already there</li><li>Taps, shower heads, toilets and the slow leak under a vanity sorted out</li><li>Plaster and drywall patched, then painted so the repair disappears</li><li>Deck boards, railings, gates, fence panels and shed doors repaired</li><li>Flat-pack furniture, closet systems and garage storage assembled and anchored</li></ul><h2>Where my work stops and a licensed trade starts</h2><p>Some jobs are not mine to do, and I would rather say so on the phone than halfway through. Anything at the electrical panel, a new circuit or fresh wiring goes to a licensed electrician. Gas work, structural changes and plumbing beyond a fixture swap go to the right licensed trade as well. <strong>Changing a light fitting, a dimmer, a tap or a toilet that is already in place is ordinary work for me.</strong> Knowing that line is part of doing the job properly, and it has never cost anybody a wasted visit.</p>','Work here is shaped by the size of the properties rather than the jobs themselves. Houses sit back from the road, garages and sheds stand apart from the house, and fence lines run for hundreds of metres, so planning a visit counts as much as the repair does. The old village near Keele Street is plaster and irregular framing; the estate streets are tall, wide and oversized. One trip that clears a whole list beats several short calls.','Gate rehanging on long rural fence lines\nGarage and shed side door repairs\nOversized mirrors hung in tall King City hallways\nPlaster patching in older village houses\nHigh stairwell light fixtures swapped safely\nDeck boards and railings repaired after winter\nCloset systems and garage storage assembled\nTaps and toilets replaced in estate bathrooms','[{\"q\":\"My house sits well back from the road behind a gate. Does that make a visit harder?\",\"a\":\"Not really, it just needs a word in advance. Tell me how you would like me to get in, where I can park near the work, and if the jobs are in the house, the garage or a shed. Five minutes of that detail with your quote request saves half an hour of walking back and forth on the day.\"},{\"q\":\"The detached garage and the garden shed need attention as well as the house. Can that be one visit?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and that is the sensible way to do it out here. Doors, latches, weather stripping, shelving and a dead light fitting in an outbuilding are all short jobs on their own, so they belong on the same list as the indoor work. Send everything together and I will price the whole visit before I start anything.\"},{\"q\":\"Everything in my house is oversized, from the doors to the artwork. Can one person handle that?\",\"a\":\"Most of the time, yes. I bring the ladders the job needs and I choose fixings for the actual weight rather than hoping a plug holds. If something genuinely takes two sets of hands, like a very large mirror or a stone vanity top, I say so at the quote stage and ask for a hand at that moment rather than improvising on the day.\"},{\"q\":\"Can a video doorbell work on a house set a long way back from the road?\",\"a\":\"Usually, though the wireless signal is the thing to check first. I fit the unit to the existing doorbell wiring where there is some, mount it square, and set it up on your network before I leave. If the signal at the front door is weak, I will tell you what it would take to put that right rather than leave you with a camera that keeps dropping out.\"}]','',1,41,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (42,'stouffville','Stouffville','York Region',2,43.9711000,-79.2447000,'L4A, L0H','Downtown Stouffville\nBallantrae\nMusselman\'s Lake\nGormley\nVandorf\nBloomington\nLemonville\nRingwood\nBethesda\nCedar Valley\nPreston Lake','Main Street Stouffville\nStouffville GO Station\nMemorial Park\nBruce\'s Mill Conservation Area\nMusselman\'s Lake\nHighway 48\nBallantrae Golf Club','markham,unionville,richmond-hill,pickering,newmarket','Handyman in Stouffville and Ballantrae','Handyman in Stouffville and Ballantrae for shelving, storage, assembly, doors, drywall, taps and deck repair. Free quotes, price agreed first. Get in touch.','Handyman in Stouffville for Storage and Repairs','Storage is the handyman job I get asked about most in Stouffville. Garage racks, pantry shelves, a closet system, a bench and hooks by the back door. The town has grown quickly, and plenty of the newer houses came with generous rooms and almost nowhere to put anything, so the call tends to come once the boxes have sat in the garage for a year.\n\nRepairs make up the rest. Doors that need adjusting, gaps in the trim, a dripping tap, a light fitting that has dated badly. Write the whole lot down and send it in one go, with photos if you have them. I quote before anything starts, agree the number with you, and tidy up before I leave.','<h2>Storage is what I get called about most</h2><p>Stouffville houses tend to be generous on floor area and short on anywhere to put things. Garages fill up, the pantry has two shelves, and the closet came with a single wire rack that sagged in the middle inside a year. Much of my week here is storage work: heavy racks fixed into ceiling framing, pantry and linen shelving cut and levelled, closet systems built properly, hooks and a bench by the back door so the boots stop landing on the floor.</p><p>Fixings are the whole job. The plastic plugs that come in the box will hold a picture, not a shelf loaded with tins. I find the framing first, use anchors rated for real weight where there is none, and load the shelf myself before I call it done.</p><h2>Main Street and the houses behind it</h2><p>The old part of town is a small pocket, and it behaves nothing like the streets around it. Brick and frame houses near Main Street have plaster walls, wide painted trim, doors that were hung by hand and floors that run downhill by a few millimetres across a room. Trim gets scribed to the wall rather than forced flat, shelves get packed level, and patches get feathered wide so they do not show under the light from the window.</p><h2>Ballantrae, Musselman\'s Lake and the roads out of town</h2><p>Head north or east and the housing changes again. Ballantrae is bungalows on quiet crescents, single storey, where a good share of the work is fittings that have been in place since the house was built: taps, toilets, ceiling fans and light fixtures nobody would choose today. Around Musselman\'s Lake there are places that started as cottages and are lived in year round now, which usually means several generations of framing, additions meeting at odd angles, tired screen doors and decks that take the full winter.</p><p>Distances out here make batching worth it. Everything on the list in one go, one price agreed first, and no repeat trips down the same concession road.</p><ul><li>Garage racks, pantry shelving and closet systems built and anchored</li><li>Flat-pack wardrobes, beds, desks and outdoor furniture assembled</li><li>Televisions, mirrors and artwork mounted on solid fixings</li><li>Interior and exterior doors adjusted, with new handles or weather stripping</li><li>Drywall and plaster repairs, followed by paint that matches</li><li>Taps, shower heads and toilets swapped like for like</li><li>Decks, railings, gates and fence panels repaired</li></ul><h2>How a visit actually works</h2><p>Quotes are free and there is no obligation attached to one. Photos sent with your request help more than anything else, because I can usually price from them and arrive with the right parts in the van. You get a fixed price for the job or an hourly rate, agreed with you before I start.</p><p>Materials come with receipts if I supply them, or you buy your own and I fit them. I clear up the work area before I go, and if something is not right afterwards I come back and sort it out. Replies usually go out within one business day, and I work Monday to Saturday, with Sunday by appointment.</p>','Growth is the thing that sets Stouffville apart. A small nineteenth-century Main Street sits inside a ring of subdivisions barely fifteen years old, with Ballantrae bungalows and old lake cottages a short drive out. That means one week is anchors and shelving in fresh drywall and the next is plaster patching and scribed trim. Distances between the outlying pockets make it worth saving jobs up for a single visit.','Garage storage racks anchored into solid framing\nPantry and closet shelving levelled properly\nMudroom hooks and benches fitted securely\nPlaster repairs in older Main Street houses\nScreen and storm doors fixed at Musselman\'s Lake\nBungalow taps and toilets replaced in Ballantrae\nDeck railings tightened after a hard winter\nFlat-pack wardrobes built in new subdivision bedrooms','[{\"q\":\"My garage is the only storage I have. Can you put up racks that will hold real weight?\",\"a\":\"Yes, as long as they go into the framing. Overhead racks get bolted into ceiling joists, wall shelving goes into studs, and nothing important hangs off a plastic plug. I find the framing properly, set the height around your car and the door opener, then load the rack myself before I call it finished.\"},{\"q\":\"Our place at Musselman\'s Lake started life as a cottage. Does that change what you can do?\",\"a\":\"It changes how I plan, not what gets done. Converted cottages usually have a few generations of framing meeting in odd places, so I check behind a wall before I trust it with anything heavy. Screen doors, draughty exterior doors, soft deck boards and shelving that never went in straight are the jobs that come up most in those houses.\"},{\"q\":\"The wire shelving in my closet has sagged in the middle. Can it be saved?\",\"a\":\"Often, yes. A sag is usually a support problem rather than a shelf problem, so extra brackets fixed into studs will bring it back level. If the run is long or the load is heavy, a solid shelf on proper cleats lasts far longer. Either way I patch and touch up the old fixing holes so the wall does not look peppered.\"},{\"q\":\"Three of us on the same street each have a short list. Can that be one trip?\",\"a\":\"That works well, and it comes up often in the newer subdivisions here. Send the three lists together with photos, and I will price each house separately so everybody knows their own number. Running them back to back on one visit means less time on the road and a tighter day, which suits all of you.\"}]','',1,42,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (43,'pickering','Pickering','Durham Region',1,43.8384000,-79.0868000,'L1V, L1W, L1X, L1Y','Bay Ridges\nWest Shore\nRosebank\nRougemount\nAmberlea\nHighbush\nDunbarton\nLiverpool\nVillage East\nBrock Ridge\nDuffin Heights\nSeaton\nClaremont\nWhitevale','Frenchman\'s Bay\nPickering Town Centre\nPickering GO Station\nPetticoat Creek Conservation Area\nMillennium Square\nRouge National Urban Park\nKingston Road\nEsplanade Park','ajax,whitby,scarborough,markham,stouffville','Pickering Handyman for Home Repairs','Handyman in Pickering from West Shore to Seaton for repairs, mounting, assembly, doors, flooring and fixture swaps. Free quotes, no obligation. Send a list.','Handyman in Pickering, from Bay Ridges to Seaton','A handyman in Pickering ends up working on houses from five different decades in the same week. Bungalows and back-splits a few streets from Frenchman\'s Bay that went up in the fifties and sixties, subdivisions in Amberlea and Highbush from the eighties and nineties, brand-new streets in Seaton, condo suites near the Town Centre, and old frame houses up in Whitevale and Claremont.\n\nDifferent houses, same short list of problems: doors that stick, walls that need patching, fittings that have given up, shelves nobody can face putting straight. I quote first, agree a fixed price or an hourly rate with you before starting, and leave the place clean.','<h2>Houses built when the lake was the edge of town</h2><p>West Shore, Bay Ridges and Rosebank filled in during the fifties and sixties, and those houses have spent sixty-odd years facing the weather that comes off Lake Ontario. Storm doors and screens take a beating, weather stripping goes hard and flat, and sliding patio doors get heavy on their rollers until somebody has to shoulder them open. Most of that is repair work rather than replacement, and it usually takes less time than people expect.</p><p>Inside, the layouts are compact and the bathrooms are small. A vanity or toilet swap here starts with a measurement, because plenty of modern units are wider than the space they are meant to sit in.</p><h2>The eighties and nineties middle of the city</h2><p>Amberlea, Highbush, Liverpool and Brock Ridge are family homes from the eighties and nineties, and they are all reaching the same stage at once. Original taps have gone stiff, brass light fittings look their age, the carpet upstairs is finished, and cabinet doors sag on tired hinges. None of that is a renovation. It is a list of swaps and small repairs that adds up to a house feeling twenty years younger.</p><p>These are the jobs that come up most often across the city.</p><ul><li>Televisions, floating shelves, mirrors and heavy artwork mounted securely</li><li>Flat-pack furniture and closet systems assembled and anchored to the wall</li><li>Light fittings, ceiling fans, dimmers and smart switches changed like for like</li><li>Taps, shower heads, toilets and leaking traps under the sink replaced</li><li>Doors adjusted, hinges and handles renewed, weather stripping fitted</li><li>Drywall dents, cracks and old mounting holes patched and painted</li><li>Laminate and vinyl plank flooring laid, with baseboard and trim to finish</li><li>Backsplash tile, fresh caulking and new cabinet hardware</li></ul><h2>Condo suites and apartments near the City Centre</h2><p>The towers around Pickering Town Centre and the GO station run to their own rules. Buildings want the service elevator booked and a name left at the desk, corridors need protecting on the way through, and there are hours when drilling is allowed and hours when it is not. Send me what your property manager asks for along with your quote request, and the visit gets built around the slot you can get.</p><p>Walls in those suites are concrete or steel stud, so ordinary wall plugs do nothing at all. Brackets go into concrete with masonry anchors, or into steel studs with toggles rated for the load. Balcony doors that drag on the track are a regular short job in the same buildings.</p><h2>North Pickering, Seaton and the hamlets</h2><p>Seaton is the newest part of the city, and a new house generates its own list: hooks and shelving going into bare drywall, blinds and curtain rods, a television over the fireplace, trim gaps that opened during the first heating season. Nothing has failed. It is a house being finished off properly after the builder has gone.</p><p>Further north, Whitevale, Greenwood and Claremont are old hamlets with frame houses, big lots and outbuildings. Work up there tends to be a longer list in a single visit, because the drive is not worth splitting into three.</p>','Five decades of housing sit within ten minutes of each other here, which is unusual. Lakeside bungalows from the sixties, family homes from the eighties, condo towers by the GO station, brand-new streets in Seaton and old frame houses in the hamlets north of the highway. Wind off the lake drives the outdoor repairs, the towers set their own access rules, and the northern drives are worth batching into one visit.','Storm and screen doors repaired near Frenchman\'s Bay\nSliding patio doors freed on West Shore back-splits\nDated light fixtures swapped in eighties Amberlea homes\nBalcony door adjustments in City Centre condo towers\nCarpet replaced with vinyl plank and new baseboards\nFence panels reset after wind off the lake\nVideo doorbells and smart switches fitted in older houses\nTrim and door fixes on new Seaton streets','[{\"q\":\"The wind off the lake has finished off my storm door and the screen with it. Repair or replace?\",\"a\":\"Repair first, in most cases. A storm door that bangs or will not latch is usually a worn closer, a dropped hinge or a screw that has lost its grip in the frame, and all three are quick fixes. Screens get re-splined with new mesh. When the frame itself has twisted out of shape, I will say so and fit a new one instead.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you go north to Whitevale, Greenwood and Claremont, or only the built-up part of the city?\",\"a\":\"I go up there regularly. The hamlets north of the highway count the same as anywhere else on my map. The one thing I ask is that the list is a decent length, since the drive is longer, so save the small jobs up and let one visit clear the lot rather than booking three separate calls over a month.\"},{\"q\":\"I want smart switches and a video doorbell in a house from the seventies. Is that possible?\",\"a\":\"Usually it is. Swapping an existing switch for a dimmer or a smart switch is straightforward work, though older houses sometimes have no neutral wire at the switch box, so I check that before anything is ordered. A doorbell needs the existing transformer to be up to it. If the answer turns out to be new wiring or panel work, that goes to a licensed electrician.\"},{\"q\":\"I take possession of a house in Amberlea next month. Is it easier to get work done before the furniture arrives?\",\"a\":\"Much easier, and it is the best window you will get. Flooring, baseboards, painting, mounting and door work all move faster with clear rooms and nothing to cover up. Send the list as soon as you have the keys, because the gap between closing and moving day is short and the end of the month fills quickly.\"}]','',1,43,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (44,'ajax','Ajax','Durham Region',1,43.8509000,-79.0204000,'L1S, L1T, L1Z','South Ajax\nCentral Ajax\nNortheast Ajax\nPickering Beach\nDuffins Bay\nLakeside\nWestney Heights\nApplecroft\nNottingham\nRiverside\nAudley\nCarruthers Creek','Ajax Waterfront Park\nRotary Park\nHarwood Avenue\nAjax GO Station\nDurham Centre\nGreenwood Conservation Area\nParadise Beach\nLakeridge Health Ajax Pickering','pickering,whitby,oshawa,scarborough,stouffville','Ajax Handyman for Small Jobs and Repairs','Handyman in Ajax for door and trim repairs, TV mounting, assembly, taps, toilets, flooring, decks and fences. Free quotes with a price agreed first. Just ask.','Handyman in Ajax for Repairs, Doors and Trim','Half my handyman work in Ajax happens in houses that were built small and have been added to ever since. The south end went up quickly in the war years and just after, on tight lots with modest rooms, low basements and bathrooms that will not take anything off the shelf without a bit of thought. Later owners extended, converted and re-covered, so what is behind a wall is rarely what you expect.\n\nNorth of Rossland Road it is a different story, and the semis, links and town homes in between are different again. Same approach either way: I look, I quote, you get a price before anything starts, then I do the work and clear up.','<h2>The south end and its post-war houses</h2><p>Ajax started in the nineteen forties around a wartime munitions plant, and the oldest streets still carry the names of navy crews. Houses from that period and the decade after are compact by modern standards: small bathrooms, narrow hallways, tight head height in the basement, light hollow doors. Modern fittings are often bigger than the gap they are going into, so a vanity swap or a shower head change starts with a tape measure rather than a shopping trip.</p><p>Walls down there vary from room to room. Plaster over lath in one, early drywall in the next, panelling with something else behind it in the basement. I check what is actually there before anything heavy goes up, because a fixing that holds well in one wall will pull straight out of another.</p><h2>Semis, links and town homes</h2><p>Semis, link houses and freehold towns make up a big share of this town, and their shape decides how a job runs. Staircases are steep and turn at a landing, so a wardrobe box that will not make the corner gets carried up in pieces and built in the bedroom instead. Rooms are narrow, which means furniture is better assembled in position than shuffled around afterwards.</p><p>Party walls need the right approach too. Mounting a television or a heavy shelf on a shared wall is normal work, as long as the bracket lands in framing or in masonry with the correct anchors, and as long as the noisy part of the job is kept to a sensible stretch of the day.</p><h2>Down by the water and along the creeks</h2><p>A long waterfront and two creek valleys run through Ajax, and both show up in the outdoor work. Wind coming off the lake finds loose fence panels, lifts gate hardware and works away at anything already tired. Ravine lots near Carruthers Creek and Duffins Creek stay damp for longer, so deck boards soften and posts give up sooner than they do further inland.</p><p>Repairs beat replacement more often than people think. If the posts and the frame are sound, a run of fence or a deck surface can be put right in one visit. If they are not, I tell you straight rather than patching over the real problem.</p><h2>Kitchens, bathrooms and the jobs that lift a house</h2><p>Houses on the north side went up through the nineties and two thousands, and their kitchens still work perfectly well while looking every bit their age. Plenty can change without a renovation: new cabinet hardware, a tile backsplash, fresh caulking around the counter and sink, a modern tap, a ceiling fitting that is not a frosted dome any more.</p><p>Bathrooms follow the same idea. Vanity, tap, toilet, mirror, and towel rails put back where they will actually hold, with the silicone renewed so the edges look clean again. <strong>Bigger projects are welcome as well, and those run over several days with the same quote-first approach.</strong></p><ul><li>Interior doors eased, rehung and fitted with sound hardware</li><li>Televisions and shelves mounted on party walls and masonry</li><li>Vanities, taps, shower heads and toilets replaced like for like</li><li>Fence panels, gates, deck boards and railings repaired</li><li>Basement handrails, treads and loose spindles made solid</li><li>Cabinet hardware, backsplash tile and fresh caulking fitted</li><li>Flat-pack furniture built in narrow bedrooms and on landings</li><li>Drywall and plaster patched, sanded and painted to match</li></ul>','Three eras sit side by side in Ajax. Compact post-war houses in the south, a heavy share of semis, links and town homes through the middle, and newer streets north of Rossland Road. Add a long lakefront and two creek valleys, and the outdoor work is mostly about wind and damp: fence panels, gates, deck boards, soft posts. Indoors it is measuring first, because the older rooms are smaller than modern fittings assume.','Compact vanity swaps in small south Ajax bathrooms\nHollow interior doors rehung in post-war houses\nTelevisions mounted on party walls in link homes\nFence panels reset after wind off the lake\nDeck railings repaired on Carruthers Creek ravine lots\nBasement stair handrails made solid again\nCabinet hardware and backsplash updates in nineties kitchens\nFlat-pack wardrobes built in narrow town home bedrooms','[{\"q\":\"The bathroom in my south Ajax house is tiny. Will a new vanity even fit?\",\"a\":\"Often yes, but it starts with measurements. Older bathrooms here were built around narrow units, so I check the width, the depth with the door open, and where the drain and supply lines sit before anything is ordered. A like-for-like swap is straightforward. If the pipework has to move more than a little, that part goes to a licensed plumber.\"},{\"q\":\"A windy night took out two fence panels behind my house. Does the whole run have to come down?\",\"a\":\"Almost never. I check the posts by hand first, because they decide everything else. If they are solid, the damaged panels and rails come out and new ones go in, matched as closely to what is there as I can manage. Where a post has rotted below ground level, I tell you plainly, since patching around a failed post never lasts.\"},{\"q\":\"The handrail down to my basement is loose and one tread creaks. Is that too small to book?\",\"a\":\"No, and a loose handrail is worth sorting sooner rather than later. Brackets need to land in the stringer or a stud, not in drywall on its own, so I refit them properly and fill the old holes. A creaking tread usually needs blocking or screws from underneath. Both of those slot easily into a visit with other jobs.\"},{\"q\":\"My nineties kitchen works fine but it looks dated. What can change without a renovation?\",\"a\":\"Quite a lot. New cabinet hardware changes a kitchen more than people expect, and a tile backsplash, fresh caulking along the counter, a new tap and a different ceiling fitting take it further again. Cabinet doors can be adjusted so they sit square once more. All of that happens in a day or two with the kitchen still usable.\"}]','',1,44,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (45,'whitby','Whitby','Durham Region',2,43.8975000,-78.9428000,'L1M, L1N, L1P, L1R','Brooklin\nDowntown Whitby\nPort Whitby\nWhitby Shores\nPringle Creek\nRolling Acres\nWilliamsburg\nBlue Grass Meadows\nLynde Creek\nTaunton North\nWerden\'s Plan\nAshburn','Iroquois Park Sports Centre\nLynde Shores Conservation Area\nHeydenshore Pavilion\nWhitby GO Station\nBrock Street\nThickson Ridge Power Centre\nBaldwin Street in Brooklin','oshawa,ajax,pickering,scarborough,markham','Handyman in Whitby, Durham Region','Handyman in Whitby and Brooklin for repairs, mounting, assembly, drywall, flooring and fixture swaps. Free quotes and a price agreed first. Send your list.','Your Handyman in Whitby and Brooklin','I am Sercan, and I am the handyman most Whitby homeowners call when a job is too small for a contractor and too fiddly to leave for another weekend. Plenty of this town went up quickly from the 1990s onward, so much of my work here is finishing what a builder rushed. Doors that never shut right, trim gaps that opened in the first winter, shelves that need proper anchors rather than the plugs in the box.\n\nWork runs from Brooklin down to the harbour and across to Thickson Road, so a few small jobs can usually go into one visit instead of three separate trips. You get a fixed price or an hourly rate before I start, and it does not move afterwards.','<h2>Newer subdivisions and what they leave behind</h2>\n<p>Most of Whitby north of Rossland Road went up from the 1990s onward, and Brooklin has kept growing ever since. A new house is not a problem-free house. It settles for the first few years, and that shows up as nail pops in the ceiling, hairline cracks above door frames, gaps opening between the baseboard and the floor, and interior doors that start rubbing the jamb by the second winter. None of it is serious. All of it is irritating enough that people want it dealt with properly rather than filled twice with the wrong stuff.</p>\n<p>Builder-grade fixing is the other thing I see constantly. Towel bars, blinds, closet rods and coat hooks go up into bare drywall with the plastic plugs from the box, so they pull out the first time somebody leans on them. I refit them into studs where I can and use proper hollow-wall anchors where I cannot, then fill the old holes so you cannot tell.</p>\n<h2>Jobs people here call about most</h2>\n<p>A normal week is a mix of hour-long fixes and half-day lists. If things have been piling up on the fridge door, write them all out and send them together rather than one at a time.</p>\n<p>These are the jobs that come up most often on this side of Durham Region.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Doors and locks</strong> - sticking doors, dropped hinges, loose handles, strike plates that no longer line up</li>\n<li><strong>Drywall and paint</strong> - nail pops, corner cracks, holes from an old mount, touch-ups after</li>\n<li><strong>Mounting</strong> - televisions, floating shelves, mirrors, heavy artwork and garage storage racks</li>\n<li><strong>Fixtures</strong> - swapping an existing light fitting, ceiling fan, dimmer or smart switch</li>\n<li><strong>Plumbing fittings</strong> - faucets, shower heads, toilets and the slow leak under the sink</li>\n<li><strong>Flooring and trim</strong> - laminate and vinyl plank, baseboard, quarter round and stair nosing</li>\n<li><strong>Outside</strong> - deck boards, railings, gate sag and fence repairs after a hard winter</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Three-storey towns and long driveways</h2>\n<p>Whitby has a lot of freehold towns with the garage on the ground floor and the living space two flights up. That changes how a job runs. A wardrobe or a sectional often cannot make the turn at the landing, so flat-pack assembly happens in the room it belongs in and the boxes come up in pieces. Mention it when you book, because it changes how long a build takes and I would rather plan for it than discover it.</p>\n<p>Out in the newer pockets off Taunton Road and Baldwin Street the driveways are long and parking is easy, which makes tool runs quick. Down near the harbour and the older streets around Brock Street it is tighter, and I allow for that in the timing instead of rushing the finish.</p>\n<h2>How a visit actually works</h2>\n<p>Send a description and a few photos. A picture of the wall, the old fixture or the label on the flat-pack box is usually enough for me to price the work without coming out first, and I reply within one business day. Quotes are free and carry no obligation.</p>\n<p>Before anything starts you get a fixed price or an hourly rate, agreed with you. Materials come with receipts, or you buy your own if you want to choose the finish yourself. Everything gets swept up and the packaging leaves with me. If something is not sitting right a week later, tell me and I will come back and put it right.</p>','Whitby splits neatly in two for repair work. The older streets near the harbour and Brock Street hold post-war bungalows with plaster in places and framing that is never quite where a stud finder claims. North of Taunton Road and out in Brooklin it is all engineered lumber, deep truss ceilings and drywall over steel corner bead. Same job, two different approaches, and knowing which one is behind the wall saves an hour.','Interior door adjustments in newer Brooklin builds\nFlat-pack furniture assembly in three-storey towns\nNail pop and settlement crack repair\nGarage shelving anchored into block walls\nDeck board and railing repairs after winter\nBacksplash tiling in 1990s subdivision kitchens\nBasement bathroom vanity and faucet swaps\nBaseboard and quarter round gap repairs','[{\"q\":\"Do you cover Brooklin as well as the south end of Whitby?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Brooklin, the newer streets north of Taunton Road, the older core around Brock Street, Port Whitby and Whitby Shores are all normal territory for me. If you sit close to the Ajax or Oshawa boundary that is fine too. Tell me roughly where you are when you get in touch and I will let you know what I can fit in that week.\"},{\"q\":\"My house is only a few years old. Why does it already need repairs?\",\"a\":\"New houses move. The framing dries out, the ground settles, and the first two or three winters push nails out of ceilings, open hairline cracks over doorways and pull baseboards away from the floor. It is normal and almost always cosmetic. The fix is doing it once properly, with the right filler and a feathered edge, so the same line does not reappear next spring.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you repair a deck after a bad winter?\",\"a\":\"Usually, yes. Lifted or cupped boards, popped fasteners, a wobbly railing, a gate that has dropped and a fence section pushed over by ice and wind are all standard work for me. What I will not do is quietly cover over a rotten post or a footing that has failed. If that is what I find, I will show you and tell you straight what the real job is.\"},{\"q\":\"Can a television go on the wall in a new-build living room?\",\"a\":\"Almost always. The catch in newer builds is that studs are rarely where you expect them, and the wall above a fireplace often has blocking or a chase in the way. I locate the framing first, fix into solid timber where possible, run the cables somewhere sensible, and set the screen at a height that suits your seating rather than the middle of the wall.\"}]','',1,45,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (46,'oshawa','Oshawa','Durham Region',2,43.8971000,-78.8658000,'L1G, L1H, L1J, L1K, L1L','Downtown Oshawa\nO\'Neill\nMcLaughlin\nVanier\nCentral\nDonevan\nEastdale\nLakeview\nSamac\nWindfields\nKedron\nTaunton\nPinecrest\nColumbus','Oshawa Centre\nParkwood National Historic Site\nLakeview Park\nTribute Communities Centre\nOntario Tech University\nOshawa GO Station\nSimcoe Street\nCanadian Automotive Museum','whitby,ajax,pickering,scarborough,markham','Oshawa Handyman for Home Repairs','Oshawa handyman for plaster and door repairs, mounting, assembly, flooring, plumbing fixtures and rental turnovers. Free quotes, no obligation. Get in touch.','Handyman Repairs and Upgrades in Oshawa','Finding a handyman in Oshawa who will take on a two-hour job is harder than it should be, and that is most of what I do. My name is Sercan and I work on my own, so the person who quotes your job is the person who turns up to do it. Ten years in, more than five hundred jobs behind me.\n\nHouses in this city cover a wide span. There are workers\' cottages and wartime homes close to downtown, big 1960s and 1970s streets east and west of Simcoe Street, and subdivisions still being finished north of Taunton Road. Each era brings its own quirks, and I plan the work around them rather than turning up with one approach.','<h2>Wartime houses and what they need</h2>\n<p>The streets through O\'Neill, Vanier and Central were built for General Motors families, mostly in the 1940s and 1950s. They are solid little houses. They also have plaster over lath in the older ones, steep narrow stairs, shallow closets, original solid wood doors that have swollen and shrunk through eighty winters, and framing that does not sit on a tidy sixteen inch grid. Patching plaster is not the same job as patching drywall. It needs backing, a bonding coat and patience while it dries, and rushing it gives you the same crack back next year.</p>\n<p>Doors are the other constant around here. A door that binds is usually a hinge that has dropped or a jamb that has shifted, not a door that needs planing. I fix the cause first, because taking a plane to a door that will move again in July only leaves you with a draught in January.</p>\n<h2>The newer streets north of Taunton</h2>\n<p>North Oshawa is a different animal altogether. Samac, Kedron, Windfields and the roads around Ontario Tech University are recent, so the work there is mounting, assembly and finishing rather than repair. Televisions, floating shelves, closet organisers, blinds, garage storage and the trim a builder never came back to touch up.</p>\n<p>Ceilings run higher in those homes, which makes anchoring matter more, not less. A bracket that would be fine at chest height on a bungalow wall needs a real fixing when it is holding a heavy screen over an open stairwell.</p>\n<h2>Rentals, student units and turnovers</h2>\n<p>Plenty of Oshawa houses near the university and the college are rented by the room, and landlords call me between tenants. A turnover list is the ideal batching job. Patch and touch up, re-hang a door, replace a broken blind, swap a leaking faucet and a running toilet, tighten every handle in the place. Give me the full list with photos and one visit usually covers the lot.</p>\n<p>If something turns out to need a licensed trade, I will say so on the spot. New circuits, panel work and anything gas go to a licensed electrician or plumber. Replacing a fixture, a switch, a dimmer, a tap or a toilet that is already installed is my work. Rewiring a house is not, and I will not pretend otherwise to keep a job.</p>\n<h2>What gets booked and how it works</h2>\n<p>Most of what I do falls into a handful of categories, and a lot of it can be grouped into a single visit.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Plaster and drywall repair</strong> - cracks, holes, patched ceilings and paint touched in afterwards</li>\n<li><strong>Doors and windows</strong> - sticking doors, hinges, handles, locks, weather stripping and screens</li>\n<li><strong>Plumbing fittings</strong> - faucets, shower heads, toilets and under-sink leaks</li>\n<li><strong>Flooring and trim</strong> - vinyl plank in basements, laminate, baseboard and stair nosing</li>\n<li><strong>Kitchen and bathroom updates</strong> - backsplash, fresh caulking, cabinet handles, a new vanity</li>\n<li><strong>Mounting and assembly</strong> - televisions, shelves, mirrors, flat-pack furniture and wall storage</li>\n<li><strong>Smart home setup</strong> - video doorbells, cameras and like-for-like thermostat swaps</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Quotes are free and there is no obligation attached. Send photos with your message and you will usually get a price back without me driving out first, normally within one business day. Before any tools come out you get a fixed price or an hourly rate, agreed with you. Materials come with receipts or you supply your own, the area is left clean, and if something is not right I come back and sort it.</p>','Oshawa holds more pre-1960 housing than most towns around it, and that shapes the work. Wartime and post-war homes bring plaster, narrow stairs, solid wood doors that swell, shallow closets and wall cavities never meant to carry a modern television. The newer streets north of Taunton Road are the opposite problem, tall ceilings and light framing. A fair share of jobs here are rental turnovers, where speed and a tidy finish matter more than anything fancy.','Sticking doors in post-war O\'Neill houses\nPlaster patching in 1940s wartime homes\nTurnover repairs in student rental units\nHandrail and stair repairs in 1.5 storey houses\nVinyl plank flooring in basement rec rooms\nCabinet hardware updates in Vanier bungalows\nWeather stripping on original wood doors\nToilet and shower head replacement in rentals','[{\"q\":\"My walls near downtown are plaster, not drywall. Can they still be patched?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and they should be repaired as plaster rather than smeared over with drywall compound. Where the lath behind has broken away I put backing in first, then build the patch up in coats and let each one dry properly. It takes longer than a drywall repair, which is why I mention it before quoting. Done that way the crack does not come straight back.\"},{\"q\":\"I rent out a house near Ontario Tech. Can you handle a list between tenants?\",\"a\":\"That is one of my favourite bookings. Send the full list with photos and I will price the lot as one visit. Patching, touch-up paint, doors re-hung, blinds replaced, handles tightened, a running toilet or a dripping tap sorted. Eight small things in one trip works out far better for you than calling somebody out eight separate times.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you replace the light fixture in a 1950s bungalow here?\",\"a\":\"Swapping a fixture that is already there for a new one is fine, and the same goes for a switch, a dimmer or a ceiling fan on an existing box. What I do not touch is new circuits, panel work or anything that changes the wiring in the house. If the box is loose, the insulation is brittle or the job grows, it goes to a licensed electrician.\"},{\"q\":\"How far north in the city do you go?\",\"a\":\"All the way up. Samac, Taunton, Kedron, Windfields and the newer roads past the university are in range, along with the older neighbourhoods closer to the lake such as Lakeview and Donevan. Oshawa is not a long drive for me, so a single small job is still worth booking rather than waiting until you have five of them saved up.\"}]','',1,46,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (47,'oakville','Oakville','Halton Region',1,43.4675000,-79.6877000,'L6H, L6J, L6K, L6L, L6M','Old Oakville\nBronte\nKerr Village\nGlen Abbey\nRiver Oaks\nCollege Park\nClearview\nJoshua Creek\nWest Oak Trails\nIroquois Ridge\nUptown Core\nFalgarwood\nPalermo\nEastlake','Oakville Harbour\nLakeshore Road\nBronte Harbour\nSixteen Mile Creek\nGlen Abbey Golf Club\nOakville Place\nGairloch Gardens\nOakville GO Station','burlington,mississauga,milton,port-credit,streetsville','Handyman Services Across Oakville','Handyman services in Oakville for high fixtures, TV mounting, plaster repair, doors, assembly and bathroom updates. Free quotes. Send a photo to get started.','A Handyman for Oakville Homes and Condos','If you have been looking for a handyman in Oakville who takes the small jobs seriously, that is what I do all week. I am Sercan, I work alone, and I have spent more than ten years fixing, hanging, assembling and repairing across the west end of the GTA. The quote comes from me and the person on the ladder is me.\n\nOakville houses run from century brick near the harbour to post-2000 builds out in West Oak Trails with rooms twice the height of a normal ceiling. Both bring their own headaches, and both go smoother when whoever turns up has seen them before. Free quotes, a price agreed before I start, and a reply usually within one business day.','<h2>Tall rooms and the ladder work nobody wants</h2>\n<p>Homes in Glen Abbey, River Oaks, Joshua Creek and West Oak Trails often have a foyer open to the second floor, a great room with a vaulted ceiling, or a fixture hanging in the middle of a stairwell void. Changing a bulb up there is a small job that people put off for a year, because the ladder it needs is not the ladder in the garage. The same goes for a chandelier that has to come down and go back up, a smoke alarm chirping five metres overhead, or artwork that belongs on a wall you cannot reach standing up.</p>\n<p>Height changes mounting too. A screen going over a fireplace in one of those rooms needs the right viewing height, real timber or proper anchors behind the bracket, and cables run somewhere sensible rather than dangling down the stone. Setting it too high is the most common complaint I hear about a television somebody else put up.</p>\n<h2>Old Oakville plaster and Bronte cottages</h2>\n<p>Closer to the lake the housing changes completely. Century homes off Lakeshore Road have plaster over lath, walls that are not plumb, and studs at whatever spacing the carpenter felt like a hundred years ago. A stud finder is close to useless in those walls, so I probe, find real timber and fix into it rather than trusting a beep.</p>\n<p>Bronte has a lot of former cottages that were added onto over decades. That can mean three different framing methods inside one house, floors that step up and down between rooms, and doors that have never closed the same way twice. None of it is a problem as long as nobody assumes it is a standard build.</p>\n<h2>Condos and apartments</h2>\n<p>There are mid-rise buildings along Lakeshore Road, Kerr Street and around the Uptown Core, and they come with their own rules. Most want the service elevator booked ahead, several restrict the hours work can happen, and some ask for paperwork from anyone doing work in a unit.</p>\n<p>Ask your concierge or property manager what is required, then tell me when you book and I will fit the visit around the building. Concrete ceilings and shear walls are no trouble, they just need a hammer drill and masonry anchors instead of the plugs that came in the bracket box.</p>\n<h2>The list I get asked for most</h2>\n<p>Most of these take a couple of hours, and several can be grouped into one booking.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High fixtures</strong> - chandeliers, foyer lights, stairwell bulbs and smoke alarm batteries</li>\n<li><strong>Wall mounting</strong> - televisions, shelves, mirrors and heavy artwork, on plaster or concrete</li>\n<li><strong>Doors and windows</strong> - sticking doors, patio door rollers, locks, screens and weather stripping</li>\n<li><strong>Bathroom updates</strong> - vanities, faucets, shower heads, fresh caulking and cabinet hardware</li>\n<li><strong>Furniture and storage</strong> - wardrobes, beds, desks, shelving units and closet organisers</li>\n<li><strong>Drywall and plaster</strong> - cracks, dents, old mount holes and paint that matches what is there</li>\n<li><strong>Outdoors</strong> - deck boards, gate hardware, fence panels and railings that have loosened</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Send a message with a description and a few photos and you will normally have a price back within one business day. Quotes are free with nothing attached to them. Before work starts you get a fixed price or an hourly rate, agreed with you first. Materials come with receipts or you buy your own, the room is left clean, and if something needs revisiting afterwards I come back for it.</p>','Two things define the work here. First, height. Foyers open to the second floor, vaulted great rooms, stairwell fixtures and screens going over fireplaces mean the right ladder is half the job. Second, the split between century homes near the harbour with plaster over lath and unpredictable framing, and the large post-1990 houses further north where everything is drywall and exactly where you expect. Condo buildings on Kerr Street add elevator bookings and concrete anchoring to the mix.','Ladder work on two-storey foyer light fixtures\nPlaster and lath repair in Old Oakville homes\nTelevision mounting above living room fireplaces\nBalcony door adjustments in Kerr Village condos\nScreen and storm door service in Bronte\nVanity and faucet replacement in Glen Abbey ensuites\nCloset organiser installs in River Oaks homes\nDeck and fence repair near Sixteen Mile Creek','[{\"q\":\"Can you reach a light fixture in a two-storey foyer?\",\"a\":\"Yes. It is one of the most common calls I get from the newer parts of town. I carry a ladder tall enough for open foyers and stairwell voids, and I can change bulbs, take a chandelier down and re-hang it, fit a fixture you have already bought, or replace the smoke alarm battery you have been listening to for a fortnight.\"},{\"q\":\"The walls in my century home are plaster. What can actually be mounted on them?\",\"a\":\"More than most people expect, provided the fixing goes into real timber. Stud finders struggle with lath and horsehair plaster, so I probe and locate the framing by hand, then fix straight into it. Mirrors, shelves, televisions and heavy artwork are all fine that way. Where there is no stud in the spot you want, I will say so and suggest a position that works.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you take on work inside Oakville condo buildings?\",\"a\":\"I do. The building sets the terms though. Ask your concierge if the service elevator needs booking, what hours work is permitted, and what documents they want from anyone working in a unit. Pass that on when you book and I will schedule around it. Concrete walls and ceilings are fine, they simply need masonry anchors and a hammer drill.\"},{\"q\":\"I have a long list of small jobs. Can they be done in one visit?\",\"a\":\"That is the best way to use me. Write out everything, from the wobbly towel bar to the door that will not latch to the shelf you bought two years ago, and send photos with it. I will tell you honestly what fits into a single visit and what needs a separate trip for parts. One booking beats four call-outs.\"}]','',1,47,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (48,'burlington','Burlington','Halton Region',2,43.4012000,-79.8261000,'L7L, L7M, L7N, L7P, L7R','Aldershot\nDowntown Burlington\nRoseland\nShoreacres\nElizabeth Gardens\nTyandaga\nBrant Hills\nMountainside\nHeadon Forest\nMillcroft\nThe Orchard\nAlton Village\nKilbride\nLowville','Spencer Smith Park\nBrant Street Pier\nRoyal Botanical Gardens\nMapleview Centre\nBurlington GO Station\nLowville Park\nMount Nemo\nLakeshore Road','oakville,milton,mississauga,port-credit,georgetown','Handyman in Burlington, Ontario','Handyman in Burlington for condo mounting, fixture swaps, door and deck repair, assembly, drywall, bathroom updates. Free quotes, no obligation. Message me.','Handyman Work in Burlington and Aldershot','A handyman in Burlington spends as much of the week in condo hallways as in garages, and that suits me fine. I am Sercan, a one-man operation, and I have been doing repairs, mounting, assembly and small home improvement projects around the GTA for more than ten years. Nothing is handed off to a subcontractor.\n\nBurlington stretches from the lake up over the escarpment, so a week here might start in a tower near Spencer Smith Park and finish at a fence line in Kilbride. Quotes are free with no obligation, the price is agreed before anything starts, and I usually reply within one business day.','<h2>Working in the condo buildings</h2>\n<p>Downtown Burlington and the Lakeshore Road strip hold a lot of apartment and condo homes, and hanging something on a wall in one of them is a different job from hanging it in a house. Interior partitions are often steel stud rather than wood. Ceilings and several walls are poured concrete, which wants a hammer drill and masonry anchors, not the plastic plugs that came with the bracket. Balcony walls and slab edges are usually off limits under the building rules, so I check before drilling anywhere near them.</p>\n<p>Buildings also run on their own timetable. Most want the service elevator booked in advance, several limit the hours work can happen, and some ask for documents from anyone working inside a unit. Find out from your concierge what is needed and pass it on when you book, and I will fit the visit around the building instead of the other way round.</p>\n<h2>Bungalows below the highway, subdivisions above it</h2>\n<p>South of the QEW you get post-war Aldershot streets and the wide 1950s and 1960s ranch bungalows through Roseland, Shoreacres and Elizabeth Gardens, with mature trees, original hardwood and plaster in a good number of them. Long low ceilings, solid doors, and cupboards built by somebody who did not own a level.</p>\n<p>North of the highway it changes. Headon Forest, Millcroft, the Orchard and Alton Village went up from the late 1980s through the 2000s, so it is drywall, engineered joists and open-plan rooms. In the older homes I plan for surprises behind the wall. In the newer ones the surprise is usually a builder shortcut rather than an unknown.</p>\n<h2>Seasonal work near the lake and up the escarpment</h2>\n<p>Wind and freeze-thaw do most of the damage in this part of Halton Region. Gates drop and stop latching, fence posts work loose in the ground, deck boards cup and lift a fastener, and exterior door weather stripping flattens out until you can feel the draught with your hand. Those lists arrive every spring and every autumn.</p>\n<p>Getting a gate squared up or a run of deck boards swapped before the ground freezes is far less work than dealing with it in March. The same goes for caulking around exterior doors and windows, which is a short job in October and a cold, miserable one in January.</p>\n<h2>What Burlington callers book me for</h2>\n<p>Most of this takes under a day, and several small jobs can be batched into a single visit.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mounting into concrete</strong> - televisions, shelves, mirrors and blinds in condo units</li>\n<li><strong>Fixture swaps</strong> - replacing an existing light, ceiling fan, dimmer or smart switch</li>\n<li><strong>Bathroom work</strong> - faucets, shower heads, toilets, fresh caulking and grab bar installs</li>\n<li><strong>Doors</strong> - adjusting, hinges, handles, locks, weather stripping and screen repairs</li>\n<li><strong>Furniture assembly</strong> - wardrobes, bed frames, desks and flat-pack storage units</li>\n<li><strong>Flooring and trim</strong> - vinyl plank, laminate, baseboard and quarter round</li>\n<li><strong>Outside</strong> - fence and gate repair, deck boards, railings and exterior caulking</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Send a description with a few photos and you will usually have a price back without me driving out first. Before any work begins you get a fixed price or an hourly rate, agreed with you. Materials come with receipts, or you buy your own if you would rather choose the finish. The work area is left clean, and if something is not right afterwards I return and put it right. Anything needing a licensed trade gets referred on instead of guessed at.</p>','Burlington covers a lot of ground for one city. Downtown and the Lakeshore Road strip are apartments and condo towers, where concrete anchoring, service elevator bookings and building rules shape the day. South of the QEW there are ranch bungalows from the 1950s with plaster and original hardwood. North of it, subdivisions from the late 1980s onward. Up on the escarpment it is gates, fencing and outbuildings taking a beating from wind and freeze-thaw.','Concrete ceiling anchoring in downtown condo towers\nGrab bar installs in Roseland bungalows\nShelving fixed into plaster in Aldershot homes\nFence post and gate repairs in Millcroft\nLight fixture swaps in Alton Village builds\nCaulking and re-grouting in lakeside condo bathrooms\nFlat-pack assembly in Brant Street apartments\nStorm and screen door fixes in Tyandaga','[{\"q\":\"My condo has concrete ceilings and walls. Can you still hang things?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Concrete needs a hammer drill and the right masonry anchors, and once they are in they hold better than anything you can fit in drywall. The parts I stay away from are the balcony slab edge and anywhere the building restricts drilling. Check the rules with your property manager, let me know what they say, and I will work inside them.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you come up to Kilbride and Lowville, or only the built-up part of the city?\",\"a\":\"I go up there. Properties above the escarpment usually mean gates, fencing, outbuildings, exterior doors that have swollen and general outside work, so it helps a lot if you send photos and a clear list before I drive out. Grouping a few jobs into one trip makes more sense than a single ten-minute fix that far north.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you help get a place ready to put on the market?\",\"a\":\"That is a good use of a day. Filling and sanding the holes left by old mounts, touching up paint, re-caulking a tub and a kitchen sink, adjusting doors that catch, tightening loose handles and replacing a tired light fitting makes a real difference in listing photos. Send me the list and I will tell you what is realistic in the time you have.\"},{\"q\":\"My Roseland bungalow still has some of its original wiring. Will you work on it?\",\"a\":\"I will replace a fixture, a switch or a dimmer that is already there, and I will tell you honestly what I find behind the plate. Older houses through Roseland and Aldershot sometimes have brittle insulation, no ground wire, or a box that has been overfilled. That is a licensed electrician\'s job, not mine, and I say so rather than push on regardless.\"}]','',1,48,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (49,'milton','Milton','Halton Region',2,43.5183000,-79.8774000,'L9T, L9E, L0P','Old Milton\nTimberlea\nBristol Survey\nDempsey\nBeaty\nClarke\nCoates\nHawthorne Village\nHarrison\nWillmott\nScott\nFord\nMilton Heights\nCampbellville','Main Street East\nMilton GO Station\nKelso Conservation Area\nRattlesnake Point\nMilton District Hospital\nMattamy National Cycling Centre\nMilton Mall\nSixteen Mile Creek','oakville,georgetown,burlington,mississauga,brampton,streetsville','Handyman Milton - Home Repairs & Assembly','Handyman services in Milton, Ontario. Repairs, furniture assembly, TV mounting, drywall patching, fixtures and trim, done by me. Send photos for a quote.','Handyman in Milton, from Old Town to Boyne','A lot of the handyman work I take on in Milton starts with a moving box. Houses here fill up faster than they get finished, so the list on the fridge grows: a TV leaning against the wall, blinds in their packaging, a bed frame in flat-pack, a closet with one bare rod in it. Nothing on that list is urgent, which is why it sits there for months.\n\nEverything is quoted before I start, as a fixed price or an hourly rate, whichever suits the job. I supply materials with receipts, or I fit what you have already bought. Small jobs get grouped into one visit so you are not booking me three times in a month. Photos sent with your request help me price it faster.','<h2>What the housing stock here means for repairs</h2><p>Milton has been built in waves. The older core around Main Street holds century brick houses and post-war bungalows, Timberlea and Bristol filled in through the 1970s, 1980s and 1990s, and the subdivisions that now ring the town went up from the early 2000s onward: Hawthorne Village, Beaty, Clarke and Coates first, then Harrison, Willmott, Ford and the Boyne surveys. Each wave was built differently, and that changes how a job goes.</p><p>Newer builds mean drywall instead of plaster, which patches cleanly, but they also bring narrow lots, tall two-storey walls and partitions with no blocking behind them exactly where you want to hang something heavy. Older houses in town are the opposite problem: solid walls, and studs sitting wherever the builder of the day felt like putting them. Either way, <strong>I find the framing before I drill</strong>.</p><h2>The jobs Milton homeowners call about</h2><p>Most of my list here is unfinished detail rather than damage. People move into a townhouse in Ford or Boyne and there is nothing on the walls, no shelf in any closet and a stack of flat-pack in the garage. Owners who have been in Dempsey or Beaty longer tend to call about wear instead: a door that drags on the carpet, a shut-off weeping under the sink, caulking gone grey around the tub.</p><ul><li>Flat-pack and furniture assembly in newly built townhouses</li><li>TV mounting on tall great-room walls, with the cables tucked away</li><li>Closet shelving and garage storage in narrow homes</li><li>Faucet, shower head and toilet swaps</li><li>Interior door adjustments, hinges, handles and locks</li><li>Baseboard, trim and drywall patching after a move</li><li>Backsplash tile, cabinet hardware and vanity updates</li></ul><h2>Hard water and the fixtures it wears out</h2><p>Water in Milton is hard, and it shows on plumbing fixtures. Aerators clog, shower heads spray sideways and the finish on a tap pits years before it should. Changing a faucet, a shower head or a toilet is routine work for me. I will bring the parts with receipts, or fit whatever you have already picked out.</p><p>Anything past a fixture swap goes to a licensed plumber. The same rule holds on the electrical side: replacing a light fixture, ceiling fan, dimmer or smart switch on a circuit that already exists is fine, while new circuits and panel work are not mine to touch. You will hear that from me up front, not halfway through the afternoon.</p><h2>Booking a visit and grouping small jobs</h2><p>Quotes are free and carry no obligation. Tell me the list, add photos if you have them, and you get a fixed price or an hourly rate agreed before anything starts. Hours are Monday to Saturday, 8:00 AM to 8:00 PM, with Sunday by appointment, and replies usually go out within one business day.</p><p>Two or three small jobs booked together make far better use of a single trip than three separate visits, so keep a running list on the fridge and hand me the lot. When the work is done the area is swept and the packaging leaves with me. If something is not sitting right a week later, say so and I will come back to it.</p>','Almost every subdivision ringing the old town went up inside the last twenty-five years, so most Milton jobs are about finishing a house rather than repairing an old one: mounting, shelving, assembly, trim. The core streets and Timberlea flip that around. Hard groundwater is the constant across both, chewing through aerators, shower heads and tap finishes. Narrow lots also mean gates and fence boards take a beating every winter.','Hanging TVs on tall great-room walls in Boyne\nAssembling flat-pack furniture for new Milton townhouses\nReplacing scale-worn faucets and shower heads\nAdjusting builder-grade interior doors that stick\nFitting closet shelving in narrow townhouse bedrooms\nPatching drywall dings left by moving day\nRe-caulking tubs and kitchen backsplashes\nRepairing gates and fence boards after winter','[{\"q\":\"My Milton house is still under builder warranty. Should I call you or the builder?\",\"a\":\"Check your warranty paperwork first. If an item is covered, let the builder handle it, because there is no sense paying me for something you are already owed. A lot of things sit outside that list, though: mounting, shelving, assembly, anything you have changed yourself, and anything that went wrong after you moved in. Send me your list and I will tell you honestly which is which.\"},{\"q\":\"Timberlea and Old Milton are nothing like the new surveys. Do you work on those houses too?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and often they are the more interesting jobs. Century houses near Main Street, the bungalows in Timberlea and the Bristol survey homes all need a slower approach: older trim profiles, doors painted a dozen times, and walls that do not follow modern stud spacing. I also get out to Campbellville and the properties along the Niagara Escarpment.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you mount a large TV on the tall wall above my fireplace?\",\"a\":\"Usually, yes. I find the framing first, use a mount rated for the screen, and pull the cables down inside the wall so nothing hangs in view. On the double-height walls in newer Milton homes I bring the right ladder and take my time. If you want a new power outlet put in behind the screen, that part goes to a licensed electrician.\"},{\"q\":\"How soon can you get out to Milton after I send a request?\",\"a\":\"I answer quote requests within one business day as a rule. The visit itself depends on what is already booked, and I will give you real dates rather than a vague promise. Milton runs are easier to schedule when a few jobs are grouped, so if your list can wait a few days you usually get a better slot. Work runs Monday to Saturday, with Sunday by appointment.\"}]','',1,49,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_locations` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `region`, `tier`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `postal_prefixes`, `neighbourhoods`, `landmarks`, `nearby`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `h1`, `intro`, `body_html`, `local_notes`, `common_jobs`, `faqs_json`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (50,'georgetown','Georgetown','Halton Region',2,43.6489000,-79.9192000,'L7G, L7J','Downtown Georgetown\nGeorgetown South\nDelrex\nMoore Park\nPark District\nGlen Williams\nNorval\nStewarttown\nLimehouse\nSilver Creek\nBallinafad\nActon','Main Street South\nGeorgetown GO Station\nGeorgetown Marketplace\nGellert Community Centre\nDominion Gardens Park\nCedarvale Park\nHungry Hollow\nLimehouse Conservation Area','milton,brampton,caledon,bolton,oakville','Handyman Georgetown - Repairs and Assembly','Handyman services in Georgetown and Halton Hills. Plaster and drywall repair, doors, fixtures, assembly and trim work by one person. Get a free quote.','A Georgetown Handyman Who Does the Work Himself','Georgetown is two towns joined together, and a handyman notices the switch within a couple of streets. Around Main Street South and the older Park District the houses are brick and stone, with plaster walls, floors that have settled and doors hung by hand. Out past Mountainview Road, Georgetown South is newer, square and quicker to work in.\n\nMy name is Sercan and I do the work myself, so the person who quotes is the person who shows up. Quotes are free, the price or hourly rate is agreed before I start, and I will say plainly if a job needs a licensed trade. Georgetown sits at the western edge of my area, so I batch small jobs into one visit. Photos help me price it in advance.','<h2>The calls that come in most often</h2><p>Assembly and mounting top the list, same as anywhere, but this town adds a steady run of door work. Old houses shift with the seasons, and a door that closed fine in February binds against the jamb in July. Planing an edge, shimming a hinge or resetting a strike plate usually sorts it, and it is a half-hour job rather than a new door.</p><p>After that come the fixture swaps: kitchen taps, shower heads, toilets, a vanity in a bathroom that has not been touched since the 1980s. Light fixtures, ceiling fans, dimmers and smart switches also come up, as long as the circuit is already there. Anything needing new wiring or a panel change goes to a licensed electrician, and older houses here do sometimes turn up wiring I will not touch.</p><h2>Plaster, lath and walls that fight back</h2><p>Plenty of houses on the older streets still have lath and plaster behind the paint. It cracks along the same lines every year, it crumbles if you drive a screw in carelessly, and stud spacing is a guess until you find the first one. I patch plaster so it sits flush with what is around it, and I use fixings that suit the wall instead of hoping a plastic anchor holds.</p><ul><li>Patching plaster cracks in older brick and stone homes</li><li>Hanging mirrors and artwork on lath walls</li><li>Easing and rehanging doors that stick in summer</li><li>Replacing worn hinges, handles and locksets</li><li>Weather stripping and screen repair on older frames</li><li>Re-caulking tubs and tiled surrounds</li><li>Fitting shelving where the studs are not where you expect</li></ul><h2>Newer houses in Georgetown South</h2><p>The south end is a different day\'s work. Walls are drywall, framing is regular, and the jobs are about finishing the place: shelving, blinds, closet storage, a TV over the fireplace, baseboards and trim after a floor goes down. Laminate and vinyl plank in a spare room or a hallway is straightforward, and I cut the transitions and quarter round to match.</p><p>Newer does not mean trouble-free. Builder hardware wears out, interior doors go out of square, and the wall where you want a big screen often has nothing solid behind the drywall. Bathrooms and kitchens in the twenty-year-old parts of the south end are now due for cabinet hardware, new taps and fresh caulking.</p><h2>Planning a visit and what to have ready</h2><p>Write the list before you call, even the tiny items. Because this is the far end of my run, one trip with eight small tasks beats three trips with two. If you are out in Glen Williams, Norval, Stewarttown or Limehouse, that matters even more. <strong>A photo of each item is the fastest way to get a price.</strong></p><p>Materials come with receipts, or you buy your own and I fit it. Hours are Monday to Saturday, 8:00 AM to 8:00 PM, and Sunday by appointment. I usually reply within one business day. When the work is finished the room gets cleaned up, and if something is not right afterwards I come back and sort it.</p>','A good part of Georgetown was built before the war and a good part after 1990, and the two halves need different tools in the van. Old brick homes bring plaster, hand-hung doors, painted-shut windows and stone foundations. The newer south end brings drywall, builder hardware and rooms waiting to be finished. Add the hamlets around the edges, where properties are older and further apart, and one trip has to cover a lot of ground.','Patching plaster cracks in century brick homes\nEasing doors that swell shut in summer\nMounting TVs over fireplaces in Georgetown South\nSwapping dated vanities and bathroom taps\nLaying laminate and vinyl plank in bedrooms\nRepairing screens and weather stripping on older windows\nAssembling flat-pack for families in newer subdivisions\nFixing deck boards and fence panels after winter','[{\"q\":\"My Georgetown house has original wood windows and doors. What can you actually do with them?\",\"a\":\"Quite a bit, short of replacing the unit. I free sashes that have been painted shut, adjust and rehang doors, replace worn hinges, handles and locksets, fit new weather stripping and rebuild or re-screen bug screens. If a frame is rotten through or a window has failed, that is a replacement job for a window company and I will say so.\"},{\"q\":\"Does Glen Williams count as Georgetown for a booking, or is that a separate trip?\",\"a\":\"Same trip as far as I am concerned. Glen Williams, Norval, Stewarttown and Limehouse all sit inside the run, along with the concession roads between them. Properties out that way are older and further apart, so I ask for the full list first and try to clear everything in one visit. Photos help me bring the right parts.\"},{\"q\":\"Every door in my Delrex bungalow sticks by mid-summer. Is that worth fixing?\",\"a\":\"It is, and it rarely needs a new door. Humidity swells timber and old houses move on their footings, so the fix is usually planing an edge, packing a hinge, moving a strike plate or trimming the bottom rail. I can go through the whole house in one visit and do the lot, which is far tidier than replacing doors one at a time.\"},{\"q\":\"I am on a well and septic outside town. Does that change what you can do?\",\"a\":\"Not for the work I do. Taps, shower heads, toilets and the shut-offs under a sink are the same job whatever feeds them. What I stay away from is the well pump, the pressure tank and anything on the septic side, and that goes to a licensed plumber. Hard water out that way does wear fixtures out sooner, so aerators and shower heads are common swaps.\"}]','',1,50,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (1,'handyman-services','Handyman Services','Handyman Services for Toronto Homes and Condos','fa-solid fa-screwdriver-wrench','handyman services toronto, handyman near me, home repair services toronto, handyman gta, odd job handyman toronto, local handyman toronto, small home repairs gta, house repairs handyman ontario',1,'Handyman Services and Home Repairs','Handyman services across the Greater Toronto Area for small repairs, mounting, patching and odd jobs. Several jobs batched into one visit. Get a free quote.','Handyman services in Toronto usually start with a list. A door that sticks, a shelf that never went up, a patch of drywall behind the couch, a tap that drips. I am Sercan, and I do this work myself across the Greater Toronto Area. Ten years and more than five hundred projects have taught me that the small jobs are the ones people put off longest, and the ones that make a home feel worse every day.\n\nYou do not need a separate trade for each item. Send me the whole list and I will tell you what I can handle, what I cannot, and roughly how long it will take. Batching several small jobs into one visit is almost always the sensible way to do it.','<h2>What handyman services actually cover</h2>\n<p>A handyman is the person you call for work that is too small for a specialist and too fiddly to enjoy on a Saturday. Mounting, patching, hanging, fixing, adjusting, sealing, assembling. Most of it takes minutes to hours rather than days, and most of it needs the right tool and a bit of patience more than it needs a big crew.</p>\n<p>I work alone, so the person who quotes your job is the person who does it and the person you call afterwards if something is not sitting right. Nothing gets lost between an office and a van, and you are not paying for a dispatcher.</p>\n<p>Ten years and more than five hundred completed projects mean that very little of it is new to me. Condos with concrete walls, semis in the older neighbourhoods with plaster and lath, newer builds in the suburbs with steel studs and hollow doors. Each has its own quirks and its own fasteners.</p>\n<h2>The jobs I take on most weeks</h2>\n<p>Across the city the same handyman services come up again and again. Here is a fair sample of a normal week.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Televisions and shelves mounted into studs or rated anchors</li>\n<li>Drywall holes patched, sanded and painted to blend</li>\n<li>Flat-pack furniture built and anchored to the wall</li>\n<li>Interior doors planed, shimmed and rehung so they latch</li>\n<li>Taps, toilet seats and shower heads swapped like for like</li>\n<li>Bathroom and kitchen caulking cut out and replaced</li>\n<li>Baseboard, quarter round and door casing repaired</li>\n<li>Deadbolts, handles and door closers fitted or adjusted</li>\n</ul>\n<p>None of those are big-ticket items on their own. Put six of them on one list, though, and you have a home that stops annoying you every time you walk through it.</p>\n<h2>Where I stop and call in a licensed trade</h2>\n<p>Honesty here saves everyone money. In Ontario some work belongs to a licensed electrician or plumber, and I will not pretend otherwise. Adding a new circuit, opening a panel, moving a drain or a supply line, anything involving gas, altering a load-bearing wall, replacing a roof or touching heating equipment are all outside what I do.</p>\n<p>Swapping an existing fixture is a different matter. A like-for-like light fixture, switch, dimmer, faucet, toilet or vanity is normal handyman work and I do it regularly. If I open something up and find a problem that crosses the line, you hear about it straight away and I point you to the right trade rather than bodging it and walking away.</p>\n<h2>What separates a tidy job from a rushed one</h2>\n<p>Preparation is nearly all of it. Finding a stud and confirming it with a small probe hole inside the bracket footprint beats trusting the beep of a detector. Cutting old caulk fully out and letting the joint dry beats squeezing a fresh bead over a mouldy one. Backing a large drywall hole with a board rather than mesh alone beats a patch that cracks by spring. None of that takes long, and skipping it is what costs you.</p>\n<p>The other half is the finish. Dust sheets down before sanding, a vacuum with a brush head at the end, screws collected off the floor, packaging flattened and taken out with me. A repair that looks invisible from two metres (about six feet) away in daylight is the standard I aim for, and I would rather spend the extra quarter hour than have you looking at a shadow every morning.</p>\n<h2>Booking handyman services across the GTA</h2>\n<p>Send me a short list with a photo or two of anything unusual. Photos speed up pricing more than any description, because I can see the wall type, the access and the condition of what I am dealing with before I load the van. Replies usually come within one business day.</p>\n<p>Hours are Monday to Saturday, eight in the morning to eight in the evening, with Sunday by appointment. Quotes are free and carry no obligation, and the fixed price or hourly rate is agreed before I pick up a tool. If something is not right after I leave, tell me and I come back.</p>','Small repairs batched into one scheduled visit\nDrywall holes patched, sanded and repainted\nTelevisions, shelves and mirrors mounted securely\nFlat-pack furniture built and wall anchored\nSticking doors adjusted, planed and rehung\nLike-for-like faucet and light fixture swaps\nCaulking renewed around tubs and counters\nBaseboard and trim repaired or replaced\nCurtain rods, blinds and towel bars fitted\nSeasonal maintenance checks around the home','[{\"title\":\"Send me your list\",\"text\":\"Write out everything that needs doing, even the small items, and add photos of anything unusual. Photos of the wall, the fitting or the damage let me price accurately without a site visit in most cases.\"},{\"title\":\"Free quote agreed\",\"text\":\"I come back with a fixed price or an hourly rate and an honest estimate of how long the list will take. Nothing is booked until you are happy with it, and there is no obligation.\"},{\"title\":\"One visit, whole list\",\"text\":\"I arrive with the tools and materials for everything you listed, work through the items in a sensible order, and keep you posted if anything underneath turns out different from what the photos showed.\"},{\"title\":\"Clean up and check\",\"text\":\"Before I pack up, you and I walk the list together. The work area is vacuumed, offcuts and packaging leave with me, and receipts for any materials I supplied are handed over. If something is not right afterwards, I come back.\"}]','Price depends on the list, not on a single job. A visit with several small items usually works out better than booking each separately, because setup and travel are shared across the whole trip. Wall type, access, ceiling height and the condition of what I find all move the figure. Materials can come from me with receipts, or you can supply your own. Awkward access, heritage plaster and rooms full of furniture take longer. Whatever the job, the fixed price or hourly rate is agreed before I start.','[{\"q\":\"What counts as a handyman job and what does not?\",\"a\":\"If it is a repair, an installation of something that already has a place to go, or a maintenance task that takes hours rather than days, it is probably mine. New circuits, panel work, gas, moving plumbing lines, structural changes and roofing are not. Swapping an existing faucet, toilet, vanity, light fixture, switch or dimmer is normal handyman work and I do it often. Anything past that line goes to a licensed electrician or plumber.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I book several small jobs in the same visit?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and it is the way I prefer to work. Setting up once for six small items is far more efficient than six separate visits, and you get the benefit of that. Send me the full list, including the things you think are too trivial to mention, and I will tell you what fits comfortably in a single visit and what needs its own slot.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you supply materials or should I buy them?\",\"a\":\"Either works. If I supply them you get the receipts, so you can see exactly what was bought and what it cost. Plenty of customers prefer to choose their own tap, light fitting or paint colour, which is completely fine. Just have it on site before I arrive. If you are unsure what to buy, tell me the job and I will give you a specification to take to the store.\"},{\"q\":\"Which parts of the GTA do you cover?\",\"a\":\"I work right across the region, from the city itself out through the surrounding municipalities. Travel time is part of how a job gets priced, so for a very small single item a long way out it can be worth waiting until you have a few things on the list. Tell me your neighbourhood when you get in touch and I will be straight with you about it.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens if something is not right afterwards?\",\"a\":\"Tell me and I come back. I stand behind my workmanship, and since I am the one who did the job there is nobody to pass you to. Most callbacks are small: a door that has settled, a caulk line that needs a touch, a shelf that wants another anchor. It is a short visit and it keeps the job finished properly, which matters more to me than the time it costs.\"},{\"q\":\"How quickly do you reply?\",\"a\":\"Usually within one business day. I am on tools most of the day, so a message or an email often gets answered in the evening rather than the moment you send it. Hours are Monday to Saturday, eight in the morning until eight at night, with Sunday by appointment. If your list includes something urgent, say so in the first message and I will work out where it fits.\"}]','tv-mounting,drywall-repair,furniture-assembly,interior-painting,door-repair','',1,1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (2,'tv-mounting','TV Mounting','TV Mounting in Toronto, Level and Solid','fa-solid fa-tv','tv mounting toronto, tv wall mount installation, mount tv on drywall, tv installation near me, tv mounting service gta, mount tv on concrete wall, tv bracket installation toronto, hide tv cables in wall, tv mounting above fireplace',1,'TV Mounting Service in Toronto','TV mounting across the Greater Toronto Area on drywall, concrete, brick or block, with cables hidden and the screen sitting level. Send a photo for a quote.','TV mounting looks simple until the bracket meets the wall. In Toronto homes I run into wood studs, steel studs, concrete, brick and hollow block, and every one of them wants a different fastener. Get that choice wrong and a screen that weighs as much as a bag of cement is hanging on hope.\n\nI mount screens of every size across the GTA, from a small bedroom set to a large panel over a fireplace. Fixed, tilting or full-motion, with cables tucked away and the picture sitting level and centred where you actually watch it from. Most mounts take an hour or two once the wall is understood.','<h2>Reading the wall before anything else</h2>\n<p>Every TV mounting job starts with the wall, not the bracket. A stud finder gives me a first guess, then I confirm the edges by probing with a fine bit inside the bracket footprint, so any test hole ends up hidden behind the plate. Wood studs in most Toronto houses sit at about forty centimetres (sixteen inches) on centre. Older homes wander, and the steel studs common in condos need a different screw and a lot more care.</p>\n<p>Concrete, brick and hollow block each get their own fastener. Solid concrete takes a hammer-drilled sleeve anchor set to the right depth. Hollow block needs a properly rated toggle that opens behind the face shell, not an expanding plug that crumbles it. Drywall on its own is never enough for a television, whatever the box says.</p>\n<h2>Choosing between fixed, tilting and full-motion</h2>\n<p>The bracket decides how the room feels afterwards. A fixed mount sits tight to the wall and looks best, but leaves almost no access behind it for cables later. A tilting mount buys you a few degrees downward, which matters when the screen has to sit high. A full-motion arm swings out and turns, which is the answer for corner rooms and open kitchens, though it needs more solid fixing because the load moves away from the wall as it extends.</p>\n<p>Weight and screen size drive the choice as much as taste does. I check the VESA pattern on the back of your set against the bracket before anything is drilled, and I carry the bolt lengths and spacers that suit curved backs and recessed mounting points.</p>\n<h2>Where the cables go</h2>\n<p>Loose cables under a mounted screen undo all the tidy work above them. There are a few honest options and I will talk you through which one suits your wall.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Paintable surface raceway glued and screwed to the wall</li>\n<li>Signal cables dropped inside the cavity between stud bays</li>\n<li>Low-voltage brackets fitted top and bottom for a clean pass</li>\n<li>Everything routed down to a shelf or console below</li>\n<li>Power left at the existing outlet, never spliced inside the wall</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That last point matters. Running a standard power cord inside a wall is not permitted, so where a hidden outlet behind the screen is what you really want, that is a job for a licensed electrician and I will tell you so rather than doing it badly. Dropping HDMI and network cables through the cavity is fine, and in most framed walls it is quick once the plate position is set.</p>\n<h2>TV mounting above a fireplace</h2>\n<p>People ask for this constantly and it can work, with two cautions. Heat is the first. I check how warm the mantel area gets and whether the manufacturer allows it, because sustained heat shortens the life of a panel. Viewing angle is the second. A screen above a mantel usually sits high enough that a tilting or pull-down mount is worth the extra cost, otherwise you spend every film looking upward.</p>\n<p>Fireplace walls are often brick, block or a framed chase with an odd stud layout behind the finish. I sound the wall out first and pick the anchor to match, rather than assuming.</p>\n<h2>Soundbars and the finish</h2>\n<p>A soundbar wants to hang centred under the screen with a small gap so the remote sensor stays clear. I fix it to the same structure or to rated anchors, level it against the television rather than the wall, since the wall is rarely the true reference, and dress its cables in with the rest.</p>\n<p>Old brackets come down too. A fair share of my TV mounting work begins with removing a bracket that went up in the wrong place or into the wrong anchor. If there is a mount already on the wall in the wrong place, I take it off, fill and sand the holes and leave the surface ready for touch-up paint. Before I go the screen is levelled again with the bracket loaded, the picture is checked on your inputs, a full-motion arm is swung through its whole travel to make sure nothing snags, and the packaging leaves with me.</p>','Stud location confirmed before any drilling\nFixed, tilting or full-motion bracket fitted\nConcrete and hollow block anchored correctly\nScreens levelled and centred to your seating\nCables concealed in raceway or wall\nSoundbar mounted centred under the screen\nExisting wall mount removed and holes filled\nDevices connected and picture checked','[{\"title\":\"Photos and details\",\"text\":\"Send a photo of the wall and the model of your TV. That tells me the screen size, the VESA pattern and usually the wall construction, which is most of what I need to quote the mount accurately.\"},{\"title\":\"Wall survey on site\",\"text\":\"On arrival I scan for studs, pipes and wiring, confirm the framing by probing inside the bracket footprint, and agree the exact height with you before a single hole is drilled. Marking is done with a level, not by eye.\"},{\"title\":\"Bracket and screen fitted\",\"text\":\"The wall plate goes up and gets checked for level, the arms are bolted to the back of the television with the correct length hardware, and the screen is lifted on and locked. Then it is levelled again under load.\"},{\"title\":\"Cables, test, tidy\",\"text\":\"Cables are routed and hidden by the method you chose, the picture is tested on your inputs, and the mount is exercised through its full movement so nothing snags. Dust and packaging go with me and the floor gets a vacuum.\"}]','Screen size and wall type drive most of it. A mid-size television into wood studs is quick work. The same screen onto concrete, brick or hollow block takes longer and needs specific anchors, and a large panel needs careful handling. Full-motion arms take more time than fixed plates. Cable concealment adds to the job depending on which method you choose, and supplying the bracket yourself or having me bring one changes things too. The price is agreed with you before I drill.','[{\"q\":\"Can my wall take the weight of a large TV?\",\"a\":\"Almost always, once the fixing is right. A screen bolted through the wall plate into two wood studs is holding onto the structure of the house, not the drywall. Concrete and solid brick are stronger again. Hollow block is the one that needs care, because the face shell is thin and an ordinary plug will crush it, so I use a toggle rated for the load. Steel studs in condos need their own hardware.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you hide the cables inside the wall?\",\"a\":\"Signal cables, yes, in most framed walls. HDMI and network leads can be dropped through the cavity and brought out behind a console. Power is different. A standard TV power cord is not permitted inside a wall, so the correct solution is either a surface raceway painted to match, or a new outlet behind the screen fitted by a licensed electrician. I will tell you which suits your wall.\"},{\"q\":\"How high should the TV go?\",\"a\":\"Lower than most people expect. The comfortable position puts the centre of the screen near eye level from where you sit, which in a normal living room is often lower than the wall seems to want. I hold the bracket up and let you look at it from the sofa before anything is marked. If the only spot is high, a tilting mount makes a real difference to your neck.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I need to buy the mount myself?\",\"a\":\"Your choice. If you already have one, have it out of the box and I will check it against your screen before starting, because the VESA pattern and weight limit have to match. If you would rather I bring the bracket, tell me the make, model and screen size and say if you want fixed, tilting or full motion, and I will source something suitable.\"},{\"q\":\"Is mounting above a fireplace a bad idea?\",\"a\":\"Not always, but go in with your eyes open. Check that your television allows it, since heat rising off a mantel shortens the life of the electronics. The bigger issue is usually the viewing angle, because the screen ends up high and you spend the evening looking upward. A tilting or pull-down bracket solves that. If the wall is masonry I anchor into it accordingly.\"}]','smart-home-installation,shelf-mirror-installation,drywall-repair,handyman-services,condo-handyman','',1,2,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (3,'furniture-assembly','Furniture and IKEA Assembly','IKEA and Furniture Assembly in Toronto','fa-solid fa-couch','furniture assembly toronto, ikea assembly toronto, flat pack furniture assembly gta, ikea furniture assembly service, furniture assembly near me, wardrobe assembly toronto, bed assembly service toronto, ikea pax installation',1,'Furniture Assembly and IKEA Builds','Furniture and IKEA assembly across the Greater Toronto Area. Flat-pack wardrobes, beds, desks and dressers built square and anchored. Get a free quote.','Furniture assembly is one of those jobs that always takes longer than the box suggests. A wardrobe with a hundred and eighty parts, a diagram with no words, a cam lock fitted the wrong way round on step nine. I build flat-pack furniture across Toronto and the GTA so your evening goes back to being an evening.\n\nTen years of doing this means I know where the manuals mislead you and which panels have a top and a bottom that look identical. Beds, wardrobes, desks, dressers, shelving, cribs and office chairs all get built square, levelled on the floor you actually have, and anchored to the wall where they need to be. The cardboard leaves with me.','<h2>Brands and pieces I put together</h2>\n<p>Flat-pack is flat-pack, but each maker has its habits. IKEA relies on cam locks and dowels and punishes you for a panel fitted upside down. Wayfair and Amazon pieces often arrive with softer particle board and screws that strip if you lean on the driver. Structube and similar mid-range lines usually go together well but need care on the finished faces.</p>\n<p>Over ten years and more than five hundred projects I have done furniture assembly on most of the common ranges more than once, which mostly means I know which step in the booklet is the one that catches people out.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wardrobes, PAX runs and sliding-door units</li>\n<li>Bed frames, slatted bases and storage beds</li>\n<li>Desks, office chairs and standing desk frames</li>\n<li>Dressers, chests of drawers and bedside tables</li>\n<li>Bookcases, shelving units and TV benches</li>\n<li>Cribs, change tables and toddler beds</li>\n<li>Dining tables, benches and bar stools</li>\n<li>Garage racking and utility shelving</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Anti-tip anchoring is not optional</h2>\n<p>Tall furniture falls forward when a drawer is open and a child climbs on it. That is not a scare story, it is basic physics, and it is why every dresser, bookcase and wardrobe I build gets strapped or bracketed to the wall. The strap in the box is usually fine. The screw supplied with it usually is not.</p>\n<p>Into a wood stud I use a proper wood screw of adequate length. Into drywall between studs I use a rated toggle rather than the plastic plug in the bag. In a concrete or block condo wall the anchor changes again and needs a hammer drill. If a wall genuinely cannot take a fixing, I say so and find another arrangement with you instead of pretending the strap is doing something.</p>\n<h2>Square, level and the floor you actually have</h2>\n<p>A cabinet built out of square racks itself apart within a year. Drawers stop closing, doors sit proud at one corner, and the thin back panel pulls the whole carcass into a parallelogram. I check the diagonals before that back goes on, because once those small nails are in, the shape is set.</p>\n<p>Floors matter just as much. Older houses in the city rarely have a level floor, and a wardrobe standing on a slope will not close properly no matter how well it was built. Adjustable feet get set with a spirit level, shims go under fixed plinths, and doors are tuned on their hinges afterwards so the gaps look even from across the room.</p>\n<h2>Where furniture assembly goes wrong</h2>\n<p>Most failures come from the same handful of causes. Cam locks turned past their stop and stripped. Dowels forced into a hole with no glue and then knocked apart during a move. Drawers built with the runners on the wrong side. Backs nailed on before anything was checked for square. Screws driven with a drill on full clutch until the head sinks straight through the board.</p>\n<p>None of that is exotic. It is what happens on part sixty of a hundred when you started at nine in the evening with a dinner plate on the floor beside you. Working slowly through the first two steps saves an hour later, every time, and it is the part I never rush.</p>\n<h2>Disassembly, moving and packaging</h2>\n<p>Taking things apart is furniture assembly in reverse. If you are moving within the GTA I can bring a wardrobe or bed down carefully, bag and label the fittings so nothing goes missing, and rebuild it at the other end. Particle board furniture only tolerates so many cycles, so I will tell you honestly if a piece is unlikely to survive the trip.</p>\n<p>At the end of any build the boxes get flattened, the polystyrene bagged, and the whole lot goes out with me unless you want to keep it. Spare fittings are bagged with the instructions in case you move the piece later, and the floor gets a vacuum before I leave.</p>','Flat-pack wardrobes, beds and dressers built\nAnti-tip straps anchored into studs or toggles\nAdjustable feet levelled on uneven floors\nDrawer runners aligned and gaps evened out\nOffice chairs and standing desks assembled\nCribs and change tables built to instructions\nExisting furniture disassembled for a move\nCardboard and polystyrene removed afterwards','[{\"title\":\"Tell me the pieces\",\"text\":\"Send the product names or a link for each item, plus how many of each. IKEA article numbers are ideal. That tells me the build time and whether a piece needs wall fixing or extra care getting it upright.\"},{\"title\":\"Boxes in the room\",\"text\":\"Have the flat packs in the room where the furniture is going, with a clear floor space beside them. Carrying a built wardrobe through a doorway rarely works, so the build happens where the piece will live.\"},{\"title\":\"Built, checked, anchored\",\"text\":\"Parts are laid out and counted first, then the carcass goes together and gets checked for square before the back panel is fixed. Doors and drawers are adjusted, feet levelled, and anything tall is strapped to the wall.\"},{\"title\":\"Packaging taken away\",\"text\":\"Boxes get flattened, foam and plastic bagged, and the lot leaves with me unless you want to keep it. Spare fittings are bagged and left with the instructions in case you move the piece later.\"}]','Build time is what you are paying for, and that varies more than people expect. A bedside table is quick. A tall wardrobe run with sliding doors is a serious afternoon. The number of pieces, the size of each one, whether the boxes are already in the right room, and how much wall anchoring is needed all count. Disassembly for a move usually takes less time than the original build. Whatever the list, the price or hourly rate is settled before I open a box.','[{\"q\":\"Do you collect the furniture from the store?\",\"a\":\"No, I build rather than deliver. Have the boxes at your place before the visit, ideally in the room where the piece is going, because a finished wardrobe usually will not fit through a doorway. If a delivery slips and the boxes are not there on the day, let me know as early as you can and I will move the visit.\"},{\"q\":\"What if a part is missing or damaged in the box?\",\"a\":\"It happens, and usually with the small fittings rather than the panels. I carry a stock of common screws, dowels, cam locks and washers, so minor shortfalls rarely stop the build. A cracked panel or a missing major component has to come from the manufacturer, so I photograph it, note the part number from the manual, and build everything else so you only need a short return visit.\"},{\"q\":\"Does everything really need to be anchored to the wall?\",\"a\":\"Anything tall and narrow does, and anything with drawers that a child could climb. Dressers, bookcases and wardrobes tip forward more easily than people imagine once a drawer is out. The strap in the box is usually fine, but the fastener supplied with it often is not, so I fit a proper screw into a stud or a rated toggle into drywall instead.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you take apart furniture I am moving?\",\"a\":\"Yes. I take the piece down in reverse order, bag and label every fitting per item, and mark panels where the manual does not. Particle board does not love being taken apart, especially if it was glued, so I will tell you honestly which pieces are likely to survive the move and which are better replaced than rebuilt.\"},{\"q\":\"I started the build and got stuck. Can you finish it?\",\"a\":\"Often, yes, and it is a common call. The first thing I do is check what is already built for square and for parts fitted the wrong way round, because carrying on top of a mistake makes it worse. Sometimes a section has to come apart and go back together. Tell me which step you reached and send a photo, and I will give you a realistic idea of the time.\"}]','shelf-mirror-installation,tv-mounting,handyman-services,condo-handyman,cabinet-repair','',1,3,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (4,'drywall-repair','Drywall Repair and Patching','Drywall Repair and Patching in Toronto','fa-solid fa-trowel','drywall repair toronto, drywall patching gta, plaster repair toronto, drywall hole repair near me, ceiling repair toronto, drywall repair and painting, popcorn ceiling repair toronto, water damage drywall repair',1,'Drywall Repair and Patching in the GTA','Drywall repair and patching across the Greater Toronto Area. Holes, cracks, nail pops and water damage filled, textured and painted to blend. Send me a photo.','Drywall repair is judged in one glance from across the room. Either the wall reads flat and even under the light from the window, or your eye keeps finding the spot. That is the whole craft: the filling is easy, the feathering and the finish are what take time. I patch walls and ceilings across the Greater Toronto Area.\n\nNail pops, dents from furniture, a door handle through the hallway wall, cracks opening at a corner bead, a section cut out by a plumber, water damage in a ceiling. Each one needs a different repair, and matching the existing texture matters as much as the hole itself. Older Toronto homes often have plaster and lath rather than board, which changes the approach again.','<h2>Matching the drywall repair to the damage</h2>\n<p>Size decides method. Anything up to about the width of a thumb gets filled, one thin coat at a time, and sanded flush. Up to roughly fifteen centimetres (six inches) a self-adhesive mesh patch with three progressively wider coats of compound is honest work and holds well. Beyond that, mesh alone flexes, and a flexing patch cracks.</p>\n<p>Larger openings need real support behind them. That means either a backing board screwed inside the cavity with a fitted plug of new board, or a hot patch cut with a paper border that becomes its own tape. Both give the compound something solid to sit on, which is the entire point.</p>\n<h2>How a large hole is properly patched</h2>\n<p>Here is the drywall repair sequence I follow, because it is the one that stops a patch showing up a year later.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cut the ragged edge back to clean, square board</li>\n<li>Check the cavity for wiring or pipe before cutting deeper</li>\n<li>Fit a backing strip or a plug of matching board thickness</li>\n<li>Tape the joints with paper tape bedded in compound</li>\n<li>Build up in thin coats, each one wider than the last</li>\n<li>Sand with a light held flat to the wall to find shadows</li>\n<li>Prime the repair so the patch does not flash through the paint</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Thin coats are the part people skip. Compound shrinks as it dries, so a thick fill sinks into a dish and needs another coat anyway. Three thin passes with proper drying time between them beat one heavy one, every single time.</p>\n<h2>Nail pops, cracks and why they return</h2>\n<p>A nail pop is the fastener backing out as the framing behind it dries and moves. Filling the bump does nothing at all. The screw or nail has to be driven back below the surface or removed, a new screw set a few centimetres away into the same stud, and both dimples filled and finished. Done that way it stays put.</p>\n<p>Cracks at corner beads and above door frames are movement cracks. I cut them open slightly, rebed the tape or the bead where it has let go, and refinish. A crack that keeps returning in the same place after a proper repair is telling you something about the structure, and I will say so rather than filling it for a third time.</p>\n<h2>Water damage and ceilings</h2>\n<p>Before any drywall repair on a ceiling, the leak has to be finished with. Patching over an active leak wastes your money and my time. Where the source is a plumbing fault I point you to a licensed plumber first, then come back and put the ceiling right once everything has dried out.</p>\n<p>Stained but sound board can often be sealed with a stain-blocking primer and repainted. Board that has gone soft, sagged, or lost its paper face has to come out. Popcorn ceilings can be patched and the texture stippled back in, though on anything installed before the early eighties I recommend having it tested for asbestos before it is disturbed, and I will not sand it until you have.</p>\n<h2>Texture, primer and paint</h2>\n<p>Toronto walls carry all sorts of finishes: flat modern board, orange peel, knockdown, light stipple, and old plaster with a trowelled face. A perfect flat patch on an orange peel wall is still an obvious repair, so I match the texture with a sprayed or rolled pass and knock it back to suit what surrounds it.</p>\n<p>Primer over bare compound is not optional either, because fresh mud drinks paint at a different rate than the wall around it and dries to a different sheen. Where you have the leftover tin from the original job, the blend is usually invisible. Where you do not, painting the full wall corner to corner is the reliable answer, and I will tell you plainly which one your wall needs before I start.</p>','Door knob holes cut back and patched\nNail pops refastened and refinished\nLarge holes backed with board and taped\nCorner bead cracks reopened and rebedded\nWater-stained ceilings sealed and repainted\nPopcorn ceiling texture patched and matched\nOrange peel and knockdown texture blended\nRepairs primed and painted to blend in','[{\"title\":\"Photo of the damage\",\"text\":\"A picture with something for scale tells me most of it: the size, the texture on the surrounding wall, and whether the board is sound or soft. Add a note about the ceiling height and the paint colour if you know it.\"},{\"title\":\"Cut back and support\",\"text\":\"The damaged area is squared off to sound board, the cavity checked for wiring and pipe, and a backing strip or a fitted plug goes in. Nothing gets filled until there is something solid behind it.\"},{\"title\":\"Tape, coat and sand\",\"text\":\"Paper tape is bedded into compound over the joints, then two or three thin coats go on, each wider than the last, with drying time between them. Sanding is done with a light raking across the wall so shadows show up.\"},{\"title\":\"Texture, prime and paint\",\"text\":\"The surrounding texture is matched, then the patch is primed so it does not flash through the topcoat. Paint is blended into the wall or taken corner to corner where a spot repair would still catch the light.\"}]','Size and finish move the price more than anything else. A small fill on a flat white wall is quick work. A large hole in a textured ceiling needs backing, several coats with drying time between them, texture matching and paint, which often means a return visit. Access matters, and so does ceiling height. Whether you supply the paint or I bring it makes a difference, and so does how many separate spots there are around the home. The price is agreed before I start cutting.','[{\"q\":\"How long does a drywall patch take to finish?\",\"a\":\"Longer than the cutting and filling suggests, because compound has to dry between coats. A small patch can be done in a single visit if the room is warm and dry. A larger repair usually means coating, leaving it, then coming back to sand, texture and paint. Rushing the drying is what produces a patch that shrinks and shows a shadow ring later.\"},{\"q\":\"Will the repair be invisible?\",\"a\":\"On a flat, freshly painted wall with the original paint available, yes, in normal light. On a textured surface or an old wall where the paint has aged, a spot repair can still catch the eye at a certain angle. That is why I match texture carefully and, where it matters to you, paint the full wall from corner to corner rather than leaving a patch to fade in.\"},{\"q\":\"My house is old and the walls are plaster. Can you still repair them?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and it needs a different hand. Plaster over wood lath is thicker and more brittle than board, and it fails when the keys behind the lath break off. Small areas can be refixed with plaster washers and built back up in setting-type compound. Where a whole section has let go, the sound approach is often to cut it out and fit board of matching thickness, then blend the surface.\"},{\"q\":\"There is a stain on my ceiling. Do you fix the leak too?\",\"a\":\"Not the plumbing itself. Moving or repairing supply and drain lines is work for a licensed plumber, and a roof leak needs a roofer. What I will do is help you work out where the water is coming from, and once the source is fixed and the area has dried out properly, cut back anything soft, replace it, seal the stain with a blocking primer and finish the ceiling.\"},{\"q\":\"How much dust does drywall sanding make?\",\"a\":\"Enough to take seriously. I sand with a vacuum-assisted sander where I can, seal the doorway with plastic on bigger jobs, cover the floor and any furniture that stays in the room, and vacuum rather than sweep at the end. Fine compound dust travels, so on a large repair it is worth clearing ornaments and electronics from the room before I arrive.\"}]','interior-painting,handyman-services,baseboard-trim-installation,tv-mounting,shelf-mirror-installation','',1,4,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (5,'interior-painting','Interior Painting','Interior Painting in Toronto and the GTA','fa-solid fa-paint-roller','interior painting toronto, house painting services toronto, room painting gta, ceiling painting toronto, trim and door painting toronto, accent wall painting, condo painting toronto, interior painter near me',1,'Interior Painting in Toronto and the GTA','Interior painting for rooms, ceilings, trim and doors across Toronto and the GTA. Careful prep, clean lines, low-odour paint. Ask me for a free quote.','Interior painting is mostly preparation, and preparation is where most of my time goes. Anyone can roll a wall. The difference shows up along the trim lines, in the corners, and in how a wall reads under late afternoon light six months later. Holes get filled and sanded flush, failed caulk gets cut out, patches get primed, and only then does colour go on.\n\nRooms, ceilings, hallways, stairwells, doors and trim, anywhere across Toronto and the GTA. One room or a full floor. Furniture moves to the centre and gets covered, floors are protected, switch plates come off instead of being masked, and the room goes back together before I leave.','<h2>What gets painted in a typical GTA home</h2>\n<p>Most jobs start with a single room. A bedroom that has been the same beige since the previous owner. A hallway scuffed by bikes, bags and shoulders. A ceiling carrying a brown ring from a leak that was fixed years ago. I take on bedrooms, living rooms, accent walls, whole floors, closets, stairwells, doors and casings, plus the interior painting nobody volunteers for, like the inside of a closet or the back of a door.</p>\n<p>Several small tasks can ride along in the same visit. Two coats on a living room, three doors freshened up and a bathroom ceiling sealed and repainted is one setup and one quote instead of three separate trips.</p>\n<h2>Preparation decides how the finish looks</h2>\n<p>Paint changes the colour of a wall. It does not hide dents, ridges, dust or dried caulk smears, and a fresh coat over an unsanded patch makes that patch more obvious rather than less. All of this happens before any finish coat goes on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nail holes and dents filled, then sanded flush by hand</li>\n<li>Failed caulk cut out of trim joints and re-run in paintable acrylic latex</li>\n<li>Kitchen walls and bathroom ceilings washed so paint can bond to them</li>\n<li>Patches spot-primed so they do not flash a duller sheen through the finish</li>\n<li>Water stains and marker sealed with a stain-blocking primer</li>\n<li>Glossy or oil-based trim scuff-sanded and primed so the new coat grips</li>\n<li>Furniture moved and covered, floors protected, switch plates taken off</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>How a room is actually rolled</h2>\n<p>Ceilings go first, walls next, trim and doors last, so anything that drips lands on a surface still waiting for its coat. Cutting in is done with an angled sash brush around 50 to 63 mm (2 to 2.5 in) wide, and the roller follows into that wet band before it can dry, which keeps the brush texture and the roller texture from reading as two different finishes. On smooth drywall I roll a short nap, around 10 mm (3/8 in). Plaster, stipple and older textured ceilings need a thicker nap to reach into the low spots.</p>\n<p>Bad work announces itself in the same few ways. Picture framing, where a dull halo shows around the edge of every wall because the cut line dried long before the roller arrived. Ridges left by an overloaded roller. Patches flashing under side light from a window. Caulk wiped onto the wall instead of tooled into the joint. Paint on hinges, screws and plates that were masked instead of removed. Good interior painting is that sequence followed in order, with two full coats over primed, sanded prep.</p>\n<h2>Sheen and colour that hold up</h2>\n<p>Flat finishes belong on ceilings, because flat hides the small waves every ceiling has. Walls in bedrooms and living rooms do well in matte or eggshell. Kitchens, bathrooms, hallways and anywhere with children get a washable eggshell or a scrubbable matte, since those clean up without polishing a shiny spot into the wall. Trim, doors and railings take satin or semi-gloss, which cures harder and takes a wipe.</p>\n<p>Colour behaves differently on a wall than on a chip under store lighting. I ask people to brush sample patches on the actual wall, look at them in the morning and again at night, and remember that a north-facing Toronto room stays cooler and greyer than the same colour in a south-facing one. Going dark over light, or light over a strong dark, means primer plus two coats.</p>\n<h2>Interior painting in condos and older Toronto homes</h2>\n<p>Plenty of houses in the older parts of the city have plaster walls and oil-based trim, and both need respect. Hairline cracks in plaster reopen if they are simply filled, so they get taped and compounded. Latex over unprimed oil trim peels away in sheets, so that trim gets a bonding primer first. Where old paint is failing across a large area in a much older home, testing before sanding is the sensible first step, and I keep sanding minimal and clean up as I go.</p>\n<p>Condo suites bring their own rules: elevator bookings, corridor protection and neighbours through the wall. Interior painting in an occupied suite calls for low-odour, low-VOC paint, which keeps the place livable while the work happens, and an open window with a fan moves what smell there is out faster. I work Monday to Saturday, so a suite can be finished without you booking time off.</p>','Single rooms and accent walls repainted\nCeilings painted and water stains sealed\nTrim, casing and baseboards finished\nInterior doors painted on both sides\nNail holes filled, sanded and primed\nOld caulk lines cut out and replaced\nScuffed hallways and stairwells touched up\nClosets, alcoves and radiator walls painted','[{\"title\":\"Walk the rooms\",\"text\":\"I look at every surface you want painted, check the trim, ceilings and past repairs, note what needs filling or priming, and talk through sheen and colour with you. Photos sent ahead of the visit speed the quote up.\"},{\"title\":\"Cover and prepare\",\"text\":\"Furniture moves to the centre and gets sheeted, floors are protected, plates and hardware come off, then holes are filled, joints are caulked and every patch is primed so nothing flashes through the finish.\"},{\"title\":\"Cut in and roll\",\"text\":\"Ceilings first, then walls, then trim and doors, two coats on each, keeping a wet edge and rolling back into the cut line so the finish reads the same right across the wall.\"},{\"title\":\"Reassemble and inspect\",\"text\":\"Hardware and plates go back on, the room is put together, rubbish leaves with me, and I walk the space with you in daylight so anything that is not right gets touched up before I go.\"}]','Painting is priced by the surface, not by the name of the room. A bedroom with smooth walls, sound trim and a light colour going over a light colour moves quickly. The same room takes far longer with cracked plaster, a dark colour underneath, tall ceilings, heavy furniture to shift, or oil-based trim that has to be scuffed and primed before it will hold paint. Supplying the paint myself adds material cost with receipts, and you are welcome to supply your own. Either way the price is agreed before I start.','[{\"q\":\"How soon can I put the room back together?\",\"a\":\"Latex paint is dry to the touch quickly and ready for a recoat within hours, but it keeps hardening for weeks. Furniture goes back the same day and hardware is reinstalled before I leave. I ask people to keep furniture a finger width off a freshly painted wall for a couple of days, hold off on taping anything to it, and wipe gently rather than scrub for the first few weeks.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you supply the paint, or should I buy it?\",\"a\":\"Either works. I can supply it and pass on the receipts, which saves you a trip and means the right primer arrives with the finish coat. Many people prefer to choose their own, especially with a colour already used elsewhere in the house. Tell me the brand and line you want. If you are buying, get a little more than the coverage calculation suggests so later touch-ups come from the same batch.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you paint over wallpaper?\",\"a\":\"I would rather remove it. Paint puts moisture into the old paste, and paper that looked well stuck will bubble along the seams within a day. Seam lines and pattern texture also telegraph through the finish. If the paper is coming off cleanly, removal followed by a skim coat and primer gives you a wall you never have to think about again. Where the drywall face tears during removal, I skim over the damage.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you match the colour already on my wall?\",\"a\":\"Usually. The reliable method is to cut a small chip from somewhere hidden, behind a door or inside a closet, and have it scanned at the paint counter. A scan reads the faded surface rather than the original colour, so it lands close but rarely exact. Because of that I prefer painting corner to corner instead of patching the middle of a wall, since a near match shows up badly in side light.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I need to empty the room or move out?\",\"a\":\"No. Clear the small things, the shelves, the electronics and anything breakable, and I handle the rest. Furniture goes to the centre under sheets, art and curtains come down, and the floor is covered. With low-odour paint most people stay in the house and use another room for the day. Pets do better shut somewhere quiet, since doors often come off their hinges to be painted.\"}]','drywall-repair,baseboard-trim-installation,door-repair,condo-handyman,handyman-services','',1,5,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (6,'faucet-installation','Faucet and Plumbing Fixture Installation','Faucet Installation and Plumbing Fixtures in Toronto','fa-solid fa-faucet-drip','faucet installation toronto, kitchen faucet replacement toronto, bathroom faucet installation gta, toilet installation toronto, shower head replacement, under sink leak repair toronto, replace shut off valve, plumbing fixture installation near me',1,'Faucet Installation in Toronto and the GTA','Faucet installation and fixture swaps across Toronto and the GTA. Kitchen and bath taps, toilets, shower heads, drains and under-sink leaks. Ask for a quote.','Faucet installation looks like a fifteen minute swap until the shut-off valve under the sink refuses to close. Most of the time it is straightforward work. Sometimes the mounting nuts have rusted onto the shank and have to be cut off, or the old stop weeps the moment it is turned. I arrive with the parts to deal with both.\n\nKitchen and bathroom taps, shower heads, toilets, drains, supply lines and shut-off valves, for homes across Toronto and the GTA, at a price agreed before anything is opened up. Anything that means cutting into a wall or moving a rough-in belongs to a licensed plumber, and I say so plainly instead of taking the job.','<h2>Fixtures I replace and leaks I chase</h2>\n<p>Bar and kitchen taps, vanity and bathroom faucets, laundry taps, shower heads and hand showers, toilets and toilet internals, pop-up drains and basket strainers, P-traps and tailpieces, braided supply lines, compression shut-off valves, and food waste disposers. Each of those is a like-for-like change of something already there, which is exactly where faucet installation belongs on a handyman list rather than a plumber list.</p>\n<p>Under-sink leaks come from a short list of suspects, and identifying the right one saves replacing parts that were never at fault. A wet cabinet floor can be a supply nut, the packing nut on the stop, a slip joint washer sitting crooked in the trap, a basket strainer that lost its seal, or a faucet body weeping down the shank where nobody can see it. I dry everything, run water, and watch for the first drop.</p>\n<h2>Where my work stops and a licensed plumber starts</h2>\n<p>Being straight about this saves everybody money. Opening a wall, moving a drain or a supply rough-in, soldering copper, cutting into a stack, replacing a stop that is soldered directly onto the pipe, or anything touching a shared riser in a Toronto condo tower is licensed plumber territory, and that is who I will point you to. The same goes for a leak inside a wall or ceiling, a drain that backs up beyond the trap arm, and sewer smell that returns after the trap has been refilled.</p>\n<p>Everything short of that line is fair game: faucet installation, replacing compression stops on an existing stub-out, clearing a blocked trap, rebuilding a toilet, and tracking down a slow drip.</p>\n<h2>How a faucet installation actually goes</h2>\n<p>A faucet installation starts with the water off at both stops, then the tap is opened to drop the pressure and drain the lines. Old supply lines come off into a tray, and the mounting nuts come off with a basin wrench or the plastic tool packed with the new fixture. The sink deck is scraped clean of hardened putty and silicone with a plastic blade so the new gasket seats flat on a dry surface. Modern faucets ship with their own rubber or foam gasket, so putty under the base is rarely needed at all.</p>\n<p>New braided stainless supply lines go on every time, sized to the stop and to the faucet, never reused from the old fixture. Thread tape belongs on tapered male threads such as a shower arm, and nowhere near a compression fitting or a rubber-sealed supply nut, where it only holds the seal apart. Joints are snugged rather than cranked, because brass and plastic strip long before they feel tight. Before I pack up:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Hot and cold run at full flow and the aerator is checked</li>\n<li>The sink is filled and drained to load the trap and its joints</li>\n<li>Every joint is dried with paper towel and rechecked after use</li>\n<li>The cabinet floor goes back dry so any later drip is obvious</li>\n<li>Shut-off valves are cycled and left working, not seized open</li>\n<li>The old fixture and the packaging leave with me</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Toilets, drains and the parts that fail quietly</h2>\n<p>Toilet work follows its own order. The tank is drained and sponged out, the closet bolts come off, and the old wax is scraped away completely. If new tile has left the flange sitting below the finished floor, an extender ring brings it back up, because stacking wax rings is a leak waiting to happen. The bowl is set on a fresh seal, shimmed if it rocks, and the nuts are drawn down in stages by hand, since china cracks well before a nut feels tight. Caulk around the base is left open at the back so a hidden leak shows on the floor instead of rotting the subfloor.</p>\n<p>Running toilets are usually a worn flapper or a fill valve past its life, and both are quick to change. A refill tube pushed down inside the overflow pipe rather than clipped above it can siphon the tank empty, which is a common cause of a toilet that cycles on its own at night.</p>\n<h2>Choosing a fixture that fits your sink</h2>\n<p>Send me a photo before you buy. Sinks come drilled as a single hole, as a centreset with the outer holes 100 mm (4 in) apart, or as a widespread at 200 mm (8 in), and a deck plate can cover holes you no longer need. Check the spout reach against the bowl and the height against any window sill above the sink, then favour a tap with a ceramic disc cartridge you can still buy parts for. On stone and composite sinks a basket strainer gets silicone rather than plumber putty, which can stain.</p>','Kitchen and bathroom faucet replacement\nToilet replacement on a fresh wax seal\nShower head and hand shower swaps\nUnder-sink leak diagnosis and repair\nCompression shut-off valve replacement\nNew braided stainless supply lines fitted\nSink drains, pop-ups and P-traps\nFood waste disposer installation and swaps\nFill valve and flapper repairs','[{\"title\":\"Send a photo\",\"text\":\"A picture of the sink, the cabinet underneath and the existing shut-off valves tells me most of what I need. From that I can usually price the work before I arrive and bring the right parts and connectors with me.\"},{\"title\":\"Isolate and remove\",\"text\":\"Water is shut at the stops, the lines are drained into a tray, and the old fixture comes out. Seized nuts get cut off rather than forced, and the sink deck is scraped back to clean, dry material.\"},{\"title\":\"Fit and seal\",\"text\":\"The new fixture sits on its own gasket, the mounting hardware is tightened evenly so the base does not sit skewed, fresh supply lines are fitted, and the drain assembly is sealed with the right material for your sink.\"},{\"title\":\"Test and dry\",\"text\":\"Hot and cold run at full flow, the basin is filled and drained to load the trap, then every joint is dried and rechecked. The cabinet floor goes back dry so a future drip is easy to spot early.\"}]','Swapping a fixture on healthy shut-off valves in an open cabinet is quick work. Cost climbs when the stops are seized and have to be replaced, when corroded mounting nuts have to be cut off, when the cabinet is deep and cramped, or when a stone counter and an undermount sink make access awkward. Several fixtures done in the same visit cost less each than booking them separately. Fixtures can come from you or from me with receipts, and the price is settled before any tools come out.','[{\"q\":\"What should I look for when buying a new faucet?\",\"a\":\"Match the hole layout in your sink or counter first, then the spout reach so water lands in the bowl and not on the rim. Look for a ceramic disc cartridge and a brand that still sells replacement parts, because a cheap tap with no parts available becomes a full replacement the first time it drips. Send me the model page before you order and I will tell you whether it fits what you already have.\"},{\"q\":\"My shut-off valve will not close. What happens then?\",\"a\":\"That is common in older Toronto homes. Water gets shut further back, usually at the main valve where the supply enters the house, and the seized stop is replaced while everything is drained and dry. On a copper stub-out that means a compression valve, which needs no torch and no soldering. If the old stop is soldered on, or the stub-out itself is damaged, that is a licensed plumber job and I will tell you on the spot.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you stop a drip without replacing the whole faucet?\",\"a\":\"Often, yes. A dripping spout is usually a worn cartridge or a set of perished O-rings, a weak stream is usually a blocked aerator, and a wet base is often a loose escutcheon rather than a failed body. If the fixture is a known brand, parts are easy to get and a repair makes sense. If it is an unbranded tap with no parts available, replacing it is the honest recommendation.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you install a food waste disposer under my sink?\",\"a\":\"Yes, where the setup allows it. The unit has to plug into a receptacle that already exists under the sink, since adding one is wiring work for a licensed electrician. If a dishwasher will drain through it, the knockout plug inside the inlet has to be removed first, which is the single most common cause of a new disposer flooding a cabinet. Many condo buildings prohibit them, so check your rules first.\"},{\"q\":\"I live in a condo. Is anything different?\",\"a\":\"Usually only the logistics. Most suites have isolating valves inside the unit, so the work is no different from a house. If your suite has no valves, the building has to shut down the riser, and that is booked through property management with notice to the neighbours on your stack. I also book the service elevator where required and protect the corridor and floors on the way in and out.\"}]','bathroom-caulking-repair,appliance-installation,cabinet-repair,condo-handyman,handyman-services','',1,6,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (7,'light-fixture-installation','Light Fixture and Ceiling Fan Installation','Light Fixture Installation and Ceiling Fans in Toronto','fa-solid fa-lightbulb','light fixture installation toronto, ceiling fan installation toronto, chandelier installation gta, replace light fixture toronto, vanity light installation, dimmer switch installation toronto, smart switch installation, ceiling fan replacement near me',1,'Light Fixture Installation in the GTA','Light fixture installation and ceiling fan swaps in Toronto and the GTA. Chandeliers, vanity lights, dimmers and smart switches. Ask me for a free quote.','Light fixture installation is a swap, not a rewire, and that difference decides who should be holding the screwdriver. If a fixture, fan, dimmer or switch is already there and the circuit behind it is sound, I can change it. If the job needs a new circuit, a new cable run or anything at the panel, it belongs to a licensed electrician and I will tell you that before you book me.\n\nChandeliers, flush mounts, vanity lights, pendants, pot light trims, ceiling fans, dimmers and smart switches, in houses and condos across Toronto and the GTA. Power off at the breaker, tested dead at the terminals, every box checked for support and a proper ground, and the room left tidy.','<h2>What I change on an existing circuit</h2>\n<p>The rule is simple: if something is already wired into that spot, it can usually be replaced, and most light fixture installation work is exactly that. Most calls are a fixture the previous owner chose, a bathroom bar with a burnt-out socket, or a fan that has developed a rattle.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chandeliers, pendants and flush mount ceiling fixtures</li>\n<li>Vanity bars, sconces and hallway lights</li>\n<li>Ceiling fans, with or without a light kit</li>\n<li>Trims and retrofit LED inserts in pot light housings you already have</li>\n<li>Dimmers, timers, motion switches and smart switches</li>\n<li>Under-cabinet strips that plug in or replace an existing unit</li>\n<li>Closet and stairwell fixtures running the wrong bulb type</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Light fixture installation, step by step</h2>\n<p>Power goes off at the breaker, not at the wall switch, and the conductors are confirmed dead at the terminals with a tester. That check matters, because a switch loop can leave an unswitched hot sitting in the same box, live and looking exactly like the wire beside it. The old fixture comes down with its canopy supported, never left hanging by its own leads.</p>\n<p>Then the box gets a proper look. Is it fastened to framing or floating in the drywall. Is there a ground wire in there. Is the plastic cracked, or the box too shallow for the splices it already holds. Conductors are stripped to the right length, joined with correctly sized wire connectors, the identified conductor goes to neutral, and the fixture ground lead lands on the ground screw in the box. Wires are folded back so nothing is pinched when the canopy seats, and every splice stays inside an accessible box, never buried behind drywall.</p>\n<h2>Ceiling fans need a box rated for them</h2>\n<p>A standard plastic or metal octagon box is made to hold a light, not a spinning weight. Fans go on a box marked as acceptable for fan support, screwed to a joist or carried on an expanding brace bar that feeds through the existing ceiling hole and locks between the joists. That brace goes in without opening the ceiling, which is why a fan swap rarely turns into drywall work.</p>\n<p>Mounting height matters as much as the box. Blades should sit about 2.1 m (7 ft) off the floor, with room above them so air can reach the blades, and at least 45 cm (18 in) between the blade tips and the nearest wall. A flush mount suits a low basement ceiling; a downrod suits a high one. Wobble is nearly always mechanical rather than electrical, so blade screws get tightened, blade alignment is checked, and a balancing kit sorts out the rest.</p>\n<h2>Dimmers, LED bulbs and smart switches</h2>\n<p>LED lamps changed the rules for dimmers. An old dimmer built for incandescent loads will make an LED buzz, flicker, drop out at the bottom of the slider or refuse to switch off completely. The fix is a dimmer rated for LED loads, matched to bulbs that are genuinely dimmable, with the low-end trim adjusted while your own bulbs are in the fixture rather than a test lamp.</p>\n<p>Smart switches add a second requirement: most of them need a neutral in the switch box, and many older Toronto switch boxes contain only a switch loop with no neutral present. Where that is the case, the choices are a model designed to work without a neutral alongside compatible bulbs, or an electrician to bring one in. Three-way locations need the matching companion switch from the same family, and box depth counts too, since smart switches are bulky and the wiring has to fold in behind without strain.</p>\n<h2>What goes to a licensed electrician</h2>\n<p>Some work is simply not mine to do, and saying so early costs you nothing. New circuits, new cable runs, panel and breaker work, cutting new pot light openings, aluminum branch wiring, knob and tube, boxes with no ground, brittle or scorched insulation, and any circuit that trips when the light goes on all belong to a licensed electrician. I will tell you exactly what I found so you can pass it on.</p>\n<p>Once their part is done, mine picks up again. Light fixture installation on the circuits they have run, patching and painting the ceiling around new openings, and setting up your dimmers and smart switches afterwards are all things I can finish for you.</p>','Chandelier and pendant light replacement\nFlush mount and vanity light swaps\nCeiling fan installation on a rated box\nFan-rated boxes and brace bars fitted\nDimmer and smart switch replacement\nPot light trims and retrofit LED inserts\nCeiling fan wobble diagnosed and balanced\nFixture wiring grounded, tested and tidied','[{\"title\":\"Check the box\",\"text\":\"Before anything comes down I confirm the power is off at the breaker and dead at the terminals, then look at the box itself: how it is fastened, whether a ground is present, and what weight it was built to carry.\"},{\"title\":\"Fit the support\",\"text\":\"If a fan or a heavy chandelier is going up, a fan-rated box or a brace bar between the joists goes in first, fed through the existing hole so your ceiling stays intact and no patching is needed afterwards.\"},{\"title\":\"Wire and hang\",\"text\":\"Conductors are stripped clean and joined with correctly sized connectors, the ground lead is fixed to the ground screw in the box, and the wiring is folded back so nothing is pinched when the canopy is drawn up tight.\"},{\"title\":\"Test and tidy\",\"text\":\"Power back on, the fixture is run through every setting, dimmers get their low-end trim set with your bulbs in place, a fan is tested at all speeds and balanced if it moves, and the packaging leaves with me.\"}]','Height and access drive most of the cost here. A vanity light at chest height is straightforward work. A chandelier over an open stairwell needs staging, careful handling and more time on site. Adding a fan-rated box or a brace bar to a ceiling that never had one takes longer than hanging a fixture where the support is already correct. Quantity helps, since fixtures done in the same visit share a single setup. Fixtures and dimmers can be supplied by you or by me with receipts, and the price is agreed first.','[{\"q\":\"There is no ground wire in my ceiling box. Can you still hang the fixture?\",\"a\":\"Not before I look at it. Older homes across Toronto often have ceiling boxes with no ground wire at all, and some still have knob and tube hiding above the plaster, which is where I stop. I will not guess with grounding or wave it through because the light happens to work. If there is no ground, or the insulation is brittle and cracking, the right answer is a licensed electrician and I will explain what I saw.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you put in pot lights?\",\"a\":\"Not new ones. Cutting fresh openings and running cable to them is wiring work for a licensed electrician, and in most homes it also means a new circuit. What I can do is work with housings that already exist: changing trims, fitting retrofit LED inserts into the existing sockets, and replacing failed units of the same type. If you have an electrician doing the wiring, I can handle the patching and painting behind them.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do my LED bulbs flicker or buzz on the dimmer?\",\"a\":\"Almost always a mismatch. Either the dimmer was built for incandescent loads, the bulbs are not dimmable, or the low-end trim on the dimmer has never been set. Mixing bulb brands in one fixture causes it too, since each driver responds differently. I change the dimmer for one rated for LED loads, put a matched set of dimmable bulbs in, and adjust the trim until the whole range is smooth.\"},{\"q\":\"My ceiling fan wobbles. Is the box loose?\",\"a\":\"Sometimes, and it is the first thing I check, because a fan hung on a box that is not rated or not fixed to framing will move all night and slowly work itself loose. More often the cause is mechanical: blade screws backed off, one blade holder sitting lower than the rest, or blades that differ in weight after a replacement. I check the mount, align and tighten the blades, then use a balancing kit.\"},{\"q\":\"Will a smart switch work in my switch box?\",\"a\":\"It depends on what is in the box. Most smart switches need a neutral, and plenty of older switch boxes have only a switch loop, so there is nothing to connect. Box depth matters too, since a smart switch is bulky and the wiring has to fold in without strain. Send me a photo of the box with the plate off and the breaker down, and I will tell you what will work there.\"}]','smart-home-installation,tv-mounting,drywall-repair,condo-handyman,handyman-services','',1,7,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:19:30');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (8,'door-repair','Door Repair and Installation','Door Repair and Installation in Toronto','fa-solid fa-door-open','door repair toronto, sticking door repair, interior door installation toronto, door not latching fix, pre hung door installation gta, patio door roller replacement toronto, closet door repair, door weather stripping toronto',1,'Door Repair and Installation in the GTA','Door repair and installation across Toronto and the GTA. Sticking doors, loose hinges, strike plates, pre-hung units, patio rollers and weather stripping.','Door repair almost always costs less than door replacement, and most of the doors I get called about do not need replacing. A door that drags on the carpet, catches at the top corner, swings open by itself or refuses to latch is nearly always a hinge, a strike plate or a jamb that has shifted, not a door that has failed.\n\nAdjusting, rehanging, replacing and weatherproofing doors across Toronto and the GTA: pre-hung units, slabs, closet bifolds, sliding doors, patio rollers, hinges, sweeps and weather stripping. Several doors handled in one visit is the sensible way to do it, and photos sent with your enquiry usually let me price the work before I arrive.','<h2>Why a door sticks, drags or will not latch</h2>\n<p>Every door repair starts with the gap around the door. That reveal should run even, roughly 3 mm (1/8 in) at the top and the sides, with a little more at the floor. When the gap closes up at the top of the latch side and opens at the bottom, the door is sagging on its hinges. A door that swings open or shut on its own is telling you the hinge jamb is out of plumb.</p>\n<p>Season and age do the rest. Solid wood swells through a humid Toronto July and shrinks again in February, so a door that catches only in summer usually needs hinge work rather than a plane. Houses settle, floors get new covering, and paint builds up in a hinge mortise until the door sits proud of where it started.</p>\n<h2>Door repair before door replacement</h2>\n<p>My first check in any door repair is the top hinge. The short screws that hold a hinge to a jamb bite only into thin jamb stock, and over the years they work loose and let the door drop. Swapping two of them for 75 mm (3 in) screws that pass through the jamb and the shims into the framing behind, then drawing them up gradually, pulls the top of the door back and lifts the latch corner. That one repair sorts out a large share of dragging doors.</p>\n<p>Stripped screw holes get glued hardwood plugs and are re-drilled, which holds far better than driving a fatter screw into a chewed-out hole. A latch that misses is diagnosed before any metal comes off: a cardboard shim behind a hinge leaf moves the door within the opening, and a strike plate that has to shift more than a hair gets its old mortise filled and a fresh one cut rather than filed into a slot. Planing is the last resort, and a planed edge always gets sealed so it does not soak up moisture.</p>\n<h2>Hanging a new door: slab or pre-hung</h2>\n<p>Pre-hung units go in faster and end up straighter, so they are my first suggestion when a jamb is damaged. The hinge jamb is set plumb in both directions and shimmed behind every hinge, fasteners go through the shims so the jamb cannot bow, and the reveal is checked top to bottom before the casing goes on.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interior doors adjusted, rehung or replaced</li>\n<li>Hinges, mortises and stripped screw holes repaired</li>\n<li>Strike plates realigned so latches catch cleanly</li>\n<li>Slab doors mortised for hinges and bored for hardware</li>\n<li>Doors undercut for new flooring or a thicker rug</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Reusing a sound jamb means fitting a slab instead. Hinge positions transfer from the old door, mortises are cut to the exact depth of the leaf so the door neither binds nor gaps, and the hardware is bored at the correct backset, either 60 mm (2 3/8 in) or 70 mm (2 3/4 in). Cutting a brand new opening into a wall is another matter, since it means framing and possible structural work, and that goes to a licensed contractor.</p>\n<h2>Sliding, bifold and patio doors</h2>\n<p>Closet doors get more use than any other door in a house and the least maintenance. Bifolds hang from a top pivot in an adjustable bracket and turn on a bottom pivot, and both can be shifted until the panels line up and close flat. Sliding closet doors depend on their rollers, a straight track and the floor guide that everyone loses during a move.</p>\n<p>Patio doors are heavier and far less forgiving. A door that scrapes usually has flat-spotted rollers and a track full of grit, and both need dealing with together, since new rollers in a dirty track wear out quickly. Rollers are changed in pairs, the adjustment screws behind the plugs at the bottom of the stiles reset the height, and the track gets a dry lubricant instead of oil, which only collects dirt.</p>\n<h2>Drafts, sweeps and weather stripping</h2>\n<p>Exterior doors leak air in three places: the sides and head, the bottom, and the corners where the two meet. Kerf-in weather stripping pushes into the groove in the jamb and is quick to replace once the right profile is identified. A worn door sweep is easy to change, and an adjustable threshold has screws along its top that raise the seal until daylight disappears under the closed door.</p>\n<p>Test it yourself before you call. Close the door on a sheet of paper and pull. If the paper slides out with no drag at all, the seal is not touching at that point. Do the same at the top corners and the latch side, mark what fails, and door repair on that draft becomes a short visit rather than guesswork.</p>','Sticking and dragging doors adjusted\nLoose hinges reset into solid framing\nStrike plates re-cut so latches catch\nPre-hung interior doors installed and cased\nSlab doors mortised, bored and hung\nBifold and sliding closet doors repaired\nPatio door rollers replaced and adjusted\nWeather stripping, sweeps and thresholds fitted\nDoors undercut for new flooring','[{\"title\":\"Read the reveal\",\"text\":\"I look at the gap around the door before touching anything. Where it is tight, where it is wide, and how the latch meets the strike tells me whether the trouble is the hinges, the jamb, the floor or the door itself.\"},{\"title\":\"Fix the cause\",\"text\":\"Most doors come right by resetting hinge screws into framing, plugging stripped holes, shimming a hinge leaf or re-cutting the strike plate. Planing stays a last resort, and any bare edge gets sealed once the fit is correct.\"},{\"title\":\"Hang or replace\",\"text\":\"Where a door or jamb is past saving, the hinge jamb is set plumb and shimmed behind every hinge, the reveal is checked from top to bottom, and hardware is bored and mortised to fit rather than forced into place.\"},{\"title\":\"Work the door\",\"text\":\"The door is swung through its full arc, left at several positions to confirm it stays put, then latched and unlatched repeatedly with the strike adjusted until it catches cleanly without needing a shove.\"}]','Adjusting a door that drags is usually short work, and several doors in the same visit share one setup. Cost rises with an opening that is out of plumb, a sagging or split jamb, hardware that has been repaired badly before, or an exterior door where the threshold and weather stripping also need attention. Fitting a slab into an existing jamb takes longer than hanging a pre-hung unit, because the hinges have to be mortised and the hardware bored. Doors and hardware can be supplied either way, and the price is agreed before work starts.','[{\"q\":\"My door sticks in summer but is fine in winter. What can be done?\",\"a\":\"That is wood doing what wood does. It takes on moisture through a humid summer and gives it back in winter, so the door grows just enough to catch. I start with the hinges, since tightening the fit at the top usually buys back the clearance you need. If planing turns out to be necessary, it is done during the humid stretch and the bare edge is sealed, otherwise it drinks moisture and swells worse next year.\"},{\"q\":\"The door closes but the latch will not catch. Why?\",\"a\":\"The latch and the strike plate are no longer lined up, and the reason is usually a dropped door rather than a bad strike. Look at the shiny mark the latch has rubbed on the plate and you can see which way it has moved. I fix the door position first with hinge work or a shim, and only then adjust the strike, filling and re-cutting the mortise so the plate sits flat and the latch catches on its own.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you cut my door down for new flooring?\",\"a\":\"Yes, within limits. A hollow core door has a solid block of wood at the bottom, and once you cut past it the skin has nothing to hold onto, so the amount that can come off is fixed. A solid core or solid wood door gives you far more room. Either way the cut is scored and taped to stop the veneer tearing out, and the raw edge is sealed afterwards.\"},{\"q\":\"Is my door worth repairing, or should it be replaced?\",\"a\":\"Most are worth repairing. Hinges, latches, strikes, sagging and sticking are all fixable, and the door you have is often better made than a new builder-grade one. Replacement makes sense when a hollow core has been punched through, when a wood door has delaminated or split, when an exterior door has rotted at the bottom, or when the jamb has been repaired so often there is nothing solid left to screw into.\"},{\"q\":\"My patio door is hard to slide. Can that be fixed?\",\"a\":\"Usually. The common causes are worn rollers with flat spots and a track packed with grit, and both get sorted in the same visit. I lift the panel out where the design allows, replace the rollers in pairs, clean and inspect the track, then reset the height with the adjustment screws so the panel runs level and the lock lines up. If the track itself is bent or crushed, that is a door company job.\"}]','lock-installation,drywall-repair,baseboard-trim-installation,window-screen-repair,handyman-services','',1,8,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (9,'shelf-mirror-installation','Shelf, Mirror and Picture Hanging','Shelf, Mirror and Picture Hanging in Toronto','fa-solid fa-images','shelf installation toronto, mirror hanging toronto, picture hanging service gta, floating shelf installation, hang heavy mirror on drywall, curtain rod installation toronto, gallery wall hanging, closet shelving installation gta',1,'Shelf and Mirror Installation Toronto','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging across Toronto and the GTA. The right anchor for your wall type, level lines and a tidy finish. Send a photo for a quote.','Shelf and mirror installation looks simple until a bracket tears out of the drywall and takes a chunk of wall with it. I hang shelves, mirrors, artwork, curtain rods and blinds across Toronto and the wider GTA, and most of the real work happens before anything touches the wall. I find the studs, confirm them, work out what the wall is actually made of, then pick a fixing rated well past the load it has to carry.\n\nLayout counts for as much as holding power. A gallery wall a few millimetres out of line reads as wrong even when nobody can say why. I set heights off a level, hold pieces up so you can see them in place, and leave the room clean.','<h2>Start with what the wall is made of</h2>\n<p>Every hanging job in the GTA begins the same way, by working out what sits behind the paint. A detached house usually has 12.7 mm (1/2 in) drywall over wood studs at 406 mm (16 in) centres. A downtown Toronto condo often has drywall furred off a poured concrete or block wall, with nothing solid in between the furring. Older houses can have plaster over wood lath, which crumbles if you drill it in a hurry.</p>\n<p>Each of those walls takes a different fixing. Guessing is how a shelf ends up on the floor with a crater above it. I use a stud finder, confirm the reading with a fine test hole or a magnet that picks up the drywall screws, and only then choose the hardware.</p>\n<h2>Choosing anchors for shelf and mirror installation</h2>\n<p>Into a wood stud, a lag screw or a heavy wood screw in a correctly sized pilot hole beats any anchor ever made. Into hollow drywall, I use toggle-style anchors that open out behind the board and spread the load across a wide area. Plastic expansion plugs are for light picture hooks and nothing more. Into concrete or block it is a masonry bit and a sleeve or screw anchor matched to the fixing.</p>\n<p>Weight is only half the story. A floating shelf puts leverage on its fixings, so a bracket carrying a modest load at the wall is working far harder once that load sits 250 mm out from the face. I size hardware for the pull-out force, not for the number printed on the packet.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wood screws or lag bolts where a stud lands behind the fixing</li>\n<li>Metal toggle anchors for drywall with no stud available</li>\n<li>Sleeve or screw-in masonry anchors for concrete and block</li>\n<li>Slow-drilled hollow-wall anchors for plaster and lath</li>\n<li>French cleats for heavy mirrors and large framed pieces</li>\n<li>Self-drilling anchors kept for light, low-leverage loads only</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Levelling, spacing and layout</h2>\n<p>Very few homes have a floor and a ceiling that agree with each other. Levelling to the ceiling line can look right in the room even when a spirit level disagrees, so I ask which reference matters to you before I mark anything. For a run of shelves I set the top line first and work down with a story stick, which keeps every gap identical instead of nearly identical.</p>\n<p>Gallery walls get laid out on the floor, or as paper templates taped up, so you can shuffle them around before there are holes in the plaster. Centring a piece at roughly 145 to 150 cm (57 to 59 in) to its middle suits most rooms, though I drop that over a sofa or a console so the artwork relates to the furniture.</p>\n<h2>What separates a good job from a bad one</h2>\n<p>Poor hanging work has the same tells every time. Fixings that missed the stud by a few millimetres. A mirror sitting proud at one end. Open holes left behind from the last attempt, and grey dust worked into a pale carpet. <strong>None of that is difficult to avoid, it just has to be done deliberately.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Vacuum nozzle held at the bit so dust never reaches the floor</li>\n<li>Pilot holes sized to the screw and to the material</li>\n<li>Both halves of a cleat checked, not only the top one</li>\n<li>Fixings pulled on by hand before the weight goes anywhere near</li>\n<li>Old anchors removed and the holes filled rather than left open</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Curtain rods, blinds and closet shelving</h2>\n<p>Window treatments look like the easy part and rarely are. The header above a window is solid framing in some houses and hollow in others, and brackets catching only drywall will sag under lined curtains inside a season. I fix into framing where it exists, use rated anchors where it does not, and set both brackets from one measurement taken off the ceiling so the rod runs true across the window.</p>\n<p>Closet organisers and wire shelving deserve the same care. The vertical standards carry the entire load, so they go into studs or proper toggles, and each shelf run gets checked along its full length before anything is stacked on it.</p>','Floating shelves fixed to studs or anchors\nHeavy mirrors hung on rated cleats\nGallery walls laid out and hung\nCurtain rods and blind brackets mounted level\nCloset organisers and wire shelving installed\nBracket shelving squared and evenly spaced\nArtwork hung on plaster or concrete walls\nOld anchors removed and holes filled','[{\"title\":\"Photos and a quote\",\"text\":\"Send a photo of the wall and of the item, with rough measurements. That usually tells me the wall type and the fixings it needs, so the quote comes back accurate rather than padded. I reply within one business day.\"},{\"title\":\"Finding what is solid\",\"text\":\"On the day I scan for studs, confirm them with a small test hole or a magnet, and check whether the wall is drywall, plaster and lath, or board furred off concrete. The anchor choice follows from what I find.\"},{\"title\":\"Marking the layout\",\"text\":\"Nothing gets drilled until you have seen the position. I mark heights with a level, hold the piece up against the wall, and adjust until it looks right in the room rather than only correct on the tape measure.\"},{\"title\":\"Fix, load and tidy\",\"text\":\"I drill with a vacuum at the bit, set the fixings, then load the shelf or mirror and test it by hand. Dust gets cleaned up, packaging and offcuts leave with me, and you get the wall back clean.\"}]','Two things move the price on hanging work: what the wall is made of, and how much leverage the item puts on its fixings. Drilling concrete or block takes longer than drywall and wears out bits. A wide gallery wall needs layout time before a single hole is made. High or awkward positions over a stairwell need extra setup. Removing old anchors and patching what they leave behind adds a little. I agree a fixed price or an hourly rate with you before I start, and hardware can come from me with receipts or from you.','[{\"q\":\"Can you hang a heavy mirror in a condo where there are no studs?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Most condo walls are either drywall over a steel or wood furring strip against concrete, or drywall over light steel studs. Both take heavy loads if the right anchor is used. Concrete gets a masonry anchor set with a hammer drill, and hollow sections get metal toggles that open behind the board. I check what is there first and tell you what the wall will safely hold.\"},{\"q\":\"How much dust and mess does drilling make?\",\"a\":\"Less than you would think, because I hold a vacuum nozzle right at the bit while drilling and put down protection underneath. Drywall dust is fine and pale, and concrete dust is worse, so both get caught at the source rather than swept up afterwards. Anything that escapes gets vacuumed before I leave, and the packaging and offcuts go with me.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I need to buy the brackets and anchors myself?\",\"a\":\"Either way works. Plenty of shelves and mirrors arrive with hardware in the box, though the supplied anchors are often the weakest part of the kit and I will usually recommend better ones. If you would rather I supply the fixings, I bring them and pass on the receipts. Tell me the item and the wall when you ask for a quote and I will confirm before the day.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you hang things on the plaster walls in an older Toronto house?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and those walls need a gentler approach. Plaster over wood lath chips if you drill it fast or with hammer action on, so I drill slowly with a sharp masonry bit, tape the surface first to stop the face from flaking, and land fixings in the lath or a stud wherever the load calls for it. Done that way, plaster holds very well.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens to the holes if I move the shelves later?\",\"a\":\"Screw and anchor holes in drywall fill and sand back to invisible under fresh paint, which is straightforward. Toggle anchors leave a wider hole that needs proper filling rather than a smear of compound. If you already have old anchors to remove, I can pull them and patch the wall in the same visit, and touch up if you have the original paint on hand.\"}]','tv-mounting,furniture-assembly,drywall-repair,interior-painting,condo-handyman','',1,9,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (10,'bathroom-caulking-repair','Bathroom Caulking and Repairs','Bathroom Caulking and Repairs in Toronto','fa-solid fa-bath','bathroom caulking toronto, recaulk shower toronto, bathtub caulking replacement, mouldy caulking removal gta, regrouting shower toronto, loose bathroom tile repair, grab bar installation toronto, bathroom exhaust fan replacement gta',1,'Bathroom Caulking and Repair Toronto','Bathroom caulking and repairs across Toronto and the GTA. Old bead cut right out, tubs and showers resealed, grout and loose tiles sorted. Get a free quote.','Bathroom caulking is the first thing in a bathroom to fail and the last thing anyone gets around to fixing. I re-seal tubs, showers and vanities across Toronto and the GTA, and the difference between a bead that lasts for years and one that goes black in a season sits almost entirely in the preparation. Cutting the old seal out completely, cleaning the joint back to bare surfaces and letting it dry is the job.\n\nAlongside sealing, I take care of the small faults that pile up in a bathroom. Cracked grout, a loose tile, a towel bar hanging out of the wall, a toilet that runs all night, a slow drip under the vanity. Batching them into one visit beats calling somebody out three times.','<h2>Why the old bead failed</h2>\n<p>Caulking in a bathroom has a hard life. It sits in standing water, it flexes every time someone steps into the tub, and once soap film builds up on the surface it becomes a food source for mould. Almost every failed seal I cut out of a Toronto bathroom went the same way: the wrong product was used, the joint was dirty or still damp when it was applied, or the tub was empty when it was sealed and the bead tore the first time it was filled.</p>\n<p>Painter\'s acrylic caulk belongs around trim, not around a shower. It shrinks, it goes chalky, and water finds a way behind it within a year. <strong>Once water is behind the bead, scrubbing the surface achieves nothing, because the mould is growing on the back of it.</strong></p>\n<h2>How I redo bathroom caulking properly</h2>\n<p>The old bead comes out completely. I score both edges with a fresh blade, pull the caulking away in strips, then take the residue off with a plastic razor so the tile glaze and the tub finish are not scratched. Silicone will not bond to old silicone, so any film left behind becomes tomorrow\'s gap. The joint then gets wiped with rubbing alcohol and left to dry. If the shower was used that morning, the sealing waits.</p>\n<p>Before sealing a tub-to-tile joint I fill the tub with water. An acrylic or steel tub drops noticeably under the weight of water and a person, so a bead applied to an empty tub is stretched to failure on the first bath. I mask both sides of the joint, run a mildew-resistant silicone made for kitchens and bathrooms, tool the bead in a single pass, and pull the tape straight away while it is still wet.</p>\n<p>Then it gets left alone. Most silicones want a full day before they meet water, and rushing that is what turns a fresh seal into a soft, peeling mess a fortnight later.</p>\n<h2>Grout, loose tiles and the rest of the room</h2>\n<p>Grout and sealant do different jobs, and confusing the two causes a lot of the damage I am called out to. Grout is a hard filler between tiles sitting on the same plane. Corners, and the joints where a wall meets a tub, a vanity or a floor, all move. Those want a flexible sealant. It is exactly why grouted corners crack in a neat line every single time.</p>\n<p>Where grout has cracked or fallen out, I rake the joint with a carbide grout saw or an oscillating tool, vacuum the dust out, and refill with unsanded or sanded grout depending on the width of the joint. Loose tiles get lifted, cleaned back and re-bedded. If the board behind a tile is soft and crumbling, that is water damage rather than a grout problem, and I will say so plainly instead of hiding it under fresh grout.</p>\n<h2>Small repairs worth batching into one visit</h2>\n<p>Bathrooms collect little faults, and having them handled together costs you less than separate call-outs across the GTA.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Towel bars and paper holders re-fixed with proper anchors</li>\n<li>Grab rails fitted into studs or solid blocking</li>\n<li>Toilet seats, flappers and fill valves replaced</li>\n<li>Shower heads, hoses and diverters swapped over</li>\n<li>Vanity doors adjusted and worn handles replaced</li>\n<li>Like-for-like exhaust fan swap on existing wiring and ducting</li>\n<li>Drips traced at the trap and supply lines under a vanity</li>\n<li>Shower doors adjusted, rollers replaced and sweeps renewed</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Where I stop and hand it over</h2>\n<p>Some bathroom work needs a licensed trade in Ontario, and I would rather tell you that at the quote than halfway through. Opening up in-wall plumbing, moving a drain or a supply rough-in, or running a new circuit for a fan or a heated floor goes to a licensed plumber or electrician. A shower leaking into the ceiling below is a waterproofing failure under the tile, not something a fresh bead of silicone can cure, and it needs the shower rebuilt.</p>\n<p>Being straight about that is part of the work. You get a clear answer about what you are dealing with, and you are not paying me to make a real problem look tidy for a few months.</p>','Old mouldy caulking cut out completely\nTubs, showers and vanities resealed\nCracked grout raked out and refilled\nLoose wall and floor tiles re-bedded\nGrab rails fixed into studs or blocking\nToilet seats, flappers and fill valves replaced\nLike-for-like exhaust fan replacement\nSlow drips traced under a vanity\nShower door rollers and sweeps renewed','[{\"title\":\"Photos and a quote\",\"text\":\"A few photos of the tub, shower corners and vanity usually tell me whether this is a reseal, a regrout, or something behind the tile. I price it from that and reply within one business day, with no obligation.\"},{\"title\":\"Strip and dry out\",\"text\":\"The old bead is cut and pulled, the residue scraped back with a plastic razor, and the joint wiped clean. A damp joint gets time and airflow to dry, because silicone applied over moisture never bonds properly.\"},{\"title\":\"Seal and tool\",\"text\":\"The tub is filled before sealing so the joint sits at its widest, both sides are masked, and the silicone is run and tooled in one pass. Tape comes off wet so the edges stay crisp and straight.\"},{\"title\":\"Cure time and cleanup\",\"text\":\"I tell you exactly how long to keep the shower dry, usually a full day. Tools, offcuts and the old caulking leave with me, surfaces get wiped down, and the bathroom is ready to use once cured.\"}]','Resealing one tub joint is quick. Stripping a full shower enclosure that has been recaulked over itself several times is not, because every layer has to come off before the new bead will bond. What moves the price here is how much old material there is, whether grout work or loose tiles come with it, how tight the access is behind a toilet or a glass door, and who supplies the sealant and fittings. Drying time can spread a job over two short visits. I agree the price with you before any of it starts.','[{\"q\":\"How long do I have to keep the shower dry afterwards?\",\"a\":\"Plan on a full day for most silicones. The surface skins over in under an hour and looks finished, but the bead cures from the outside in and needs that time to reach full strength through its thickness. Using it early traps moisture behind a half-cured seal and undoes the whole point of the job. If you only have the one bathroom, say so when you book and I will time the visit around it.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you just caulk over the old caulking to save time?\",\"a\":\"No, and I would not charge you for it. New silicone does not bond to cured silicone, so a fresh bead laid over an old one is only stuck at its edges and will peel away in strips within months. Worse, it seals mould and moisture in behind it. Full removal takes longer on the day and is the only version of this job worth paying for.\"},{\"q\":\"Black spots keep returning in the corner of my shower. Why?\",\"a\":\"Usually because water is sitting behind the bead rather than on it. Once a seal has split anywhere along its length, water wicks in and the mould grows on the hidden face, so surface cleaners never reach it. Poor ventilation makes it faster. Cutting the old bead out, drying the joint, resealing with a mildew-resistant silicone and running the fan longer normally settles it.\"},{\"q\":\"There is water under my vanity. Can you find where it comes from?\",\"a\":\"Usually yes. Most vanity leaks are at the trap connections, the supply line nuts, the shut-off valves or the sink drain flange, and those I can dry, test and repair. If the leak turns out to be inside the wall or at the drain rough-in, that goes to a licensed plumber and I will tell you before running up any more time on it.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you have to regrout the whole shower or only the bad sections?\",\"a\":\"It depends on how the existing grout looks. Patching a few failed joints is fine when the rest is sound and the colour still matches, and that is the cheaper route. Where grout is powdery across a whole wall or stained beyond cleaning, patching just leaves an obvious repair, so the full wall makes more sense. I will show you what I am seeing and let you decide.\"}]','faucet-installation,drywall-repair,backsplash-tile-installation,condo-handyman,rental-property-maintenance','',1,10,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (11,'baseboard-trim-installation','Baseboard and Trim Installation','Baseboard and Trim Installation in Toronto','fa-solid fa-ruler-combined','baseboard installation toronto, trim carpentry gta, crown moulding installation toronto, quarter round installation, replace baseboards toronto, door casing installation gta, chair rail installation, baseboard installer near me',0,'Baseboard and Trim Installation GTA','Baseboard and trim installation across Toronto and the GTA. Coped corners, scribed to uneven floors, filled and caulked ready for paint. Ask me for a quote.','Baseboard and trim installation is the detail nobody mentions when it is right and everybody notices when it is wrong. Gappy corners, wavy lines along the floor, filler shrinking back out of nail holes. I fit baseboard, quarter round, casing, crown moulding and chair rail across the GTA, and the finished job is judged from two metres away in ordinary daylight, which is exactly how I check my own work before I pack up.\n\nNearly every room needs more fitting than cutting. Floors dip, walls bow and corners are almost never square, so the trim gets worked to suit the house instead of pretending the house is flat.','<h2>Choosing a trim material</h2>\n<p>Most of the baseboard going into GTA homes is primed MDF, and for a dry living room or bedroom it is a sensible choice. It comes dead straight, it takes paint beautifully, and profiles run consistent from length to length. It also swells and crumbles once it gets wet, so I keep it out of bathrooms, laundry rooms and basements.</p>\n<p>Primed finger-joint pine costs more, dents more easily than people expect, and survives damp far better. Solid poplar or oak suits stain-grade work next to hardwood. PVC trim earns its money in a basement or beside a shower wall where moisture is a certainty rather than a risk.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Primed MDF for dry rooms and painted finishes</li>\n<li>Finger-joint pine where knocks and damp are likely</li>\n<li>Poplar or oak for stain-grade rooms</li>\n<li>PVC trim for basements, laundry and bathroom walls</li>\n<li>Quarter round or shoe moulding to close a floor gap</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Coped inside corners, mitred outside corners</h2>\n<p>Here is the single thing that separates trim staying tight from trim opening up over a winter: inside corners should be coped, not mitred. Two forty-five degree cuts only meet cleanly if the corner is exactly ninety degrees, and almost no corner in a real house is. <strong>A coped joint has one piece running square into the corner and the second cut to the profile of the first, so it sits tight on an out-of-square wall and stays tight as the material moves.</strong></p>\n<p>Outside corners are mitred, glued and pinned, usually cut a shade past forty-five degrees so the face closes first and the joint disappears. Long walls get a scarf joint, two opposing angled cuts overlapping over a stud, rather than a square butt joint that will show a dark shadow line forever.</p>\n<h2>Baseboard and trim installation in older Toronto houses</h2>\n<p>Nothing in an old house is straight. Floors sag towards the middle of the room, plaster walls bow between studs, and corners are rounded. Baseboard and trim installation in those rooms is mostly patient fitting. Scribing the bottom edge to follow a wavy floor, planing off the high spots, packing behind the trim where a wall dishes inward, and shimming around old plaster build-up.</p>\n<p>Fastening matters as much as fitting. I nail into the studs and into the bottom plate, two fixings at each stud, with 18 gauge brads on delicate profiles and 16 gauge finish nails where the material is heavy. Glue goes on the mitres and copes, not along the wall, so a length can be removed later without tearing the drywall paper off with it.</p>\n<h2>Casing, crown and chair rail</h2>\n<p>Door and window casing lives or dies on the reveal, the consistent strip of jamb left showing inside the casing, usually a shade under 5 mm (3/16 in). I mark that with a gauge instead of eyeballing it, because an uneven reveal is visible from across a room even to people who cannot say what is bothering them.</p>\n<p>Crown moulding is a different exercise again. It sits at a spring angle off both the wall and the ceiling, so cuts get made either nested against the saw fence at that angle or laid flat with compound settings. Inside corners are coped here too. Where a ceiling waves, the crown gets eased into the curve and the gap closed with caulk rather than fought with force.</p>\n<h2>Filling, caulking and paint</h2>\n<p>Trim is not finished until the fixings vanish. Nail holes get filled and sanded flush, joints take a touch of filler and a light sand, and the top edge against the wall gets a thin bead of paintable caulk to close the shadow line. Stain-grade work uses coloured wax or a matched filler instead, because caulk has no place on a clear finish.</p>\n<p>Painting can happen before or after fitting and I will talk you through which suits your room. Pre-painting the lengths and touching up the joints afterwards is quicker and gives a cleaner line against a finished wall. Either route, the offcuts, the dust and the old trim leave with me at the end.</p>','New baseboard supplied, cut and fitted\nOld baseboard removed without wrecking drywall\nQuarter round and shoe moulding fitted\nDoor and window casing replaced\nCrown moulding cut and installed\nChair rail and picture rail fitted\nCoped inside corners and mitred outside corners\nNail holes filled, sanded and caulked\nTrim primed and painted on request','[{\"title\":\"Measure and choose profile\",\"text\":\"I measure every wall, check how far the floors run out, and go through profile heights and materials with you. Taller baseboard suits high ceilings and hides a wavy floor better, which matters more in older rooms than most people expect.\"},{\"title\":\"Strip out the old\",\"text\":\"Existing trim comes off after the caulk line is scored with a knife, so the paint and drywall paper stay on the wall instead of peeling away with the board. Old nails get pulled and any damage behind gets patched.\"},{\"title\":\"Cut, cope and fit\",\"text\":\"Lengths are cut long, coped at inside corners, mitred at outside corners and scribed to the floor where needed. Each piece gets dry fitted before a single nail goes in, and long walls are joined with scarf cuts over studs.\"},{\"title\":\"Fill, caulk, finish\",\"text\":\"Nail holes and joints get filled and sanded flush, the top edge gets a fine bead of paintable caulk, and I paint or touch up if that is part of the job. Offcuts and dust go out with me.\"}]','Trim work is priced by the run and by how difficult the room is. A square modern room with flat walls and a straight floor moves quickly. An older room with bowed plaster, a sagging floor and a lot of corners takes far longer for the same length of material, because every piece needs scribing. Stripping out old trim, patching what it hides, tall or complex profiles, crown moulding at height and painting all add time. Materials can come from me with receipts or from you. Either way, the price is agreed before I start cutting.','[{\"q\":\"Can you install baseboard without lifting my flooring?\",\"a\":\"Yes, that is the usual order of things. Baseboard sits on top of a finished floor, with a small gap left underneath on a floating floor so it can expand. Where the floor waves and leaves gaps under the board, quarter round or shoe moulding closes them neatly. If the floor is being replaced too, it makes sense to fit the flooring first and the trim after.\"},{\"q\":\"Is it better to paint the trim before or after it goes up?\",\"a\":\"Both work. Painting lengths before they go on is faster and avoids cutting a brush line against your wall, so I prime and coat them flat, fit them, then fill and touch up the nail holes and joints. Painting after fitting gives the smoothest single finish but needs careful masking. I will suggest whichever fits your room and how soon you need it back.\"},{\"q\":\"My floors are wavy and there are gaps under the existing baseboard. What can be done?\",\"a\":\"Two options, often used together. The bottom edge of the baseboard can be scribed, meaning it is marked to the floor contour and planed or sanded to match, which gives the cleanest result. Or shoe moulding is run along the bottom, and because it is thin it flexes down into the dips. Deep hollows sometimes need both plus a little caulk.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you match trim in an old house when the profile is no longer sold?\",\"a\":\"Often, yes, to a point. Some heritage profiles are still stocked by specialist mouldings suppliers around Toronto, and others can be built up by combining two simpler profiles that together read very close to the original. Where an exact match is impossible, matching within one room is what actually matters visually, so I would trim the whole room rather than patch it.\"},{\"q\":\"Is MDF baseboard a bad choice?\",\"a\":\"Not at all, in the right room. MDF is straight, stable, cheap to profile and takes paint better than most softwood, which is why it dominates new construction. Its weakness is water. A burst supply line, a leaking dishwasher or a damp basement wall turns the bottom edge into pulp. For bathrooms, laundry rooms and basements I would rather fit pine or PVC.\"}]','interior-painting,flooring-installation,door-repair,drywall-repair,handyman-services','',1,11,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (12,'flooring-installation','Laminate and Vinyl Plank Flooring','Laminate and Vinyl Plank Flooring in Toronto','fa-solid fa-layer-group','laminate flooring installation toronto, vinyl plank flooring installer gta, luxury vinyl plank toronto, click lock flooring installation, floor installer near me toronto, basement vinyl plank flooring, laminate floor repair toronto, squeaky floor repair gta',0,'Laminate and Vinyl Plank Flooring GTA','Laminate and vinyl plank flooring fitted across Toronto and the GTA. Subfloor prep, correct expansion gaps, neat transitions. Ask me for a free quote today.','Laminate and vinyl plank flooring is the quickest way to change how a room feels, and I fit it across Toronto and the wider GTA. Click-lock planks go over most existing subfloors in a day or two per room, with no adhesive smell and no waiting around before you can walk on it. The catch is that a floating floor copies whatever sits underneath it, so preparation is where the quality actually lives.\n\nMy price covers the whole job rather than the easy part of it. Lifting the old floor, flattening and screwing down the subfloor, underlay, planks, undercut casings, transitions, and the baseboard or shoe moulding back on at the end.','<h2>The subfloor decides how the floor turns out</h2>\n<p>Click-lock floors float. They are not fastened down, so every hump and hollow underneath is transmitted up through the planks as bounce, as a click underfoot, and eventually as separated joints where the locking edges have worked themselves loose. Most manufacturers ask for flatness within roughly 3 mm over a 1.8 m span, about an eighth of an inch over six feet, and that tolerance is the line between a floor that lasts and a warranty claim that gets refused.</p>\n<p>So the first job is always the floor you already have. High spots in plywood get sanded or planed off, hollows get filled with a levelling compound, loose sheets get screwed back into the joists with proper subfloor screws, and squeaks get chased down while everything is stripped and easy to reach.</p>\n<h2>Choosing between laminate and vinyl plank flooring</h2>\n<p>The first of the pair is a printed layer over a fibreboard core. Laminate looks sharp, resists scratching well and suits bedrooms, hallways and living rooms above grade. Water is its weakness. A spill left sitting at a joint swells the core, and there is no undoing that once it happens.</p>\n<p>Luxury vinyl plank is plastic the whole way through, so it shrugs water off. That makes it the sensible pick for basements, kitchens, laundry rooms and rental units where spills get reported late. It is softer underfoot and marks more easily under heavy furniture legs, so felt pads matter. For a below-grade slab anywhere in the GTA, I will nearly always steer you towards vinyl plank.</p>\n<h2>How the floor goes down</h2>\n<p>Boxes come into the room and sit for a couple of days first, so the material settles to the temperature and humidity it will live in. Over a concrete slab the build-up needs a vapour barrier, either a poly sheet or an underlay with one built into it. Over plywood, an underlay suited to the product and to how quiet you want the room.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Expansion gap of roughly 8 to 10 mm at every wall and fixed object</li>\n<li>End joints staggered by at least 300 mm (12 in) between rows</li>\n<li>Planks drawn tight with a tapping block, never struck on the edge</li>\n<li>Door casings undercut so planks slide underneath them</li>\n<li>Rows planned so the final one is not a thin sliver</li>\n<li>Underlay seams taped rather than left open</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That expansion gap is not a detail to skip. Vinyl in particular moves with temperature, and a floor pinned tight against the walls will lift into a ridge somewhere in the middle of the room. The gap disappears under the baseboard or the shoe moulding, so nothing shows.</p>\n<h2>Doors, transitions and the finishing details</h2>\n<p>Undercutting the door casings and jambs is what makes a floor look installed rather than laid down. I cut them with an oscillating tool using a scrap of plank and underlay as the height guide, so the flooring runs underneath the trim with no visible gap and none of the ugly notching you see when someone has cut the plank around the moulding instead.</p>\n<p>Transitions get chosen for the situation. A T-mould where two floating floors of equal height meet, a reducer stepping down to tile or sheet vinyl, an end cap at a sliding door. Interior doors that now catch on a thicker floor come off and get trimmed at the bottom. Appliances go back, trim goes back on, and the room is usable as soon as I finish.</p>\n<h2>Condos, repairs and things worth knowing</h2>\n<p>Many Toronto condo declarations set a minimum acoustic rating for underlay before any hard surface floor can be installed, and property management will want the specification before they approve the work. I fit to whatever your building requires rather than find out about it afterwards.</p>\n<p>One damaged plank in the middle of a room can usually be swapped without redoing the floor, either by unclicking rows back from the nearest wall or by cutting the board out and gluing in a replacement with its lower tongue trimmed off. <strong>If the existing floor is old vinyl tile from before the nineties, I do not sand it or break it up. That vintage can contain asbestos, so it either stays undisturbed under the new floor or goes to an abatement contractor.</strong></p>','Old flooring lifted and hauled away\nSubfloor flattened, screwed down and squeaks fixed\nUnderlay and vapour barrier laid\nClick-lock laminate installed room by room\nLuxury vinyl plank fitted in basements and kitchens\nDoor casings and jambs undercut\nT-moulds, reducers and thresholds fitted\nInterior doors trimmed to clear the new floor\nDamaged planks replaced in an existing floor','[{\"title\":\"Measure and plan\",\"text\":\"I measure the rooms, check the subfloor for flatness and moisture, and work out the plank direction, the starting row and where transitions land. That tells you the real material quantity, including the waste factor for cuts.\"},{\"title\":\"Acclimatise and prep\",\"text\":\"Material sits in the room for a couple of days. Meanwhile the old floor comes up, the subfloor gets levelled and refastened, squeaks are dealt with, and casings are undercut so nothing has to be notched around later.\"},{\"title\":\"Lay the floor\",\"text\":\"Underlay goes down with taped seams, then planks run with staggered end joints, spacers holding the expansion gap at every wall, and each row tapped home with a block. Cuts around pipes and corners are templated first.\"},{\"title\":\"Trim, transitions, cleanup\",\"text\":\"Spacers come out, baseboard or shoe moulding goes back on, transitions and thresholds are fitted, and doors are trimmed if they now catch. Furniture goes back, offcuts and packaging leave with me, and the floor gets swept.\"}]','Floor pricing follows the square footage, but the room decides how fast that footage goes down. Long open runs are quick. Small rooms full of corners, closets, pipes and door openings are slow, because almost every plank needs a cut. Ripping out old carpet or tile, levelling a bad subfloor, chasing squeaks, replacing damaged plywood and hauling away debris all add to the day. Whether you supply the flooring and underlay or I bring it with receipts changes the total as well. You get the price and the scope agreed before anything is lifted.','[{\"q\":\"Can the new floor go straight over my existing tile or vinyl?\",\"a\":\"Often it can, if the existing surface is sound, firmly stuck down and flat enough. Loose tiles have to come out and grout lines deeper than a couple of millimetres usually need filling first, otherwise the joint pattern telegraphs through as a hollow spot. Carpet always comes up, underlay and all. I check the floor before quoting so you know which way it is going.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you move the furniture and take the old floor away?\",\"a\":\"Yes to both, and I include it in the quote so there is no surprise. Furniture gets shifted room by room, or into an adjoining space if the layout allows. Old carpet, underlay, tack strips and staples come out, and I bag and remove the debris. Very heavy items like a piano or a full aquarium need specialists, and I will say so upfront.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does a floating floor need a gap around the edges?\",\"a\":\"Because the planks expand and contract with temperature and humidity as a single connected sheet. Toronto swings from dry winter heating to humid summers, and a floor with nowhere to move pushes against the walls until it buckles into a peak, usually somewhere in the middle of the largest run. The gap sits hidden under the baseboard or shoe moulding, so you never see it.\"},{\"q\":\"One plank is chipped in the middle of the room. Can it be replaced?\",\"a\":\"Usually yes, without redoing the floor. If the damaged board is a short distance from a wall, the quickest route is lifting the baseboard and unclicking rows back to it. Further into the room, the board gets cut out in place and a replacement glued in with its lower tongue trimmed away. Keep any leftover planks from the original job, because dye lots change.\"},{\"q\":\"How long does a room take, and can I walk on it right away?\",\"a\":\"An average bedroom is often a single day including prep, with larger open areas running into a second. Because these floors are mechanical rather than glued, you can walk on them as soon as the last plank is in and put furniture back straight away. The bigger variable is what the subfloor needs, and levelling compound wants time to cure before anything goes over it.\"}]','baseboard-trim-installation,door-repair,drywall-repair,interior-painting,condo-handyman','',1,12,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (13,'backsplash-tile-installation','Kitchen Backsplash and Tile','Kitchen Backsplash Tile Installation in Toronto','fa-solid fa-border-all','backsplash installation toronto, kitchen backsplash tile installer, subway tile backsplash toronto, peel and stick backsplash installation, backsplash tile repair, tile backsplash gta, kitchen tile installer near me, regrouting a backsplash toronto',0,'Backsplash Tile Installation in Toronto','Kitchen backsplash tile installation across the Greater Toronto Area. Subway, mosaic and glass tile set level and grouted right. Ask for a free quote.','Kitchen backsplash tile installation is one of the quickest ways to change how a kitchen feels, and preparation matters more than the tile itself. I am Sercan, and I have worked as a handyman across the Greater Toronto Area for more than ten years. A backsplash is a small area with a lot of edges: outlets, window returns, the underside of the upper cabinets, and the seam where the counter meets the wall. Those edges are what people look at.\n\nPrep is where the time goes. Walls get cleaned and checked for flat before any adhesive, cuts around outlets are measured rather than guessed, and grout lines stay even from the first row to the last. Send a photo with your quote request and I can price it quickly.','<h2>What kitchen backsplash tile installation involves</h2><p>Backsplashes look simple and are full of small decisions. The tile has to sit level even when the counter is not, the cut along the underside of the upper cabinets has to be straight, and the run has to end in something deliberate. Most kitchens in the Greater Toronto Area have at least one awkward feature: a window return, a bulkhead, a range wall that goes full height, or an outlet sitting exactly where a grout line wants to land.</p><p>My part runs from the bare wall to the finished grout. That covers taking off an old backsplash, patching what comes away with it, setting the new tile, cutting around outlets and switches, grouting, and running a soft joint where the tile meets the counter.</p><h2>Tile I work with and how each one behaves</h2><p>Ceramic subway tile is still the most requested and it is forgiving. Porcelain is harder and wants a wet saw with a diamond blade rather than a score-and-snap cutter. Glass tile is the fussiest of the three: it shows the adhesive through the body, so it gets white thinset instead of grey, and it chips badly if it is cut with the wrong blade. Mosaic sheets look easy and are not, because each sheet has to be set so the joint between sheets matches the joints inside them. Get that wrong on the second sheet and the whole grid drifts.</p><p>Peel-and-stick has a place. On a smooth, clean, fully cured painted wall in a rental or a quick refresh it can look fine. It is not for the wall directly behind a range, it will not sit flat over texture, and taking it off later can pull paint or paper with it. I will tell you honestly whether your wall suits it.</p><h2>The difference between a good and a bad job</h2><p>Bad backsplashes fail in the same handful of places. Avoiding them is mostly discipline.</p><ul><li>Degrease the wall properly, then scuff any glossy paint so the adhesive can grip</li><li>Snap a level line and work to it, because counters and cabinets are rarely square to each other</li><li>Dry-lay the first run so the cut tiles at each end are balanced instead of a sliver at one side</li><li>Use the right notched trowel and back-butter where needed for full contact behind the tile</li><li>Hold the joints with spacers rather than judging them by eye</li><li>Let the adhesive cure before grouting instead of rushing both into one visit</li></ul><p>One more rule matters more than people expect: flexible silicone, not grout, goes where the tile meets the countertop and into the inside corners. A counter moves slightly against the wall with temperature and weight. Grout is rigid, so it cracks along that seam within a season. A colour-matched silicone joint flexes and stays intact.</p><h2>Outlets, edges and the details you actually see</h2><p>Outlets are where sloppy work shows. Tile is cut to the box, not to the cover plate, so the plate covers the cut cleanly. Once the tile adds thickness, the electrical box normally needs an extender ring and longer device screws so the receptacle sits at the finished surface. I remove and refit cover plates with the breaker off. If the wiring is loose, the box is damaged, or you want an outlet added or moved, that is work for a licensed electrician and I will say so rather than open it up.</p><p>Ends need a plan too. Metal edge trim set into the adhesive gives a crisp finish on an open end, bullnose tile suits a more traditional kitchen, and a run that dies into a return wall can simply be caulked. Deciding this before the first tile goes on is what makes the last tile look deliberate.</p><h2>Booking backsplash tile work in Toronto</h2><p>Send a photo of the kitchen and the rough length of the counter run and I can usually price backsplash tile installation from that. Tell me if the tile is already bought and which one it is, because glass and natural stone change both the materials and the time. Replies normally come back within one business day, and the quote is free with nothing owed if you decide against it.</p><p>Small tile repairs are welcome as well. Cracked tiles, a section that has come loose, tired grout that needs raking out and replacing: those batch neatly with other jobs around the house so one visit covers several things.</p>','Subway tile backsplash over a counter run\nMosaic sheet backsplash set square and even\nPeel-and-stick backsplash on prepared smooth walls\nTile cut around outlets and switch boxes\nMetal edge trim or bullnose end finishing\nOld backsplash removal and wall repair\nTired grout raked out and replaced\nSilicone soft joint at the counter seam\nCracked or loose tiles replaced','[{\"title\":\"Photos and measurements\",\"text\":\"Send a photo of the kitchen and the length of the counter run. I confirm the tile, the wall condition and any awkward areas, then give you a fixed price or an hourly rate before anything is booked.\"},{\"title\":\"Wall prep and layout\",\"text\":\"Counters and appliances get covered, the wall is degreased and sanded where it is glossy, and old tile comes off if there is any. Then I mark level lines and dry-lay the first row to balance the end cuts.\"},{\"title\":\"Setting the tile\",\"text\":\"Adhesive goes on with the correct notched trowel, tiles are pressed and checked for full contact, and every cut around outlets, corners and the range wall is made on a wet saw. Spacers hold the joints while it sets.\"},{\"title\":\"Grout, silicone and clean-up\",\"text\":\"Once the adhesive has cured I grout, sponge the joints and clear the haze. The counter seam and inside corners get colour-matched silicone. Cover plates go back on, the kitchen is wiped down, and offcuts leave with me.\"}]','What a backsplash costs depends mostly on the wall behind it and the tile in front of it. A clean, flat, painted wall over a straight counter run is quick. Removing an old backsplash, patching the damage underneath, or tiling full height behind a range adds time. Mosaic sheets, glass and natural stone cut slower than plain ceramic, and every outlet, corner and edge trim is another set of cuts. Materials can be supplied with receipts, or you can buy your own. The price is agreed before I start.','[{\"q\":\"Can you tile over my existing backsplash?\",\"a\":\"Sometimes, though I usually advise against it. Tiling over old tile pushes the wall out past the electrical boxes and past the edge of the counter, and the new tile is only as well stuck as the old one underneath. If the existing tile is solid, flat and thin it can work. More often I take it off, repair the drywall paper that comes away with it, and start from a proper surface. I will look and tell you which one your kitchen needs.\"},{\"q\":\"How much tile should I order?\",\"a\":\"Measure the length of the run and the height from counter to cabinet, multiply, then add about ten percent for cuts. Add more for mosaic, herringbone or anything laid on an angle, because the offcuts are less usable. Keep a few spare tiles in the basement afterwards. Dye lots change between production runs, so a tile bought a year later rarely matches, and a spare box makes a future repair invisible.\"},{\"q\":\"How long will my kitchen be out of action?\",\"a\":\"Most single-run backsplashes are a two-visit job. The first visit is prep, layout, cutting and setting the tile. Adhesive then needs to cure before grout goes on, so I come back to grout, clear the haze and run the silicone joint. A small area, or peel-and-stick on a sound wall, can be finished in one visit. You get the schedule with the quote so nothing is a surprise.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you move outlets or add new ones while tiling?\",\"a\":\"No. Adding a circuit, moving a box or running new cable is licensed electrical work in Ontario, and I refer it to a licensed electrician. What I do is work around the existing outlets: breaker off, cover plates removed, tile cut to the box, box extenders fitted so the device sits at the new surface, then the plates back on. If your kitchen needs electrical changes, have them done before the tile goes up.\"},{\"q\":\"Does the grout need sealing?\",\"a\":\"Cement based grout is porous, so a penetrating sealer is worth applying once it has fully cured, usually a few days after grouting. It does not make grout waterproof. What it buys you is time to wipe a splash off before it stains. Epoxy grout needs no sealer at all, though it is less forgiving to install and slower to work with. Either way, the counter seam and inside corners stay in silicone so they can move.\"}]','cabinet-repair,interior-painting,faucet-installation,light-fixture-installation,handyman-services','',1,13,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (14,'cabinet-repair','Cabinet Repair and Hardware','Cabinet Repair and Hardware Fitting in Toronto','fa-solid fa-kitchen-set','cabinet repair toronto, kitchen cabinet door adjustment, replace cabinet hinges toronto, drawer slide replacement, soft close hinges installation, cabinet handle installation gta, kitchen cabinet repair near me, vanity door repair toronto',0,'Cabinet Repair and Hardware in Toronto','Cabinet repair across the Greater Toronto Area. Doors realigned, hinges and drawer slides replaced, knobs and pulls fitted straight. Ask me for a free quote.','Cabinet repair is a much smaller job than replacing a kitchen, and most of the faults people live with for years are minor. Doors that will not line up, a drawer that scrapes, a hinge screw spinning in soft particleboard, a toe kick swollen at the dishwasher. I am Sercan, and I sort these out across the Greater Toronto Area, often in a single visit.\n\nHardware is the other half of this work. Swapping tired knobs for pulls, adding soft-close so doors stop banging, or replacing worn drawer slides changes how a kitchen feels without touching the boxes. Every hole gets drilled from a template so the run lines up, because crooked hardware is more noticeable than old hardware. Quotes are free and the price is agreed first.','<h2>Doors that will not line up</h2><p>Nine out of ten misaligned doors are a hinge adjustment, not a broken cabinet. Modern frameless kitchens use concealed cup hinges that adjust three ways: side to side for the gap between doors, in and out for how the door sits against the box, and up and down at the mounting plate. A quarter turn on the right screw, worked along the run, brings a whole kitchen back into line. Older face-frame cabinets have less adjustment built in, so they sometimes need the mounting holes plugged and re-drilled instead.</p><p>The other common cause is a screw that no longer holds. Cabinet sides are often particleboard, and once a hinge plate has been overtightened the screw simply spins in a soft hole. Toothpicks and glue are a temporary trick that lasts a few months. The proper fix is to drill the hole out, glue in a hardwood dowel, let it set, then re-drill a fresh pilot so the screw bites into solid wood again.</p><h2>Drawers that scrape, stick or drop</h2><p>Drawer trouble is usually the slides. Older kitchens around Toronto still have epoxy-coated roller slides that wear a groove and start to sag; newer ones use ball-bearing side mounts or undermount slides hidden below the box. Each type has its own rules. Side mounts need a set clearance on each side of the drawer box, so a like-for-like swap is straightforward. Undermounts need the box built to suit them, with notches cut at the back, which means moving between the two systems changes the drawer box, not just the slide.</p><p>Boxes fail as well. Bottoms sag out of their grooves, corner joints open up, and screws pull out of the back of a drawer front until the front hangs loose. Re-gluing and clamping a box, refitting a bottom panel or fixing a front back on properly is quicker than ordering a replacement drawer that may not match the rest of the kitchen.</p><h2>Knobs, pulls and getting the holes right</h2><p>Changing handles looks like the easiest job in a kitchen and it is the one that goes wrong most, because every hole is permanent. I mark from a hardware jig so knobs sit the same distance from the edge on every door and pulls line up across a run of drawers. Each hole is drilled with a backer block clamped behind the face so the bit cannot blow out the inside of the door.</p><p>Two things are worth knowing before you buy. Pulls come in fixed hole spacings, so replacing existing pulls without new holes means matching the centre-to-centre measurement exactly. And going from a single knob to a two-hole pull leaves an old hole behind: on painted wood it can be filled and touched in, but on thermofoil or laminate doors it rarely disappears completely. I point that out before anything is drilled.</p><h2>Cabinet repair jobs I get called for most</h2><p>Kitchens, bathroom vanities, laundry rooms and built-in units across the Greater Toronto Area throw up the same short list.</p><ul><li>Realigning doors and evening out the gaps along a run</li><li>Replacing broken hinges and adding clip-on soft-close dampers</li><li>New drawer slides, including full-extension replacements</li><li>Fitting knobs and pulls from a drilling template</li><li>Repairing swollen toe kicks and water damaged sink bases</li><li>Re-drilling shelf pin holes and stopping shelves from sagging</li><li>Touching up chips, scratches and worn door edges</li><li>Easing doors that rub after a cabinet has settled</li></ul><h2>What can be saved and what cannot</h2><p>Most cabinets are worth repairing. Solid or plywood boxes with tired hardware come back to life with new hinges and slides, and the finish usually outlasts the moving parts. Particleboard that has been wet is different: it swells and never returns to shape, so a sink base floor that has gone soft gets cut out and replaced with plywood once the leak is dealt with. Thermofoil doors where the white film is lifting off the edges near the oven cannot be repaired invisibly and are better replaced.</p><p>Plumbing under the sink is often part of the story. Swapping a faucet, a supply line or a trap is straightforward and I can do it. If water is coming from inside the wall or the rough-in needs moving, that is a licensed plumber\'s job and I will tell you rather than guess. An honest answer about what is not worth fixing is worth as much as the repair itself.</p>','Cabinet doors realigned and gaps evened out\nBroken hinges replaced with matching soft-close hinges\nClip-on dampers added to existing hinges\nWorn drawer slides swapped for full-extension slides\nKnobs and pulls drilled from a template\nStripped hinge screw holes plugged and re-drilled\nWater damaged toe kicks and sink bases replaced\nShelf pin holes re-drilled and shelves supported\nChips, scratches and worn edges touched up','[{\"title\":\"Look and diagnose\",\"text\":\"I open and close every door and drawer, check the boxes for square and find what is actually failing. Some of it is adjustment, some is worn hardware, and the two need very different answers.\"},{\"title\":\"Hardware matched\",\"text\":\"Hinges, slides and handles vary by brand, overlay and hole spacing. I measure and photograph what you have, source the matching part or the closest equivalent, and confirm it with you before anything is ordered or drilled.\"},{\"title\":\"Fitted and adjusted\",\"text\":\"Parts go on, then everything gets tuned: even gaps down the run, doors closing without a bang, drawers running true. Anything I moved goes back, the floor is vacuumed, and I show you what changed.\"}]','Cabinet work is priced by how many pieces are involved and what condition they are in. A run of doors that only needs adjusting is quick. Replacing hinges or slides depends on whether the parts are still made, whether the screw holes are sound, and how easy the cabinet is to reach behind an appliance or under a sink. Handles are priced by the number of holes, not the number of doors. Hardware can come from me with receipts, or you can supply your own. You get the price first.','[{\"q\":\"My cabinet doors have gone crooked. Do they need replacing?\",\"a\":\"Almost never. Concealed hinges adjust in three directions, and most crooked doors come back into line with careful turns of the right screws along the whole run rather than one door at a time. If a hinge is bent or a screw hole has given way, the hinge gets replaced or the hole gets plugged with a hardwood dowel and re-drilled. Replacement doors only make sense when the door itself is damaged or delaminating.\"},{\"q\":\"Can soft-close be added to cabinets that did not come with it?\",\"a\":\"Usually yes. Many concealed hinges accept a clip-on damper that snaps onto the hinge arm in seconds, which is the simplest route when your hinges are a common brand. If they will not take a damper, I swap them for soft-close hinges matched to the same overlay and mounting plate so the doors sit exactly where they did. There are also small dampers that mount inside the cabinet for older hinge types.\"},{\"q\":\"The hinge screws just spin. Is the door finished?\",\"a\":\"Not at all, and this is one of the most satisfying small repairs there is. The screw has chewed out the particleboard around it, so tightening it harder only makes the hole bigger. I drill the damaged hole out cleanly, glue a hardwood dowel in, trim it flush and let the glue cure. A fresh pilot hole then gives the screw solid wood to bite into, and it holds better than the original.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you change my knobs to longer pulls?\",\"a\":\"Yes, with one thing to check first. A knob has a single hole and a pull needs two at a set centre-to-centre spacing, so new holes are drilled and the old one is left behind. On painted or stained wood I can fill and touch that in reasonably well. On thermofoil, laminate or high-gloss doors the patch tends to show, so it is worth choosing pulls whose spacing hides the old hole, or accepting the mark.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you refinish or repaint a whole kitchen?\",\"a\":\"I do touch-ups, edge repairs and small paint work as part of cabinet repair, and I can fit replacement doors and drawer fronts ordered to size. A full cabinet repaint is a separate job with its own steps: heavy degreasing, sanding, a bonding primer and a hard-wearing finish, done with the doors off and labelled. I quote that as painting work rather than folding it into a repair visit.\"}]','backsplash-tile-installation,interior-painting,faucet-installation,appliance-installation,handyman-services','',1,14,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (15,'deck-repair','Deck Repair and Restoration','Deck Repair and Restoration Across the GTA','fa-solid fa-hammer','deck repair toronto, deck board replacement, deck staining toronto, deck restoration gta, loose deck railing repair, deck sanding and sealing, replace rotten deck boards, deck repair near me',0,'Deck Repair and Restoration in Toronto','Deck repair and restoration across the Greater Toronto Area. Rotten boards, loose railings, wobbly stairs, cleaning and staining. Ask me for a free quote.','Deck repair is mostly about water and where it has been sitting. Boards cup and split, screws pop, a railing goes soft at the post, and the stairs start to move underfoot. Freeze, thaw, snow load and summer sun work on a deck all year. I am Sercan, and I repair and restore decks across the Greater Toronto Area.\n\nSome decks need three boards and a coat of stain. Others have reached the end and need a rebuild, and I will say so. Structural work, footings and ledger connections go to a licensed contractor rather than being patched over. What I do is the repair and restoration side: replacing what has failed, making the deck safe to use, and getting a finish on it that lasts.','<h2>Finding what has actually failed</h2><p>Every deck repair starts with a probe and an awl. Soft wood gives itself away, so I push into the joists, the post bases, the stair stringers and the area under any planter or hot tub. Most decks I see around Toronto are pressure-treated spruce built in the last twenty years, and they fail in predictable places. Rot lives wherever water sits and cannot dry, which is why the top edge of a joist under a gap between boards goes first.</p><p>Grey, splintery boards are not the same thing as rotten boards. Pressure-treated lumber weathers to grey on the surface and can still be perfectly sound underneath. That distinction decides whether your deck needs a wash and a stain or a serious amount of new lumber, so it is worth settling before you buy anything.</p><h2>Boards, fasteners and why the screws matter</h2><p>Cupped, split and cracked boards get replaced one at a time. A board that has curled into a shallow channel holds water and will keep rotting, and flipping it over is a short-lived trick because the underside has usually been sitting damp for years. New boards go down with two screws into every joist, pre-drilled near the ends so the wood does not split.</p><p>Fasteners are where a lot of deck repairs quietly go wrong.</p><ul><li>Modern pressure-treated lumber corrodes plain and electro-galvanized steel, so screws must be hot-dipped galvanized, stainless or coated for treated wood</li><li>Popped nails get pulled and replaced with screws, not hammered back down, because they will lift again next season</li><li>Board gaps depend on moisture: wet lumber goes down close because it shrinks, dry lumber gets a small gap</li><li>Old fastener holes are avoided so the new screw bites fresh wood</li><li>Joist tops that are already chewed up can take a self-adhesive flashing tape to slow the next round of rot</li></ul><h2>Railings and stairs</h2><p>A railing you can rock is the most common safety complaint I get, and it is nearly always the post connection rather than the railing. Posts fixed with screws or nails through the rim board work loose within a few seasons. A sound connection uses bolts through the rim with solid blocking behind it, or hardware made for that purpose. Guards have rules as well: once a deck surface sits more than about 600 mm (two feet) above the ground a guard is required, and its openings must be small enough that a child cannot slip through.</p><p>Stairs move for two reasons. Either the stringers are not properly hung from the deck, or the bottom of the flight sits on soil or a paver that has sunk. Fixing the top and ignoring the base just relocates the problem. I set the bottom on something solid and level, then secure the stringers with hangers so the flight stops flexing.</p><h2>Cleaning, sanding and staining</h2><p>Restoration is the part people underestimate. Stain will not hold on a dirty or damp deck, and it will not hold on a deck that has been pressure washed too hard. High pressure tears the soft grain out of softwood and leaves a fuzzy surface that takes stain unevenly. The order of work keeps that from happening.</p><ul><li>Wash with a proper deck cleaner and a wide fan tip held well back from the boards</li><li>Let the wood dry through several dry days rather than overnight</li><li>Sand the raised grain and rough patches with a coarse grit, then sweep and vacuum</li><li>Check the wood drinks water in rather than beading it before any tin is opened</li><li>Apply penetrating stain out of direct sun, working each board its full length so there are no lap marks</li></ul><p>New pressure-treated lumber is a special case. It leaves the yard wet and has to weather and dry before it will accept a finish, so a freshly boarded section is often stained on a later visit. Solid, film-forming stains hide more but peel eventually and then need stripping. Semi-transparent penetrating stains simply wear thin, which makes the next recoat far easier.</p><h2>Where repair stops and a rebuild starts</h2><p>Not every deck is worth saving, and pretending otherwise helps nobody. If the ledger board where the deck meets the house is loose or was never flashed, if posts have shifted because the footings have heaved, or if joists are soft along their length, that is structural work for a licensed contractor and it may need a permit. I will show you photos of what I find so you can make the call.</p><p>Plenty of decks across the Greater Toronto Area sit in the middle ground: a solid frame carrying tired boards. Those come back beautifully for a fraction of the work of a rebuild, and that is the job I enjoy most.</p>','Rotten or cupped deck boards replaced\nPopped nails pulled and replaced with screws\nLoose railing posts bolted and blocked\nWobbly stairs re-hung and the base levelled\nDeck washed, sanded and stained or sealed\nSplit boards and raised splinters made safe\nFailed screws swapped for rated deck fasteners\nDeck gate and handrail repairs\nComposite decking cleaned and boards refastened','[{\"title\":\"Walk the deck\",\"text\":\"I probe the boards, joists, posts and stair stringers, note what is soft and what is only weathered, and photograph anything that concerns me. You get a clear list of what needs doing now and what can wait.\"},{\"title\":\"Lumber and hardware\",\"text\":\"Boards are matched for width and thickness to what is already down, and fasteners are chosen to suit treated lumber. I can supply the materials with receipts, or you can have them delivered and I work with what arrives.\"},{\"title\":\"Repairs first\",\"text\":\"Failed boards come out, railings and stair connections are made solid, and every loose fastener is dealt with before any finish is considered. There is no point staining over a board that is coming out next season.\"},{\"title\":\"Clean, dry, finish\",\"text\":\"The deck is washed and left to dry properly, then sanded where the grain has lifted and stained or sealed. Furniture goes back, offcuts and old boards leave with me, and the yard is swept.\"}]','Deck pricing follows the amount of lumber coming out and going back in. A handful of boards on an easy, ground level deck is a small job. A raised deck with tight access underneath, boards fastened in awkward places, railings that need rebuilding, or a full wash and stain all add hours. Weather plays a part too, because staining waits for dry wood and a dry forecast. Lumber and stain can be supplied with receipts or bought by you. Either way, a fixed price or an hourly rate is agreed before the first board comes up.','[{\"q\":\"Can you replace just the boards and keep the frame?\",\"a\":\"Often yes, and it is the repair that changes a deck most. If the joists, beams and posts probe solid, new decking can go straight back onto the existing frame. I check the joist tops first, because that is where water collects between boards. Where the tops are soft but the joists are still strong, flashing tape over them gives the new screws sound wood to sit in and slows the next round of rot.\"},{\"q\":\"How do I tell rot from normal weathering?\",\"a\":\"Push a screwdriver or an awl into the wood. Weathered lumber is grey and rough on the surface but stays hard, and the point will not sink in. Rot feels spongy and the point goes in easily, often taking a crumbly plug with it. Check the areas that stay damp: the bottom of posts, the ends of joists, under planters and the base of stair stringers. Colour alone tells you very little.\"},{\"q\":\"How soon can a new pressure-treated deck be stained?\",\"a\":\"Longer than most people hope. Treated lumber arrives from the yard saturated with preservative solution, and stain cannot soak into wood that is already full of moisture. It normally needs weeks of dry weather, sometimes a full season, before it is ready. The test is simple: sprinkle water on the boards, and if it soaks in within a minute or so the deck is ready. If it beads, wait longer.\"},{\"q\":\"My railing wobbles. Is that a quick fix?\",\"a\":\"Sometimes, and I will not pretend otherwise if it is not. If the post is sound and only the fasteners have loosened, bolting it through the rim board with blocking behind makes it firm again within the hour. If the post itself is rotten at the base, or the rim board it is bolted to has gone soft, the repair is bigger and it becomes a safety matter rather than a nuisance.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you work on composite decking?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Composite boards do not rot or take stain, so the work is different: cleaning off mould and algae, replacing damaged boards, and refastening boards that have lifted. Hidden fastener systems vary by brand, so I need to know the make, or a photo of an exposed clip, before I can source parts. The frame underneath is still ordinary lumber, and that gets checked the same way as any other deck.\"}]','fence-gate-repair,pressure-washing,eavestrough-cleaning,handyman-services','',1,15,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (16,'fence-gate-repair','Fence and Gate Repair','Fence and Gate Repair in the Greater Toronto Area','fa-solid fa-torii-gate','fence repair toronto, gate repair toronto, sagging gate fix, fence post replacement gta, wood fence board replacement, fence gate hardware installation, chain link fence repair toronto, fence repair near me',0,'Fence and Gate Repair in Toronto','Fence and gate repair across the Greater Toronto Area. Sagging gates rehung, rotted boards and rails replaced, loose posts sorted. Free quote, no obligation.','Fence and gate repair is one of those jobs that gets put off until the gate will not close. Gates take the most abuse on any fence: they hang off one post, swing several times a day and catch the wind. Add ten Ontario winters and something has to give. I am Sercan, and I repair fences and gates across the Greater Toronto Area.\n\nMost of what I see is fixable without replacing a whole fence. A sagging gate can be squared and rehung, rotted boards swapped, a leaning post reset, and hardware upgraded to something that will hold. Where a fence has gone past repair I will tell you straight, and I will be honest about property lines and by-laws before any post hole gets dug.','<h2>Why gates sag and how that gets fixed</h2><p>A dragging gate has two possible causes and they need opposite treatments. Either the gate frame has racked out of square, or the post it hangs from has leaned. Before touching anything I lift the latch side and watch what moves. If the gate squares up in my hands, the frame is the problem. If the post travels with it, the post is.</p><p>Racked frames get pulled back square and held there. A tension cable with a turnbuckle has to run from the top corner on the hinge side down to the bottom corner on the latch side, so that tightening it lifts the latch side. Fitted the other way round it makes the sag worse, and that is the single most common mistake I find on gates people have tried to fix themselves. A rigid wooden brace works in the opposite direction: it runs from the bottom of the hinge side up towards the latch side and holds the gate in compression.</p><p>Hinges come next. Screws driven into the end grain of a post pull out over time, and small hinges on a heavy privacy gate were never going to last a decade. Heavier strap or bolt-through hinges, fixed with carriage bolts where the post allows it, carry the load properly instead of hanging on threads.</p><h2>Posts decide everything else</h2><p>Posts rot at ground level, where the wood is damp, warm and still getting air. Even pressure-treated posts give out there eventually, and a post set in a concrete collar that dishes inwards fails sooner because the collar holds water against the wood. Once a post has gone, no amount of work on the boards will keep that section of fence standing.</p><p>Replacing a post means digging out the old one and its concrete, setting the new post deep enough to sit below the frost line, and backfilling with either compacted gravel or concrete crowned above grade so water runs away from the wood. Where only the buried section has failed and the rest of the post is sound, a galvanized post spur bolted to the good timber is a lighter repair that holds for years. I will tell you which one your fence deserves rather than defaulting to the bigger job. Before any hole is dug I have the underground services located through Ontario One Call, which is free and required by law.</p><h2>Boards, rails and hardware</h2><p>The rest of a fence is straightforward carpentry done with the right materials.</p><ul><li>Rotted or cracked boards replaced and matched as closely as the lumber yard allows</li><li>Board bottoms kept clear of the soil so they stop wicking moisture upwards</li><li>Sagging rails re-supported with galvanized brackets rather than more nails</li><li>Hot-dipped galvanized or coated fasteners, because treated lumber eats plain steel</li><li>Latches, drop rods, catches and self-closing hinges fitted and adjusted</li><li>Chain link repairs: tension bars, top rails, caps and re-tensioned fabric</li><li>Privacy screens and lattice panels refixed or replaced</li><li>Cleaning and re-staining a fence once the repairs are finished</li></ul><h2>Property lines, by-laws and pool gates</h2><p>Being upfront about this saves everybody trouble. A fence sitting on the boundary between two properties is normally a shared responsibility in Ontario, so a conversation with the neighbour before work starts is worth more than any argument afterwards. I do not decide where a property line runs. Only a surveyor can do that, and where there is real doubt I would rather you find out before I start digging.</p><p>Height limits come from your local fence by-law, and Toronto and the surrounding municipalities each set their own, with different rules for front and back yards. Putting a fence back the way it was is simple. Making it taller may not be allowed, so that is a question for the city before the lumber is ordered. Pool enclosures are the strict case: they carry their own requirements, need a gate that closes and latches by itself, and are inspected. I can fit self-closing hinges and a suitable latch, but the enclosure as a whole has to satisfy the by-law.</p><h2>Fence and gate repair across the GTA</h2><p>Send photos with your quote request: one of the whole fence, one of the gate from the latch side, and one of the base of any post you are worried about. That usually tells me more than a phone call does. Quotes are free, replies normally come back within one business day, and nothing is owed if you decide to leave it for now.</p><p>Spring and autumn are the busy stretches, and small jobs batch well. If the gate needs rehanging and there are three broken boards at the far end of the yard, they get done in one visit instead of two.</p>','Sagging gates squared, rehung and re-latched\nAnti-sag cable or brace fitted the right way\nRotted fence boards and pickets replaced\nLoose or rotten posts reset or spurred\nBroken rails re-secured with galvanized brackets\nGate hardware, latches and drop rods fitted\nSelf-closing hinges fitted to pool gates\nChain link fabric re-tensioned and rails repaired\nFence cleaned and re-stained after repairs','[{\"title\":\"Photos and a look\",\"text\":\"Send photos of the fence, the gate and the base of any leaning post. On site I check every post for movement and rot at ground level, then price the repair as a whole rather than one symptom.\"},{\"title\":\"Locates and materials\",\"text\":\"If posts are coming out, the underground services get located first through Ontario One Call. Lumber and hardware are matched to what is already there, and I confirm with you whether I am supplying them or you are.\"},{\"title\":\"Repair day\",\"text\":\"Posts and boards are dealt with first, then the gate is hung, squared and adjusted until it swings clean and latches without being lifted. Hinge and latch screws go into solid wood, never back into old worn holes.\"},{\"title\":\"Tidy and check\",\"text\":\"Old boards, broken concrete and offcuts leave with me and the ground is raked back. You open and close the gate yourself before I go, and if anything settles in the first weeks I come back and adjust it.\"}]','Fence pricing turns on how much comes apart. Rehanging a gate that needs squaring and new hinges is a short job. Digging out a post set in concrete, working on a slope, or carrying lumber to a back fence through a narrow side yard all take longer. Quantity matters as well, since a run of boards done together takes less time per board than a single one. Lumber and hardware can come from me with receipts, or you can supply your own. You get a fixed price or an hourly rate before I start.','[{\"q\":\"My gate drags on the ground. Can it be fixed without a new gate?\",\"a\":\"Usually. Most dragging gates are either racked frames or leaning posts, and both are repairable. A racked frame comes back square with a tension cable and turnbuckle run from the top hinge corner down to the bottom latch corner, or with a compression brace running the other way. A leaning post gets reset or spurred. Only when the frame joints have rotted through does a new gate make more sense than a repair.\"},{\"q\":\"Can a leaning post be saved without digging it out?\",\"a\":\"Sometimes. If the post has rotted only where it enters the ground and the timber above is still hard, a galvanized post spur or repair spike can be driven or set beside it and bolted to the sound section. That is a genuine repair, not a bodge, and it avoids disturbing the concrete. If the post is soft well above grade, or the whole run is leaning, replacement is the honest answer.\"},{\"q\":\"The fence sits on the property line. Who is responsible for it?\",\"a\":\"A fence on the boundary between two properties is generally a shared matter in Ontario, and the neighbourly route is a conversation before anything is touched. I am happy to quote either owner, and I am happy to explain the work to both. What I cannot do is tell you where the line legally runs. That is a surveyor\'s job, and it is worth settling first if a post is coming out.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I need a permit to repair my fence?\",\"a\":\"For ordinary repairs, replacing boards, rails, posts or a gate on the same footprint, normally no. Building a fence taller than the local by-law allows is a different matter, and the rules differ between front and back yards and between municipalities. Pool enclosures are the exception that catches people out: they have their own requirements and inspections. Check with your city before ordering materials, and I will work to whatever they tell you.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you match the boards on an older fence?\",\"a\":\"I get as close as the lumber yard allows. Board widths and profiles have changed over the years, so an exact match on a twenty year old cedar fence is not always possible. New boards also start out much lighter than weathered ones. They dull down over a season, and a coat of stain over the whole fence afterwards is the quickest way to make a repair disappear.\"}]','deck-repair,pressure-washing,handyman-services,eavestrough-cleaning','',1,16,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (17,'smart-home-installation','Smart Home Device Installation','Smart Home Device Installation in Toronto','fa-solid fa-wifi','smart home installation toronto, video doorbell installation toronto, smart thermostat installation, security camera installation gta, smart lock installation toronto, smart switch installation, smart home installer near me, doorbell camera installer toronto',0,'Smart Home Installation Toronto','Smart home installation across the Greater Toronto Area: video doorbells, thermostats, cameras, smart locks and switches fitted and tested. Ask for a quote.','Smart home installation is one of those jobs that looks simple in the box and turns awkward on the wall. I fit video doorbells, smart thermostats, cameras, locks, switches and dimmers in houses and condos across Toronto and the wider GTA, and I do the parts most people dread: drilling brick, finding a neutral wire, and getting the thing to stay online afterwards.\n\nEvery device I fit works with the wiring you already have. Swapping an existing switch, dimmer, doorbell or thermostat is normal handyman work. Running a new circuit is not, and that goes to a licensed electrician. Before I leave, the device is mounted straight, paired to your Wi-Fi, tested from your phone, and set up under your own account.','<h2>Smart home installation I take on across the GTA</h2>\n<p>Most of my smart home installation work is a straight swap of something that already exists. A doorbell for a video doorbell. A wall switch for a smart switch. A round thermostat for a rectangular one with a screen. The device is new, but the wiring behind it is whatever the builder or the last owner left there, and that is what decides how the job goes.</p>\n<p>Typical calls around Toronto are a video doorbell on a brick porch, two or three cameras under a soffit, a smart deadbolt on the front door, dimmers in a living room, a thermostat upgrade, and mesh nodes so the far bedroom stops dropping. Several of those can be handled in one visit, which saves you a second call-out.</p>\n<h2>The wiring realities behind the box</h2>\n<p>A neutral wire is the first thing I look for. Most smart switches and dimmers need one in the box, and older houses here often ran a switch loop with only a hot and a switched leg, so there is nothing to land on. Some models are built to run without a neutral and others use a bypass module at the fixture, which is why I check before you buy.</p>\n<p>Dimmers carry a second catch. LED bulbs draw very little, so the dimmer has to be rated for LED loads and matched to a minimum load, or you get flicker, buzz and a bottom end that drops the lights out entirely. Three-way circuits need the matching companion switch from the same range, wired to the traveller the manufacturer expects.</p>\n<p>Wired video doorbells run off a low-voltage transformer, usually 16 to 24 volts, and that transformer has to supply enough power for a camera that streams. One sized years ago for a mechanical chime will leave a doorbell rebooting, dropping video or refusing to charge. Most kits also include a bypass module that fits at the chime itself.</p>\n<h2>What separates a good job from a bad one</h2>\n<p>The failures I get called out to fix on a smart home installation are nearly always mechanical, not digital. A doorbell screwed into soft mortar with the wrong plug. A camera hole drilled through brick with no sealant and no drip loop, so water tracks along the cable into the wall. A smart lock bolted onto a door that binds, which burns batteries in weeks because the motor is fighting the strike.</p>\n<p>My habits on these jobs are simple and they do not change:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Power off at the breaker and confirmed dead at the terminals, never assumed</li>\n<li>Anchors sized to the bit and the substrate, not whatever came in the box</li>\n<li>Exterior penetrations sealed and given a drip loop below the entry point</li>\n<li>Doors, strikes and latches adjusted before a smart lock ever goes on</li>\n<li>Devices paired to your account and your email address, never mine</li>\n<li>Old plates, packaging and offcuts taken away with me</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Cameras, doorbells and Wi-Fi that holds</h2>\n<p>Camera height matters more than resolution. Mount too high and you record the tops of heads; a little above eye level, angled down, gives you faces and plates. I aim each one on your phone while it is still loose, then fix it, because the view from the top of a ladder is never the view on the screen.</p>\n<p>Coverage is a placement problem, not a hardware problem. Concrete cores in condos, ductwork, mirrors and foil-backed insulation all cut range, so a mesh node parked in a basement corner does nothing useful. I put nodes between the router and the dead zone, use a wired backhaul where a cable run already exists, and test speeds in the rooms you actually sit in.</p>\n<h2>Where I stop and call a licensed trade</h2>\n<p>Swapping an existing switch, dimmer, fixture or thermostat on the wiring already in place is handyman work. Running a new circuit, adding a box, touching a panel or replacing a doorbell transformer is not, and that goes to a licensed electrician rather than a guess. If a thermostat needs a common wire pulled or work inside the furnace, that belongs to an HVAC technician.</p>\n<p>Garage door controllers are the other honest limit in smart home installation. They wire to the existing wall-button terminals on the opener and rely on the safety photo eyes near the floor. If your opener predates those sensors, nothing should be added to it, and I will say so rather than sell you a hookup that leaves a heavy door under remote control.</p>','Video doorbell mounted on brick or siding\nSmart thermostat swapped on existing low-voltage wiring\nSmart switches and LED-rated dimmers fitted\nWi-Fi and PoE cameras mounted and aimed\nSmart deadbolt fitted to existing door prep\nMesh Wi-Fi nodes placed and speed tested\nGarage door controller wired to existing opener\nChime bypass module fitted at the chime\nApps, accounts and device names set up','[{\"title\":\"Check it before you buy\",\"text\":\"Send me photos of the switch box, the thermostat wires or the doorbell chime before you order anything. A five minute check on compatibility saves a return trip to the store and a device that will not do what you wanted.\"},{\"title\":\"Power down and mount\",\"text\":\"On the day, power comes off at the breaker and I confirm the circuit is dead at the terminals. Then the device is mounted level, into studs, masonry anchors or the existing box, with the fasteners the substrate actually calls for.\"},{\"title\":\"Pair and test\",\"text\":\"Every device gets joined to your Wi-Fi under your own account, named sensibly, and tested the way you will use it. Doorbells get rung, cameras get walked past, locks get thrown and released from the app and the keypad.\"},{\"title\":\"Show you the settings\",\"text\":\"Before I pack up I walk you through motion zones, notification settings, charging habits and how to add someone else to the account. Most people never open those menus again, so it is worth ten minutes with me standing there.\"}]','What moves the price on smart devices is mostly what sits behind them. Drilling brick, stone or a concrete condo wall takes longer than drywall. A switch box with no neutral, a doorbell fed by a weak transformer or a camera that needs cable pulled all add time. Height matters too, since anything reached from a ladder outside is slower and more careful work. Quantity helps you, because devices done in one visit share the setup time. You get a fixed price or an hourly rate agreed before I start.','[{\"q\":\"Should I buy the devices myself or do you supply them?\",\"a\":\"Buy them yourself if you have a brand in mind, since you get the warranty and the account in your name. Send me the model first and I will tell you whether it suits your wiring. If you would rather not choose, I can pick up a sensible option and pass you the receipt for it.\"},{\"q\":\"There is no neutral wire in my switch box. Can I still get a smart switch?\",\"a\":\"Often yes. Some smart switches are built to run without a neutral, and some ranges use a small bypass module that fits in the fixture box instead. Both have limits, mostly around LED bulbs and three-way circuits, so the choice depends on what I find when I pull the old switch out. If neither option works safely, I say so instead of forcing it.\"},{\"q\":\"Will a video doorbell work with my existing chime?\",\"a\":\"Usually, with a bypass module fitted at the chime, which most kits include. The bigger question is the transformer feeding it. A doorbell camera needs steady power, and an older transformer sized for a simple chime can leave it rebooting or dropping video. Replacing a transformer is electrical work, so that part goes to a licensed electrician. Battery doorbells sidestep the whole issue.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you install cameras or a doorbell on a condo or a rental?\",\"a\":\"Inside your unit, yes. Anything on a corridor door, a balcony or an exterior wall usually belongs to the corporation or the landlord, so get written permission first and check what the rules say about drilling and about cameras pointing at shared space. I am happy to do the work once that is sorted, and to keep the fixing points as small as possible.\"},{\"q\":\"What if the device will not connect to my Wi-Fi?\",\"a\":\"That is part of the job, not an extra. Most connection trouble comes down to a weak signal at the mounting point, a router set to hide one of its bands, or a device that only joins the slower band. I test signal strength at the spot before I mount anything, move a node or change a setting if needed, and I do not call the work done until it is on and staying on.\"}]','lock-installation,light-fixture-installation,tv-mounting,condo-handyman,garage-door-repair','',1,17,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (18,'window-screen-repair','Window and Screen Repair','Window and Screen Repair in Toronto and the GTA','fa-solid fa-window-maximize','window screen repair toronto, screen replacement toronto, re-screen window screens, window crank replacement toronto, patio screen door repair, window hardware repair gta, window draft repair toronto, screen door repair near me',0,'Window and Screen Repair Toronto','Window and screen repair across Toronto and the GTA: re-screening, new frames, cranks and operators, weather stripping and caulking. Ask for a free quote.','Window and screen repair is small work that changes how a house feels. A torn screen means mosquitoes all July. A crank that spins without pulling the sash tight means a cold line across the room every January. I re-mesh screens, build new frames, replace operators and hardware, and sort out drafts and sticking sashes for homes across Toronto and the GTA.\n\nMost screens are worth re-meshing rather than replacing. Where the frame is still square and the corners are tight, new mesh and new spline bring it back for years. Where the frame has bowed or the corner keys have crumbled, I build a fresh frame to your opening. Either way the mesh, spline and hardware come with me, and the old material leaves with me.','<h2>What window and screen repair usually involves</h2>\n<p>The list under window and screen repair is scattered: mesh, spline, frames, cranks, rollers, balances, weather stripping and the caulking around the outside. Plenty of homes in Toronto need several of those at once, which is why I like to walk the whole house and deal with everything in one visit rather than come back for a single screen.</p>\n<h2>Re-screening: mesh, spline and tension</h2>\n<p>A screen is four pieces: the frame, the corner keys, the mesh and the spline that squeezes the mesh into the groove. Get the spline diameter wrong and the repair fails, either straight away or next summer. Too thin and the mesh pulls out of the channel on the first push from a cat or a kid. Too thick and it splays the frame out of square.</p>\n<p>Tension is the other half of it. Mesh needs to be drawn just taut, not stretched drum tight, because a light aluminum frame will bow inward and stop fitting its opening. I roll the spline in with a proper screen roller, work opposite sides in turn, then trim the waste back into the groove with a fresh blade so no fringe shows along the edge.</p>\n<h3>Mesh choices that matter</h3>\n<p>Standard fibreglass is soft, forgiving and fine for most windows. Aluminum mesh holds its shape and suits doors and low windows that get pushed on. Pet mesh is a heavy vinyl-coated polyester that shrugs off claws. A tighter no-see-um weave keeps out the very small insects near ravines and the lake, and solar mesh cuts glare and heat on west-facing glass at the cost of a little view.</p>\n<h2>Cranks, operators and window hardware</h2>\n<p>Casement operators wear out from the inside. The worm gear strips, so the handle turns while the sash barely moves, or the arm shoe drags in its track. Replacements are matched by manufacturer, by hand, and by arm length, which is why photos of the operator, the arm and any numbers stamped on the sash help me source the right part before I come out.</p>\n<p>Grit is the quiet killer here. The track that the arm slides in fills with dust and paint, and oil makes it worse because oil holds dirt. I clean the track, use a dry lubricant, check the hinge arms at the top and bottom, and replace the handle when the splines inside it have rounded off.</p>\n<h2>Sashes that stick, drop or will not lock</h2>\n<p>Sliding and hung windows have their own faults. Vinyl sliders ride on small nylon rollers that flat-spot and drag. Older hung sashes hang on spiral or block-and-tackle balances that lose tension, so the window creeps down or feels heavy to lift. Paint build-up in the channel is the simplest cause of all, and often the real one.</p>\n<p>Locks and keepers go out of line once a sash has dropped or a frame has moved. Forcing the handle bends the keeper, and then nothing latches at all. I adjust the sash first, move or shim the keeper second, and replace parts only when they are genuinely worn. Adjusting first and replacing last is the habit that makes window and screen repair worth paying for.</p>\n<h2>Drafts, condensation and caulking</h2>\n<p>Weather stripping is what actually stops a draft, and it is the part of window and screen repair people forget is consumable. Kerf-in bulb seal compresses flat over the years and foam-backed tape hardens. Fresh seal pushes into the same kerf, cut slightly long so the corners stay compressed. A strip of paper closed in the sash gives a quick check: if it slides out freely all the way round, the seal is finished.</p>\n<p>Condensation confuses people. Water on the room side of the glass in winter is indoor humidity meeting a cold surface, which is a ventilation problem more than a window problem. Fog trapped between the panes is a failed sealed unit, and that unit has to be replaced by a glass company, so I will point you there instead of pretending a repair exists.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Old perimeter caulking cut out fully before new sealant goes on</li>\n<li>Backer rod pushed into wide joints so the bead can flex</li>\n<li>Exterior-grade sealant tooled into a clean, dry joint</li>\n<li>Weep holes at the bottom of the frame left open, never caulked shut</li>\n<li>Interior stop and trim gaps sealed where drafts track in</li>\n<li>Sills cleared out so water drains away instead of sitting</li>\n</ul>','Window screens re-meshed with new spline\nNew aluminum screen frames built to size\nPatio screen door rollers and latches replaced\nCasement operators and cranks matched and replaced\nKerf-in weather stripping renewed around sashes\nSticking sashes freed, cleaned and adjusted\nExterior window caulking cut out and replaced\nWeep holes cleared so water drains out\nScreen corner keys and pull tabs replaced','[{\"title\":\"Photos and measurements\",\"text\":\"Send photos of the screen frame, the crank or the numbers stamped on the sash. I measure the frame outside to outside, check the spline groove and note the mesh type, so I arrive with the right material in the van.\"},{\"title\":\"Work done on site\",\"text\":\"Screens are re-meshed at your place on a flat surface, usually a table or a clean floor, so nothing leaves the property. Frames that need rebuilding get cut, keyed and squared before the new mesh goes anywhere near them.\"},{\"title\":\"Hardware and seals\",\"text\":\"Operators, rollers, balances, locks and weather stripping go in next, with each window opened and closed several times to confirm it runs smoothly, latches without a shove and seals against the frame all the way round.\"},{\"title\":\"Refit and clean up\",\"text\":\"Screens go back into their own openings, since they are rarely interchangeable, and I mark them if you store any over winter. Old mesh, spline offcuts and packaging leave with me, and the sills get wiped down.\"}]','Screen work is priced mostly by count and by mesh. A row of standard windows goes quickly once I am set up, while pet mesh, solar mesh and rebuilt frames take more material and more time. Hardware is different again, because obsolete operators and balances take sourcing before they take fitting. Upper floors, fixed screens behind storm units and anything reached from a ladder are slower. You will have a fixed price or an hourly rate agreed before I start, and receipts for anything I supply.','[{\"q\":\"Can you re-screen my windows without taking them away?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and that is how I prefer to do it. I work on a flat surface at your place, re-mesh the frames one at a time and put each one back in the opening it came from. Screens that look identical often differ by a few millimetres, so keeping them with their own windows avoids ending up with a set that almost fits.\"},{\"q\":\"My windows are fogged up between the panes. Can that be repaired?\",\"a\":\"Not by me, and not properly by anyone. Fog between the panes means the seal on the sealed unit has failed and moist air is inside the cavity. The honest fix is a new sealed unit from a glass company, which I am glad to point you toward. Drilling and venting a unit clears the view for a while but does not restore the seal or the gas fill.\"},{\"q\":\"Which mesh should I pick if I have a cat or a dog?\",\"a\":\"Pet mesh, which is a thick vinyl-coated polyester woven far stronger than standard fibreglass. It stands up to claws and to a dog leaning on a patio screen door, and it costs more per screen because the material is heavier. It does dim the view slightly, and darker colours see through better than light ones. On doors I often pair it with an aluminum frame for stiffness.\"},{\"q\":\"I do not know who made my windows. Can you still replace a broken crank?\",\"a\":\"Usually. Operators are identified by their shape, the mounting hole pattern, the arm length and any numbers stamped on the hardware or printed on a label in the sash channel. Clear photos with a tape measure in the shot get me most of the way there. If a part is truly discontinued, universal operators fit some common patterns, and I will tell you before ordering anything.\"},{\"q\":\"Why do my windows drip and sweat every winter?\",\"a\":\"Cold glass plus humid indoor air. Outdoor air is dry in winter, but a sealed house holds moisture from showers, cooking, laundry and people, and the coldest surface in the room is the glass, so that is where it lands. Running the bathroom and kitchen fans longer, turning a humidifier down and keeping curtains off the glass usually fixes it. Worn weather stripping makes it worse by chilling the frame.\"}]','door-repair,lock-installation,handyman-services,pressure-washing,rental-property-maintenance','',1,18,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (19,'lock-installation','Lock Change and Door Hardware','Lock Change and Door Hardware in Toronto','fa-solid fa-lock','lock change toronto, deadbolt installation toronto, change locks new home, keyed alike locks toronto, smart lock installation toronto, door hardware installation gta, strike plate reinforcement, lock replacement near me',0,'Lock Change and Door Hardware Toronto','Lock change and door hardware across Toronto and the GTA: deadbolts, keyed-alike sets, smart locks, strike plates and latch alignment. Ask for a free quote.','A lock change is the first job worth booking after you take possession of a place in Toronto, because you have no idea how many keys are out there. I change deadbolts and knobsets, fit keyed-alike sets so one key runs the whole house, install smart locks, and correct the alignment problems that make a door hard to lock in the first place.\n\nHardware is only half of a secure door. A good deadbolt in a jamb held by short screws gives way to one solid kick, so the strike plate and the framing behind it matter as much as the cylinder does. That is where I spend the extra ten minutes on every door I touch across the GTA.','<h2>When a lock change is worth doing</h2>\n<p>Moving in is the obvious trigger. Keys travel: previous owners, tenants, cleaners, contractors, the neighbour who fed the cat. A lock change on the exterior doors resets all of that in an afternoon. The other common reasons are a break-in or an attempt, a separation, a lost key that had an address tag on it, and a cylinder that has started to feel gritty.</p>\n<p>Wear is worth taking seriously. A lock that has to be jiggled is telling you the pins or the key are worn, and that story usually ends with a key snapped off in the dark on a January night. Replacing it while it still works is far easier than dealing with it after it quits.</p>\n<h2>Keyed-alike sets and one key for the house</h2>\n<p>One key for the front, back and garage side door is the upgrade people appreciate most. Locks from the same manufacturer and keyway can be bought as keyed-alike sets, or matched to each other with the maker\'s own rekeying kit where the range supports it. Tell me how many doors and how many keys you want as part of the lock change and I will set the whole house up on one key.</p>\n<p>Mixing brands is where this falls apart. Different makers use different keyways, so a deadbolt from one and a handleset from another will never share a key, whatever the packaging suggests. Keeping every opening on one line of hardware also means the finishes match and spare parts stay easy to find years later.</p>\n<h2>The parts that decide whether a lock lasts</h2>\n<p>Grades give you a rough guide. Grade 1 is commercial duty, Grade 2 is a solid residential choice, and Grade 3 is builder-basic. A deadbolt should throw a full bolt of about 25 mm (1 in) into the strike, and with the door closed that bolt should slide home under two fingers rather than a shove.</p>\n<p>Strike plates are the weak link in almost every Toronto house I visit. Builders fasten them with short screws that bite only the jamb trim, and the jamb itself is thin softwood tacked to the framing. I swap those for screws long enough to reach the framing behind, usually around 75 mm (3 in), fit a box strike where the mortise allows, and add a reinforcement plate on doors that have already been forced.</p>\n<p>What I check on every job:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Backset, bore size and door thickness before any hardware is ordered</li>\n<li>Bolt throwing fully into the strike with the door closed</li>\n<li>Long screws into the framing at the strike and the top hinge</li>\n<li>Latch and strike aligned so the door needs no lifting or leaning</li>\n<li>Weather stripping compressing without fighting the latch</li>\n<li>Every key tried in every lock before I leave</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Smart locks on doors that already exist</h2>\n<p>Smart locks are mechanical before they are clever. The motor that throws the bolt is small, so a door that binds will chew through batteries and eventually strip the drive. Alignment gets sorted first, then the lock goes on. Retrofit models that sit over your existing deadbolt on the inside keep your keys and your exterior look, which suits condo doors and rentals well.</p>\n<p>Battery life, a backup key or keypad, and who holds the codes are the practical questions worth answering during a lock change. I set the account up in your name, add codes you can revoke later for cleaners or guests, and show you the manual override. Locks with a Wi-Fi bridge need a decent signal at the door, which is worth testing before you commit to one.</p>\n<h2>Where a locksmith is the right call</h2>\n<p>Lockouts are not my work. If you are standing outside your own door, call a locksmith, because that is a different skill set with different tools. Rekeying a cylinder to an existing key, high-security cylinders and master key systems also sit with a locksmith rather than with me, and I would rather send you straight there than fumble it.</p>\n<p>Condo doors come with their own rules. A unit entry door onto a corridor is usually fire-rated and often falls under the corporation\'s hardware standard, so changing it can need approval and matching parts. Check with property management first, and I will fit whatever they allow, including door closers that have to be adjusted rather than removed.</p>','Exterior deadbolts and knobsets changed\nKeyed-alike sets so one key fits\nSmart deadbolts fitted and set up\nStrike plates screwed into the framing\nLatch and strike alignment corrected\nJamb reinforcement plates fitted after forced entry\nWide-angle peepholes and digital viewers installed\nSurface door closers fitted and adjusted\nInterior passage and privacy sets replaced\nNew deadbolt bores cut in wooden doors','[{\"title\":\"Look at the door\",\"text\":\"I check backset, bore size, door thickness and how the door sits in the frame before any hardware is chosen. A door that has to be lifted to lock will defeat the best deadbolt on the market, so that comes first.\"},{\"title\":\"Choose matching hardware\",\"text\":\"Finish, grade, keyway and how many doors share a key all get settled up front. You can supply the locks or I can bring them with receipts, and I keep every door on the same manufacturer so the keyed-alike set actually works.\"},{\"title\":\"Fit and reinforce\",\"text\":\"Old hardware comes off, the new set goes in square, and the strike gets long screws into the framing behind the jamb. Where a door has been forced before, a reinforcement plate spreads the load across the jamb instead of one thin point.\"},{\"title\":\"Test every key\",\"text\":\"Each key goes into each lock, from inside and outside, with the door open and then closed. I adjust the strike until the bolt drops in cleanly without lifting or pulling, then hand over all the keys and the packaging.\"}]','Door count is the main thing. Swapping like for like on doors that already close well moves quickly, and each extra door after the first adds less than you would expect. Cutting fresh prep into a door that never had a deadbolt takes longer, and steel or fibreglass doors take longer again. Alignment work, reinforcement plates and multipoint hardware all add time. Whether you supply the locks or I bring them changes the material side only. Either way the price is agreed before I pick up a tool.','[{\"q\":\"Should I change the locks or have them rekeyed?\",\"a\":\"Changing gives you new hardware and new keys, which is the better move when the existing locks are worn, mismatched or builder-grade. Rekeying keeps the hardware and changes the pins so old keys stop working, and that is locksmith work. If your locks are decent and already match, rekeying makes sense. If they are tired, or you want one key for every door, changing them is the cleaner answer.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you make my front, back and garage doors use the same key?\",\"a\":\"Yes, as long as every door takes hardware from the same manufacturer and keyway. I order them as a keyed-alike set or match them with the maker\'s rekeying kit, then test each key in each lock before I go. Existing hardware you want to keep can sometimes be brought into the set and sometimes cannot, which I can usually tell from a photo of the lock face.\"},{\"q\":\"My door only locks if I lean on it. Is that the lock?\",\"a\":\"Rarely. It is nearly always the door sitting out of position, so the bolt no longer lines up with the hole in the strike. Seasonal movement, a sagging top hinge or compressed weather stripping all cause it. The fix is usually a long screw into the framing at the top hinge, then filing or shifting the strike a couple of millimetres. Replacing the lock without correcting that changes nothing.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you help if I am locked out?\",\"a\":\"No, and I would rather say that plainly than waste your evening. Lockouts are a locksmith job, with picks, bypass tools and the training to open a door without wrecking it. Call one, get inside, then call me if you want the hardware changed afterwards. That order of events works out better than forcing something and ending up with a damaged door edge as well.\"},{\"q\":\"Do smart locks fit any door?\",\"a\":\"Most standard doors, yes, but three things decide it: the door thickness, the backset from the edge to the centre of the bore, and whether the bolt already moves freely. Steel doors with narrow stiles and doors with multipoint hardware often need a specific model or cannot take one at all. Send me a photo of the existing deadbolt and the door edge and I will tell you before you buy.\"}]','door-repair,smart-home-installation,handyman-services,rental-property-maintenance,condo-handyman','',1,19,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (20,'appliance-installation','Appliance Installation and Hookup','Appliance Installation and Hookup in Toronto','fa-solid fa-plug','appliance installation toronto, dishwasher installation toronto, over the range microwave installation, washer and dryer hookup toronto, fridge water line installation, range hood installation gta, appliance hookup near me, dryer vent installation toronto',0,'Appliance Installation Toronto and GTA','Appliance installation and hookup across Toronto and the GTA: dishwashers, microwaves, washers, dryers, fridge water lines and hoods. Ask for a free quote.','Appliance installation is where a straightforward delivery turns into a problem. The truck arrives, the crew slides the box into the kitchen, and someone points out that the old shut-off is seized, the cabinet needs cutting or the vent is the wrong size. I finish those jobs properly for homes across the Greater Toronto Area: dishwashers, over-the-range microwaves, washers, dryers, fridges with water lines and range hoods.\n\nConnections to existing water, drain, vent and electrical outlets are handyman work. Anything gas is not, and that goes to a licensed gas technician. New drain lines, new supply runs and new circuits go to a licensed plumber or electrician. What I hand back is an appliance that is level, secured, connected and tested through a full cycle.','<h2>What appliance installation actually involves</h2>\n<p>Hooking up a machine is rarely the long part. The long part is everything around it: a shut-off valve that has not moved in years, a drain hose that needs a high loop, a cabinet opening a few millimetres too tight, or a vent made of the white vinyl hose that should never have been used.</p>\n<p>Retailer delivery teams work to a tight script and a tighter schedule. If the connection is not textbook they are usually not allowed to proceed, and that is when a new machine ends up sitting in the middle of the kitchen. Most of my appliance installation work in Toronto starts at exactly that point.</p>\n<h2>Dishwashers and the connections that leak</h2>\n<p>Three connections decide whether a dishwasher stays dry: the supply, the drain and the power. The supply comes off the existing shut-off under the sink with a braided stainless line and a brass elbow at the inlet, sealed at the threads and snugged rather than cranked. Every joint gets checked again after the first cycle, because that is when a slow weep shows itself.</p>\n<p>The drain hose has to rise into a high loop fastened up under the counter, or run through an air gap on the sink deck, so dirty sink water cannot siphon back into the machine. Where the drain ties into a garbage disposer, the knockout plug inside the disposer inlet has to be driven out first. Leaving it in is the most common reason a new dishwasher will not drain.</p>\n<p>Levelling and fixing come last. Feet get adjusted so the door closes evenly and the tub sits flat, then the unit is anchored under a wood counter or with side brackets to the cabinets. Nothing gets screwed into stone or quartz.</p>\n<h2>Washers, dryers and venting</h2>\n<p>Shipping bolts are the first thing off a new front-load washer, and they go in a drawer in case the machine ever moves again. Left in, they hold the drum rigid and wreck the suspension. The machine is then levelled on all four feet with the lock nuts tightened, because one that rocks under spin will walk across a laundry room.</p>\n<p>Dryer venting is where I see the most risk in appliance installation. Flexible white vinyl duct is a fire hazard and lint catches in every ripple. Rigid or semi-rigid metal is the answer, with joints sealed by foil tape rather than screws, the shortest run with the fewest elbows, and an exterior hood whose flap swings freely. Screws inside a duct snag lint.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rubber fill hoses replaced with braided stainless steel</li>\n<li>Hot and cold connected the right way round and checked</li>\n<li>Drain hose held in the standpipe with a retainer, not jammed deep</li>\n<li>Shipping bolts removed and left with you for later</li>\n<li>Dryer cord matched to your existing receptacle, bonding strap set correctly</li>\n<li>Vent run in metal duct and taped, never screwed</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Microwaves, hoods and fridge water lines</h2>\n<p>An over-the-range microwave is a two-person lift onto a plate that has to land in studs. The template goes on the wall, studs are found and confirmed, the plate is lagged in, and the cabinet floor above is drilled for the cord and cut for the duct. The blower also has to be rotated and the damper set for rear vent, top vent or recirculating.</p>\n<p>Range hoods follow the same logic with more duct. Rigid metal, the largest diameter the hood supports, a backdraft damper, and taped joints. A hood blowing into the cabinet above it only moves grease around the kitchen.</p>\n<p>Fridge water lines connect to an existing supply with a quarter-turn stop where one is reachable. Saddle valves clamped onto a copper pipe are a known leak source, so I replace them rather than reuse them. The line gets a service loop behind the fridge so it can be pulled out without kinking, and the first few batches of ice go in the bin while the filter clears.</p>\n<h2>Gas, new circuits and new plumbing</h2>\n<p>Gas ranges, gas dryers and gas cooktops are not mine to connect. That work belongs to a licensed gas technician, and I say so on the phone. A kitchen with no dishwasher rough-in is the same story: the new drain and supply belong to a licensed plumber, and a new dedicated circuit belongs to a licensed electrician.</p>\n<p>Anti-tip brackets deserve a mention of their own. Every free-standing range comes with one, it anchors the appliance to the floor or the wall, and it stops the range tipping when weight goes on an open oven door. Delivery crews skip it constantly. I fit it, and I check for one on any range I am working beside across the GTA.</p>','Dishwasher connected, levelled and leak tested\nOver-the-range microwave mounted into studs\nWasher hoses replaced and shipping bolts removed\nDryer vented in rigid metal duct\nFridge water line run to the ice maker\nRange anti-tip bracket anchored as required\nRange hood ducted with a backdraft damper\nOld appliance disconnected and moved out\nStacking kit fitted for washer and dryer\nCabinet openings trimmed for a tight fit','[{\"title\":\"Measure before delivery\",\"text\":\"Send me the model number and I will check the opening width, height and depth, the door swing, the location of the shut-off and receptacle, and whether the machine will actually fit through your doorway and down the stairs.\"},{\"title\":\"Disconnect and clear out\",\"text\":\"Water off, power off, old unit drained and pulled out without dragging it across the floor. I look at the condition of the valve, the drain and the duct at this point, because that is when problems are visible and easy to solve.\"},{\"title\":\"Fit, level and connect\",\"text\":\"The new appliance goes in, gets levelled on its feet, and every connection is made with new hoses, clamps and tape as needed. Anti-tip brackets, mounting plates and vent connections are fitted at this stage rather than left for later.\"},{\"title\":\"Run a full cycle\",\"text\":\"Nothing is finished until the machine has run. Dishwashers get a full cycle with a towel under the toe kick, washers get a spin, dryers get checked for airflow at the exterior hood, and the fridge line gets watched for a weep.\"}]','Straight swaps are the quick end of this work, where the new machine matches the old one and the existing valve, duct and outlet are sound. Time goes up when a shut-off is seized, a cabinet needs cutting, a vent run has to be rebuilt in metal, or a microwave has to come out of a tight opening overhead. Stairs, condo elevator bookings and a machine that has to travel across the house all add to it. Parts can come from you or from me with receipts, and the price is agreed first.','[{\"q\":\"The store includes installation with delivery. Where do you fit in?\",\"a\":\"Delivery installs work when everything is standard. If the shut-off is seized, the duct is vinyl, the cabinet needs cutting or the old unit is hard-plumbed, the crew will usually decline and leave the box in your kitchen. That is the call I get most often. I can also come the day before to prepare the space so the delivery goes ahead without a hitch.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you hook up a gas range or a gas dryer?\",\"a\":\"No. Gas connections are for a licensed gas technician, and there is no grey area in that. What I can do is the rest of the job: pulling the old appliance out, levelling the new one, fitting the anti-tip bracket, sorting the venting and putting the kitchen back together once the gas fitter has made the connection and tested it.\"},{\"q\":\"There has never been a dishwasher in my kitchen. Can one be added?\",\"a\":\"Sometimes, and it depends on what is already under the sink. If there is a cabinet bay the right width, a supply with a shut-off, a drain connection and a receptacle in reach, the install is straightforward. If a new drain or supply line has to be run, that part is plumbing work and goes to a licensed plumber. Send me photos under the sink and I will tell you which one you have.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you take the old appliance away?\",\"a\":\"I disconnect it and move it wherever you want it on the property, usually the garage or the curb. Hauling it off is a different service, and the retailer will often take the old unit on delivery day if you ask for that when you order. Scrap collectors take metal appliances too, and the city collects large items by appointment, so it rarely sits there long.\"},{\"q\":\"My new washer shakes and moves during the spin. What went wrong?\",\"a\":\"Three usual causes. The shipping bolts were never taken out, which is the most common by a distance. The feet are not level, or the lock nuts under them were never tightened. Or the floor itself flexes, which happens on upper floors and older joists. I check all three, and on a springy floor a solid platform under the machine does more than any anti-vibration pad.\"}]','faucet-installation,cabinet-repair,handyman-services,backsplash-tile-installation,rental-property-maintenance','',1,20,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (21,'garage-door-repair','Garage Door Tune-Up and Repair','Garage Door Repair and Tune-Ups Across the GTA','fa-solid fa-warehouse','garage door repair toronto, garage door tune up, garage door roller replacement, garage door opener sensor alignment, garage door weather seal, garage door repair near me, garage door off track, gta garage door maintenance',0,'Garage Door Repair and Tune-Up in Toronto','Garage door repair and tune-ups across the Greater Toronto Area: rollers, hinges, tracks, seals, opener sensors and remotes. Send a photo for a free quote.','Most garage door repair in Toronto starts with the same small parts: worn rollers, dry hinges, a loose track bracket or a bottom seal that has gone hard and cracked. Those parts are cheap. Left alone, they make the opener work harder than it should, and a strained opener is a much bigger bill. I do the tune-up work and the parts replacement that keeps a door running quietly for years.\n\nTorsion springs are the exception. The spring above your door holds enough stored energy to injure someone badly, so I leave those to a garage door specialist and tell you so straight away. Everything else on a residential door across the GTA is fair game.','<h2>What actually goes wrong with a garage door</h2><p>A sectional door is a simple machine. Panels ride on rollers, the rollers sit in tracks, hinges join the panels, and a spring system carries the weight so the opener only has to guide the door rather than lift it. When something binds, the noise tells you where. A grinding rumble usually means dry or flat-spotted rollers. A bang at one point in the travel is often a bent track or a loose lag bolt in a track bracket. A shudder near the floor points at a hinge with an elongated pin hole.</p><p>Doing nothing is the expensive choice. Every extra kilogram of drag gets passed to the opener motor and the gear inside it, and openers are far dearer than a set of rollers. Most of the garage door repair I get called for in the Greater Toronto Area could have been a tune-up a year earlier.</p><h2>What a tune-up includes</h2><p>I work through the whole door rather than only the part that is complaining. A garage door repair that treats one symptom tends to come back, because a door that binds in one place wears everywhere.</p><ul><li>Tighten every hinge, bracket and track lag bolt</li><li>Replace worn, cracked or flat-spotted rollers</li><li>Check track alignment and plumb, then adjust the brackets</li><li>Lubricate rollers, hinges, springs and the opener rail with a proper garage door lubricant</li><li>Balance test the door with the opener disconnected</li><li>Test the auto-reverse on both the sensors and the force setting</li><li>Clean the tracks of grit, road salt and old grease</li></ul><p>One note on lubricant. Household spray oil is the wrong product: it strips grease, attracts grit and leaves the door dirtier than it started. A lithium or silicone garage door lubricant belongs on the pivot points and rollers, never on the track face itself, which the rollers need to roll along rather than slide.</p><h2>Where garage door repair stops and a specialist starts</h2><p>Spring work is where I stop and hand it over. Torsion and extension springs are under enormous tension and need proper winding bars and training to touch safely. If your door will not stay put halfway when the opener is disconnected, or you can see a gap in the spring coil, that is a spring job and I refer it to a specialist. The same goes for a cable that has jumped its drum, a door that has come fully off its tracks under load, and any hardwired electrical work beyond plugging the opener into an existing outlet. New circuits go to a licensed electrician.</p><h2>Openers, safety sensors and remotes</h2><p>Photo-eye sensors sit within about 15 cm (6 in) of the floor on each side and have to face each other squarely. A knocked bracket, a spider web across the lens or a bike leaning on one will stop the door closing and give you the flashing light and the reversing door. Realigning them takes minutes once you know what the indicator lights are telling you.</p><p>Programming remotes and wireless keypads is part of the same job. I replace dead remote batteries, reset the travel limits so the door seats fully without straining, and set the force so the door reverses on an obstruction the way it should. Testing that reversal with a piece of timber laid flat under the door is the last thing I do before packing up. Sensor and opener faults are the most common garage door repair calls I take, and most are sorted in a single visit.</p><h2>Seals, draughts and winter</h2><p>The rubber astragal along the bottom of the door hardens and splits after a handful of Ontario winters, and once it does the door stops sealing against an uneven floor. Cold air, meltwater, road salt and mice all follow. Replacing it means sliding a new profile into the retainer, and where the concrete is out of level a taller seal closes the gap without forcing the door.</p><p>Side and top weatherstripping matters just as much, especially on an attached garage under a bedroom. I fit fresh vinyl stop moulding so it just kisses the door face, tight enough to seal and loose enough that the door is not dragging along it every cycle.</p>','Full door tune-up and lubrication\nWorn roller and hinge replacement\nTrack alignment and bracket tightening\nBottom weather seal replacement\nSide and top weatherstripping renewal\nOpener safety sensor realignment\nRemote and keypad programming\nTravel limit and force adjustment\nAuto-reverse safety test on every visit\nSqueaks and rattles traced and fixed','[{\"title\":\"Photos and a quick call\",\"text\":\"Send me a photo of the door open and closed, plus the label on the opener if you can reach it. That is usually enough for me to quote the parts and the time before I load the van.\"},{\"title\":\"Balance and safety check\",\"text\":\"On arrival I pull the emergency release and lift the door by hand. A balanced door stays where I leave it. That one test tells me whether this is a tune-up or a spring specialist\'s job.\"},{\"title\":\"Parts, adjustment and lubrication\",\"text\":\"Rollers, hinges and seals get swapped, every bolt gets checked, tracks get cleaned and set true, and the moving parts get the right lubricant. The garage stays tidy and the old parts leave with me.\"},{\"title\":\"Cycle test and handover\",\"text\":\"I run the door a dozen times, watch the travel and the reversal, then show you the release cord, the sensor lights and how to keep the tracks clean between visits.\"}]','What a garage door job costs comes down to how much of it needs replacing. A straight tune-up on a door that has simply gone dry is quick work. A door with every roller flat-spotted, a bent track section and a split bottom seal takes longer and needs parts. Door height, whether it is a single or double, and how much clutter I have to move to reach the tracks all play a part. I can supply the parts with receipts or fit yours. Either way the price is agreed before I start.','[{\"q\":\"My garage door is really loud. Can that be fixed without replacing it?\",\"a\":\"Almost always. Noise on a sectional door comes from dry pivot points, worn rollers and loose hardware, not from the door itself. Steel rollers with no bearings are the usual culprit and nylon rollers run far quieter. Tightening the hinges and brackets, cleaning the tracks and lubricating properly makes a surprising difference on its own. I would try all of that before anyone talks about a new door.\"},{\"q\":\"The door starts to close then goes back up. What is wrong?\",\"a\":\"That is the safety system doing its job. Nine times in ten it is the photo-eye sensors near the floor: one has been knocked out of line, the lens is dirty, or something is sitting in the beam. The indicator light on the sensor will usually be off or blinking. If the sensors are clean and aligned, the closing force or the down limit on the opener needs resetting, which I can do.\"},{\"q\":\"How often should a garage door be serviced?\",\"a\":\"Once a year suits most homes across the GTA, and late autumn is the sensible time. Road salt, grit and cold thicken old grease and stiffen the rubber, so a door that ran fine in September can drag by January. If the door runs many cycles a day, or the garage is unheated and damp, twice a year is money well spent.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you replace the spring on my garage door?\",\"a\":\"No, and I would rather tell you that up front. Torsion springs store a large amount of energy and need proper winding bars and experience to change safely. I will diagnose it, confirm with the balance test, and point you to a garage door company that does spring work every day. If the spring is fine and the problem is elsewhere, I can carry on and put it right.\"},{\"q\":\"Mice and cold air are getting under my garage door. Is that fixable?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and it is one of the more satisfying jobs. The bottom seal slides into a retainer on the door and pulls out once the door is raised and supported. Old seals go hard and split, and a concrete floor that has settled leaves a gap even with a new one. A deeper seal profile handles that. I finish with fresh stop moulding down both sides and across the top.\"}]','door-repair,lock-installation,pressure-washing,fence-gate-repair,handyman-services','',1,21,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (22,'eavestrough-cleaning','Eavestrough and Gutter Cleaning','Eavestrough Cleaning Across Toronto and the GTA','fa-solid fa-cloud-showers-heavy','eavestrough cleaning toronto, gutter cleaning toronto, eavestrough cleaning near me, downspout cleaning, gutter cleaning gta, eavestrough repair toronto, clogged gutter cleaning, downspout extension installation',0,'Eavestrough Cleaning in Toronto and the GTA','Eavestrough cleaning across the Greater Toronto Area: troughs and downspouts cleared and flushed, loose hangers refastened. Ask for a free quote.','Eavestrough cleaning is a job that costs nothing to ignore until it costs a great deal. A blocked trough overflows down the wall, soaks the soil against the foundation and finds its way into the basement. In winter that same blockage freezes, holds meltwater on the roof edge and starts an ice dam. Twice a year, once in spring and again after the leaves drop, keeps that from happening.\n\nHand clearing is what I do, not a blower from the ground. Debris comes out into a bucket, the troughs and downspouts get flushed with a hose, and I check the hangers and the fall while I am up there. I take single-storey homes and two-storey homes across the GTA where a ladder foots safely on level ground.','<h2>Why eavestrough cleaning matters more than it looks</h2><p>Rainwater off a typical Toronto semi runs to a few hundred litres in a single storm, and every drop of it is supposed to leave through the trough and the downspout. Block that path and the water finds another one. It runs behind the fascia and rots the board. It sheets down the brick and stains it. Worst of all it dumps at the base of the wall, saturates the backfill and puts pressure on a basement that was never meant to take it.</p><p>Nesting birds, maple keys, shingle grit and the shredded leaves that fall late all end up as a dense mat in the low end of the trough. That mat holds water, and standing water is heavy enough to pull hangers out of the fascia over a season or two.</p><h2>How I clear a trough properly</h2><p>Scooping by hand beats every shortcut. A leaf blower on a roof edge throws the debris into the neighbour\'s yard and packs the wet sludge harder into the outlet. My method is slow and boring, which is the point.</p><ul><li>Set and foot the ladder on level ground with a stabiliser off the trough</li><li>Hand scoop debris into a bucket, never over the side</li><li>Flush the full run with a hose to prove the fall</li><li>Clear each downspout from the top and check flow at the bottom</li><li>Snake or split a stubborn elbow rather than force it</li><li>Wipe the trough face and rinse anything that landed on the siding</li><li>Bag the debris and take it away</li></ul><p>The flush is the part people skip and the part that proves the job. A trough can look spotless and still hold water because it has lost its fall or because the outlet is choked at the elbow behind the downspout. Water tells you the truth in about thirty seconds.</p><h2>Small repairs while the ladder is up</h2><p>Cleaning and fixing go together, so I carry spikes, hidden hangers, screws and sealant on every eavestrough cleaning visit. A hanger that has worked loose gets refastened into solid fascia, not back into the same chewed hole. A section that sags and ponds gets lifted and re-hung to a proper fall, roughly six millimetres over every three metres (about a quarter inch in ten feet). Open end caps and dried-out seams get cleaned back and re-sealed with a gutter sealant that stays flexible in the cold.</p><p>Loose downspouts are the other common find. Straps pull off brick, elbows separate, and the bottom section ends up dumping right at the wall. Refastening those and adding a proper extension is quick work once the ladder is already set.</p><h2>Guards, extensions and ice dams</h2><p>Gutter guards help, within limits. A decent mesh or micro-mesh guard keeps leaves and maple keys out and turns a twice-yearly clean into a yearly check. No guard stops shingle grit and fine pollen from building a sludge layer, and the cheap plastic hedgehog inserts tend to trap debris rather than shed it. I will fit guards where the roof and trough profile suit them and say so when they do not.</p><p>Downspout extensions do more for a dry basement than almost anything else on the outside of a house. Water needs to land at least a metre and a half from the wall, on ground that slopes away. Ice dams are a roof and attic problem at heart, driven by heat escaping into the attic, but a trough packed with frozen debris makes it much worse, which is why the late autumn clean matters across the GTA.</p><h2>Where I draw the line on height</h2><p>Ladder work has a sensible ceiling, and I stay under it. Single-storey homes are straightforward. Two-storey homes are fine where I can set a ladder on firm, level ground with clear access and a stabiliser. Steep lots, third storeys, walkouts with a long drop to the trough and anything needing a lift or roof anchors are not my work, and I will tell you that before you book rather than after I arrive. Nothing about eavestrough cleaning is worth an ambulance.</p>','Hand clearing of all eavestrough runs\nDownspouts cleared and flushed from the top\nFull hose flush to confirm drainage\nLoose hangers and spikes refastened\nSagging sections re-hung to correct fall\nEnd caps and seams resealed\nDownspout extensions fitted away from the foundation\nDebris bagged and hauled away\nMesh gutter guard installation\nPhotos of the roofline sent afterwards','[{\"title\":\"Photos and a walk-round\",\"text\":\"Send photos of the front and back of the house, including the roofline, and tell me how many storeys and how many downspouts. That plus your address is usually enough for a firm quote.\"},{\"title\":\"Ladder set and clear\",\"text\":\"I set the ladder on firm ground with a standoff so it never rests on the trough itself, then work the runs section by section, scooping wet debris into a bucket instead of throwing it on the lawn.\"},{\"title\":\"Flush and fix\",\"text\":\"Every run and every downspout gets hosed through while I watch the outlet at ground level. Anything loose, sagging or leaking that I can put right from the ladder gets sorted before I move on.\"},{\"title\":\"Tidy up and report\",\"text\":\"Debris leaves with me, the driveway and beds get rinsed, and you get photos of the cleared troughs plus a plain note about anything I saw up there that needs watching.\"}]','Price on eavestroughs follows the length of run, the height and the state of what is up there. A bungalow with short runs and mesh guards already fitted is quick. A two-storey with a wraparound roofline, several downspouts and a season of packed maple keys takes far longer. Tree cover matters, so does access, since a fenced side yard or a deck under the trough slows the ladder down. Repairs and extensions are quoted on top once I can see what is needed, and everything is agreed before I begin.','[{\"q\":\"How often do eavestroughs need cleaning?\",\"a\":\"Twice a year is the honest answer for most homes with mature trees nearby: once in late spring after the maple keys and blossom drop, and again in late autumn once the last leaves are down. A house with no trees overhead can often go once a year. If you see water spilling over the front lip during a downpour, it needs doing regardless of the calendar.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I need to be home while you do it?\",\"a\":\"No. All the work happens outside, so as long as I can reach the sides of the house and get to an outdoor tap for the flush, you can be at work. I send photos of the troughs before and after so you can see what came out. If gates are locked or a dog is in the yard, just let me know beforehand.\"},{\"q\":\"Are gutter guards worth fitting?\",\"a\":\"They are worth it under heavy tree cover, and less so on an open lot. Good mesh keeps leaves and seeds out and cuts the cleaning down to an annual check rather than a proper clear-out. Nothing keeps shingle grit and fine debris out entirely, so guards reduce the work instead of ending it. Anyone telling you a guard means never touching the troughs again is overselling.\"},{\"q\":\"My trough leaks at a joint. Can that be repaired or does it need replacing?\",\"a\":\"Most leaks at a corner, an end cap or a seam can be repaired. The trick is preparation: the old sealant has to be scraped right out and the metal cleaned and fully dry, otherwise the new bead peels within a season. A proper gutter sealant stays flexible through a cold winter. If the aluminium is split, corroded through or badly bent, replacement of that section is the honest answer.\"},{\"q\":\"Will you go on the roof?\",\"a\":\"I work from a ladder, not from the roof surface. That keeps me off shingles that can be brittle in cold weather and it keeps my weight where it belongs. Troughs are reachable from a ladder on almost every home I visit. If a section genuinely cannot be reached safely from ground level, I will say so and suggest a company set up for roof access.\"}]','pressure-washing,deck-repair,fence-gate-repair,rental-property-maintenance,handyman-services','',1,22,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (23,'pressure-washing','Pressure Washing','Pressure Washing in Toronto and the GTA','fa-solid fa-spray-can-sparkles','pressure washing toronto, power washing driveway, deck pressure washing, house siding washing, patio cleaning toronto, pressure washing near me, driveway cleaning gta, fence pressure washing',0,'Pressure Washing Driveways and Decks in Toronto','Pressure washing for driveways, patios, decks, fences and siding across the Greater Toronto Area, at the right pressure for each surface. Get a free quote.','Pressure washing looks simple from the outside and it is, right up until the wrong nozzle meets the wrong surface. Too much pressure carves lines into a concrete driveway, strips the soft grain out of a cedar deck and drives water behind vinyl siding. Too little and the algae stays put. The skill is picking the pressure, the nozzle and the standoff distance for what is actually in front of you.\n\nAround the GTA the usual jobs are winter road salt and grime off a driveway, green algae off a north-facing walkway, and years of grey off a deck before it gets re-stained. I clean driveways, walkways, patios, steps, decks, fences, garage doors and siding, and I pre-treat where the staining needs chemistry rather than force.','<h2>Driveways, walkways and interlock</h2><p>Concrete and interlock take the worst of a Toronto winter. Salt, sand, oil drips, tire marks and a black film of algae along the shaded edge all sit in the surface pores. A surface cleaner attachment is the right tool here rather than a bare wand: it spins two jets under a shroud, covers a wide path evenly and leaves none of the zebra striping you get from waving a wand about. Oil patches get a degreaser first and a dwell time, because pressure alone pushes oil deeper into concrete instead of lifting it.</p><p>Interlock brings one extra step. Washing pulls polymeric sand out of the joints, so those joints need topping up and re-setting afterwards or the pavers start to shift and weeds return quickly. Anyone who leaves an interlock patio with hollow joints has done half a job.</p><h2>Matching pressure washing to the surface</h2><p>Every pressure washing job starts with two decisions: how much pressure, and which nozzle. Nozzles are colour coded by spray angle, and the wider the fan the gentler the hit. A narrow red tip belongs almost nowhere on a house. Standoff distance does as much work as the dial, since the same machine is aggressive at ten centimetres and mild at fifty.</p><ul><li>Concrete and interlock: high pressure with a surface cleaner</li><li>Cedar and pressure-treated wood: low pressure, wide fan, kept moving</li><li>Vinyl and aluminium siding: low pressure, always washing downward at the laps</li><li>Brick and mortar: moderate pressure, never a narrow tip at a joint</li><li>Painted or stained wood: a cleaning solution first, then the lightest rinse that works</li><li>Composite decking: manufacturer limits respected, soap and a soft brush for the rest</li></ul><p>Testing in a hidden corner takes a minute and settles the argument. I do it on every surface I have not washed before, because a mortar joint or a soft board tells you very quickly what it will tolerate.</p><h2>Decks and fences without the fuzz</h2><p>Wood is where most damage happens. Cedar and pressure-treated softwood are soft between the growth rings, and too much pressure blasts that soft wood away, leaving a furry, raised grain that drinks stain unevenly and never looks right again. The correct approach is a wide fan at low pressure, held well back, worked along the length of each board rather than across it, with an even pass so there are no dark stripes where the wand paused.</p><p>Grey, weathered wood usually needs a cleaner rather than more force. A deck cleaner lifts the dead surface fibres and the mildew, then a rinse takes it away. Where the boards will be re-stained afterwards, a brightener brings the pH back down and opens the grain so the new stain actually penetrates. Skipping that step is why so many re-stained decks look blotchy by the following summer.</p><h2>Siding, garage doors and brick</h2><p>Siding is a rinse, not a blast. Vinyl and aluminium laps are designed to shed water running down, so washing upward under the laps forces water into the wall cavity where it has no way out. Low pressure from above, working down, with a soap applied first and given time to work, takes off the green film and the pollen without soaking the sheathing. Around windows and doors I keep the wand well away from the caulking joints.</p><p>Garage doors and painted steel doors clean up beautifully at modest pressure and repay a wax afterwards. Brick and block need more care than people expect: mortar joints are softer than the brick, and a narrow tip held close will cut mortar out of a joint in seconds. Older homes across the Greater Toronto Area with lime mortar get the gentlest setting that does the job, or a soft wash instead.</p><h2>Before, during and after</h2><p>Preparation saves trouble. Plants get soaked with clean water and covered where a cleaning solution is in play, outdoor outlets and light fittings get taped, windows get shut and checked, and furniture and the barbecue move off the deck. Runoff needs somewhere sensible to go, which on a sloped city lot is worth thinking about before the machine starts rather than after.</p><p>Results are honest, not magical. Pressure washing removes what sits on a surface: dirt, salt film, algae, mildew, moss, cobwebs and loose material. It does not remove rust bleed, deep oil that has soaked in for years, tire scuffing burned into concrete, or the shadow left where something sat in the sun for a decade. I will tell you before I start which marks are likely to lift and which are permanent.</p>','Driveway and walkway washing with surface cleaner\nInterlock cleaning with joint sand topped up\nDeck washing at safe low pressure\nFence cleaning before staining or sealing\nVinyl and aluminium siding soft washing\nGarage door and entry door cleaning\nPatio, steps and porch cleaning\nDegreasing of oil stained concrete\nAlgae and moss pre-treatment\nSalt film removal after winter','[{\"title\":\"Photos and surface list\",\"text\":\"Send photos of the driveway, deck or wall and tell me the rough size and the surface material. Wood, interlock and vinyl each need a different setup, so knowing that up front makes the quote accurate.\"},{\"title\":\"Protect and test\",\"text\":\"On the day I move furniture and planters, wet down and cover nearby plants, tape vulnerable fittings and run a test patch in a hidden spot to confirm the pressure and nozzle before the first full pass.\"},{\"title\":\"Treat, wash and rinse\",\"text\":\"Heavy algae, mildew and oil get a cleaner and a dwell time first. Then the wash itself runs in overlapping passes so nothing stripes, and everything below the work gets a final rinse of clean water.\"},{\"title\":\"Reset and walk-round\",\"text\":\"Joint sand goes back into interlock, furniture returns where it belongs, hoses and covers come off, and you and I walk the area together so you can point at anything you want another pass over.\"}]','Square area is the main driver, then how dirty the surface is and what it is made of. A clean concrete drive that gets washed every spring goes quickly. A north-facing walkway under heavy moss, or a deck that has not been touched in years and needs a cleaner and a brightener, takes far longer. Interlock costs more than plain concrete because the joints have to be re-sanded. Water access, a power outlet and how far I have to drag hose all factor in. You get the figure before I unload.','[{\"q\":\"Will pressure washing damage my interlock or my deck?\",\"a\":\"Not if the pressure and nozzle suit the surface. Damage happens when someone uses a narrow tip at full pressure on soft cedar or aged mortar, or holds the wand too close. Interlock is durable but loses joint sand, so I top the joints back up afterwards. Wood gets a wide fan at low pressure, worked along the boards. If a surface is fragile, I say so and clean it more gently.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you need my water and power?\",\"a\":\"Yes, an outdoor tap with decent flow and a standard outlet are all I need. Flow matters more than pressure at the tap, so if your outside tap dribbles, let me know and I will bring a longer hose to reach a better one. If a tap has been shut off inside for winter, please turn it back on before I arrive.\"},{\"q\":\"How soon after washing can a deck be stained?\",\"a\":\"Not straight away, and rushing it is the most common mistake I see. Wood has to dry right through before any stain or sealer goes on, which normally means a couple of dry days depending on the weather and how much sun the deck gets. Staining damp boards traps moisture underneath and the finish peels within a season. I clean, let it dry, then come back to stain if you want that done.\"},{\"q\":\"Will the oil stain on my driveway come out?\",\"a\":\"Some of it usually will. Fresh oil that has not soaked far responds well to a degreaser, a dwell time and a stiff brush before the rinse. Oil that has been sitting in porous concrete for years has gone deep into the pores and will lighten rather than disappear. Rust bleed from a planter or a car part behaves the same way. I would rather set that expectation before I start than after.\"},{\"q\":\"What time of year is best?\",\"a\":\"Late spring once the risk of frost has passed is ideal, because the salt film and sand from winter are still sitting there and everything has time to dry. Early autumn works well too, especially before a deck gets sealed. I avoid washing when temperatures are near freezing, since water on a walkway turns into a skating rink and cleaners stop working properly in the cold.\"}]','deck-repair,fence-gate-repair,eavestrough-cleaning,garage-door-repair,handyman-services','',1,23,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (24,'condo-handyman','Condo Handyman Services','Condo Handyman Services in Toronto and the GTA','fa-solid fa-building','condo handyman toronto, handyman for condos gta, mounting tv on concrete wall condo, condo blinds installation toronto, apartment handyman toronto, condo repairs toronto, hang shelves concrete wall, condo storage solutions',0,'Condo Handyman in Toronto and the GTA','Condo handyman work across Toronto and the GTA: mounting into concrete and block, blinds, closets, shelving and small repairs. Ask for a free quote.','A condo handyman needs a different toolkit and different manners than someone working on a detached house. Walls are poured concrete or hollow block instead of studs and drywall. The corridor is shared, the service elevator has to be booked, and the building has rules about noise, balconies and who comes through the loading dock. I work in condo and apartment suites across Toronto.\n\nSmall suites also change what a job is. Storage has to go up the wall because there is no floor to spare, a TV has to sit exactly where the outlet and the concrete allow, and every hole drilled is one you may have to make good when you sell. Careful, tidy, quiet work matters more here than anywhere.','<h2>Concrete, block and drywall over concrete</h2><p>The first thing any condo handyman should do is work out what is behind the paint. Interior partitions between rooms are usually steel-stud drywall and behave normally. Demising walls between suites, and the walls around the corridor, are often concrete or concrete block with a thin layer of drywall furred out in front. Tapping tells you a lot, a magnet finds steel studs, and a small pilot hole confirms it before anything bigger goes in.</p><p>Each of those walls wants a different anchor. Steel studs take self-drilling toggles rather than wood screws. Hollow block takes a properly rated toggle that opens inside the void. Solid concrete takes a masonry bit in a hammer drill and a sleeve or wedge anchor sized for the load. Plastic plugs pushed into a drywall skin over concrete hold nothing at all, and they are the reason so many shelves end up on the floor.</p><h2>What a condo handyman gets asked for most</h2><p>Requests barely change from building to building across the Greater Toronto Area.</p><ul><li>TVs mounted flush or on articulating arms over concrete</li><li>Blinds, roller shades and curtain tracks fitted to concrete ceilings and headers</li><li>Floating shelves, picture ledges and gallery walls set out and levelled</li><li>Closet organisers, shoe racks and over-door storage</li><li>Mirrors, coat hooks and towel bars in tiled bathrooms</li><li>Faucets, shower heads, toilet seats and vanity swaps</li><li>Interior doors adjusted, hinges tightened, handles and locks changed</li><li>Cabinet doors realigned and drawer runners replaced</li></ul><p>Because getting a condo handyman into a building takes planning, batching pays off. Most people save up a list, and I would rather come once and work through eight items than make four short visits. Send the list with photos and I will tell you honestly what fits in one booking.</p><h2>Elevators, quiet hours and building rules</h2><p>Every building runs its own system, and the newer Toronto towers tend to be the strictest. It pays to check yours before booking anything. Many require the service elevator to be reserved for deliveries or for any contractor bringing tools and materials through, and some restrict work to weekday daytime hours so neighbours are not listening to a hammer drill on a Sunday morning. Drilling concrete is loud and it carries through a slab, so I get the noisy part done early in the visit and work quietly after that.</p><p>Property managers often ask for paperwork before they will let a contractor start. Tell me what your building requires and I will tell you plainly whether I can meet it, before you book anything. Balconies come with their own rules as well: many corporations do not permit drilling into the slab, the railing or the exterior wall, so balcony work usually means free-standing solutions rather than fixed ones.</p><h2>Getting more out of a small suite</h2><p>Vertical space is the only space a condo has going spare. A run of shelves above a door, a proper closet system with double hanging and drawers, a wall-mounted bike rack, a fold-down desk or a floating nightstand all buy back floor without making the room feel smaller. Setting them out matters as much as fixing them: shelves lined up with a window head or a door casing look deliberate, and shelves at a random height look like an afterthought.</p><p>Dust control is part of the job in a suite you live in. Drilling concrete throws grey dust everywhere, so I drill with a vacuum nozzle held at the bit, drop sheets go down before anything starts, and the suite gets cleaned before I leave. That matters far more in a one-bedroom suite than in a house with a garage to work in.</p><h2>Jobs that belong to a licensed trade</h2><p>Swapping a light fixture, a dimmer, a faucet or a toilet on existing connections is straightforward and I do it often. Anything past that point in a condo goes to the right trade. New wiring, a panel, moving a plumbing rough-in, cutting into a demising wall, touching the sprinkler system, the in-suite heat pump or the make-up air unit is not handyman work, and in most buildings it needs the corporation\'s approval as well. I refer those to a licensed electrician or plumber and say so at the quote stage rather than halfway through.</p><p>Honesty on that line saves everybody money. Turning up, starting, then stopping is the worst outcome for a condo handyman and for the person paying, so I would rather sort it out over photos and a message first.</p>','TV mounting on concrete and block walls\nBlinds and roller shades fitted to concrete\nFloating shelves levelled and properly anchored\nCloset organisers and storage systems installed\nMirrors and artwork hung on masonry\nBathroom towel bars and hooks in tile\nFaucet, shower head and toilet seat swaps\nInterior door and hardware adjustments\nCabinet door alignment and drawer runners\nSeveral small suite jobs in one visit','[{\"title\":\"Send the list\",\"text\":\"Message me your whole list with photos of each spot and a wide shot of the room. Photos of the wall and the wall around it tell me the anchor type and how long the visit needs to be.\"},{\"title\":\"Check the building rules\",\"text\":\"Before the date is set, check with your concierge or property manager about the service elevator, permitted work hours and any paperwork they want from a contractor. I will tell you straight away whether I can meet what they ask.\"},{\"title\":\"Noisy work first\",\"text\":\"Drop sheets go down, then the drilling into concrete gets done in one block early on, with a vacuum at the bit. Quieter assembly, levelling and adjustment follow so the neighbours get one short burst of noise instead of several.\"},{\"title\":\"Clean and check off\",\"text\":\"Every item on the list gets ticked off with you, the concrete dust gets vacuumed, packaging leaves with me, and anything that turned out to need a licensed trade gets explained clearly before I go.\"}]','The wall behind the paint and the length of your list are what move the number on a condo job. Anchoring into solid concrete takes longer than a steel-stud partition and eats masonry bits, so a single TV over concrete is more work than it looks. A long list brings the cost per item down, because the setup and the elevator booking only happen once. It also matters if you supply the mount, the blinds or the shelving yourself. I quote the whole visit before booking it, and the figure does not move unless the job does.','[{\"q\":\"Can a TV be mounted on a concrete condo wall?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and concrete is actually the strongest thing you can hang a television on. It needs a hammer drill, a masonry bit and sleeve or wedge anchors rated well above the weight of the set and the bracket. The catch is cable management, since you cannot run wires inside solid concrete. A surface raceway painted to match the wall, or a slim conduit down to the console, is the usual answer.\"},{\"q\":\"Does my building need to approve the work?\",\"a\":\"It depends on what you are having done. Hanging shelves, mounting a television, fitting blinds and swapping a faucet inside your own suite are normally your business alone. Anything touching a demising wall, the sprinklers, the heating unit, the balcony or a common element usually needs the corporation\'s sign-off. Ask your property manager if you are unsure, and send me their answer before the visit so nobody is surprised.\"},{\"q\":\"How loud is drilling into concrete, and when can it be done?\",\"a\":\"Honestly, it is loud, and the sound travels through the slab to the suites above and below. Each hole only takes seconds though, so I plan the drilling into one short stretch near the start of the visit and keep the rest quiet. Most buildings allow work through weekday daytime hours. If yours has tighter quiet hours, tell me and I will schedule around them.\"},{\"q\":\"I am renting. Should I ask the landlord first?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and it is worth getting the answer in writing. Most leases allow small fixings and many landlords are happy with shelves or a television bracket, but the holes are yours to make good when you move out. If you would rather not drill, there are tension rods, freestanding shelving and heavy-duty adhesive systems that work well for lighter items. I am happy to go that route instead.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you hang something on my balcony?\",\"a\":\"Usually not by drilling. Most condo corporations treat the balcony slab, railing and exterior wall as common elements and forbid fixing anything to them, partly because of the waterproofing membrane under the surface. What does work is free-standing: railing planters that clamp without penetrating, weighted screens, outdoor rugs and freestanding shelving. Check your declaration or ask the property manager, and I will build around whatever they allow.\"}]','tv-mounting,shelf-mirror-installation,furniture-assembly,light-fixture-installation,handyman-services','',1,24,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (25,'rental-property-maintenance','Rental and Property Maintenance','Rental Property Maintenance Across the GTA','fa-solid fa-key','rental property maintenance toronto, landlord handyman toronto, handyman for rental properties, tenant turnover repairs gta, make ready repairs toronto, rental unit repairs, property management handyman gta, apartment repairs for landlords',0,'Rental Property Maintenance in Toronto','Rental property maintenance across the GTA: turnover punch lists, tenant-reported repairs and make-ready work by one handyman. Send your list for a quote.','Rental property maintenance is a large part of what I do across the Greater Toronto Area, and it runs on two clocks: the empty unit with a deadline, and the occupied unit where something has just broken. I am Sercan, and I handle both myself, from turnover punch lists to tenant-reported faults to the small repairs that quietly cost you a renewal.\n\nLandlords, small investors and property managers tend to want the same three things from a handyman: a fix that holds up to tenant use, a schedule that does not create complaints, and a record of what was done. You get photos of the problem, photos of the finished work, and an invoice that matches the price agreed before I started.','<h2>Rental property maintenance, start to finish</h2><p>Most rental property maintenance runs on two rhythms. There is the empty unit with a deadline, where a dozen small jobs have to be finished before the next tenant collects the keys. Then there is the occupied unit, where one thing has failed, someone is living around it, and the repair has to be quick, tidy and quiet. I cover both for owners and property managers across Toronto.</p><p>Because I do the work myself, you deal with one person from the first message to the final invoice. Nothing is lost between a booking desk and a crew, and nobody turns up at your unit who has not read your list.</p><h2>Turnovers and make-ready punch lists</h2><p>Send the punch list and I will price the visit as a whole rather than item by item. A make-ready runs in a set order: fill and sand wall damage, spot prime the repairs, cut in and roll the walls, adjust doors and strikes so they latch without a shove, swap failed fixtures, cut out and renew the bathroom caulking, then refit hardware, blinds and cover plates last so they stay clean.</p><p>Sequence is most of the skill. Paint over filler that has not cured and the patch will telegraph through within weeks. Caulk a tub before the surround is properly dry and the bead lifts the first time someone runs a hot shower. Turnovers in the GTA usually go wrong on small ordering mistakes like those, not on the big items.</p><ul><li><strong>Walls and ceilings</strong> — anchor holes filled, gouges patched, repairs feathered and repainted</li><li><strong>Doors</strong> — sticking edges eased, hinges reset, strikes and door stops replaced</li><li><strong>Kitchens</strong> — cabinet doors realigned, drawer runners renewed, handles tightened</li><li><strong>Bathrooms</strong> — old caulking cut out and replaced, toilet seats, fill valves and flappers</li><li><strong>Fixtures</strong> — like-for-like swaps of faucets, light fittings, switches and dimmers</li><li><strong>Final pass</strong> — blinds rehung, window screens repaired, battery smoke alarms remounted</li></ul><h2>Tenant-reported repairs and getting access</h2><p>Occupied units need a plan for entry before anything else. Ontario asks for written notice at least twenty-four hours ahead of a non-emergency visit, with a stated window falling between eight in the morning and eight at night. You issue the notice, I confirm the time with the tenant and send a text before I set off. That one habit removes most of the friction from rental property maintenance.</p><p>Batching helps as well. If three tenants in the same building have each reported something small, one visit clears all three and each household is disturbed once instead of three times. Tell me who works nights or has a baby napping in the afternoon and I will schedule around it where the calendar allows.</p><h2>What makes a repair hold in a rental</h2><p>Finishes in a rental take more punishment than in an owner-occupied home, so the method has to be tougher than a quick fill. A towel bar that has pulled out of drywall goes back on a toggle anchor rated for the load, never the plastic plug it arrived with. A patch wider than about 15 cm (6 in) gets a backing board or a hot patch behind it, because tape over an unsupported void cracks again the first time the door slams.</p><p>Hardware follows the same logic. Stripped cabinet hinge holes get plugged with glued hardwood dowel and re-drilled rather than driven with a longer screw that splits the stile. A loose door strike is fixed with screws long enough to reach the framing behind the jamb. Bathroom joints get the old bead cut out completely and the surface dried before fresh sanitary silicone goes on.</p><p>Material choice does the rest. Eggshell or satin wall paint can be wiped clean where flat paint burnishes and shows every rub. Lever handles outlast round knobs under heavy use. A cushioned stop on the wall behind a door is far less trouble than patching the same hole at the end of every lease.</p><h2>Photos, paperwork and the jobs I hand over</h2><p>Every visit ends the same way: the work area cleaned up, photos of what I found and what I did, and an invoice that matches the quote. Owners living outside the country get the photo set the same evening. If you look after units for other people, I can split invoices by address so the paperwork lands where it belongs.</p><p>Some items are not mine to touch. New circuits, panel work, moving plumbing rough-ins, gas appliances and anything structural go to a licensed electrician, plumber or contractor, and I will say so at quote stage rather than halfway through. Swapping an existing light fixture, faucet, toilet or vanity is ordinary rental property maintenance and part of most punch lists I take on.</p>','Make-ready punch lists between tenancies\nDrywall patching and touch-up painting\nDoors adjusted, strikes and stops replaced\nFaucet, fill valve and flapper swaps\nCabinet doors realigned and hardware tightened\nBathroom caulking cut out and renewed\nBlinds, curtain rods and shelves refitted\nWindow and patio screens repaired\nLike-for-like light fixture and switch swaps\nBefore and after photos with every invoice','[{\"title\":\"Send the punch list\",\"text\":\"Email or text the list of what needs doing, unit by unit, with photos where you have them. Photos of wall damage, a leaking fixture or a failing door tell me what to bring, and they usually let me price without a site visit.\"},{\"title\":\"Quote before booking\",\"text\":\"You get a fixed price for the visit or an hourly rate, agreed before anything starts. I reply to most quote requests within one business day, and I will flag anything on the list that needs a licensed trade instead.\"},{\"title\":\"Access arranged around tenants\",\"text\":\"For an empty unit, a lockbox or key handover is enough. For an occupied one, you issue the written notice the province requires and I confirm the window directly with the tenant, then text on the way.\"},{\"title\":\"Photos and invoice\",\"text\":\"The list gets worked through in one visit where possible, the area is left clean, and you receive before and after photos with the invoice. Material receipts come with it if I bought anything for the job.\"}]','What a visit costs follows the list, not the door. A unit with a long punch list is far better value per item than a single call-out, which is why saving jobs up pays. Damage that was patched badly before takes longer than fresh damage. Paint quantity, the condition of the hardware, elevator booking and parking in a condo building, and who supplies the materials all move the figure. I quote free, you get a fixed price or an hourly rate agreed before I start, and receipts follow anything I buy.','[{\"q\":\"Can you arrange access with my tenant directly?\",\"a\":\"Yes, once the tenant has had proper written notice from you. Ontario asks for at least twenty-four hours notice in writing for a non-emergency entry, with a window stated between eight in the morning and eight at night. Pass me the tenant contact and I will confirm the time and text before setting off. Most tenants would rather deal with the same person twice than a different crew every visit.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I need to be at the unit while you work?\",\"a\":\"No. Plenty of the landlords I work for live in another part of the GTA, another province or another country. Give me lockbox or key access and I will send photos as I go, then a final set showing the finished work and the unit left clean. If I find something you did not know about, you get a photo and a price before I touch it.\"},{\"q\":\"Can a unit be turned around between two tenancies in a few days?\",\"a\":\"Often, if the list reaches me before the old tenant is out. Booking early is what makes a short turnover work. Paint needs drying time between coats, filler has to cure before sanding, and fresh caulking needs hours before water hits it, so the calendar matters more than the hours on site. Send photos with the list and I can plan the sequence before I arrive.\"},{\"q\":\"How do you handle materials and receipts?\",\"a\":\"Either way works. I can buy the paint, hardware and parts and hand you the receipts with the invoice, or you supply your own if you keep stock across several units. Owners with a few properties usually settle on one paint colour and one hardware finish. That single decision makes repairs faster, keeps touch-ups invisible, and means leftovers from one unit finish the next one.\"},{\"q\":\"What happens if a repair does not hold?\",\"a\":\"Tell me and I will come back. I stand behind my own workmanship, and something that fails in normal use gets put right on my own time. What I cannot carry is fresh damage from tenant use, or a part you asked me to reuse that was already at the end of its life. I will always say up front when a fix is a patch rather than a proper repair.\"},{\"q\":\"Do you look after more than one property for the same owner?\",\"a\":\"Multiple properties are straightforward, and several units in one building are the easiest work I do because everything is covered in a single trip. Some owners send me one unit a year, others send a list every month. If you manage buildings for other people, invoices can be split by address and unit so the costs land against the right property.\"}]','handyman-services,drywall-repair,interior-painting,condo-handyman,bathroom-caulking-repair','',1,25,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_services` (`id`, `slug`, `name`, `h1`, `icon`, `keywords`, `is_pillar`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `body_html`, `jobs`, `process_json`, `pricing_notes`, `faqs_json`, `related`, `hero_image`, `is_published`, `sort_order`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (26,'commercial-handyman','Office and Commercial Handyman','Commercial Handyman for Toronto Offices and Shops','fa-solid fa-briefcase','commercial handyman toronto, office handyman services, retail store repairs toronto, commercial property maintenance gta, handyman for small business toronto, shop fitting repairs, office maintenance services gta, restaurant repairs toronto',0,'Commercial Handyman in Toronto and the GTA','Commercial handyman for offices, shops, clinics and restaurants in the GTA. Repairs, hardware and fixtures fitted around your opening hours. Ask for a quote.','A commercial handyman is the person you call when the entrance door starts slamming, a shelf standard pulls loose, or the washroom faucet drips in front of paying customers. I am Sercan, and I handle that work myself for small offices, retail units, clinics, salons and independent restaurants across the Greater Toronto Area.\n\nMost of that work is booked outside trading hours. Early mornings before the doors open, evenings after the last customer, or a slow weekday when the floor is empty. Hours are Monday to Saturday, eight in the morning until eight at night, with Sunday by appointment, and I usually reply to a quote request within one business day.','<h2>What a commercial handyman actually covers</h2><p>Businesses collect a long tail of small faults: the door that slams, the shelf standard pulling away from the wall, the grout going black at the washroom sink. None of it justifies a specialist contractor, and all of it is visible to the people paying your bills. That backlog is what a commercial handyman is for.</p><p>My customers are small offices, retail units, clinics, salons and independent restaurants around Toronto. The list can be one urgent item or twenty things saved up since spring, and a saved list is nearly always the better buy.</p><ul><li><strong>Doors and hardware</strong> — closers, hinges, strikes, sweeps, kick plates</li><li><strong>Fixtures</strong> — shelving, mirrors, whiteboards, blinds and coat rails</li><li><strong>Signage</strong> — lettering, menu boards and panels mounted level</li><li><strong>Washrooms</strong> — faucets, seats, dispensers, grab rails, caulking, grout</li><li><strong>Finishes</strong> — drywall damage, trim repairs, touch-up painting</li><li><strong>Fit-out extras</strong> — desks, storage and reception furniture assembled</li></ul><h2>Work booked around your opening hours</h2><p>Timing is the part most owners care about, so nearly all the commercial handyman work I do happens before opening, after the last customer leaves, or during a slow midweek stretch. Booking a few days out means I arrive with everything needed, with no supply run halfway through.</p><p>Dust sheets and floor protection go down before any tool comes out. A vacuum runs on the drywall sander, cordless tools keep the noise down, and the floor is clean and everything packed away before staff arrive. Dust that cannot be contained is better split over two evenings than handed back as a space you cannot open.</p><h2>Doors, closers and hardware under heavy traffic</h2><p>An entrance door in a busy shop cycles hundreds of times a day. Most closers have separate valves for sweep speed and latch speed, and often a backcheck, so a door that bangs shut normally needs the latch valve turned down rather than a new closer. A closer weeping oil is finished, and no amount of adjustment brings it back.</p><p>Hinge screws pulled out of a wooden jamb can be re-anchored with screws long enough to reach the framing behind, or with glued dowel in the stripped holes. Hollow metal frames work differently, since the threads are in the frame itself and a stripped hole needs re-tapping or a proper repair rather than packing. A latch catching on the strike is nearly always alignment, not a broken lock.</p><p>Fire-rated doors have rules of their own. Door, frame, hinges, closer and latch are approved as a labelled set, so if a door is part of a rated assembly I will say so and leave it to the specialists who inspect them. The same goes for panic hardware on an exit route.</p><h2>Shelving, signage and display fixtures</h2><p>Fixing method depends on what sits behind the finish, and commercial walls are rarely simple wood studs. Steel studs are thin and will not hold a coarse drywall screw under load, so a standard either lands in the stud with the correct fine-thread screw or hangs on toggles rated for the weight. Concrete block takes an anchor set into the solid web, never the mortar joint. Poured concrete needs the right drill diameter and depth, with the hole blown clean before the anchor goes in.</p><p>Levelling matters as much as anchoring. A laser line across the whole run beats measuring down from a ceiling that is not flat, and the load is shared across every bracket, not just the two on the ends. Heavy signage goes on a French cleat so the weight sits along the wall and the panel lifts off for painting.</p><h2>Washrooms, kitchens and a standing list</h2><p>Washrooms are where customers quietly judge a business. Faucet and seat swaps, flush valves, soap and paper dispensers refitted to anchors that hold, grout made good, and old caulking cut out and replaced with sanitary silicone are all routine. Grab rails are the exception worth naming: they must land in solid blocking or be through-bolted, never held by drywall anchors alone.</p><p>Keeping a running list works better than calling on every fault. A recurring commercial handyman visit, monthly or quarterly depending on how hard the space is used, clears the lot in one booking and catches problems while they are small. A hinge tightened this season is a door that still closes next year, and plenty of small businesses across the GTA work that way.</p><p>Some work is not mine to do, and saying so early saves everyone time. Gas connections, exhaust hoods, fire suppression, sprinkler heads, HVAC and new electrical circuits go to the licensed trade that covers them. Swapping an existing light fitting, switch, faucet or door closer is ordinary handyman work, and I will tell you plainly which side of that line your job sits on.</p>','Door closers adjusted, replaced or rehung\nShelving and display standards anchored properly\nSignage and wall panels mounted level\nWashroom faucets, seats and dispensers replaced\nWall repairs and touch-up painting after hours\nOffice furniture and workstations assembled\nMirrors, whiteboards and blinds installed\nRecurring maintenance visits on a set schedule','[{\"title\":\"Walk the space\",\"text\":\"I come by during a quiet hour, or work from your photos, and write down every item with the fixing method it needs. Steel studs, block walls and rated doors all change the plan, so it is worth checking first.\"},{\"title\":\"Quote and time window\",\"text\":\"You get the price in writing, fixed or hourly, along with the slot that suits the business: before opening, after closing or a slow midweek morning. Nothing starts until the price and the timing are both agreed.\"},{\"title\":\"Off-peak visit\",\"text\":\"Protection goes down before tools come out, the work is done inside the agreed window, and the space is swept and put back so staff walk into a room that looks untouched apart from the repair.\"},{\"title\":\"Walk-round and invoice\",\"text\":\"Before I leave, or first thing the next morning, you check the finished items against the list. Photos and the invoice follow, and anything still outstanding goes onto the next scheduled visit rather than being forgotten.\"}]','Cost on a commercial job follows access and timing as much as the work itself. Evening and early-morning visits need setting up and packing down inside a fixed window. Anchoring into concrete or steel studs is slower than wood framing, and height means ladder or platform work. Quantity helps, since a saved-up list handled in one booking prices better than the same items spread over weeks. Supplied materials, protection of finished floors and shopfronts, and building access rules all count. Everything is agreed in writing before I start.','[{\"q\":\"Can you work after the shop closes for the day?\",\"a\":\"Yes, and for most retail and food businesses that is the sensible option. I work Monday to Saturday, eight in the morning to eight at night, with Sunday by appointment, so an early start before you open up or a session after the last customer is usually easy to arrange. Book a few days ahead and I will bring everything the job needs, so nothing stalls while you are shut.\"},{\"q\":\"Do I need my landlord\'s permission for work in a leased unit?\",\"a\":\"Usually, yes, and it is worth checking before I quote. Most commercial leases put anything fixed to the structure, the shopfront, or the sprinkler and alarm systems under the landlord\'s control. Reversible work such as shelving on a partition wall rarely raises an eyebrow, while drilling the storefront or cutting into a demising wall does. Get the approval in writing and the job runs without an awkward conversation later.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you mount signage, menu boards and shelving on the wall?\",\"a\":\"The fixing method depends on what is behind the finish, so that is the first thing I check. Steel stud partitions will not hold a coarse screw, so the fixing either lands in the stud with the right screw or goes on toggles rated for the load. Block walls take anchors in the solid web, not the mortar joint. Heavy signs go on a French cleat so the weight spreads.\"},{\"q\":\"What can you do in a restaurant and what needs a licensed trade?\",\"a\":\"I handle the non-gas, non-hood side: shelving, wall panels refixed, door hardware, washroom fixtures, grout and caulking, wobbly tables, damaged trim and paint touch-ups. Anything tied to gas, the exhaust hood, the fire suppression system, sprinklers or a new electrical circuit goes to the licensed trade for it, every time. Inspections and your own coverage both depend on that paperwork, so it is not a corner worth cutting.\"},{\"q\":\"Can you come on a regular schedule instead of only when things break?\",\"a\":\"That is how most of my small business customers end up working. Keep a running list rather than phoning on every fault, and I come through on a set rhythm, monthly or quarterly, and clear it in one visit. Problems get caught while they are still small, which is the whole point. It also makes the work predictable for your staff and easy to plan around.\"}]','handyman-services,door-repair,shelf-mirror-installation,lock-installation,drywall-repair','',1,26,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (1,10,1,'Bathroom Caulking and Repairs in Toronto','Bathroom Caulking and Repairs, Toronto','Bathroom caulking and repairs in Toronto, from mortar set tile in old semis to windowless condo bathrooms. Mould cut out, joints resealed. Ask for a quote.','Bathroom caulking and repairs in Toronto usually start with the same sight: a black line along the top of the tub that no cleaner will shift. Once silicone lets go, water gets behind it, and scrubbing only spreads the stain. I cut the old bead right out, dry the joint, and lay a fresh one.\n\nOld houses here often have tile set in a mortar bed, so a loose tile needs bedding rather than a smear of adhesive. Downtown condo bathrooms have no window at all. I also regrout, refit grab rails and swap tired exhaust fans while I am there.','In a pre-war Riverdale or Annex bathroom the wall tile sits in a thick mortar bed over wire lath, so a tile that has come loose leaves a deep void that has to be filled before it will sit flush again. The tubs are usually cast iron and barely move, which means the joint fails from age rather than flex. Downtown in the King West and CityPlace towers the bathroom is interior, with no window, and the fan ties into a shared exhaust riser the building controls.','Resealing a cast iron tub in a Riverdale semi\nRegrouting mortar set wall tile in an Annex bathroom\nMouldy shower caulking replaced in a King West condo\nLoose tiles rebedded in an old east end bathroom\nExhaust fan swapped in a windowless downtown unit','My old Toronto bathroom has no window. Will new caulking just go mouldy again?','Probably, if the room never dries out. A lot of older Toronto bathrooms have a small window painted shut or no window at all, and an original fan that barely pulls. Cutting out the old bead and sealing the joint properly buys you years, but only if moist air leaves the room. I check the fan while I am there and swap it if it is not doing its job.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (2,10,2,'Bathroom Caulking and Repairs in North York','Bathroom Caulking Repair in North York','Bathroom caulking in North York: mouldy tub joints, tired grout and loose tile put right. Old sealant cut out, never covered over. Get a free quote today.','Bathroom caulking and repairs in North York often mean working on an original bathroom that has never been touched. The pink or mint four inch wall tile in a Willowdale or Don Mills bungalow is set hard in mortar, and the grout has gone soft in the corners where water lands.\n\nI cut out the failed silicone at the tub, rake and refill tired grout, and reset any tile that has let go. If the bathroom sits in a basement suite or a Yonge Street condo, I plan around what the building or the tenant needs.','North York bungalows and back-splits from the fifties and sixties came with a pressed steel tub, and steel flexes underfoot. That movement is what splits a rigid bead, so the joint wants a flexible silicone and a tub filled with water while it cures. The other common call here is a basement second suite: a shower stall dropped into a below-grade room that stays cool, where condensation keeps the grout damp long after the shower is off.','Resealing a steel tub in a Willowdale bungalow\nRegrouting original four inch tile in Don Mills\nBasement suite shower stall resealed after mould\nVanity backsplash joint redone in a Bayview condo\nGrab rail fitted beside a Downsview bathtub\nNoisy exhaust fan replaced in a Sheppard apartment','The shower in my North York basement apartment keeps going mouldy. Can caulking fix that?','Fresh silicone will stop water sneaking behind the base, but a basement shower also needs air moving. Below-grade rooms stay cool, so steam condenses on the tile and sits there. I cut out the old bead, reseal the pan and corners, and check the fan actually vents outside rather than into a joist space. If it is weak I can swap it.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (3,10,3,'Bathroom Caulking and Repairs in Scarborough','Bathroom Caulking and Repairs, Scarborough','Bathroom caulking, regrouting and grab rails across Scarborough bungalows and apartment buildings. Old sealant removed, not covered up. Message me anytime.','Bathroom caulking and repairs in Scarborough often come down to one bathroom serving a whole household. In a Wexford or Bendale bungalow there is no second tub to fall back on, so I keep the disruption short and leave the fresh silicone to cure overnight.\n\nI cut out old mouldy beads, rake and refill grout, rebed loose tile and fit grab rails securely into the wall. Tell me the household routine and I will book a time that fits around it.','A lot of the rental towers along Kingston Road, Markham Road and Eglinton East in Scarborough date from the sixties and seventies, and their tub surrounds still carry the original tile. Over the decades those joints have been caulked over and painted over more than once, so there is a hard ridge of layered material to cut back to bare tile before anything new will stick. Bungalow owners here ask for something else again: a grab rail anchored well enough to trust getting out of the tub.','Tub surround resealed in an Agincourt rental apartment\nGrab rail anchored beside a Wexford bungalow tub\nOld layered caulk cut back to bare tile\nRegrouting a Guildwood bathroom before new tenants arrive\nLoose tiles reset above a Bendale bathtub','I rent in a Scarborough apartment building. Can you do the caulking, or is that the landlord job?','Either, depending on your lease and your building. Some Scarborough landlords look after the tub joint themselves and would rather you did not hire anyone. Others are slow, and tenants pay me to sort it out. Ask your property manager first so you are not paying for something already covered. If you go ahead, I keep the work tidy and reversible.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (4,10,4,'Bathroom Caulking and Repairs in Etobicoke','Bathroom Caulking Repair in Etobicoke','Etobicoke bathroom caulking and tile repairs, from Humber Bay condos to south end bungalows. Failed beads cut out and resealed. Send a photo for a quote.','Bathroom caulking and repairs in Etobicoke cover two very different jobs. In the Kingsway and Mimico the bathroom often sits over a finished ceiling, so a split bead at the tub shows up as a brown ring in the room below long before anyone notices the bathroom itself.\n\nIn the Humber Bay Shores towers the bathroom is a sealed interior room with hard walls and a fan that answers to the building. Both need the old silicone cut right out and the joint dried before new material goes on. I do that first, every time.','The south end of Etobicoke is full of small post-war bungalows in Alderwood, Long Branch and New Toronto where the bathroom is barely five feet wide and the tub runs wall to wall. There is no room to work from the side, so the whole job happens kneeling in the tub. Up at Humber Bay Shores the opposite applies: big units, but a concierge, a booked service elevator and set hours for anything noisy, which is why I schedule the tile work there rather than turning up.','Tub joint resealed to stop a ceiling stain\nShower caulking renewed in a Humber Bay Shores condo\nRegrouting a narrow Alderwood bungalow bathroom\nVanity and backsplash resealed in a Mimico apartment\nLoose wall tile rebedded in a Kingsway bathroom','There is a stain on my Etobicoke living room ceiling under the bathroom. Is caulking enough?','Sometimes, and sometimes not. If water is escaping at the tub joint or where the spout meets the wall, cutting out the failed bead and resealing it stops the source. If the leak is a supply line or the drain, that is a plumber job and I will tell you plainly. I look before I quote, so you are not paying me to guess.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (5,10,25,'Bathroom Caulking and Repairs in Mississauga','Bathroom Caulking and Repairs, Mississauga','Bathroom caulking in Mississauga: tubs, showers and fibreglass surrounds resealed, grout renewed, fans swapped. The old bead comes right out. Book a quote.','Bathroom caulking and repairs in Mississauga very often involve a one piece fibreglass tub surround, the standard fit in Erin Mills, Meadowvale and Rathwood houses from the seventies and eighties. Those units flex when you stand in them, which is exactly what tears a hardened bead away from the wall.\n\nThe fix starts the same way: cut the old material out completely, clean and dry the joint, then lay a flexible bead that can take the movement. I also regrout tiled showers, reset loose tile and swap out fans that have stopped pulling.','The eighties builder bathrooms across Mississauga share a detail people forget about: a cultured marble vanity top with the bowl moulded in and a matching backsplash stuck to the wall. That backsplash joint is usually the first thing to go, and because the top is one piece you cannot just replace the sink. It gets cut out and resealed. Around Square One the picture changes to condo bathrooms with hard demising walls, where a grab rail needs the right anchor rather than a stud.','Resealing a fibreglass tub surround in Erin Mills\nCultured marble vanity backsplash rejointed in Meadowvale\nShower regrouted in a Square One condo\nMouldy caulk replaced in a Port Credit bathroom\nBathroom exhaust fan swapped in a Rathwood ensuite','My Mississauga tub surround is fibreglass, not tile. Does caulking still work on it?','Yes, but the preparation matters more. Fibreglass and acrylic move, and old silicone clings to them stubbornly, so I cut the bead out and scrape the surface back rather than laying new material over the remains. New silicone will not bond to old silicone. Once it is clean and dry, a flexible bead handles the flex and stays put.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (6,10,26,'Bathroom Caulking and Repairs in Brampton','Bathroom Caulking Repair in Brampton','Brampton bathroom caulking and repairs, including tiled tub decks, cracked grout and mould in ensuite corners. Neat, lasting work. Text me for a free quote.','Bathroom caulking and repairs in Brampton usually start in the ensuite, because the ensuite is where the tiled tub deck lives. Springdale, Fletchers Meadow and Mount Pleasant houses were built with a soaker tub dropped into a tiled surround, and the long flat joints around it hold water instead of shedding it.\n\nI cut those joints out, dry everything properly and reseal so the water runs off. I also regrout showers, reset tile that has lifted and reseal the vanity where the counter meets the splash.','A drop-in tub on a tiled deck is the Brampton signature, and the deck gets treated as a shelf. Bottles sit on it, water pools behind them, and the joint between the deck tile and the tub rim goes first, usually at the corners where the tile turns. Grout there will crack no matter how well it is packed, because the tub rises and drops with the weight of the water. That joint has to be silicone, and it has to stay serviceable.','Resealing a tiled tub deck in a Springdale ensuite\nRegrouting a heavily used main bathroom shower\nBasement suite shower and vanity resealed\nCorner joints redone around a Mount Pleasant soaker tub\nWeak ensuite exhaust fan replaced in Fletchers Meadow','The grout around my Brampton ensuite tub keeps cracking. Why does it not last?','Because grout is rigid and that joint moves. A soaker tub sitting in a tiled deck drops slightly when it is full and lifts again when it drains, and rigid grout has nowhere to go but crack. The joint where the tub meets the tile needs flexible silicone instead. I rake the failed grout out of that line, clean it back and seal it properly.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (7,10,31,'Bathroom Caulking and Repairs in Vaughan','Bathroom Caulking and Repairs, Vaughan','Bathroom caulking and repairs in Vaughan, done with the right sealant for marble, porcelain and frameless glass showers. Ask me about your ensuite today.','Bathroom caulking and repairs in Vaughan usually involve stone or large format porcelain, because that is how Woodbridge, Maple and Kleinburg bathrooms were finished. Marble and travertine will not tolerate the wrong sealant, so the product matters as much as the workmanship here.\n\nFrameless glass enclosures are the other constant. The bead where the glass meets the tile is on show from the doorway, so it has to be straight as well as watertight. I cut out the old, clean the surfaces, and lay a neutral cure bead that will not mark the stone.','Vaughan bathrooms are finished to be looked at, which changes the job. A marble or travertine surround needs neutral cure silicone, because the acid type sold in every hardware aisle can stain porous stone at the edge of the bead and there is no undoing that. Frameless glass panels add their own rule: seal the outside of the enclosure and leave the inside face open so any water that gets past the glass drains back into the shower rather than sitting in the wall.','Resealing a frameless glass shower in Woodbridge\nNeutral cure silicone on a marble tub surround\nRegrouting large format porcelain in a Maple ensuite\nShower bench and curb joints resealed in Thornhill\nLoose stone tile rebedded in a Kleinburg bathroom','Will resealing my Vaughan shower stain the marble around it?','Not if the right product goes on. Standard acetic silicone gives off acid as it cures and can leave a dark halo along the edge of the bead on marble, travertine or limestone. I use neutral cure sealant on stone for that reason, and I mask the edges so the line stays crisp. If your surround is porcelain, either will do, but I still prefer neutral.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (8,10,32,'Bathroom Caulking and Repairs in Markham','Bathroom Caulking Repair in Markham','Every bathroom in the house resealed in one Markham visit. Caulking, regrouting, loose tile and grab rails, with the price agreed up front. Free quotes here.','Bathroom caulking and repairs in Markham are rarely about one bathroom. A Cornell or Berczy house is tall and narrow with three or four bathrooms stacked up it, and they all reach the end of their sealant at roughly the same time.\n\nSo I usually work through the house in one visit: ensuite, main bath, powder room, basement shower. Cut out, clean, dry, reseal, plus any regrouting or loose tile along the way. Batching it that way means one visit instead of four and gets the whole place sorted.','The newer neighbourhoods in Markham put a lot of bathrooms in a small footprint. In Cornell and Berczy the powder room, main bath and ensuite sit one above the other on a narrow lot, and the top floor bathrooms often have the laundry beside them, so any water that escapes travels down through two storeys before you see it. Older Unionville homes are the opposite job: one dated family bathroom, tile that is fine, grout that is grey and crumbling in the shower corners.','All four bathrooms resealed in one Cornell visit\nRegrouting a tired shower in a Unionville home\nGrab rails fitted in a main floor bathroom\nPowder room vanity resealed before a house sale\nEnsuite shower corners regrouted in a Berczy home','Can you do every bathroom in my Markham house in one visit?','Usually yes. Cutting out and resealing three or four bathrooms is a full day, and it is the sensible way to do it because they all age together. Regrouting adds time, since the grout needs to firm up before I seal over it. I will look at what you have, tell you honestly whether it is one visit or two, and agree the price before I start.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (9,10,33,'Bathroom Caulking and Repairs in Richmond Hill','Bathroom Caulking Repair in Richmond Hill','Richmond Hill bathroom caulking, jetted tub rims, regrouting and loose tile put right. Old sealant fully removed first. Get in touch for a free quote.','Bathroom caulking and repairs in Richmond Hill often mean dealing with a jetted tub, the feature of choice in Bayview Hill and Oak Ridges ensuites through the nineties. Those tubs have a pump under the deck and an access panel that has to stay removable, which rules out sealing everything solid.\n\nSo I seal what should be sealed and leave what has to open. Alongside that I regrout showers, reset tile that has lifted off the wall, redo vanity joints and swap out fans that no longer clear the mirror.','A whirlpool tub shakes when the jets run, and vibration is brutal on a stiff joint. In the Richmond Hill ensuites that still have one, I see the same failure every time: a hairline split along the tub rim that has been filled and refilled. The other trap is the access panel for the pump. Someone eventually seals it shut, and then a motor that needs attention cannot be reached without breaking tile. I keep that hatch free and seal around it.','Resealing a jetted tub rim in a Bayview Hill ensuite\nAccess panel freed and resealed around a tub pump\nRegrouting a shower in an Oak Ridges home\nMould cut out of a Mill Pond bathroom joint\nVanity top resealed in a Yonge Street condo','My Richmond Hill ensuite has an old whirlpool tub I never use. Should I still reseal it?','Yes, if it is staying. An unused tub still gets splashed from the shower head or wiped down, and an open joint lets that water into the deck framing where nobody looks. Resealing it is a small job. If you are thinking of pulling the tub out later, tell me and I will keep the work simple rather than doing more than the tub is worth.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (10,10,47,'Bathroom Caulking and Repairs in Oakville','Bathroom Caulking and Repairs, Oakville','Careful caulking and regrouting for Oakville bathrooms, including original tile in older homes. Nothing chipped, nothing rushed. Ask me for a free quote.','Bathroom caulking and repairs in Oakville often come with an instruction: keep the tile. Old Oakville, Kerr Village and Bronte have plenty of houses with original hex floors and square edge wall tile that the owners have no intention of losing, so the work is careful regrouting rather than removal.\n\nThat means raking the old grout out by hand where the tile is thin and brittle, matching the colour, and cutting the tub joint back to clean edges. In the newer Glen Abbey and River Oaks ensuites it is bigger showers and longer runs of sealant.','Bathrooms in the older parts of Oakville have usually been half renovated two or three times, and every round left its sealant behind. What looks like one bead is often four, with the oldest sitting hard against tile that is thinner and more brittle than anything made today. Power tools take chips out of that glaze, so it comes off slowly with a blade. Keeping the original tile intact is the whole point of the job for most owners here.','Regrouting original wall tile in an Old Oakville bathroom\nLayers of old caulk removed without chipping glaze\nResealing a large ensuite shower in Glen Abbey\nHex floor tile grout renewed in Kerr Village\nBronte bathroom tub joint cut out and resealed','My Oakville bathroom tile is original and I want to keep it. Can you regrout without damaging it?','That is most of what I do in the older streets here. Old glazed tile chips easily, so I take the grout out with hand tools and a carbide blade rather than an angle grinder, working slowly along each line. Some hairline crazing in the glaze is already there and will still be there afterwards. I will show you the tile before I start so you know what is old damage.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (11,10,43,'Bathroom Caulking and Repairs in Pickering','Bathroom Caulking Repair in Pickering','Bathroom caulking and repairs in Pickering, plus exhaust fan swaps that stop the mould returning. Joints cut out and resealed. Reach out for a free quote.','Bathroom caulking and repairs in Pickering nearly always come back to airflow. The Amberlea and Liverpool houses from the seventies and eighties were built with a small builder grade fan, and a lot of them are still running, pulling almost nothing while the shower fills the room with steam.\n\nSealant does not stand a chance in a bathroom that stays damp. So I cut out the mouldy bead and reseal it properly, then look at the fan and the duct behind it. Regrouting, loose tile and vanity joints get sorted in the same visit.','When I swap a fan in a Pickering two storey I always check where the duct actually goes. In houses of that age the flexible pipe often runs across the attic and has worked loose at the joint, so every shower has been dumping warm wet air into the roof space instead of outside. A new fan will not fix that on its own. Down by Frenchmans Bay the older bungalows have the same fan problem in a much smaller room.','Exhaust fan swapped and duct connection checked in Amberlea\nMouldy tub joint cut out and resealed in Liverpool\nRegrouting a steamy shower in a West Shore bungalow\nLoose tiles reset above a Highbush bathtub\nVanity and backsplash resealed in a Duffin Heights townhome','I have replaced the caulking in my Pickering bathroom twice and the mould came back. What am I doing wrong?','Most likely the room, not your technique. If the mirror is still fogged an hour after a shower, that bathroom is not drying out, and no sealant survives permanent damp. In a lot of Pickering houses the fan is still the original one from the build and it was never strong enough for the room. I reseal the joint back to clean surfaces, then sort the airflow so the new bead lasts.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (12,10,44,'Bathroom Caulking and Repairs in Ajax','Bathroom Caulking and Repairs in Ajax','Getting an Ajax bathroom resealed before you move in. Old caulking stripped out, tubs, showers and vanities done fresh. Send photos and I will quote it free.','Bathroom caulking and repairs in Ajax are often booked before anyone moves in. You get the keys to a Westney Heights or Nottingham house, walk into the bathroom, and the sealant around the tub is grey and peeling at the edges. That is a job worth doing before the first shower, not after.\n\nI strip the old bead out completely, clean the surfaces, and lay a fresh one. Regrouting, loose tiles, a wobbly grab rail and a fan that has stopped working can all be handled on the same visit.','Ajax gets a lot of first time buyers moving out from the city, and the bathroom goes on the list for move in week. Nobody wants to use a stranger shower with grey sealant in the corners, so the call here is often about starting clean rather than fixing a leak. In the older core off Harwood the wartime era houses kept their original bathrooms, where the wall tile stops partway up and the top of the bead has painted plaster to grip instead of glaze.','Whole bathroom resealed before moving into a new home\nGrey peeling sealant stripped from a Westney Heights tub\nRegrouting a shower in a Nottingham two storey\nOriginal bathroom joints redone near the Harwood core\nLoose vanity and backsplash resealed in south Ajax','I take possession of my Ajax house next week. How soon can the bathroom be resealed?','I reply within a business day and can usually fit it into the week. Doing the bathroom before the furniture arrives makes it a simpler job. Send me a couple of photos of the tub and the shower corners and I will tell you what it needs. Silicone wants a night to cure, so book the work for the day before you want to use the shower properly.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (13,8,1,'Door Repair and Installation in Toronto','Door Repair and Installation Toronto','Sticking doors, worn hinges and settled frames in Toronto houses, repaired or replaced by Sercan, a one-man handyman in the old city. Ask for a free quote.','Door repair and installation in Toronto usually starts with a door that no longer fits the opening it was hung in. Old city houses move. Frames go out of square, latches miss the strike, and the bottom rail drags on the floor.\n\nI am Sercan, and I do this work myself across the old city, from Parkdale and the Annex through Riverdale and the Beach. Some doors need an hour of adjustment. Others are past saving and want a new pre-hung unit. I will tell you which, and agree the price before I start.','Downtown Toronto houses built before 1950 have plaster and lath walls and door frames set into openings that have shifted with the foundation. Hinge screws pull out of soft old jamb wood, and decades of paint have usually sealed the hinge knuckles and the stop. Many still carry mortise locks with a keyhole rather than a modern bore, so a new slab means matching old hardware or filling and re-boring. On permit-parking streets I unload once and carry everything in.','Freeing bedroom doors painted shut in old semis\nRehanging a sagging front door on a settled frame\nFitting a pre-hung door into an out-of-square opening\nMoving strike plates so latches catch again\nNew closet doors in a downtown condo bedroom','My Toronto semi has doors that stick every summer. Can they be fixed without replacing them?','Usually yes. In older Toronto houses the swelling is seasonal, and the fix is planing the exact edge that binds, then sealing the bare wood so it stops taking on moisture. Sometimes the real problem is a hinge screw that has lost its grip in old jamb wood, which I repair with longer screws driven into the framing. I only suggest a new door when the slab itself is warped.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (14,8,2,'Door Repair and Installation in North York','Door Repair & Installation in North York','Narrow bungalow doors, basement suite doors and patio sliders in North York, fitted and repaired by Sercan working on his own. Get in touch for a free quote.','Door repair in North York usually starts with a size problem. The post-war bungalows and side-splits around Bathurst Manor, Downsview and Willowdale were framed with openings narrower than anything a builder uses today, so a replacement rarely drops straight in.\n\nI am Sercan, and I do this work myself. Sometimes the answer is a new slab cut and bored to suit the old frame. Sometimes the frame itself is done, and a pre-hung unit with fresh trim is the honest call. I look at the opening, tell you which it is, and agree a price before I start.','North York\'s post-war bungalows and side-splits were framed at sixteen inches on centre and hung with doors narrower than today\'s standard, so a bedroom or bathroom opening here often takes a twenty-four inch slab rather than the common thirty. Basements across Downsview and Bathurst Manor are frequently finished as second suites, which means solid doors, a proper closer and a bottom gap that clears new flooring. Along the Yonge corridor above Sheppard the towers add concrete jambs and a service elevator to book first.','Matching a narrow bedroom door in a 1950s bungalow\nSolid-core doors for a finished basement suite\nAdjusting patio sliders onto Willowdale backyard decks\nReplacing tired bifold closet doors in side-splits\nHanging a new door in a Yonge corridor condo','Can you match the narrow doors in my North York bungalow?','Usually, yes. Many of the post-war bungalows here were hung with twenty-four and twenty-six inch doors, and those widths are still made, though not always in the style you already have. If your frame is straight I can fit a new slab to it, cut the hinge mortises and bore it for your existing handle. If the frame has twisted, a pre-hung unit is the steadier answer.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (15,8,3,'Door Repair and Installation in Scarborough','Door Repair and Installation Scarborough','Patio sliders, bifold closets and side entry doors across Scarborough, repaired or replaced by Sercan, who does every job himself. Call for a free quote.','Door repair and installation in Scarborough is mostly patio doors and closet doors. The bungalows and backsplits through Wexford, Agincourt, Guildwood and West Hill were built with a slider onto the rear deck and long runs of bifold closets, and both wear out at the hardware first.\n\nI am Sercan and I come out on my own. A slider that fights you often needs rollers and a track, not a new door. A closet door that jumps out needs the top track set straight again. I will say plainly which parts your job needs before any work begins.','Scarborough\'s 1960s and 70s bungalows and backsplits nearly all have an aluminum sliding door onto a rear deck, and after fifty winters the rollers are worn flat and the bottom track is dented, so the door is dragged rather than rolled. That is the call I get most often here. The separate side entrances common in Agincourt, Wexford and West Hill are frequently the door to a basement apartment, so they need a solid slab, a tight weather seal and a strike bored to line up with the deadbolt.','New rollers for aluminum patio sliders onto rear decks\nStraightening bent bottom tracks on backsplit sliders\nSolid entry door for a side-door basement suite\nBifold closet doors rehung on straightened top tracks\nStorm door repairs on Kingston Road bungalow entries','My patio slider barely moves. Do I need a whole new door?','Often not. On the aluminum sliders in Scarborough bungalows the rollers wear flat and the bottom track gets dented, and both can be renewed with the panel lifted out and set back in. While it is out I check the frame for square and look at the glass seals. If the frame has rusted through or the glass has fogged between the panes, a new unit is the sensible route.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (16,8,4,'Door Repair and Installation in Etobicoke','Door Repair and Installation Etobicoke','Drafty lakeshore entries, dragging interior doors and worn seals in Etobicoke, all handled by Sercan personally. Send me the details for a free quote.','Door repair in Etobicoke changes depending on how close you are to the lake. Along Lake Shore Boulevard in Mimico, New Toronto and Long Branch, exterior doors take wind and damp all winter, and the seals are what give out first.\n\nI am Sercan, and I handle everything myself, from a bedroom door that drags on carpet to a full pre-hung exterior unit. Inland, in Humber Valley and Islington Village, the older houses came with heavy solid doors that are usually worth rehanging. Quotes are free, and the price I give you is the price you pay.','The lakeshore streets of Mimico, New Toronto and Long Branch take wind straight off the water, so front and back doors there lose their weather stripping and sweeps far faster than doors a few kilometres inland, and a door that shuts sweetly in July whistles in January. Inland the 1950s Humber Valley houses came with heavy solid doors worth rehanging rather than swapping. In the older apartment blocks along Kipling and Islington I work to the building\'s hours and use the service entrance.','New weather stripping on lakeshore front doors\nRehanging heavy solid doors in Humber Valley houses\nInterior door swaps in older Kipling apartment units\nGarage-to-house door adjusted to close and latch properly\nPatio door sweeps replaced after a windy winter','The wind off the lake comes straight through my front door. What can be done?','In Mimico and Long Branch that is nearly always the weather stripping and the door sweep, both of which harden and shrink after a few winters. I replace them, then move the strike so the door pulls tight against the new seal instead of resting against it. If the slab itself has bowed away from the frame, I will show you that first so you can decide how far to go.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (17,8,25,'Door Repair and Installation in Mississauga','Door Repair and Installation Mississauga','Eight-foot slabs, double front entries and condo closet doors in Mississauga, fitted and adjusted by Sercan alone. Message me and I will quote at no charge.','Door repair and installation in Mississauga runs from eight-foot slabs in Churchill Meadows to closet doors in a Square One tower. The city was built in waves, and each wave used a different door size, so measuring comes before anything else.\n\nI am Sercan and I do the work myself. Most calls here are a mix: one door that binds, one that will not latch, and a closet that needs new hardware. I can take them in a single visit, quote before I start, and the price does not move after that.','In Mississauga\'s newer subdivisions, through Churchill Meadows, east Erin Mills and upper Meadowvale, the main floor is often hung with eight-foot doors rather than the usual six-foot-eight, so a replacement slab has to be ordered in and carries an extra hinge. Double front entries with two leaves and an astragal are common here as well, and getting both to close together takes patience. Downtown near Square One I book the loading dock and service elevator before a delivery date is set.','Replacing an eight-foot interior door in Churchill Meadows\nAligning both leaves of a double front entry\nCloset doors fitted in a Square One condo\nGarage entry door rehung so it self-closes\nWhole-house door tune-up after buying an Erin Mills home','My main floor has eight-foot doors. Can you replace just one of them?','Yes, though it means an order rather than a trip to the shelf. Eight-foot slabs come in fewer styles and carry a third hinge, so I measure the opening, the hinge spacing and the bore height before anything is bought. If the rest of your doors are a discontinued profile, I will tell you up front, so you can decide whether one close match is good enough.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (18,8,26,'Door Repair and Installation in Brampton','Door Repair and Installation Brampton','Loose hinges, missed latches and rubbing doors across Brampton homes, sorted in a single visit by Sercan himself. Ask for a free quote on the whole list.','Door repair in Brampton is usually a list rather than a single door. Homes in Springdale, Mount Pleasant and Fletcher\'s Meadow have a lot of doors, and builder-grade hinges tend to loosen on all of them at about the same time.\n\nI am Sercan and I work alone, so I would rather come once and deal with the whole list. Doors that rub, doors that swing open on their own, latches that miss, closets that will not shut. I look at everything, give you one free quote, and stick to it.','Brampton houses in Springdale, Mount Pleasant and Fletcher\'s Meadow are large, and a hollow-core builder door on every bedroom means the calls here are lists rather than single doors: eight or nine that rub, rattle or will not latch. Hinge screws in those homes strip out of thin jamb stock, so I go through into the stud behind instead. Older Bramalea houses from the sixties are a different job again, with narrower openings and doors of solid wood that reward planing.','Tightening hinges on eight or nine builder doors\nLatches adjusted so bedroom doors stop swinging open\nNew doors hung in a newly finished basement\nDouble front doors realigned to close together\nHollow-core doors swapped in Springdale bedrooms','I have about eight doors with problems. Can they all be done in one visit?','Usually yes, and that is how I prefer to work. Most of what goes wrong on builder doors in Brampton homes comes down to the same handful of faults, so once I am set up the second door takes far less time than the first. I walk the house with you first, note what each door needs, and give you one quote covering the lot.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (19,8,31,'Door Repair and Installation in Vaughan','Door Repair and Installation Vaughan','Heavy eight-foot doors, sagging hinges and new pre-hung units across Vaughan, all handled by Sercan on his own. Reach out for a free, no-obligation quote.','Door repair and installation in Vaughan usually means heavy doors. The 2000s houses in Woodbridge, Maple and Kleinburg were built with high main-floor ceilings and eight-foot openings, many of them filled with solid-core slabs, and weight is what pulls a door out of line.\n\nI am Sercan and I do this work myself. A door that has dropped at the latch corner is a hinge problem, not a door problem, nine times out of ten. I will tell you the difference honestly, quote for free, and agree a price before anything comes off its hinges.','Woodbridge, Maple and Kleinburg homes from the 2000s were built with high main-floor ceilings and eight-foot doors, and a good number are solid core rather than hollow. A slab that heavy sags at the hinges long before it swells at the edge, so the usual complaint here is a latch corner that has dropped, not a sticking edge. Those doors hang on three or four hinges, and the top one is shimmed first. Around the Vaughan Metropolitan Centre the towers are concrete, so fixings are anchors rather than wood screws.','Resetting hinges on heavy eight-foot Woodbridge doors\nSolid-core door hung in a taller than standard opening\nCloset doors anchored into concrete in a VMC tower\nDouble den doors realigned in a Maple home\nLatch and strike work on Kleinburg interior doors','My solid eight-foot door drags on the carpet. Does that mean it is warped?','Rarely. A door that heavy pulls hard on the top hinge, and once that hinge shifts even a couple of millimetres the bottom corner swings low and catches. I take the weight off, drive longer screws through the jamb into the framing, and reset the hinge. Planing is a last resort on a solid slab, because once the material is off you cannot put it back.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (20,8,32,'Door Repair and Installation in Markham','Door Repair and Installation Markham','Clashing townhome doors, settled frames and rehangs in Markham, done by Sercan personally, one job at a time. Tell me the problem and I will quote free.','Door repair in Markham depends on which part of town you are in. The newer freehold towns in Cornell, Wismer and Box Grove have tight halls where doors get in each other\'s way. Unionville and Markham Village have older frames that have settled instead.\n\nI am Sercan, and I come out alone and do the job myself. Sometimes the answer is a rehang, so the door swings the other way. Sometimes it is planing and a set of new hinges. I look first, explain the options in plain terms, and agree a price before starting.','Many of Markham\'s newer homes in Cornell, Wismer and Box Grove are narrow three-storey freehold townhomes, and the doors there fight each other: a bathroom door and a bedroom door swinging into the same short hall, or a door that hits the stair railing. Rehanging one so it opens into the room, or onto the opposite jamb, solves it more often than a new door would. In Unionville and Markham Village the frames have settled instead, leaving a gap wider at the top than the bottom.','Rehanging a door to swing into the room\nBathroom and bedroom doors that clash in narrow halls\nThird-floor bedroom doors adjusted in freehold townhomes\nPlaning and shimming doors in older Unionville houses\nNew pre-hung door for a main-floor den','Two doors in my townhouse hallway hit each other. Can that be changed?','Usually it can. In the narrow three-storey towns around Cornell and Wismer the answer is often a rehang, so one door swings into the room rather than the hall, or moves to the opposite jamb. That means fresh hinge mortises, a relocated strike and some filling on the old side. I check the light switch and the trim first, because they sometimes decide which way the door can go.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (21,8,33,'Door Repair and Installation in Richmond Hill','Door Repair and Installation Richmond Hill','Walkout basement sliders, worn rollers and interior door fixes in Richmond Hill, carried out by Sercan himself. Get a free quote before anything is booked.','Door repair and installation in Richmond Hill often ends up in the basement. The town sits on sloping ground, so houses in Oak Ridges, Jefferson and Bayview Hill commonly have a walkout with a sliding or garden door at the back.\n\nI am Sercan and I do the work myself. Those lower doors wear faster than the front, because grit and runoff from the slope end up in the track. Interior doors, closet doors and the entry upstairs can all be part of the same visit. Free quote first, and the price is agreed before I start.','Richmond Hill sits on sloping ground, so a large share of the houses in Oak Ridges, Jefferson and Bayview Hill have walkout basements with a sliding or garden door at the back, sitting at grade. Those doors catch runoff and grit washing down the slope, which packs the sill and grinds the rollers, so they wear out long before the front entry does. Down around the Mill Pond the houses are older and smaller, with settled frames and doors that were hand-trimmed once already.','Rollers replaced on walkout basement garden doors\nSliding doors cleared of grit from sloped yards\nNew sweeps fitted on lower level patio doors\nInterior doors planed in older Mill Pond homes\nDouble front entry doors realigned in Bayview Hill','The garden door in my walkout basement sticks after every storm. Why is that?','On the sloped lots in Oak Ridges and Jefferson, water and grit run down toward the walkout and settle in the door track and under the sweep. That debris grinds the rollers and packs the sill so the panel binds. Cleaning the track, fitting new rollers and a fresh sweep usually restores it. If the sill has lifted where the ground settled, that needs shimming rather than new hardware.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (22,8,47,'Door Repair and Installation in Oakville','Door Repair and Installation Oakville','Original panel doors repaired and builder doors replaced across Oakville by Sercan, working solo on every job. Ask me to look at yours and quote it free.','Door repair in Oakville splits between keeping an old door and fitting a new one. Near the harbour and through Kerr Village the houses are pre-war and the original solid doors are worth saving. In Glen Abbey and Joshua Creek the doors are builder stock from the 1980s and 2000s.\n\nI am Sercan and I do this myself, so the person who looks at the job is the person who does it. I will say honestly whether your door wants repair or replacement. Quotes are free, and the price is agreed before anything starts.','In Old Oakville and around Kerr Village the houses predate the war, and the original doors are solid stiles and rails with mortise locks and moulded panels, better repaired than swapped for anything sold today. Owners on the heritage streets near the harbour normally want the door kept and the frame brought back to square. Out in Glen Abbey and Joshua Creek the job reverses: builder-hung hollow-core doors from the 1980s and 2000s, where a new pre-hung unit makes more sense than nursing the old one.','Repairing original panel doors near the harbour\nMortise latches freed and adjusted in pre-war homes\nPre-hung replacements in Glen Abbey bedrooms\nPatio door hardware renewed in Joshua Creek houses\nSquaring settled frames in older Kerr Village houses','Is it worth keeping the original doors in an older Oakville house?','In most cases yes. The doors on the older streets near the harbour are solid wood with proper stiles and rails, and nothing on a shelf today matches them for weight or detail. Sticking, rattling and a latch that misses are frame and hardware faults, and all of those are fixable. I would only suggest replacing one if the slab has split right through or rot has reached the rails.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (23,8,43,'Door Repair and Installation in Pickering','Door Repair and Installation Pickering','Patio door rollers, bifold closets and tired weather stripping in Pickering, put right by Sercan on his own. Drop me a line and I will quote it for free.','Door repair in Pickering is mostly hardware, not doors. The 1970s and 80s houses in Bay Ridges, West Shore and Amberlea came with sliding patio doors and wide bifold closets, and it is the rollers, pivots and tracks that give out long before the doors themselves do.\n\nI am Sercan and I do this work on my own. If new hardware will fix it, that is what I will tell you. If the frame is finished, I will say that too. Either way you get a free quote and a price agreed in advance.','Pickering\'s 1970s and 80s neighbourhoods, Bay Ridges, West Shore and Amberlea, were built with wood-framed sliding patio doors and full-width bifold closets, and both fail the same way: the track and the pivot hardware go while the doors are still sound. Nearer Frenchman\'s Bay the wind pushes rain hard at the back doors, so sweeps and weather stripping there need renewing more often. The newer Duffin Heights towns are the opposite, with narrow halls and light doors that mostly need latch work.','Rollers and tracks renewed on 1980s patio sliders\nBifold closet doors put back on their pivots\nWeather stripping replaced on back doors near the bay\nSplit-level entry doors adjusted to latch cleanly\nInterior doors hung in Duffin Heights townhomes','My bifold closet doors keep jumping off their track. Is that fixable?','Almost always. On the wide closets built into 1970s and 80s Pickering houses the top track sags in the middle and the pivot pins wear round, so the doors lose their hold. New pins, new guides and a track screwed back into solid framing will hold it. If the panels have delaminated at the corners, I will suggest replacing them while the track is already down.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (24,8,44,'Door Repair and Installation in Ajax','Door Repair and Installation Ajax','Older solid doors planed and new hollow-core doors hung across Ajax by Sercan himself. Let me know what needs doing and the quote will cost you nothing.','Door repair and installation in Ajax depends on which side of Taunton Road you live on. In Pickering Village and south Ajax the doors are older and solid, hung in frames that have moved. North of Taunton the subdivisions are recent, with lighter hollow-core doors and bifold laundry closets.\n\nI am Sercan, and I do the work myself. Older doors usually want planing, shimming and a set of new hinges. Newer ones usually want hardware or a straight swap. I look at yours, quote for free, and agree the price before I start.','Ajax splits neatly in two for this trade. South of Kingston Road and through Pickering Village the houses are older and smaller, the doors are solid wood, and the frames have moved with the ground, so the work is planing, shimming and new hinges. North of Taunton Road the subdivisions are recent: hollow-core slabs, laundry closets shut by bifolds that jump their guide, and unfinished basements that need three or four new doors hung as the space gets finished.','Planing and shimming solid doors in Pickering Village\nLaundry closet bifolds put back on track\nNew interior doors hung as a basement is finished\nHollow-core bedroom doors replaced north of Taunton\nPatio slider hardware renewed on fenced backyards','I am finishing a basement in north Ajax. Should I buy the doors myself?','Either way works. If you want a particular style, buy them and I will hang them for you. If you would rather not guess, I will measure the rough openings and tell you exactly what to order, or collect them as part of the job. What matters is measuring after the flooring is chosen, because the gap under each door depends on it.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (25,4,1,'Drywall Repair and Patching in Toronto','Drywall and Plaster Repair in Toronto','Drywall and plaster repair in Toronto, from hairline cracks in old semis to holes, patches and water damage. Sercan does the work himself. Ask for a quote.','Drywall repair in Toronto often turns out to be plaster repair. Much of the old city, from Riverdale to the Annex, was built before drywall existed, so what looks like a simple hole opens onto wood lath. I check what is behind the damage before I decide how to fix it.\n\nI am Sercan and I do the work myself, from the first cut to the final sanding. Small cracks, doorknob holes, a ceiling opened up for a plumber, or a wall that needs a full skim coat. I keep the dust contained and leave the surface ready for paint.','In pre-1950 Toronto houses the walls are horizontal wood lath with plaster pushed through it. When the keys behind the lath break off, the plaster sounds hollow and pressing on it makes things worse, so I refasten the field before I fill anything. Cracks running at an angle from door and window heads are old settlement in the brick, so they get taped, not just mudded. On narrow downtown streets I unload once and keep the site tight.','Skim coating cracked plaster walls in Riverdale semis\nPatching lath ceilings opened up for plumbing work\nRepairing corner cracks above doorways in century homes\nFilling anchor holes and dents before a condo move-out\nBlending patches into old textured hallway ceilings','My Toronto house is plaster, not drywall. Can you still patch it?','Yes. Most homes east and west of downtown built before the 1950s are plaster over wood lath, and I work on both. Depending on how much has let go, I either refasten and skim the existing plaster or cut back to the studs and set drywall flush with the old surface. Either way the finished wall reads flat once it is painted.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (26,4,2,'Drywall Repair and Patching in North York','Drywall Repair and Patching, North York','Drywall repair in North York: nail pops, holes and stipple ceiling patches in bungalows, side-splits and apartments. Sercan works solo. Get a free quote.','Drywall repair in North York usually starts on a ceiling. The bungalows and side-splits around Bathurst Manor, Willowdale and Don Mills went up in the 1950s and 1960s, when board was nailed rather than screwed, and those nails work loose over the decades.\n\nI am Sercan and I handle each job on my own. I pull the loose fasteners, screw the board back to the joist, then fill, sand and feather the patch out far enough that it disappears under a roller. If the ceiling is stippled, I match the texture before you repaint.','Two things come up again and again here. First, original nailed board keeps popping in rows along a joist, so I refasten either side of every pop instead of just filling the dimple. Second, most of these ceilings were sprayed with stipple, and a smooth patch in the middle of a textured ceiling is obvious from the doorway. I rebuild the pattern by hand. Basement suites add a third: repairs around relocated ductwork and bulkheads.','Refastening nail pops across post-war bungalow ceilings\nMatching stipple texture on patched Willowdale ceilings\nRepairing basement suite walls around bulkheads and ducts\nFixing cracked corner bead in 1960s side-split hallways\nPatching walls where old baseboard heaters were removed','Can you patch a stipple ceiling in my North York bungalow without redoing the whole thing?','Usually yes. I repair the board first, then rebuild the texture over the patch by hand so the pattern lines up with what surrounds it. It will not vanish until it is primed and painted, because fresh material always sits brighter than an old ceiling. If that ceiling has already been patched several times, a full skim and a smooth finish often looks better than more texture.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (27,4,3,'Drywall Repair and Patching in Scarborough','Drywall Repair in Scarborough','Drywall repair in Scarborough for water-damaged basement walls, holes and cracked corners. Sercan cuts back, boards, tapes and sands. Send me a photo.','Drywall repair in Scarborough is often basement work. The bungalows and backsplits through Wexford, Agincourt and Guildwood have finished lower levels, and the board sits close to the floor where a slow leak or a spring thaw finds it first.\n\nI am Sercan and I do every step myself. I cut back to sound material, check the framing behind it is dry, then board, tape and sand and feather out so the repair does not telegraph. If a wall is only marked rather than soaked, I say so instead of opening more of it.','Below-grade rooms are the difference here. When a basement wall gets wet the paper facing wicks moisture well above the visible stain, so I cut a clean horizontal line above it rather than chasing a ragged edge. The base trim comes off, the bottom plate gets checked, and things dry before new board goes on. Many of these 1960s finished basements also have furring or old panelling behind the drywall, so the patch has to be shimmed to sit flush.','Cutting back and reboarding water-marked basement walls\nPatching ceilings opened for drain and plumbing repairs\nRepairing bottom-of-wall damage behind removed baseboards\nFixing holes in backsplit stairwell and landing walls\nFinishing new walls in a converted rec room','There is a water stain on my Scarborough basement wall. How much of it has to come out?','More than the stain shows, usually. The paper facing draws moisture upward, so I cut a straight line about a hand\'s width above the damage and check the framing and insulation behind it. If everything is dry, I board and finish right away. If it is still damp, I leave it open to dry first, because new board over wet framing comes back.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (28,4,4,'Drywall Repair and Patching in Etobicoke','Drywall Repair in Etobicoke Homes and Condos','Drywall patching in Etobicoke condos and post-war bungalows, with dust kept to one room and a clean blended finish. Call Sercan for a no-obligation quote.','Drywall repair in Etobicoke covers two very different buildings. Along the lake at Humber Bay Shores the walls are thin board strapped off concrete, while the bungalows in Alderwood and Long Branch are ordinary framing that has moved a little over seventy years.\n\nI am Sercan and I arrive alone with what the job needs. In a condo that means sealing off one room, cutting where the mess is easiest to control and carrying every offcut back out with me. In a house it is the usual sequence: fasten, tape, mud, sand, blend.','In the Mimico and Humber Bay towers the exterior walls are furred off concrete and the partitions are steel stud, so there is almost no cavity behind the board and no room for standard back-blocking. I plan the backing to suit before I cut. Those buildings also want the service elevator booked and the corridor protected, which fixes my arrival time more than my own schedule does. Older Etobicoke bungalows are the opposite: real stud cavities, but original plaster on the walls.','Patching lakefront condo walls after brackets come down\nRepairing dented corner bead in Mimico hallways\nFilling holes left by removed shelving in rentals\nFixing ceiling cracks in Alderwood post-war bungalows\nSkim coating Kingsway plaster walls before repainting','I am in a condo near Humber Bay Shores. Will the repair make a mess of my unit?','No more than it has to. I hang plastic across the room opening, run a vacuum at the sanding head and bag the offcuts before they leave the unit. Most single patches take two short visits, one to board and coat and one to sand and finish, because compound needs drying time. Tell me the building rules for elevators and work hours and I book around them.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (29,4,25,'Drywall Repair and Patching in Mississauga','Drywall and Ceiling Crack Repair, Mississauga','Drywall repair in Mississauga for seasonal ceiling cracks, screw pops and holes in houses and condos. Sercan tapes, coats and sands flat. Ask for a quote.','Drywall repair in Mississauga is often a crack that keeps coming back. In the subdivisions through Erin Mills, Meadowvale and Churchill Meadows, roof trusses lift slightly in winter and settle again in summer, and the line where the ceiling meets an interior wall opens up every year.\n\nI am Sercan, working on my own from the first look to the last sand. I will tell you honestly which cracks are cosmetic and which are seasonal, and treat them differently, because filling a moving joint with compound alone buys you one winter.','Truss uplift is the local signature. It shows as a clean gap along interior partition walls, usually a hallway or a closet, and it reopens each cold season no matter how carefully it was filled last time. The lasting answer is to release that joint and cover it, rather than locking it with rigid tape and waiting. The other Mississauga regular is the two-storey foyer, where dents and cracks sit high on a stairwell wall and need proper staging on the steps.','Repairing seasonal ceiling gaps along interior partition walls\nPatching high stairwell walls in two-storey foyers\nFilling anchor holes and scuffs before a house sale\nFixing drywall around replaced attic hatches and vents\nRepairing condo walls near Square One after move-outs','The crack where my ceiling meets the hallway wall opens every winter. Can that be stopped?','That is truss uplift, and it is common in the newer Mississauga subdivisions. The trusses in the attic arch upward in cold weather and pull the ceiling with them. Filling it again gives you one more season. I would rather free that edge and finish it so the movement is hidden and the ceiling can float, which keeps the line looking right all year.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (30,4,26,'Drywall Repair and Patching in Brampton','Drywall Repair in Brampton','Drywall repair in Brampton subdivision homes: screw pops, door-head cracks and holes. Sercan refastens, tapes and blends the paint line. Call to book a time.','Drywall repair in Brampton is mostly newer-house work. The subdivisions across Springdale, Mount Pleasant and Castlemore are young enough that the framing is still drying, and the first winters push screw heads through the paint and open thin lines above door openings.\n\nI am Sercan and I do the patching, taping and sanding myself. I fix the fastener rather than fill over it, feather the coats wide, and tell you upfront where the paint needs to run to a corner so the repair does not sit there as a bright square.','New framing lumber shrinks as it dries, and in Brampton\'s recent subdivisions that reads as rows of screw pops on the upper floor and short cracks off the top corners of door openings. I back the screw out, drive a fresh one an inch away into solid wood, then coat both. The other constant is builder flat paint on long nine-foot hallway walls in linked and townhouse plans, where any patch shows under side light, so the blend runs to the nearest corner.','Filling rows of screw pops on upper-floor ceilings\nRepairing hairline cracks above door and window openings\nPatching stairwell walls scraped during furniture moves\nFixing garage-side wall holes after shelving removal\nRepairing basement apartment walls between tenants','My Brampton house is only a few years old. Why is the drywall cracking already?','It is normal in a new build. The lumber holding the board dries and shrinks through the first winters and the drywall moves with it, so you get screw pops and short cracks over doors. In almost every case it is cosmetic. I refasten into solid wood beside each pop, tape the door-head cracks properly and finish them so the same spots stay put.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (31,4,31,'Drywall Repair and Patching in Vaughan','Drywall and Bullnose Corner Repair, Vaughan','Drywall repair in Vaughan for rounded corners, arches, coffered ceilings and wall holes. Sercan rebuilds the profile before finishing. Book a free quote.','Drywall repair in Vaughan runs into shapes, not just flat walls. Homes through Woodbridge, Maple and Kleinburg were finished with rounded corners, arch openings, columns and coffered ceilings, and a chip on a curved corner is a different repair from a hole in the middle of a wall.\n\nI am Sercan and the work is all mine, start to finish. I rebuild the profile before I worry about the surface, because a bullnose that sits a millimetre out shows the moment the hall light is on. Then I coat, sand and blend.','Rounded bullnose corner bead is everywhere in Vaughan\'s larger homes, and it does not patch like a square corner. The radius has to carry cleanly into the sound bead above and below or the eye catches the kink on the way up the stairs. Arch openings and the inside edges of coffered ceilings have the same problem with more angles in play. Those open two-storey foyers also put the damage three or four metres up, so it gets staged properly rather than reached at.','Rebuilding chipped bullnose corners in Woodbridge homes\nRepairing coffered ceiling edges and inside corners\nPatching arch openings damaged by moving furniture\nFixing cracks high on two-storey foyer walls\nFilling holes after wall units and mirrors come down','My walls have rounded corners instead of square ones. Is that harder to patch?','It adds a step. A rounded corner has a set radius, and the repair has to carry that same curve into the good bead above and below it or the corner looks kinked. I splice in matching bead, build the coats out slowly and check the line by eye and by hand in raking light. Painted, you should not be able to find the join.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (32,4,32,'Drywall Repair and Patching in Markham','Drywall Patching and Ceiling Repair in Markham','Drywall repair in Markham for pot light holes, ceiling cuts and wall damage. Sercan backs, boards, tapes and sands every patch. Message me for a quote.','Drywall repair in Markham often follows a change to the lighting. Houses through Unionville, Milliken and Berczy were built with pot lights and ceiling speakers in fixed spots, and when those move, the old cut-outs are left behind as neat round holes in a painted ceiling.\n\nI am Sercan and I take the repair end to end. Round holes need backing and a proper plug, not filler pushed into the gap, or the circle shows through the paint within a year. I cut a clean shape, back it, board it, tape it and sand it flat.','Circular cut-outs are the Markham regular: retired pot lights, moved speakers, old detector bases and grille openings in ceilings that are otherwise perfect. Each one wants a backer and a board plug so it cannot shrink into a visible ring. Cornell\'s three-storey lane houses add height to the same job, because the stairwell wall runs from the ground floor to the top landing with nowhere flat to stand. I set up on the stairs properly instead of stretching off a step.','Plugging pot light and speaker holes in ceilings\nPatching tall stairwell walls in Cornell lane houses\nRepairing ceiling cuts left after fixtures were relocated\nFixing dents and holes in finished basement suites\nSkim coating plaster in older Markham Village homes','I removed some pot lights and left round holes in the ceiling. Can those disappear?','Yes, if they are plugged rather than filled. I cut each hole to a clean shape, fit a backer behind it, set a piece of board in flush and then tape and coat the joint like any other seam. Filler on its own shrinks and the ring comes back within a season. Once it is sanded and the ceiling is painted, there is nothing to find.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (33,4,33,'Drywall Repair and Patching in Richmond Hill','Drywall Repair in Richmond Hill','Drywall repair in Richmond Hill for walk-out basement damage, ceiling cracks and holes. Sercan finds the cause, then boards and sands. Ask about a quote.','Drywall repair in Richmond Hill often means a lower level that is only half underground. The lots north through Oak Ridges and around Bayview Hill slope, so plenty of houses have walk-out basements with full windows, patio doors and finished walls that see real weather.\n\nI am Sercan and I handle the whole repair myself. On a walk-out the damage usually sits near a door or window head, so I work out where the water came from before I board anything. After that it is the standard sequence: cut, back, tape, coat, sand and blend.','Sloped lots change this job. A walk-out lower level has grade-level doors and windows, so the drywall around those openings takes the knocks and the occasional leak that a fully buried basement never sees. Higher up, plenty of Richmond Hill houses run a furnace humidifier hard all winter, and the swing between damp January air and dry summer air keeps cathedral ceiling ridges and tall wall joints working. Those get taped rather than filled, so next season does not undo the repair.','Repairing walk-out basement walls beside grade-level doors\nTaping cracks along cathedral ceiling ridge lines\nPatching holes left by removed wall-mounted shelving\nFixing damaged corner bead in Oak Ridges hallways\nRepairing ceilings after bathroom fan or fixture changes','The drywall by my walk-out patio door has gone soft. What happens first?','I find out why it is soft before anything gets closed up. Around a grade-level door the usual causes are a failed seal or water running back along the sill. I open enough to see the framing, let it dry, and tell you if a caulking line or a door adjustment is needed first. Then I board, tape and finish so the corner is straight again.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (34,4,47,'Drywall and Plaster Repair in Oakville Homes','Plaster and Drywall Repair in Oakville','Plaster and drywall repair in Oakville, from heritage-era walls downtown to newer subdivision houses. Sercan skims, tapes and sands. Request a free quote.','Drywall repair in Oakville splits by neighbourhood. Downtown, Kerr Village and Old Oakville are full of pre-war houses with plaster walls, deep baseboards, picture rails and moulded cove, while Glen Abbey and River Oaks are conventional board from the 1980s onward.\n\nI am Sercan and I work alone, which suits older houses where care matters more than speed. In a heritage-era room I would rather refasten and skim a whole wall than cut a rectangle out of it and break a run of moulding. In a newer house, a quick cut-and-patch is the right call.','In the older houses near the lake, the plaster is tied to trim you cannot easily replace. Cutting a patch out of a wall means meeting a picture rail, a plaster cove or a baseboard thicker than anything sold today, and the join always lands somewhere you look. So I stabilise the loose plaster, bridge the cracks with tape and skim the full surface until the wall reads as one plane again. Newer Oakville subdivisions get the ordinary cut-and-patch treatment instead.','Skim coating cracked plaster walls in Old Oakville\nRepairing ceilings without disturbing original plaster cove\nPatching around thick heritage baseboards and picture rails\nFilling holes and dents before a home is listed\nFixing bathroom ceiling damage after a fan replacement','My Old Oakville house still has its original mouldings. Can you repair the walls without harming them?','That is usually the point of the job. Where plaster meets a picture rail or a moulded cove, I work up to it and stop, instead of cutting through and trying to rebuild the profile afterwards. Loose sections get refastened, the cracks get taped, then the wall is skimmed flat. The trim stays exactly where it has always been and only needs its own paint line.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (35,4,43,'Drywall Repair and Patching in Pickering','Drywall Repair in Pickering','Drywall repair in Pickering for basement walls, strapped ceilings and holes. Sercan boards, tapes, coats and sands to a paint-ready finish. Get a quote.','Drywall repair in Pickering often arrives with a renovation. The 1970s houses in West Shore, Bay Ridges and Amberlea still have wood panelling and strapped ceilings in the lower levels, and as those rooms get updated the new drywall has to meet what is already there.\n\nI am Sercan and I take the job from bare framing to a sanded, paint-ready surface. Where old and new material meet at different thicknesses, I shim and float the transition rather than leaving a step for the paint to point out.','Those 1970s Pickering basements are the reason. Panelling was often nailed straight to furring strips, so once it comes off the wall sits at an odd depth and fresh board will not land flush with the framing beside it. Ceilings in the same houses are strapped across the joists, which means a patch can fall between strapping with nothing to screw into, so backing goes in first. Rooms over an attached garage are the other regular, since that ceiling joint moves with the cold.','Boarding basement walls after old panelling comes off\nAdding backing where patches fall between ceiling strapping\nRepairing ceiling joints in rooms above attached garages\nPatching stipple ceilings in West Shore bungalows\nFinishing drywall in updated 1970s rec rooms','I am pulling the panelling off my Pickering basement. Can you board and finish what is behind it?','Yes, that is a job I do often down there. Once the panelling is off I check the strapping and framing, shim where the depths do not match, then board, tape, coat and sand the room so it is ready for primer. If part of the wall is sound and only the lower section needs replacing, I will say so and keep the work smaller.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (36,4,44,'Drywall Repair and Patching in Ajax','Drywall Repair and Patching in Ajax','Drywall repair in Ajax semis and townhouses: stairwell scrapes, dents and holes. Sercan can clear a list of small repairs in one visit. Book a free quote.','Drywall repair in Ajax is often a narrow-space problem. The semis and townhouse rows through Nottingham, Audley and the older streets south of Bayly have tight stairwells and slim hallways, and much of the damage I see was done by a sofa or a mattress on the way up.\n\nI am Sercan and I come on my own, which helps in a house where two people and a ladder will not fit on the landing. I patch, tape, sand and blend, and I can take a list of small marks around the house in one visit.','In an Ajax semi or town, the stairwell wall is the busiest wall in the house. It is long, it is seen from two floors at once, and it collects scraped corners and punched-in spots at exactly hip height. Working it means standing on the stairs, so I stage it properly and sand with a light held sideways to catch what a straight-on look misses. Tenant turnover in the basement suites is the other regular, where a whole floor of small marks has to go in one pass.','Repairing scraped stairwell corners in Ajax townhouses\nPatching hip-height dents along narrow hallways\nClearing anchor holes and marks between tenants\nFixing ceiling damage below an upstairs bathroom\nRepairing party wall damage after shelving removal','Can you fix a list of small dents and holes around my Ajax house in one visit?','That is the efficient way to do it. Compound needs drying time between coats, so a dozen small repairs take little more time than one, as long as I see them all on the first pass. Walk me through the house, I will note every mark, then you and I agree the price before I open a bag. Most lists like that take two short visits.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (37,6,1,'Faucet and Fixture Installation in Toronto','Faucet Installation in Toronto Homes','Faucet, toilet, shower head and shut-off valve replacement across Toronto homes, condos and basement suites. Call Sercan for a free, no-obligation quote.','Faucet installation in Toronto usually means working around plumbing older than the last three owners of the house. In the semis and Victorians of Riverdale, Cabbagetown and The Junction, the supply stops under the sink are often original and will not turn, and the wall behind the escutcheon is plaster, not drywall. I plan for that before I start.\n\nI replace kitchen and bathroom faucets, shower heads, toilets, drains, supply lines and shut-off valves. I quote first, agree the price with you, then do the work. Downtown condos and basement suites are both fine.','Toronto\'s pre-1950 housing is the real variable. In Riverdale, Cabbagetown and The Junction I often find no fixture shut-offs at all, so the water goes off at the main stop and the job has to move quickly. Old compression stops rarely reseat once turned, so I plan to replace them. Walls behind escutcheons are plaster and lath, which chips. Downtown, a tower riser shut-off has to be arranged through the concierge, and street parking on narrow one-ways decides how much I can carry in at once.','Seized shut-off valves replaced under old kitchen sinks\nPedestal sink taps changed in pre-war bathrooms\nPull-down faucet fitted on a narrow galley counter\nToilet replacement in a Leslieville semi\nUnder-sink leak repair in basement apartment kitchens\nShower head and tub spout swaps downtown','There are no shut-off valves under my sink. Can you still change the faucet?','Yes. A lot of older Toronto houses were built without fixture stops, so I shut the water at the main valve, drain the line and work from there. While the water is off I usually fit new quarter-turn stops under the sink, which makes every future repair simpler for you. If the main valve itself is seized, that is a licensed plumber\'s job and I will say so.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (38,6,2,'Faucet and Plumbing Fixture Installation in North York','Faucet Installation in North York','Kitchen and bathroom faucet, toilet and drain replacement for North York bungalows, side-splits and basement kitchens. Ask Sercan for a free quote today.','Faucet installation in North York often starts in a 1950s or 1960s bungalow or side-split, where the original two-handle taps sit on four-inch centres in a small steel-rimmed sink. The cabinet opening is tight, the nuts underneath are rusted, and the new faucet is a different shape entirely.\n\nI replace kitchen and bathroom faucets, shower heads, toilets, drains, supply lines and shut-off valves. Willowdale, Bathurst Manor, Downsview and the Yonge corridor are regular ground for me, houses and apartments both.','Two things shape this work in North York. The first is the 1950s and 1960s stock in Bathurst Manor, Downsview and old Willowdale, where basin taps sit on four-inch centres, the cabinet opening is barely wider than my forearm, and the chrome-plated brass tailpiece crumbles when touched. The second is the number of finished basements with a second kitchen. That doubles the fixtures in a house, and the basement sink usually sits under low joists with an awkward drain run, so I carry spare traps and tailpieces.','Two-handle basin taps replaced in 1950s bungalows\nSecond-kitchen faucet swaps in finished North York basements\nDeep island sink faucet changes in rebuilt Willowdale homes\nToilet and fill valve replacement in Downsview houses\nLaundry tub taps and hoses in older basements','Can you change the taps in my basement kitchen as well as upstairs?','Yes, and doing both in one visit is the sensible way. Second kitchens are common in North York, and the basement sink is usually the harder of the two because the drain sits low and the cabinet is shallow. I look at both before I quote, so you get one price and one visit rather than two separate callouts.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (39,6,3,'Faucet and Fixture Installation in Scarborough','Faucet Installation in Scarborough','Tap, toilet, tub trim and drain replacement for Scarborough bungalows, back-splits, rentals and second suites. Send Sercan your list and get a free quote.','Faucet installation in Scarborough is often part of a longer list. The bungalows and back-splits around Wexford, Bendale and Agincourt were built in the 1960s and 1970s, and the original taps, tub valves and pop-up drains tend to give out within a year or two of each other.\n\nI replace kitchen and bathroom faucets, shower heads, toilets, drains, supply lines and shut-off valves. Several small jobs can be batched into a single visit, which suits owners with a tenant downstairs and two sets of fixtures to keep working.','Scarborough\'s 1960s and 1970s back-splits and bungalows nearly all came with a steel tub, a two-handle valve and a chrome escutcheon, and that trim is the fixture I get asked about most here. Swapping the visible trim and the spout is straightforward; anything behind the tile is a licensed plumber\'s work and I say so up front. The other Scarborough pattern is the second suite. Owners with a basement apartment want the upstairs and downstairs fixtures sorted in the same visit, so I quote the whole list.','Two-handle tub trim and spout replacement\nKitchen faucet swaps in tenanted Scarborough bungalows\nBasement apartment sink and toilet fixture changes\nSeveral taps replaced across one rental property\nSlow bathroom drains and pop-up assembly repairs','I have a tenant downstairs. Can you do both units in one appointment?','Usually yes. Second suites are common in Scarborough and the fixtures in both kitchens and both bathrooms are often the same age. Give me the full list and I will quote it as one visit, then agree a time that works for your tenant. Booking it that way saves you a second callout and keeps the disruption to one afternoon.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (40,6,4,'Faucet and Fixture Installation in Etobicoke','Faucet Installation in Etobicoke','Faucet, shower head, toilet and valve replacement for Etobicoke condos and bungalows, from Humber Bay Shores to Alderwood. Free quote, price agreed first.','Faucet installation in Etobicoke splits neatly in two. Along Lake Shore Boulevard West and through Humber Bay Shores the work is in condo towers, where the building controls the water and the job has to fit a booked window. Inland, in Alderwood, Mimico and Long Branch, it is 1950s bungalows with original taps and a basement laundry tub.\n\nI replace kitchen and bathroom faucets, shower heads, toilets, drains, supply lines and shut-off valves, and I quote before anything is touched.','In the Humber Bay Shores and Islington towers, nothing starts until the water is arranged. If a suite has no working isolation valves, the riser has to be shut down by building staff with notice given to neighbours, and I need the service elevator booked to bring in a toilet or a sink. Builder single-lever faucets in those units usually fail at the cartridge first. The Alderwood and Mimico bungalows are the opposite problem: plenty of access, but sixty-year-old laundry taps that come off in pieces.','Condo kitchen faucet swaps at Humber Bay Shores\nCartridge and single-lever faucet replacement in tower suites\nLaundry tub taps in Alderwood and Mimico basements\nToilet replacement in Islington-area high-rise units\nUnder-sink supply lines and shut-off valves renewed','My condo has no isolation valves. How does the water get shut off?','In most Etobicoke towers the building has to shut the riser, which means giving notice to property management and to the units above and below. I will tell you what your building needs and work to the window they give me. Where valves do exist in the suite, I fit new quarter-turn stops while I am in there so the next repair needs no shutdown.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (41,6,25,'Faucet and Fixture Installation in Mississauga','Faucet Installation in Mississauga','Bathroom and kitchen faucet, toilet and drain replacement across Mississauga, from Erin Mills two-storeys to Square One condos. Free quote, no obligation.','Faucet installation in Mississauga is rarely a single tap. The 1980s and 1990s two-storeys in Erin Mills, Meadowvale and Applewood came with a main bathroom, an ensuite, a powder room and often a basement bath, and each one has been updated at a different time by a different owner.\n\nSo I get asked to bring the whole house back to one look. I replace faucets, shower heads, toilets, drains, supply lines and shut-off valves, and I price the full list before starting.','The pattern here is four bathrooms in four different decades. A Meadowvale or Erin Mills two-storey might have a widespread faucet in the ensuite, a centreset in the main bath, and a builder single-lever in the powder room, each with a different hole spacing. I measure every deck before you buy anything, because the wrong spread means a new sink, not just a new tap. Around Square One the picture changes to condo suites where the shut-off hides in a ceiling access panel or a closet.','Matching faucet finishes across three or more bathrooms\nEnsuite double vanity taps replaced in one visit\nSquare One condo kitchen faucet and drain swaps\nPowder room pedestal taps changed in Erin Mills\nShower heads and hand showers updated in Meadowvale','I want new taps in every bathroom. Is that one visit or several?','Normally one, as long as I know the count in advance. I ask for a photo of each sink deck and the space under it, then I bring the right supply lines and stops for all of them. A Mississauga two-storey with three or four bathrooms is usually a single day. If a sink turns out to be cracked underneath, I will show you before going further.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (42,6,26,'Faucet and Fixture Installation in Brampton','Faucet Installation in Brampton Homes','Faucet, toilet and drain replacement in Brampton, from Springdale and Mount Pleasant builds to older Bramalea homes. Get a free quote from Sercan today.','Faucet installation in Brampton is often a batch job, because the builder fixtures in Springdale, Mount Pleasant, Credit Valley and Fletcher\'s Meadow all went in the same week and now fail in the same season. One kitchen tap starts dripping and the ensuite is not far behind.\n\nI replace kitchen and bathroom faucets, shower heads, toilets, drains, supply lines and shut-off valves. Give me the whole list and I will price it as one visit, with the price agreed before I start.','Brampton\'s newer subdivisions are big houses with four or five plumbing fixtures fed by the cheapest parts the builder could buy, and after fifteen to twenty years the plastic shut-off stops and the kitchen cartridges are the first to go. Households here are often multi-generational, so the main kitchen faucet does the work of two, and many finished basements have a second kitchen sink under low joists. I bring stops, braided lines and traps in quantity rather than making a trip out for one part.','Builder-grade kitchen faucets replaced in Springdale homes\nPlastic shut-off stops swapped for quarter-turn valves\nSecond-kitchen faucet replacement in finished basements\nFour bathrooms updated across a single booking\nToilet swaps in Mount Pleasant two-storey homes\nUnder-sink leak repair before the cabinet swells','The taps in my Brampton house are all about twenty years old. Should I replace them together?','It usually costs you fewer visits if you do. Builder fixtures in these subdivisions went in at the same time and wear at the same rate, so replacing one often just moves the next leak along. I will look at all of them, tell you which are worth keeping, and quote the rest as one job.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (43,6,31,'Faucet and Fixture Installation in Vaughan','Faucet Installation in Vaughan Homes','Faucet, shower head and toilet replacement in Vaughan, including widespread taps on stone counters in Woodbridge and Maple. Book a free quote with Sercan.','Faucet installation in Vaughan usually involves stone. The 1990s and 2000s homes in Woodbridge, Maple and Thornhill were finished with granite or quartz counters and undermount sinks, so the tap mounts through thick stone with the bowl hanging below it and very little room for a wrench.\n\nThat is a job to take slowly. I replace kitchen and bathroom faucets, shower heads, toilets, drains, supply lines and shut-off valves, and I protect the counter and the cabinet floor before I open anything.','A widespread faucet on a Vaughan vanity is three separate pieces bolted through a stone deck you cannot afford to chip, with an undermount bowl stealing the space your hands need. The mounting nuts sit deep, the basin wrench barely reaches, and old brass shanks are often corroded to the underside of the granite. In the larger Woodbridge and Kleinburg houses the vanities are tall and the cabinet floor is finished wood, so I pad it and work with the doors off rather than forcing an angle.','Widespread faucets fitted to stone vanity tops\nUndermount kitchen sink faucet and drain replacement\nEnsuite shower heads and hand showers changed\nWoodbridge kitchen taps replaced without marking granite\nToilet replacement in two-storey Maple homes','Can a faucet be changed without damaging my granite counter?','Yes, with care. The risk is not the stone itself but the tools swinging under it, so I pad the cabinet, take the doors off for room, and cut a seized mounting nut rather than lever against the underside. Old silicone comes off the surface with a plastic blade. If a shank is truly fused, I will tell you before applying any force.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (44,6,32,'Faucet and Fixture Installation in Markham','Faucet Installation in Markham Homes','Bathroom and kitchen faucet, drain and toilet replacement in Markham, from Unionville vanity tops to Cornell townhome kitchens. Free quote from Sercan.','Faucet installation in Markham runs into two very different builds. The 1980s and 1990s houses in Milliken, Armadale and around Unionville were finished with cultured marble vanity tops where the bowl is moulded into the counter, and the taps sit on four-inch centres in a surface that does not forgive force.\n\nThe Cornell and Box Grove townhomes are newer and tighter, with galley kitchens and shallow cabinets. I replace faucets, shower heads, toilets, drains, supply lines and shut-off valves in both.','The cultured marble tops in Markham\'s 1980s and 1990s houses are the detail worth knowing about. Bowl and counter are one moulded piece, so a cracked deck means replacing the whole top, not a sink. The old centreset taps corrode onto it and the nuts have to be cut rather than turned. Those same houses have one-piece acrylic tub surrounds, where a shower arm has to come out without twisting the pipe behind it. In the Cornell townhomes the constraint is simply reach under a shallow galley cabinet.','Centreset taps replaced on cultured marble vanity tops\nGalley kitchen faucet swaps in Cornell townhomes\nShower arms and heads in one-piece tub surrounds\nToilet and supply line changes in Unionville homes\nSeized angle stops replaced under bathroom sinks','My vanity top has the sink moulded into it. Can the taps still be changed?','Usually yes. The bowl and counter being one piece does not stop a tap swap, it just means the new faucet has to match the existing hole spacing, which on these Markham tops is almost always four inches. I cut the old nuts off instead of forcing them, since cultured marble chips easily. If the deck is already cracked, I will show you first.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (45,6,33,'Faucet and Fixture Installation in Richmond Hill','Faucet Installation in Richmond Hill','Faucet, toilet, laundry tap and valve replacement in Richmond Hill, including upstairs laundry rooms and Oak Ridges kitchens. Free quote, no obligation.','Faucet installation in Richmond Hill covers everything from a 1960s bathroom near the Mill Pond to a second-floor laundry room in an Oak Ridges build. The newer homes put water above finished ceilings, which changes how carefully the supply lines and valves have to be handled.\n\nI replace kitchen and bathroom faucets, shower heads, toilets, drains, laundry taps, supply lines and shut-off valves. Free quote first, and the price is agreed before any work starts.','The 2000s homes east of Bayview and up in Oak Ridges put the laundry room on the second floor, directly over a finished ceiling. Rubber machine hoses and tired plastic taps up there are the fixtures I most often recommend changing, because a failure there ruins two floors instead of one. Kitchen islands in the same houses are deep, and the shut-offs sit at the back of a cabinet you can only reach lying down. The older Mill Pond streets are the reverse: small bathrooms, easy access, tired chrome.','Laundry taps and hoses in upstairs laundry rooms\nDeep island cabinet faucet replacement in Oak Ridges\nFiltered water taps refitted beside new kitchen faucets\nBathroom tap replacement in older Mill Pond homes\nToilet fill valve and flush valve changes','My laundry room is upstairs. Is changing the taps up there riskier?','The work is the same, but the consequence of getting it wrong is not, which is why I treat it differently. Above a finished ceiling I fit quarter-turn laundry valves and braided steel hoses rather than rubber, and I run the machine through a full cycle before I leave so any weep shows up while I am still standing there.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (46,6,47,'Faucet and Fixture Installation in Oakville','Faucet Installation in Oakville Homes','Faucet, toilet and drain replacement in Oakville, from period taps in Old Oakville to ensuite updates in Glen Abbey. Ask Sercan for a free quote by phone.','Faucet installation in Oakville often comes with a look to match. In the older streets off Lakeshore Road and through Kerr Village, owners want cross-handle or bridge-style fittings that suit the house, fitted to basins that were never designed around a modern tap body.\n\nFurther north, in Glen Abbey and Joshua Creek, it is more often a straight upgrade of builder fixtures. I replace faucets, shower heads, toilets, drains, supply lines and shut-off valves in both kinds of home.','Old Oakville and Kerr Village keep a lot of original pedestal and wall-hung basins, and a period-look tap has to match the hole spacing that basin was cast with, not the one the box assumes. Bridge faucets need the two supply shanks to line up exactly, and on a pedestal there is no cabinet to hide a misalignment. So I measure the centres before anything is ordered. Down by Bronte harbour the towers are the other job, with shut-offs and elevator access to arrange first.','Period-style cross-handle taps on original pedestal basins\nBridge faucets fitted to older Oakville kitchen sinks\nBronte condo bathroom faucet and drain swaps\nGlen Abbey ensuite taps and shower heads replaced\nPowder room fixtures updated before listing a house','I want a period-looking tap on my old pedestal sink. Will it fit?','Often, but the measurement decides it. Older Oakville basins were drilled to spacings that do not always match what a modern tap expects, and a pedestal gives you nowhere to hide an adaptor. Send me a photo with a tape measure across the holes before you buy, and I will tell you which styles sit properly on that basin.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (47,6,43,'Faucet and Fixture Installation in Pickering','Faucet Installation in Pickering Homes','Tap, tub trim, toilet and drain replacement in Pickering, from West Shore and Bay Ridges houses to new Seaton builds. Free quote with no obligation at all.','Faucet installation in Pickering tends to fall at one of two ends. In Bay Ridges, West Shore and Amberlea the houses date from the 1960s and 1970s, and the fixtures are often still the ones the builder fitted, chrome gone dull and washers long past saving.\n\nIn the new Seaton streets it is the opposite: everything works, but the owner wants better than builder standard. I replace faucets, shower heads, toilets, drains, supply lines and shut-off valves in both.','The 1960s and 1970s houses in West Shore and Bay Ridges usually still have the original two-handle tub valve, a steel tub and a chrome escutcheon that has been painted over at least once. I can renew the visible trim, the spout and the head; anything inside the wall belongs to a licensed plumber and I say so before quoting. Those basements also hold heavy concrete laundry tubs with wall-mounted taps that snap rather than turn. New Seaton homes are the clean opposite, modern stops and easy access.','Original two-handle tub trim replaced in West Shore homes\nConcrete laundry tub taps swapped in older basements\nBuilder faucets upgraded soon after closing in Seaton\nKitchen faucet and basket strainer replacement\nPop-up drain and tailpiece leak repair','The taps in my 1970s Pickering bathroom are original. Are parts still made for them?','Sometimes, but it is rarely worth chasing them. On fixtures that old the seats and stems are usually worn past the point where a new washer helps, and matching parts take longer to find than the repair takes to do. I will price both options honestly and let you choose. Replacing the whole tap generally lasts far longer.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (48,6,44,'Faucet and Fixture Installation in Ajax','Faucet Installation in Ajax Homes','Kitchen and bathroom faucet, toilet and drain replacement across Ajax and Pickering Village, including four-hole sink decks. Free quote from Sercan by phone.','Faucet installation in Ajax most often means a stainless double-bowl sink with four holes in the deck, the standard fit in the 1990s and 2000s subdivisions around Westney and Harwood. The old single-lever tap and its side spray came as a pair, and the modern pull-down replacing them only needs one hole.\n\nSo the spare holes have to be dealt with properly. I replace faucets, shower heads, toilets, drains, supply lines and shut-off valves, and I quote before I start.','Almost every Ajax kitchen built in the 1990s and 2000s has the same four-hole stainless sink: tap, two spare, and a side spray that has probably stopped working. A pull-down faucet fills one hole, which leaves two or three staring at you. I fit a soap dispenser, an air gap or a matching cover plate so the deck looks intended rather than patched. Pickering Village is the exception, with older houses, smaller sinks and shut-offs that need replacing before the tap can come off.','Pull-down faucets fitted to four-hole stainless sinks\nSide spray holes filled with dispensers or plates\nTownhouse powder room taps and toilets swapped\nPickering Village bathroom fixture replacement\nSeveral small plumbing fixes batched into one visit','My sink has extra holes from the old side spray. What happens to them?','They get covered or used, never left open. Most pull-down faucets come with a deck plate that hides one or two, and beyond that a soap dispenser is the tidiest fix and costs little. I will ask how many holes your sink has before I quote, so the parts arrive with me and the job finishes in one visit.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (49,3,1,'Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Toronto','IKEA and Furniture Assembly in Toronto','Flat-pack and IKEA furniture assembly across Toronto, from Riverdale semis to downtown condos. Built level, anchored, cardboard removed. Ask for a quote.','Furniture assembly in Toronto usually happens in a room that was built long before flat-pack existed. I work in the old city every week, in Riverdale semis, Annex apartments and Parkdale walk-ups, where the floor slopes and the doorway is narrower than the box.\n\nI bring the tools, sort the hardware, build the piece where it will stand, level it against the floor you actually have, and anchor it to something solid. Then the cardboard leaves with me. Free quote first, and the price is agreed before I start.','Most of the old city predates drywall. Behind the paint in a pre-1950 Toronto house there is often plaster over wood lath, which crumbles around a plain screw, so a tall wardrobe or bookcase gets a toggle or a screw driven into a located stud instead. Floors have settled too, so the levelling feet do real work, and a cabinet door that swings shut on its own is usually the house rather than the furniture. On permit-parking streets I unload fast and move the van.','Tall wardrobes built in narrow Victorian semi bedrooms\nBookcases anchored to plaster and lath in Annex apartments\nBed frames carried up third-floor walk-up stairs in pieces\nDesks and shelving for downtown condo work-from-home corners\nSideboards and storage units levelled on sloping century floors','My place is a third-floor walk-up with a tight turn. Can the box even get up there?','Usually yes, because the box is flat. The problem is normally the finished piece, not the carton, so I carry the panels up and build in the room where it will live. If a landing is too tight even for the flat box, I open it downstairs and take the parts up in trips. Send me a photo of the stairs and I will tell you before you buy.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (50,3,2,'Furniture and IKEA Assembly in North York','IKEA Furniture Assembly in North York','Flat-pack and IKEA furniture assembly in North York, from Willowdale apartments to Bathurst Manor bungalows. Levelled, anchored, tidied. Book a free quote.','Furniture assembly in North York often comes down to ceiling height. Tall wardrobes are drawn for a modern room, and a 1960s bungalow basement or a finished lower-level suite in Willowdale rarely gives the clearance needed to tip one upright.\n\nSo I measure floor to ceiling before the first panel goes together, and build standing rather than flat when the room is short. Everything gets levelled, anchored back to the framing and cleared of packaging. I work Monday to Saturday and can take several rooms in one visit.','A lot of North York is post-war frame housing, the bungalows and side-splits through Bathurst Manor, Newtonbrook and Don Mills, with studs at sixteen inches and finished basements offering about seven feet of headroom. A 236 cm wardrobe cannot be built flat and tilted up down there, so it goes together upright, one section at a time, braced as I go. Ducts and bulkheads run low in those basements as well, so a tall bookcase often lands under a drop and the top shelf has to be planned around it.','Tall wardrobes built upright in low-ceiling basement rooms\nBunk beds and study desks for Willowdale family homes\nDressers anchored into studs in post-war bungalow bedrooms\nSliding-door wardrobes fitted along a side-split hallway wall\nDesks and shelving for finished lower-level suites','Will a full-height IKEA wardrobe fit in my basement bedroom?','Measure floor to ceiling first and send me the number. Tall frames need more than their own height to swing upright, so in a seven-foot basement I build them vertically in place instead. If it still will not clear once the levelling feet are wound up, a shorter frame with separate top boxes gives the same storage. Better to sort that out before you order than after.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (51,3,3,'Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Scarborough','Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Scarborough','IKEA and flat-pack furniture assembly in Scarborough, from Agincourt backsplits to Kingston Road towers. Built level and wall anchored. Get a free quote.','Furniture assembly in Scarborough means working around split-level stairs and concrete apartment walls. A finished dresser will not turn the half-landing in an Agincourt backsplit, and an anti-tip anchor in a Kingston Road tower is not going into wood.\n\nSo I build in the room the piece is staying in, and I carry masonry bits alongside the usual drywall fixings. Beds, bunk beds, wardrobes and shelving all get squared, levelled and fixed back to the wall, and the flattened cardboard goes out with me.','Two things shape this work in Scarborough. The side-splits and backsplits through Wexford, Agincourt and Bendale have short stair runs with a turn at every landing, so anything wider than a doorway has to be assembled upstairs rather than carried up finished. And the rental towers along Markham Road, Lawrence and Kingston Road are poured concrete and block, which means a hammer drill and sleeve fixings for the anti-tip bracket, plus a booked service elevator to get boxes up from the loading dock.','Bunk beds built upstairs in split-level Scarborough bedrooms\nWardrobes anchored into concrete walls in rental apartments\nFull bedroom sets assembled after a move into Agincourt\nShelving and wardrobes for basement second suites\nDressers and TV units levelled on uneven basement floors','The walls in my apartment are solid concrete. Can you still anchor a tall wardrobe?','Yes. Concrete and block just need a different approach: a hammer drill, a masonry bit and a sleeve or screw anchor rated for that wall, rather than the plastic plugs in the box. It takes a few minutes longer and ends up stronger than any drywall fixing. Tell me if your building restricts drilling hours and I will work inside them.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (52,3,4,'Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Etobicoke','IKEA Assembly in Etobicoke Condos and Homes','Flat-pack and IKEA furniture assembly in Etobicoke, from Humber Bay condos to Alderwood bungalows. Elevator windows respected, boxes gone. Ask for a quote.','Furniture assembly in Etobicoke splits between the condo towers at Humber Bay Shores and the post-war bungalows of Alderwood, Mimico and Islington. The tower jobs run on the building\'s clock. The bungalow jobs run on small bedrooms and old, narrow doorways.\n\nIn a tower, book the service elevator and tell me the window, and I will plan the visit around it and flatten every carton for the recycling room. In a house I unload at the door and build the piece in the room it belongs in.','In the Humber Bay and Mimico towers the loading dock, the service elevator and the moving window decide the whole day, so I want to know the booking before confirming a visit, and I flatten cartons on the spot because the recycling room will not swallow a full-size wardrobe box whole. Those living rooms are shallow between the balcony wall and the kitchen, which matters when a media unit is deeper than the wall it faces. Out in Alderwood and Islington the bedrooms are simply small.','Wardrobes and beds assembled inside a booked elevator window\nMedia units sized to shallow condo living rooms\nBedroom sets built in small Alderwood bungalow rooms\nBalcony storage and slim shelving for Humber Bay units\nCartons flattened and carried to the recycling room','My condo only gives me a two-hour elevator booking. Is that enough time?','For most single pieces, yes, provided the boxes are already inside the unit or waiting on the dock when I arrive. The elevator window is for moving, not for building, and once the cartons are in I can keep working after it closes. Tell me the booked time and the number of boxes and I will say honestly whether it fits.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (53,3,25,'Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Mississauga','IKEA and Flat-Pack Assembly in Mississauga','Furniture and IKEA assembly in Mississauga, from Churchill Meadows new builds to Square One condos. Beds, wardrobes and desks anchored. Request a quote.','Furniture assembly in Mississauga is usually a whole-house list rather than one item. In the newer subdivisions off Erin Mills and through Churchill Meadows, people close on a bigger place and end up with a hallway of flat-pack boxes covering four rooms.\n\nThat suits the way I work. I set a day aside, build room by room, anchor everything tall, and either stack the flattened cardboard in the garage or take it away. The quote is free and the price is agreed before a single box is opened.','Newer Mississauga homes give me room most jobs do not have: a deep double garage to lay panels out in, nine-foot main floors and wide upstairs halls, so a long wall unit can be built flat and walked in whole rather than assembled in a corner. The catch is the walls. Builder framing runs at sixteen inches, but the broad bedroom wall a wardrobe lands on may have only one stud within reach, so I find it, mark it and anchor to that instead of trusting drywall.','Whole-home flat-pack lists after closing in Churchill Meadows\nWall units built flat in the garage, then carried in\nWardrobes and closet organizers for new-build bedrooms\nDesks and shelving for Square One condo home offices\nBeds and dressers assembled during a weekend move','I have boxes for four rooms sitting in the garage. Can it all be done in one visit?','Often yes. Send me the product names and I will work out how long that list honestly takes, then tell you whether it is one long day or two. Building several pieces on one visit costs you less than booking them separately, and I would rather stage it properly than rush the last bedroom at eight at night.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (54,3,26,'Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Brampton','Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Brampton','Flat-pack and IKEA furniture assembly across Brampton, from Springdale homes to basement suites in Mount Pleasant. Built, anchored, tidied. Message me today.','Furniture assembly in Brampton is usually about volume. A Springdale or Castlemore house has more bedrooms than most, and when a family moves in there can be five beds, three dressers and a study to put together at the same time.\n\nI batch that into one visit and work through it room by room, so the house is usable by the end of the day instead of after a week of evenings. Everything tall gets anchored, everything sits level, and the boxes go out flat.','Brampton\'s 2000s subdivisions were built with bedrooms for everybody, and the lower level is very often a separate suite, which changes the job twice over. The basement stair is long, straight and narrow with a door at the bottom, so a built bed frame or wardrobe will never make that turn and has to go down as loose panels. And furniture in a suite usually has to sit against a bulkhead or a duct chase rather than a clear flat wall, so the layout gets decided before anything is opened.','Several bedroom sets built in one Springdale visit\nBeds and wardrobes carried down as panels to basement suites\nStudy desks and shelving for older children\nDressers and bunk beds anchored back to bedroom studs\nStorage cabinets fitted around basement bulkheads and ducts','Can you do the main house and the basement apartment on the same day?','Usually, as long as I know the full list beforehand. The two parts of the house work differently, so I plan the basement first while the stairs are still clear, then move upstairs. Give me the item names and the number of rooms and I will tell you honestly whether it is one visit or two before you book anything.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (55,3,31,'Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Vaughan','IKEA and Furniture Assembly in Vaughan','IKEA and furniture assembly in Vaughan, from Woodbridge two-storeys to Vaughan Metropolitan Centre condos. Wardrobes and beds built to fit. Call for a quote.','Furniture assembly in Vaughan often happens in rooms taller than the furniture. Woodbridge and Maple houses from the last twenty years have nine and ten-foot main floors, so a standard bookcase or wardrobe leaves a band of bare wall floating above it.\n\nThat is fixable with extension units, a taller frame, or a run built in two heights that reads as one piece. I plan the layout with you before the first screw goes in, then level the run, anchor it back to the wall and clear away the packaging.','Ceiling height is the running theme in Vaughan. Through the Woodbridge, Maple and Kleinburg-edge subdivisions the main floor is commonly nine or ten feet with a two-storey foyer, so shelving and wall units come up short and need extension frames stacked on top, which only holds together if the whole run is plumb and screwed back to the wall. Bedrooms upstairs sit at eight feet, so the same product gets built two different ways inside one house.','Wall units with extension frames for ten-foot ceilings\nMedia walls built tall for open Woodbridge family rooms\nWardrobes assembled in large Maple primary bedrooms\nCondo furniture built at Vaughan Metropolitan Centre\nWalk-in closet systems built out in Kleinburg homes','My living room ceiling is ten feet and the shelving looks short. What can be done?','Extension units are made for exactly this. The frame you already have takes a matching top section, and once the whole run is levelled, joined and anchored back to the wall it reads as a built-in rather than a stack of boxes. Send me the model name and your ceiling measurement and I will tell you which parts to order.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (56,3,32,'Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Markham','Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Markham','Flat-pack and IKEA furniture assembly in Markham, from Cornell townhomes to Unionville houses. Beds, wardrobes and desks anchored. Book a free quote.','Furniture assembly in Markham frequently means a three-storey freehold townhouse. In Cornell, Wismer and Greensborough the staircase runs up the middle of the house and turns at every floor, and the bedroom wanting the wardrobe sits two flights above the garage.\n\nSo I carry flat and build on the floor the piece is staying on. It is the only sensible way in those houses, and it saves your railings and drywall corners along the way. At the end the cartons come back down flattened and leave with me.','Markham\'s newer freehold towns are tall and narrow, so everything about this job is vertical. Garage and entry sit on the ground level, living space in the middle, bedrooms on top, and the stair turns twice on the way. A built bed frame or a six-drawer dresser will not make that trip, so the panels go up in the carton and the build happens in the bedroom. The older Unionville and Milliken two-storeys are easier, though their bedroom doorways are narrower than what builders use now.','Wardrobes built on the top floor of Cornell townhomes\nBeds and dressers carried up two flights as panels\nMiddle-floor dining and storage units for narrow towns\nDesks and shelving for Unionville home offices\nNursery furniture assembled and anchored before a due date','The wardrobe is going on the third floor of a townhouse. Will it fit up the stairs?','Built, almost never. Flat, nearly always. I take the cartons up before opening anything, lay a blanket down in the bedroom and put the piece together where it will stand. That keeps the railings and drywall corners intact and avoids that moment where something is wedged on a landing. Tell me the model and I will confirm the carton sizes first.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (57,3,33,'Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Richmond Hill','IKEA Furniture Assembly in Richmond Hill','Furniture and IKEA assembly in Richmond Hill, from Bayview Hill bedrooms to Oak Ridges basements. Levelled, anchored, packaging removed. Get in touch today.','Furniture assembly in Richmond Hill often lands in a room with a sloped or vaulted ceiling. The 1990s houses through Bayview Hill and Devonsleigh have generous primary bedrooms where the ceiling climbs across the room, and a full-height wardrobe only works on one wall.\n\nI check that before building rather than after. The same goes for the study and library shelving people add later: measured, plumb, anchored into studs, then the packaging leaves with me. The quote is free and the price is agreed before I start.','The larger 1990s Richmond Hill houses have shape to them, with vaulted and tray ceilings in the primary bedroom, angled walls in the bonus room over the garage, and window walls eating the space a wardrobe wants. A full-height unit usually has exactly one wall it can stand against, and finding that wall is half the visit. Up in Oak Ridges the older houses and their additions are less predictable behind the drywall, so I locate studs properly instead of trusting a sixteen-inch guess.','Wardrobes placed on the one full-height bedroom wall\nStudy and library shelving anchored in Bayview Hill homes\nBonus-room furniture built around angled ceilings over garages\nBeds and dressers assembled after a Richmond Hill move\nBasement office and storage units in Oak Ridges','My bedroom ceiling slopes. Can a full-height wardrobe still work in there?','It can, but usually only against the tall wall, and that decides the layout of the whole room. I measure at the exact spot the unit will stand, crown moulding included, before a box is opened. If the numbers come out tight, a lower frame with separate top boxes on the sloped side gives you the storage without the fight.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (58,3,47,'Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Oakville','Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Oakville','IKEA and flat-pack furniture assembly in Oakville, from Kerr Village century homes to Joshua Creek new builds. Careful on hardwood. Ask about a quote.','Furniture assembly in Oakville covers two very different houses. Around Kerr Village, Bronte and the old downtown the homes are older, with narrow doorways, low cellar headroom and floors that have moved. Out in Joshua Creek and River Oaks the rooms are big and the finishes are new.\n\nBoth need care with the floor. I work on a blanket, keep hardware off the boards, and felt every foot before a piece takes its own weight. Then it is levelled, anchored where it needs to be, and the cardboard goes.','Old Oakville and Bronte have doorways closer to thirty inches than the thirty-two used now, plus tight cellar stairs and beams you duck under, so a wide dresser is built in the room it is staying in or not at all. In the newer east-end houses the problem reverses: acres of engineered hardwood and an owner who will spot a single scratch. Panels go on a blanket, screws never sit loose on the boards, and every foot gets a felt pad before the load goes on.','Dressers built in place behind narrow century-home doorways\nWardrobes and closet systems for Joshua Creek bedrooms\nFurniture felted and protected on new engineered hardwood\nBasement shelving fitted under low Bronte cellar beams\nDining and sideboard units assembled before a family event','My floors are new hardwood throughout. How do you keep them from getting marked?','I work on a blanket, never on the bare floor. Panels, hardware and tools stay on it while I build, cam locks and screws never sit loose on the boards, and every foot or plinth gets a felt pad before the piece takes its weight. Anything heavy is lifted and placed rather than slid across. It costs a few minutes and saves the floor.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (59,3,43,'Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Pickering','IKEA and Furniture Assembly in Pickering','Flat-pack and IKEA assembly in Pickering, from Bay Ridges bungalows to Duffin Heights new builds. Desks, beds and shelving built right. Reach out for a quote.','Furniture assembly in Pickering is very often a home office. Plenty of the town commutes into Toronto a few days a week, so the spare bedroom or a corner of the basement ends up with a desk, a filing unit and a wall of shelving.\n\nBasements are where it gets interesting, because of the posts, beams and duct drops. I set the run out on the floor first so nothing has to be cut short later, then build, level and anchor it. Packaging goes out with me at the end.','Pickering basements set the terms. The 1970s and 1980s houses through Bay Ridges, Amberlea and West Shore have a steel post mid-room, a main beam across it and bulkheads carrying ducts, and the ceiling under those drops loses several inches. A bookcase or wardrobe measured against the open part of the room will not stand under the bulkhead, so I take the lowest measurement rather than the friendliest one. Standing desks want the same check, since they run out of travel under a duct.','Home office desks and shelving in spare bedrooms\nBasement storage laid out around posts and beams\nBookcases sized to stand under duct bulkheads\nBed frames and dressers for Amberlea family homes\nFlat-pack shelving for garages and utility rooms','Can you set up a home office in the basement without it looking like an afterthought?','Yes, and it starts by measuring under the lowest bulkhead instead of in the open part of the room. I put the desk where the daylight and the outlets actually are, run the shelving on a line that clears the beam, and keep the cables tucked behind the unit. If a piece is going to be too tall down there, I will say so before it is opened.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (60,3,44,'Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Ajax','Furniture and IKEA Assembly in Ajax','Furniture and IKEA assembly in Ajax, from newer subdivisions to older streets near Harwood Avenue. Bunk beds, cribs and dressers anchored. Send me your list.','Furniture assembly in Ajax is usually for a young family. The newer subdivisions north of Taunton Road are full of first houses, and the calls that come in are cribs, change tables, bunk beds and a dresser that has to be safe with a toddler in the room.\n\nAnchoring is not an optional extra on those jobs. Every tall piece gets fixed back to a stud, bunk rails and ladders are checked and tightened once the build is done, and the packaging and plastic straps leave with me.','Most of the flat-pack going into Ajax right now is children\'s furniture, and that changes what matters. Bedrooms in the newer parts of town are modest, usually with a window on one wall and a closet on another, so the bunk bed and the dresser end up on an outside wall where the framing sits behind insulation and a vapour barrier. I find the real stud rather than guessing at spacing, use the restraint that came in the carton, and check the top bunk still clears the light fixture.','Cribs and change tables assembled before a baby arrives\nBunk beds built and anchored in small bedrooms\nDressers strapped back to the wall for toddler safety\nDesks and storage for growing children\'s rooms\nFull bedroom sets after a first-home move','How do you make a bunk bed safe in a builder-basic bedroom?','I build it square, tighten the fixings in the right order, and fix the frame back to a stud rather than into drywall plugs. Rails and ladder get checked at the end, then again after my tools are loaded. In a small room I also look at the light fixture and the window latch, so the top bunk is not pressed against either one.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (61,1,1,'Handyman Services in Toronto for Repairs and Odd Jobs','Handyman Services in Toronto - Small Repairs','Small repairs and odd jobs for Toronto homes and condos, from plaster walls in the old city to a full list in a downtown unit. Ask Sercan for a free quote.','Handyman services in Toronto cover a lot of ground, because the housing does. In one week I might be patching plaster in a Victorian semi off Dundas, then swapping a light fixture in a King West condo. I am Sercan, and I do the work myself, so the person you speak to on the phone is the person at your door.\n\nMost Toronto calls come as a list rather than one job. Downtown parking and elevator bookings make repeat visits awkward, so I would rather work through the whole list in a single trip and leave you with nothing outstanding.','Much of old Toronto was built before 1950, so the walls are plaster over wood lath rather than drywall. A standard drywall anchor spins uselessly in it, and the keyed coat behind crumbles if you lean on the drill. I find the studs, use the right fastener, and patch with setting compound so the repair does not shrink back. Century-old settlement also racks door frames, which is why so many downtown doors bind in summer and rattle in winter.','Patching plaster and lath walls in older downtown semis\nEasing and rehanging doors that bind in humid weather\nCondo repair lists done in one booked elevator window\nSwapping dated light fixtures in Annex and Riverdale homes\nAnchoring shelves and coat racks in narrow front halls','Can you do several small jobs in my Toronto condo in one visit?','Yes, and that is usually the sensible way to do it. Tell me the full list when you call, and I will estimate how long it takes so you can book the service elevator for the right window. I confirm the price before I start. If something on the list turns out to need a licensed trade, I will say so rather than guess.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (62,1,2,'Handyman Services in North York for Home Repairs','Handyman Services in North York - Repairs','Home repairs, maintenance and small fixes across North York, from Willowdale bungalows to Yonge Street condo units. Send me your list and get a free quote.','Handyman services in North York usually start with a list on the fridge. A closet door off its track, a tap that drips, a shelf that never went up. I am Sercan, and I do the work myself across Willowdale, Bathurst Manor, Downsview and the rest of the area.\n\nI would rather come once and clear the whole list than come three times for three small things. Tell me everything when you call, I will give you a free quote, and the price is agreed before I pick up a tool.','Most North York calls are a list rather than a single job, and the list usually spans three eras of the same house: an original post-war bungalow, a basement finished in the seventies, and a kitchen or bathroom redone in the last decade. That mix decides the order I work in. Panelled basement walls and drop ceilings get checked before anything is hung, while the rooms upstairs take ordinary fixings. Working through the whole list in one visit saves you paying for a second trip.','Fixing closet and interior doors in post-war bungalows\nMounting shelves in panelled seventies basement rec rooms\nSwapping tired light fixtures through a Willowdale bungalow\nOne-visit repair lists for Yonge corridor condo units\nRehanging sagging kitchen cabinet doors and drawer fronts','I have about eight small jobs around my North York bungalow. Is that too many for one visit?','That is a good size for a single booking. Send me the list with a photo or two and I will tell you honestly what fits in one visit and what needs a second. Nothing gets started until you have the price. If one item turns out to be bigger than it looked, I will explain the options before doing anything.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (63,1,3,'Handyman Services in Scarborough for Repairs and Upkeep','Scarborough Handyman Services and Repairs','Repairs and upkeep for Scarborough houses, finished basements and second suites, done by Sercan himself. Free quote and an agreed price before work starts.','Handyman services in Scarborough often mean a whole house, not one room. The bungalows and backsplits here have been added to and finished over the years, so there is usually a main floor list and a basement list. I am Sercan, and I take care of both in the same visit.\n\nI work across Wexford, Agincourt, Bendale, Guildwood and the streets off Kingston Road. Free quote first, price agreed before anything starts, and a reply back to you usually within one business day.','A lot of Scarborough work happens below grade. The bungalows and backsplits around Wexford, Agincourt and Bendale have concrete block foundation walls, and once a basement is finished the framing is often strapping fastened straight to that block. Hitting block instead of stud changes the fastener and the drill, so I carry masonry bits as a matter of course. Many of these houses also have a second kitchen and a separate entrance downstairs, which doubles the doors, taps and handles that wear out.','Anchoring shelving to concrete block basement walls\nFixing doors and handles in basement second suites\nRepair lists for older Wexford and Agincourt bungalows\nSmall fixes in detached garages and backyard sheds\nTightening railings on backsplit half-flight stairs','Can you look after both the main floor and the basement apartment on one trip?','Yes, and it usually works out better that way. A lot of Scarborough houses have a separate unit downstairs with its own kitchen and bathroom, so the same worn hinges, taps and handles show up twice. I do both sets in one visit and quote it as a single job. Just let me know if a tenant needs notice before I arrive.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (64,1,4,'Handyman Services in Etobicoke for Homes and Condos','Handyman Services in Etobicoke - Home Fixes','Odd jobs and repairs in Etobicoke, from Humber Bay condo units to Alderwood and Long Branch bungalows. Tell me your list and I will quote the whole lot free.','Handyman services in Etobicoke cover two very different kinds of home. There are the lakeside condo towers at Humber Bay, and there are the older bungalows through Alderwood, Long Branch and New Toronto. I am Sercan, and I do the work myself in both.\n\nThe approach is the same either way. Send me the full list, I give you a free quote, and the price is settled before I start. If your building needs a booked elevator window, tell me and I will plan the visit around it.','Etobicoke splits neatly in two for me. In the Humber Bay and Mimico towers the outside is glass curtain wall, so blinds, shelves and TVs have to land on the few solid partitions or on the concrete slab overhead, and that means hammer drilling rather than a stud finder. In the Alderwood and Long Branch bungalows the problem is the opposite: low basement head height, so shelving, ceiling fixtures and door swings all have to be measured before anything gets ordered.','Condo repair lists finished inside one elevator booking\nDrilling and anchoring into concrete condo ceilings and walls\nFitting blinds and shades on Humber Bay windows\nSmall repairs in low-ceilinged Alderwood basements\nAdjusting sliding patio doors that stick and rattle','My condo is glass right around the outside. Where can things actually be mounted?','Not on the window wall, and that catches people out. In the Humber Bay towers the exterior is glass and framing, so anything heavy goes on an interior partition or into the concrete slab above. I will walk the unit with you first, find what is solid, and suggest a spot that works before drilling a single hole.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (65,1,25,'Handyman Services in Mississauga for Home Repairs','Handyman Services in Mississauga - Repairs','Repairs, adjustments and small jobs for Mississauga homes in Erin Mills, Meadowvale and Streetsville. Batch the whole list into one visit and ask for a quote.','Handyman services in Mississauga are usually about a house catching up with itself. The Erin Mills, Meadowvale and Streetsville homes are old enough now that the doors, hinges, taps and closets all want attention in the same year. I am Sercan, and I work through that list myself.\n\nWrite down everything, including the jobs you think are too small to mention. Those are the ones that get skipped for years. I quote the whole lot for free, agree the price with you, and clear as much as fits in one visit.','The big Mississauga subdivisions in Erin Mills, Meadowvale and Applewood went up from the late seventies through the nineties, and builder-grade fittings all reach the end of their life at roughly the same time. So the calls here are rarely one item. Bifold closet doors off their track, brass hinges that sag, a hollow-core door rubbing the jamb, a wobbling stair spindle, a screen door that no longer closes. That kind of list is exactly what one visit is for.','Rehanging bifold closet doors that jump the track\nReplacing worn hinges and handles through a whole house\nLong repair lists in Erin Mills and Meadowvale homes\nRefitting screen doors and weatherstripping before winter\nTightening loose stair spindles and wobbling handrails','Is it worth calling for jobs as small as a sticking door and a loose towel bar?','Yes, as long as they come as a group. One small item on its own is hard to justify once the travel is counted. Five or six together makes a proper visit and much better use of the trip. Most Mississauga customers send me a list of eight or ten, and I work down it in the order that matters most to them.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (66,1,26,'Handyman Services in Brampton for Repairs and Odd Jobs','Handyman Services in Brampton - Home Repairs','Repairs and odd jobs for Brampton homes, including tall foyers, upper floors and finished basements. Free quotes and an agreed price before any work begins.','Handyman services in Brampton mean working in newer houses with taller rooms than most. Nine-foot ceilings, an open foyer, a finished basement below. I am Sercan, and I bring the ladders and stands that those heights actually need.\n\nBrampton families tend to save jobs up, which suits me. Springdale, Heart Lake, Fletcher\'s Meadow, Bramalea, it is the same pattern: a list built up over months. I quote it free, agree the price first, and get through it in one visit where I can.','Most Brampton houses I work in went up after 2000, which means nine-foot main floors and, in a lot of them, a foyer open to the second storey. A light or an alarm up there is not a step-ladder job; it needs a proper stand-off setup that sits safely on the stair treads. The other Brampton pattern is the finished basement used by extended family, so I often end up doing the same short list twice, once upstairs and once down.','Changing light fixtures in two-storey open foyers\nReplacing high foyer bulbs and battery smoke alarms\nRepair lists split between main floor and basement\nMounting shelves and racks in deep attached garages\nFixing doors and hardware in Springdale and Bramalea homes','The light in my Brampton foyer is two storeys up. Can you reach it?','Yes. That is a common call here because so many Brampton homes open the foyer to the second floor. I use a stand that sits level and secure on the stair treads rather than balancing a ladder on a step. If the fixture is being replaced rather than just relamped, send me a photo of it and the ceiling first so I bring the right parts.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (67,1,31,'Handyman Services in Vaughan for Larger Homes','Handyman Services in Vaughan - Home Repairs','Repairs and small jobs for larger Vaughan homes in Woodbridge, Maple and Kleinburg, worked through floor by floor. Send over your list for a free quote.','Handyman services in Vaughan tend to involve more house than average. Woodbridge, Maple, Thornhill and Kleinburg are full of big homes, and a big home collects a long list. I am Sercan, and I walk the whole place before I start so the visit runs in a sensible order.\n\nDoors, hardware, shelving, fixtures, the small things that never quite got finished. I quote for free, the price is agreed before any work begins, and I usually reply within one business day.','Vaughan houses in Woodbridge, Maple and Kleinburg tend to be large, and the jobs spread across three floors plus a finished basement. That changes how I plan a visit: I walk the whole house first and order the list by floor so I am not carrying tools up and down all day. The homes themselves are heavier built than most, with solid-core interior doors and substantial hardware, so a sagging door usually means the hinge screws have pulled rather than that the door has warped.','Resetting solid-core doors that have dropped on their hinges\nWhole-house lists worked through floor by floor\nHanging heavy mirrors and artwork in tall great rooms\nReplacing cabinet handles and hinges throughout a large kitchen\nSmall fixes gathered up across Woodbridge and Maple homes','My list runs to about twenty items across the whole house. How do you handle that?','I ask for it in writing, then walk the house with you and group the items by floor. Some will take five minutes, some will need a part ordered first. I tell you which is which, give you a price for the group, and you decide what gets done on the first visit and what waits for a second.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (68,1,32,'Handyman Services in Markham for Home Repairs','Handyman Services in Markham - Repairs','Repairs, home office fit-outs and small jobs for Markham houses in Unionville, Wismer and Berczy. One visit, one agreed price. Get a free quote this week.','Handyman services in Markham often start with a room that changed use. A bedroom became an office, a basement became a study space, and the walls were never set up for it. I am Sercan, and a lot of my Markham visits are about making rooms work harder.\n\nThe rest is ordinary upkeep in nineties and 2000s houses through Unionville, Wismer, Berczy and Cornell. Send me the list, get a free quote, agree the price, and I get through it in one trip.','A large share of my Markham list work is home-office related. In Unionville, Wismer and Berczy a spare bedroom has usually become a working office, so I am mounting monitor arms and shelves on ordinary bedroom partitions that were never meant to carry weight, tidying cable runs and fitting a proper desk light. The other steady Markham request comes from nineties builder kitchens: cabinet hinges that no longer hold a door closed, drawer slides that drag, and handles swapped across every door at once.','Mounting monitor arms and shelves in home offices\nTidying cable runs behind desks and media units\nSwapping cabinet hinges and handles in nineties kitchens\nFixing doors and closets in Wismer and Berczy houses\nWall storage fitted in narrow Cornell townhome rooms','I am setting up a home office in a spare Markham bedroom. What can you do in a day?','Quite a lot, if the parts are on site. A typical day is two monitor arms, a run of shelving, cable management down the wall, a blind adjusted so the screen is not washed out, and the door eased so it closes quietly. Tell me the room size and what you have already bought, and I will confirm what fits.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (69,1,33,'Handyman Services in Richmond Hill for Home Upkeep','Handyman Services in Richmond Hill - Repairs','Indoor and outdoor repairs for Richmond Hill homes with walkouts, decks and exterior stairs. Sercan does the work himself. Ask for a free no-obligation quote.','Handyman services in Richmond Hill often send me outside as well as in. The land here rises, so walkout basements, rear decks and exterior stairs are part of nearly every house. I am Sercan, and I handle the outdoor list along with the indoor one.\n\nI cover Oak Ridges, Bayview Hill, Mill Pond and the Yonge Street buildings. Free quote, price agreed before I start, and a reply back to you usually within one business day.','Richmond Hill sits on rising ground, and builders used that grade, so walkout and lookout basements are everywhere here. That puts a good share of my work outside the house rather than inside it: exterior stair treads, a loose deck railing above a walkout, a rear door that has dropped and no longer latches, weatherstripping that gave up after a winter of wind across an open back slope. Inside, the Oak Ridges and Bayview Hill homes usually mean tall stairwell walls to work on safely.','Tightening deck railings above walkout basement doors\nAdjusting rear doors that no longer latch properly\nReplacing worn weatherstripping before the first cold snap\nRepairing loose exterior stair treads and handrails\nIndoor repair lists in Oak Ridges and Bayview Hill homes','Can you take care of jobs outside the house as well as inside?','Yes, and in Richmond Hill they often come together. Because so many homes here have a walkout, the back of the house takes real weather, so the same visit might cover a rear door that has dropped, a loose railing post and a set of shelves in the basement. I go through the outside list in daylight and finish inside afterwards.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (70,1,47,'Handyman Services in Oakville for Careful Home Repairs','Handyman Services in Oakville - Home Repairs','Careful repairs for Oakville homes, from original trim in the old town to move-in lists in Glen Abbey and River Oaks. Free quote before anything is started.','Handyman services in Oakville have to suit the house. In the old town and Bronte the trim, doors and plaster are original, and the point is to repair them rather than replace them. I am Sercan, and I work carefully in houses like that.\n\nNorth of the QEW the homes are newer and the lists are longer, usually gathered up after a move. Either way you get a free quote, an agreed price before work starts, and one visit that clears as much as possible.','In old Oakville and Bronte the houses still carry their original wood trim, and the owners want it kept. So the ask there is careful: refitting a sticking casement, easing a door without trimming it down, repairing a section of baseboard so the profile still runs through, touching in paint rather than repainting a whole room. North of the QEW in Glen Abbey, River Oaks and Joshua Creek the homes are newer and the work is different, usually a full list gathered up after moving in.','Easing sticking doors without cutting original trim\nRepairing baseboard and casing to match existing profiles\nFreeing painted-shut windows in older Bronte houses\nMove-in repair lists in Glen Abbey and River Oaks\nRefitting cupboard doors and drawers in older kitchens','My Oakville house is old and the trim is original. Will repairs match what is there?','That is the aim. On older Oakville houses I repair the piece that is damaged rather than pulling out a whole run, and I match the profile as closely as the stock allows. Where a modern moulding will not match, I say so before starting so you can decide. Painted-shut windows and doors I free by hand rather than forcing them.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (71,1,43,'Handyman Services in Pickering for Home Repairs','Handyman Services in Pickering - Repairs','Repairs for Pickering homes near the lake, covering doors, screens, decks and indoor jobs batched into one trip. Contact Sercan for a free quote on the list.','Handyman services in Pickering usually include something the weather caused. West Shore and Bay Ridges sit close to the lake, and open wind is hard on doors, screens, decks and anything painted. I am Sercan, and I sort those jobs out along with the indoor ones.\n\nNewer areas like Duffin Heights bring different lists, mostly fittings and storage after moving in. Whichever end of town you are in, the quote is free and the price is agreed before I start.','The older Pickering neighbourhoods near the lake, West Shore and Bay Ridges, sit in open wind off the water, and it shows on anything facing south. Exterior door seals flatten early, screen doors rack out of square, deck boards and railings weather faster than they do inland. Those streets are also full of split-levels, where the stairs run in half flights with short landings, so handrails, spindles and anything carried up to the top level need planning rather than brute force.','Replacing flattened door seals on lake-facing entries\nSquaring up screen doors that have racked\nFixing loose deck boards and railing posts\nFitting handrails on split-level half-flight stairs\nRepair lists in West Shore and Bay Ridges bungalows','My front door lets in a draught every winter. Is that something you deal with?','Usually yes. Near the lake in Pickering the seals compress faster than they do inland, and a door that fit properly five years ago now has daylight along one edge. I check the hinges, the strike plate and the threshold before replacing anything, because more often than not the door has simply dropped and needs resetting rather than a new seal.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (72,1,44,'Handyman Services in Ajax for Homes and Townhomes','Handyman Services in Ajax - Home Repairs','Repairs and small jobs for Ajax homes and freehold townhomes, planned around narrow rooms and tight stairs. Message me your list for a no-obligation quote.','Handyman services in Ajax are shaped by the townhomes. North Ajax is full of freehold towns with narrow rooms and tight stairs, so storage, fixtures and door adjustments make up most of the calls. I am Sercan, and I plan the work around the space I have.\n\nSouth Ajax is older and closer to the lake, which brings its own list. Wherever you are, tell me everything at once, take a free quote, and I will clear what I can in a single visit.','North Ajax is heavy on freehold townhomes, and the shape of the house drives the work. Rooms are narrow, the stairs turn back on themselves on a tight winder, and getting a full sheet of anything to the top floor is genuinely awkward, so I cut and plan downstairs first. Every wall has to do a job in a narrow room, which is why so many Ajax calls are about wall storage. I also keep drilling on the shared party wall short, out of respect for the neighbour.','Wall storage fitted in narrow townhome bedrooms\nGetting shelving and boards up tight winder stairs\nMounting TVs and shelves away from party walls\nDoor and closet adjustments through freehold townhomes\nRepair lists in older South Ajax bungalows','I share walls with my neighbours in Ajax. Will the work be noisy for them?','There will be some noise, but I keep it short and sensible. On a shared wall I set out and drill in one go rather than spreading it across the day, and I avoid early mornings and evenings. If a job needs sustained hammer drilling, I will tell you beforehand so you can give your neighbour a heads-up.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (73,5,1,'Interior Painting in Toronto Homes and Condos','Interior Painting in Toronto Homes','Interior painting in Toronto, from plaster walls in old semis to a single condo bedroom. Careful prep, clean lines, low-odour paint. Call for a free quote.','Interior painting in Toronto often starts with what earlier coats left behind. In the older semis and row houses east and west of downtown, walls are plaster over lath, trim carries decades of oil paint, and no corner is quite square. I read the surface first, then decide what needs a bonding primer, what needs filling and what can be sanded and coated.\n\nI do the work myself, so the same person quotes the job and finishes it. Furniture gets moved and covered, floors get protected, and I use low-odour paint so you can stay in the house while I work.','Plaster and lath behaves nothing like drywall. Filler has to be feathered wide or the patch telegraphs under Toronto\'s low winter light, and a skim coat is often faster than chasing hairline cracks one by one. The trim in these houses is usually old oil paint, so it gets deglossed and primed for adhesion before any latex touches it. Parking is permit-only on most side streets, so I load in once and keep the van tight to the curb.','Repainting plaster walls in a Victorian semi\nSkim coating and painting a cracked hallway ceiling\nPainting layered oil trim and doors in white\nOne-bedroom condo repaint before a new tenant moves in\nAccent wall in a narrow downtown living room','My Toronto house has plaster walls. Can they still be painted well?','Yes. Plaster takes paint beautifully once it is stable. I check for loose sections, refasten or cut out anything drummy, fill and feather the repairs, then prime the patches so they do not flash through the finish coat. Hairline cracks in a ceiling usually need tape or a skim rather than filler alone. Tell me what the walls look like and I will give you a free quote.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (74,5,2,'Interior Painting in North York Homes','Interior Painting in North York','Looking for interior painting in North York? I paint rooms, stippled ceilings, trim and basement suites, one job at a time. Get a free quote from Sercan.','Interior painting in North York covers a lot of ground, from Willowdale bungalows with original plaster to rental suites along the Yonge and Sheppard corridor. Most of the calls are one of three things: a tired room that needs to look new, a basement apartment turning over, or a whole floor being changed to one colour before family moves in.\n\nI quote the job before I start, and the price does not move unless you add rooms. Everything is moved, covered and put back, and I work with low-odour paint so a family can stay in the house.','A lot of North York ceilings from the 1960s and 70s are still stippled. That texture drinks paint and lets go if it is over-rolled, so it takes a long-nap roller, a loaded first pass and no going back over a wet stretch. Bathurst Manor and Willowdale walls are often plaster on gypsum lath, harder and less forgiving than drywall. Basement suites here get repainted between tenants, usually the whole unit at once and on a short turnaround.','Painting a stippled ceiling in a 1960s bungalow\nFull repaint of a basement apartment between tenants\nBedroom and hallway repaint in a Yonge Street condo\nPainting kitchen and bathroom walls with a washable finish\nRefreshing doors and baseboards through a Willowdale side-split','Can you paint over a stippled ceiling in my North York bungalow?','Usually yes. Old stipple takes paint well if it has never been painted before, so it gets a light vacuum, a stain-blocking primer over any water marks and one loaded pass with a long-nap roller. What I will not do is roll it twice while it is wet, because that pulls the texture off. If the ceiling is already loose or damaged, I will tell you before I start.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (75,5,3,'Interior Painting for Scarborough Homes','Interior House Painting in Scarborough','Interior painting in Scarborough houses, from bright bedrooms to panelled basements and dated trim. Honest prep, agreed price. Message me for a free quote.','Interior painting in Scarborough is often about undoing the seventies. The bungalows and back-splits around Wexford, Agincourt and Bendale still have their original dark trim, panelled rec rooms and heavy stained doors, and paint changes how the whole house reads faster than anything else.\n\nI handle the prep properly, because that is where these jobs are won or lost. Old finishes get cleaned, deglossed and primed so the new colour actually sticks. I give you a price before I start, and I do the work myself, start to finish.','Scarborough basements are full of 1970s wood panelling and dark stained trim, and both need a stain-blocking primer or the old finish bleeds through as a yellow shadow. If you want the panel grooves gone, they get filled and sanded first, which is real work and I will price it separately. The back-splits around Wexford and Bendale also put stairwell walls at half-level heights, so a lot of the cutting in happens off a ladder standing on a landing.','Painting over dark panelling in a basement rec room\nWhole-house repaint after buying a Wexford bungalow\nBrightening a back-split stairwell and upper hallway\nPainting stained doors and trim a clean white\nRefreshing a rental unit before new tenants arrive','Can the dark panelling and trim in my Scarborough basement be painted light?','Yes, and it changes a rec room more than anything else you can do down there. The panelling gets cleaned, deglossed and given a stain-blocking primer, because old stain and knots will bleed straight through latex otherwise. Grooves can be filled if you want flat walls, or left as they are for a boarded look. Same story with the trim. I will quote both options.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (76,5,4,'Interior Painting in Etobicoke Homes and Condos','Interior Painting in Etobicoke','Interior painting in Etobicoke, for lakeside condo suites and post-war bungalows alike. Low-odour paint, tidy work, clear pricing. Ask Sercan for a quote.','Interior painting in Etobicoke means two very different kinds of home. There are the condo suites along Lake Shore Boulevard West and Humber Bay, where the ceilings are concrete and the windows do not open, and there are the post-war bungalows in Alderwood, Long Branch and Islington with small square rooms and painted wood trim.\n\nBoth get the same approach. I look at the surface, agree a price with you before anything is opened, then prep, prime and coat in that order. Furniture is moved and covered, and the suite or the house is left clean at the end of each day.','In the Humber Bay Shores towers the windows barely open, so paint smell has nowhere to go. I use low-odour products and work with the suite fans running, and I book the service elevator and loading dock through the concierge because a load of gear will not go up the passenger car. Ceilings in those buildings are sprayed concrete, which patches differently than drywall. Alderwood and Long Branch bungalows are the opposite problem: small rooms, low ceilings, everything cut by hand.','Repainting a Humber Bay condo suite before move-in\nAccent wall behind a bed in a small suite\nFull repaint of a post-war Alderwood bungalow\nPainting bulkheads and ceilings in an open-plan suite\nDoors, trim and closets refreshed throughout a townhouse','My Etobicoke condo has strict rules for trades. Does that slow the job down?','Not really, it just needs planning. I ask for the building rules before the day, book the service elevator and loading dock through the concierge, and keep the work inside your suite and the agreed hours. Low-odour paint matters more here because the windows barely open, so I use it as standard and run the bathroom and kitchen fans while I work.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (77,5,25,'Interior Painting in Mississauga Homes','Interior Painting in Mississauga','Interior painting in Mississauga, including tall foyer walls, bedrooms, trim and doors. I do the work myself and quote before starting. Reach out today.','Interior painting in Mississauga usually starts with the biggest wall in the house. In the two-storey homes through Erin Mills, Meadowvale and Churchill Meadows, the foyer and stairwell run two full floors, and that is the part most people would rather not do themselves.\n\nThe rest is straightforward work done carefully: bedrooms, hallways, ceilings, doors and baseboards. I move and cover what is in the room, fill and sand before I open a can of colour, and give you the price in writing first. Sunday work is by appointment.','The two-storey foyer is the standard Mississauga problem. In the Erin Mills and Meadowvale homes from the eighties and nineties, the wall over the staircase runs the full height of the house with no floor under half of it, so it gets done off a levelled ladder set on the treads rather than by leaning something on a railing. Square One condos are the other end of the scale: one open room, a lot of glass, and every roller mark visible.','Painting the two-storey foyer and stairwell wall\nRepainting bedrooms and hallway after new flooring\nGreat room and kitchen walls in one colour\nCondo repaint near Square One between tenants\nCeilings patched and painted after a leak repair','Can you reach the wall above my staircase without scaffolding?','In most Mississauga two-storeys, yes. A ladder with adjustable legs sits level on the stair treads and gets me to the top of that wall safely, and for the very tallest foyers I set up a small platform instead. What I do not do is lean a ladder on a railing or a banister. If a room is beyond safe reach, I will say so.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (78,5,26,'Interior Painting in Brampton, Room by Room','Interior House Painting in Brampton','Interior painting in Brampton homes with open main floors, nine-foot ceilings and busy hallways. Neat cut lines, agreed price. Book a free quote with me.','Interior painting in Brampton is mostly newer houses with tall, open main floors. In Springdale, Mount Pleasant and Credit Valley the kitchen, dining and great room read as one space, so a repaint down there is one continuous job rather than a set of separate rooms.\n\nThat suits me. I work through a floor in order, cut every edge by hand and keep the lines straight where the wall meets nine-foot ceilings. You get a price before I start, and several smaller jobs can be batched into the same visit.','Most of the newer Brampton subdivisions were built with nine-foot main floors and no doors between kitchen, dining and great room. That matters because a colour has to stop somewhere, and with nothing but open archways the break has to land in an inside corner or it looks like a mistake. The builder flat on those walls also burnishes wherever someone has wiped it, so touch-ups never blend and the wall gets rolled corner to corner instead.','Repainting an open main floor in one colour\nAccent wall in a Springdale great room\nPainting a finished basement and second-suite bedrooms\nDoors, frames and baseboards through a two-storey home\nNine-foot walls and upper hallway repainted after move-in','My Brampton main floor is open from the kitchen to the great room. Where does the colour stop?','At an inside corner, almost always. With no doors between those rooms there is nothing to frame a colour change, so I look for a corner, a bulkhead edge or the return beside an archway and land the break there. If you want two colours anyway, pick a pair that sit close in tone so the join reads as a change of light, not a line.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (79,5,31,'Interior Painting in Vaughan Homes','Interior Painting in Vaughan','Interior painting in Vaughan, including crown moulding, wainscot, doors and oak railings painted white. Careful brushwork. Contact Sercan for a free quote.','Interior painting in Vaughan tends to involve more trim than wall. Houses in Woodbridge, Maple and Kleinburg were built with crown moulding, wide casings, wainscot panels and coffered ceilings, and those details are what make a repaint look finished or look rushed.\n\nI brush trim by hand and take the time it needs. Walls get filled, sanded and rolled to a clean edge, and I keep the paint low-odour so the house stays comfortable. The price is agreed before the first drop sheet comes out of the van.','Woodbridge and Maple houses carry a lot of millwork, so these jobs are mostly brushwork rather than rolling and the hours land on crown, wainscot panels, coffered ceilings and wide casings. The other common request is stained oak stairs and spindles going white. Oak grain telegraphs through paint unless the surface is filled and sanded between coats, and every spindle is a separate small job. I say that up front because it changes the estimate more than the wall colour does.','Painting crown moulding and wainscot panels in a dining room\nStained oak railing and spindles painted white\nCoffered ceiling and beams repainted in a family room\nWhole upstairs repainted while the family is away\nDoors and casings refreshed through a Woodbridge home','Will painted oak spindles still show the grain?','A little, unless the grain is filled. Oak has open pores, so paint sinks into them and the finish looks slightly striped. If you want it smooth, the spindles and railing get filled, sanded, primed with a bonding primer and then two coats of enamel. That takes time on a full staircase, so I quote it as its own line rather than folding it into the walls.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (80,5,32,'Interior Painting in Markham Homes','Interior Painting in Markham','Interior painting in Markham, from an empty house before move-in to one repainted bedroom. Clean edges and no rush jobs. Send me a message for a quote.','Interior painting in Markham is often on a deadline. A lot of my work here happens in the short window after a sale closes and before the furniture arrives, which is the easiest time to repaint a whole house and the hardest time to be late.\n\nThe rest is ordinary living room and bedroom work in Berczy, Wismer, Unionville and Milliken. Old plaster and new drywall both get the same attention: fill, sand, prime the repairs, then two coats. I quote first, I do the work myself, and I clean up as I go.','A lot of my Markham painting happens in the gap between closing and moving day, when the house is empty and the whole main floor and upstairs can be done in one run. Empty is faster and cleaner, but the deadline is fixed, so I plan the order of rooms around it. Cornell adds its own wrinkle: narrow deep plans, garages off the rear lane and three floors of stairs, so everything gets carried in through the front door.','Whole-house repaint in the week before move-in\nNeutral colours through a home listed for sale\nRepainting a third-floor loft and stairwell in Cornell\nOne bedroom turned into a home office\nPlaster walls patched and painted in an Unionville house','Can you paint my Markham house in the days between closing and moving in?','That is the best time to do it, and it is a common request here. An empty house means no furniture to move, full access to every wall and faster drying with the windows open. Tell me the closing date early so I can hold the days, because those windows are short and fixed. I will confirm the price before I pick up a brush.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (81,5,33,'Interior Painting in Richmond Hill Homes','Interior Painting in Richmond Hill','Interior painting in Richmond Hill, including tall stairwell walls, sunlit rooms and whole-house colour changes. Get in touch for a free, no-obligation quote.','Interior painting in Richmond Hill has to survive a lot of daylight. The homes through Bayview Hill, Mill Pond and Oak Ridges have big windows and tall rooms, and that light shows up every shortcut in a paint job, from an unprimed patch to a lap mark on a long wall.\n\nSo I work to the light rather than against it. Repairs get primed, walls get rolled a full height at a time, and I talk through sheen with you before the colour is bought. The quote is free, the price is set first, and I do the work myself.','Bayview Hill and Oak Ridges houses have big two-storey windows, and that side light is unforgiving. Sun raking across a long wall shows every lap mark and every patch that was not primed, so on those rooms I keep a wet edge, roll floor to ceiling in one pass and lean toward a flat or matte sheen that scatters light instead of bouncing it. Skylight shafts in the same houses are tall, narrow and awkward, and they get cut in by hand.','Two-storey family room walls repainted in one colour\nSkylight shaft and upper hallway ceiling painted\nBedrooms repainted while the rest stays lived in\nCondo living room and den on Yonge Street\nTrim and doors updated from cream to white','Why do the walls in my sunlit Richmond Hill family room show every roller mark?','Because strong side light exaggerates any change in texture or sheen. A patch that was not primed drinks paint differently and shows as a dull spot, and a roller that dried before the next pass leaves a lap line. On those tall bright walls I prime all repairs, work a full height at a time and usually suggest a matte sheen, which hides far more than eggshell does.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (82,5,47,'Interior Painting in Oakville Homes','Interior House Painting in Oakville','Interior painting in Oakville, from old-town trim and mouldings to smooth new doors and bright rooms. Careful, unhurried work. Call Sercan for a free quote.','Interior painting in Oakville splits between old trim and new finishes. In Old Oakville, Kerr Village and Bronte the houses have deep original mouldings buried under decades of oil paint, while out in Glen Abbey, River Oaks and Joshua Creek people want doors and trim that look like they came that way.\n\nBoth need patience more than speed. I sand back what needs it, prime for adhesion, and let each coat dry the full time before the next one goes on. You get the price before I start, and I am the person who shows up to do it.','In Old Oakville and Kerr Village the original casings and baseboards are deep, and after a century of repainting the profile is half buried in old oil. Those get sanded back enough to bring the detail out without carving into the wood, then primed for adhesion. Out in Glen Abbey and Joshua Creek the ask is different: doors and trim that look factory finished. That means a self-levelling enamel, light sanding between coats and waiting out the recoat time instead of rushing it.','Original mouldings and casings repainted in Old Oakville\nSmooth enamel finish on interior doors and trim\nRepainting bedrooms and the upstairs hall in Glen Abbey\nPowder room and kitchen walls in a washable finish\nWhole main floor repainted before a house goes up for sale','Can you get a smooth factory look on my Oakville doors and trim without spraying?','Yes, with the right enamel and patience. A self-levelling waterborne enamel laid off with a good brush flattens as it dries, so brush marks disappear. Between coats everything gets a light sand, and each coat is left the full recoat time rather than being rushed. On older Oakville trim I also sand back the built-up layers first, so the profile shows again.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (83,5,43,'Interior Painting in Pickering Homes','Interior Painting in Pickering','Interior painting in Pickering, for new Seaton builds and older homes near the lake. Proper patching, primer and two coats. Ask me for a free quote today.','Interior painting in Pickering covers houses at both ends of their life. Up in Seaton the drywall is barely two years old and still moving, while down in Bay Ridges, Amberlea and Liverpool the walls have been painted several times since the seventies.\n\nThe prep is different, the standard is not. Patches get primed, seams get retaped when they have opened, trim gets sanded and cleaned before enamel goes on. I agree the price with you first, and I can group a few small rooms into one visit.','New houses in Seaton come with a single thin coat of builder flat over bare drywall. In the first year the framing dries and nail pops and seam cracks show up, so the first real repaint means resetting screws, taping what has opened and priming the repairs before any colour goes on. South of the 401 the housing is much older. The Bay Ridges and Amberlea homes from the seventies still have textured ceilings and stained trim from the original build.','First proper repaint of a new Seaton house\nNail pops and seam cracks fixed, then walls painted\nTextured ceiling repainted in a seventies bungalow\nBasement rec room walls and stairs painted\nChildren\'s bedrooms repainted in washable colours','My Pickering house is nearly new. Why does it already need repainting?','Builder paint is one thin coat over new drywall, and it marks the first time anyone wipes it. New framing also dries out in the first couple of years, which pops screws and opens seams. So the first repaint is really a repair job with colour on top: reset the fasteners, retape what has cracked, prime the patches, then two proper coats.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (84,5,44,'Interior Painting in Ajax Homes','Interior House Painting in Ajax','Interior painting in Ajax, from compact older rooms to three-storey stairwells and basements. Tidy prep, fair pricing. Send a message for your free quote.','Interior painting in Ajax means small rooms in the south end and tall stairwells in the north. The older streets near the lake have compact houses with low ceilings, and the newer subdivisions above the 401 are full of three-storey towns where the hallway looks down into the room below.\n\nEach one gets planned before a brush is opened. I check what is on the walls now, tell you what needs filling or priming, then give you a fixed price. Furniture is moved and covered, floors are protected, and the room is put back together when I finish.','South Ajax still has the compact houses that went up when the town grew out of the wartime plant. Low ceilings and small rooms mean the ceiling colour matters as much as the walls, and a flat white overhead does more for the room than a bold shade on one side of it. North of the 401 the freehold towns are the opposite: narrow, three storeys, with a stairwell that runs the whole height and a hall open to the floor below.','Small older rooms brightened with ceiling and wall paint\nThree-storey stairwell and hallway repainted in a townhouse\nBasement walls and trim painted after drywall repairs\nFull interior repaint before a family moves in\nSingle room repainted in a rented house','The rooms in my older Ajax house feel small and dark. What helps most?','Paint the ceiling as well as the walls. In those compact south Ajax houses the ceiling is close, so a clean flat white overhead lifts the whole room, and keeping the trim and walls near the same tone stops the space from being chopped up. A soft warm neutral usually beats a bright white on the walls, since the windows are small.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (85,7,1,'Light Fixture and Ceiling Fan Installation in Toronto','Light Fixture Installation in Toronto','Chandeliers, vanity lights and ceiling fans swapped in Toronto homes, from plaster ceilings to downtown condo slabs. Call Sercan for a free quote today.','Light fixture installation in Toronto means working with ceilings that were built long before anyone thought about a heavy ceiling fan. I swap chandeliers, flush mounts, vanity lights and fans on circuits that already exist, from Victorian semis in Cabbagetown to condos on the waterfront.\n\nI take the old fixture down carefully, check what the box is actually fastened to, and tell you straight if the mount needs changing before the new piece goes up. The price is agreed before I start.','Most homes below St Clair were plastered over lath, so the ceiling around the box is brittle and the old octagon box is often nailed to one joist with no ground wire. That matters for a fan: it needs a fan-rated box or a brace, not the light box that is there. Two-wire switch loops in these houses also rule out most smart switches, since they need a neutral. Downtown condos are the opposite problem: poured concrete, so the fixture stays where the box is.','Replacing a hallway chandelier in a Cabbagetown Victorian\nSwapping a bathroom vanity light in a Parkdale apartment\nHanging a new pendant over a condo kitchen island\nFitting a fan-rated box before a bedroom ceiling fan\nChanging dated flush mounts through a Leslieville semi','My Toronto house has plaster ceilings. Can you still hang a ceiling fan?','Usually yes, but not straight onto the old box. Plaster and lath will not carry a spinning fan, so I open a small area, fit a fan-rated box braced to the framing, and make the plaster good around the canopy. If the framing above will not take a brace, I tell you before anything comes down and suggest a lighter fixture instead.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (86,7,2,'Ceiling Fan and Light Fixture Installation in North York','Light Fixture and Fan Install in North York','Ceiling fans, chandeliers and dimmers fitted in North York bungalows, backsplits and Yonge Street condos. Message Sercan and get a free quote back.','Light fixture installation in North York usually starts with a 1950s or 1960s ceiling. Willowdale, Bathurst Manor and Don Mills are full of bungalows and backsplits where the original box is shallow and the ceiling is stippled, and both of those change how a new fixture sits.\n\nI swap chandeliers, flush mounts, vanity bars, ceiling fans, dimmers and smart switches on the wiring you already have. I bring my own ladders, drop sheets and patching kit, and I clean up before I leave.','Post-war North York bungalows tend to have a shallow pancake box screwed to the underside of a joist, which is fine for a flush mount and wrong for a fan. The stipple finish is the other catch: a smaller canopy leaves a ring of bare texture, so I match sizes or feather the ceiling back before painting. Newer Yonge and Sheppard towers flip it around, with concrete slabs above and fixtures that have to live exactly where the builder put the box.','Replacing kitchen fluorescent boxes in Willowdale bungalows\nFitting bedroom ceiling fans in Don Mills backsplits\nSwapping a dining chandelier in a Bathurst Manor home\nInstalling dimmers and smart switches in a Sheppard condo\nChanging vanity lights in a basement apartment bathroom','Can you put a ceiling fan in my North York bungalow bedroom?','Most times yes. The catch in those bedrooms is the original box, which is usually a thin pancake type meant to hold a few pounds of glass. I take it out and fit a fan-rated box or a brace tied to the joists, then hang the fan from that. If the ceiling is low, I will steer you toward a hugger model so nobody ducks.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (87,7,3,'Light Fixture and Ceiling Fan Installation in Scarborough','Light Fixture Installation in Scarborough','Fixture swaps, ceiling fans and dimmer upgrades for Scarborough houses, split levels and basement suites. Send Sercan a few photos for a free quote.','Light fixture installation in Scarborough covers a lot of ground, from Guildwood bungalows to Agincourt sidesplits to the towers along Kennedy. I swap chandeliers, flush mounts, vanity lights and ceiling fans onto the circuits already in the ceiling, and I fit dimmers and smart switches while I am there.\n\nScarborough houses often come with a long list rather than one job. If you have three rooms of dated fixtures and a fan still waiting in its box, I can do them all in one visit.','A fair number of Scarborough houses built between the mid sixties and the mid seventies have aluminium branch wiring. When I open a ceiling box and see it, I stop and tell you, because that connection belongs to a licensed electrician rather than me. The other Scarborough regular is the finished basement suite with a drop ceiling and low headroom, where a fan is the wrong call and a slim flush mount tucked into the tile grid is the one that works.','Swapping dated dining chandeliers in Agincourt two-storeys\nFitting flush mounts in basement apartment hallways\nInstalling a ceiling fan in a Guildwood bedroom\nReplacing bathroom vanity lights in Wexford bungalows\nAdding dimmers to living room and dining circuits','What happens if you find aluminium wiring in my Scarborough ceiling?','I stop and explain what I found. Aluminium branch wiring turns up in a lot of houses here from the seventies, and joining it safely is licensed electrical work, not handyman work. I will make the box safe, put the old fixture back if you like, and point you to a licensed electrician who can do that part properly. Nothing gets connected that I am not allowed to connect.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (88,7,4,'Light Fixture and Ceiling Fan Installation in Etobicoke','Light Fixture and Fan Install in Etobicoke','Pendants, chandeliers and ceiling fans installed in Etobicoke condos and older lakeside houses. Book a free quote with Sercan at a time that suits you.','Light fixture installation in Etobicoke splits neatly in two. Along the lake at Humber Bay Shores and Mimico the ceilings are concrete and the buildings have rules; inland in Alderwood, Long Branch and Islington Village the houses are older, lower and framed in wood.\n\nI handle both. That means swapping chandeliers, pendants, vanity lights and ceiling fans on existing circuits, plus dimmers and smart switches, and knowing which of the two ceilings I am standing under before I start drilling.','In the Humber Bay towers the slab above you is poured concrete, so a pendant cannot move sideways to centre it over an island. The box is where it is, and the honest fix is a fixture with an adjustable canopy or a swag. Those buildings also want the service elevator booked and the work done inside quiet hours, so I plan around your concierge. Alderwood and Long Branch are the reverse: shallow joists, low ceilings and fans that need a hugger mount.','Hanging pendants over a Humber Bay condo island\nSwapping builder flush mounts in a Mimico rental\nFitting a low profile fan in an Alderwood bungalow\nReplacing a dining chandelier in Islington Village\nInstalling bathroom vanity bars in a lakeshore condo','Do I need the service elevator booked for a light install in my Etobicoke condo?','For a single fixture usually not, since I carry hand tools and a folding ladder up in the passenger lift. If a large chandelier or a boxed fan is being delivered, book the service elevator with your concierge and let me know the window. I work Monday to Saturday, so I can match whatever slot the building gives you.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (89,7,25,'Light Fixture and Ceiling Fan Installation in Mississauga','Light Fixture Installation in Mississauga','Foyer chandeliers, ceiling fans and vanity lights fitted across Mississauga homes and Square One condos. Ask Sercan for a free no-obligation quote.','Light fixture installation in Mississauga often means a two-storey foyer. Erin Mills, Meadowvale, Churchill Meadows and Lisgar are full of houses where the entry light hangs above the stairs, and that one spot is the reason most people call rather than doing it themselves.\n\nI also do the ordinary work: dining chandeliers, bedroom fans, kitchen pendants, vanity bars, dimmers and smart switches on the circuits you already have. Photos of the room and the old fixture are enough for me to quote.','Those 1990s Mississauga builders loved a tall foyer with the light centred over the first few stair treads. You cannot stand a stepladder there safely, so I bring a ladder that sits on the stairs with levelling legs, and I take the old brass fixture down in pieces rather than as one lump. Heavier replacements get checked against what the original box will carry. Around Square One the towers are concrete again, so surface fixtures only and no fishing wire through a slab.','Changing a foyer chandelier above the front stairs\nFitting ceiling fans in Erin Mills bedrooms\nSwapping kitchen track lighting for LED pendants\nReplacing vanity lights in an ensuite bathroom\nInstalling smart switches through a Churchill Meadows home\nHanging a dining fixture in a Square One condo','The light in my Mississauga foyer is two storeys up. Can you reach it?','Yes. That is one of the most common calls I get out there. I use a ladder that levels itself on the stair treads rather than balancing something on a landing, and I take multi-arm fixtures down in sections so nothing is being juggled overhead. Send me a photo from the bottom of the stairs and I will tell you what is needed.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (90,7,26,'Ceiling Fan and Light Fixture Installation in Brampton','Light Fixture and Fan Install in Brampton','Ceiling fans, great room chandeliers and dimmer swaps for Brampton subdivisions and older Bramalea homes. Send Sercan a photo and a free quote follows.','Light fixture installation in Brampton is mostly newer housing stock, and that brings its own quirks. Mount Pleasant, Fletcher\'s Meadow and Springdale homes have nine-foot main floors, roof trusses instead of solid rafters, and builder fixtures that everybody wants gone within a year or two.\n\nI swap those out for what you have bought: chandeliers, pendants, fans, vanity bars, dimmers and smart switches, all on the circuits already in place. Several rooms in one visit saves you booking me twice.','Brampton great rooms are the interesting part. A vaulted or sloped ceiling needs a fan with a sloped-ceiling adapter and a downrod long enough to clear the pitch, and on a nine-foot flat ceiling a hugger mount leaves the blades too high to feel. Above the drywall you are usually into engineered trusses, so a fan brace has to bear on the truss chords, not on the webbing. The builder box in a bedroom is rarely fan-rated even when the ceiling looks ready for one.','Fitting bedroom ceiling fans in Mount Pleasant homes\nHanging a chandelier in a vaulted great room\nReplacing builder flush mounts through a new build\nAdding a sloped ceiling adapter and longer downrod\nSwapping bathroom vanity lights in Springdale ensuites\nInstalling dimmers in a Bramalea living room','My Brampton builder left a plain light in the primary bedroom. Can it take a fan?','Not as it sits, in most cases. Builders fit a light box rated for a fixture that hangs still, and a fan puts load and vibration on it. I remove that box, slide in an adjustable fan brace between the trusses, and mount the fan to the new box. It is a short job and it is the difference between a fan that stays quiet and one that wobbles.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (91,7,31,'Light Fixture and Ceiling Fan Installation in Vaughan','Light Fixture Installation in Vaughan','Heavy chandeliers, coffered ceiling fixtures and fans installed in Vaughan homes from Woodbridge to Maple. Reach Sercan for a free quote this week.','Light fixture installation in Vaughan tends to involve bigger and heavier pieces than average. Woodbridge, Kleinburg and Patterson houses were built with tall entries, coffered dining ceilings and rooms that were designed around a statement chandelier rather than a flush mount.\n\nWeight is the first thing I check. A multi-tier fixture can outweigh what the original box was ever meant to hold, so I look at the mount and the framing above it before anything is unpacked, and I tell you what it needs.','A standard ceiling box in these Vaughan homes is rated for a modest load, and the crystal fixtures people buy for a Woodbridge dining room can go well past it. That means a saddle or bar hanger tied into the joists, sometimes with access from above if there is attic space over the entry. Coffered and tray ceilings add another wrinkle, because the box sits in a raised panel and the canopy has to land flat on the drywall inside the coffer, not bridge the edge.','Hanging a heavy crystal chandelier in a Woodbridge dining room\nFitting fixtures into coffered and tray ceiling panels\nReplacing an entry chandelier in a Kleinburg home\nInstalling bedroom ceiling fans in Maple two-storeys\nSwapping dated pot light trims for LED units\nAdding dimmers to a Patterson living room','How heavy a chandelier can go up in my Vaughan dining room?','It depends on what is behind the drywall, not on the fixture. A plain ceiling box holds far less than most people assume, so for anything substantial I fit a bar hanger or a saddle fixed to the joists and hang the chandelier from that. Tell me the weight from the box and the ceiling height, and I will tell you what support it needs before you buy.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (92,7,32,'Light Fixture and Ceiling Fan Installation in Markham','Light Fixture Installation in Markham','Fixture swaps, ceiling fans, dimmers and smart switches for Markham townhomes and houses. Send Sercan your list and a free quote comes straight back.','Light fixture installation in Markham runs into a lot of freehold townhomes. Cornell, Berczy, Wismer and Greensborough hold rows of narrow townhomes with nine-foot main floors, and the living rooms are longer than they are wide, which changes what fits above them.\n\nI swap chandeliers, pendants, flush mounts, vanity lights and ceiling fans on existing circuits, and I do a lot of dimmer and smart switch work here as well. Give me the list and I will work through the house in one go.','Townhome switch banks in Markham are usually three or four devices in one box, often with three-way switching between the main floor and the stairs. Smart switches are deeper and wider than the toggles they replace, so four of them rarely fit in the original box, and the traveller wiring has to be sorted out before anything is buttoned up. On the plus side these houses are new enough to have a neutral at the switch, which is exactly what smart switches need.','Replacing a stairwell fixture in a Cornell townhome\nFitting smart switches into a crowded switch bank\nHanging pendants over a narrow kitchen island\nSwapping the dining chandelier in a Unionville home\nInstalling bedroom ceiling fans sized for small rooms','Will smart switches work in my Markham townhome?','In houses built from the late nineties onward, usually yes, because there is a neutral wire in the switch box and that is what these devices run on. The real problem is space. A bank of four smart switches takes up more room than four toggles, so I sometimes fit a deeper box or a slim model. Send me a photo with the plate off and I will confirm.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (93,7,33,'Light Fixture and Ceiling Fan Installation in Richmond Hill','Light Fixture Installation in Richmond Hill','LED upgrades, chandeliers, fans and dimmers fitted in Richmond Hill houses from Mill Pond to Oak Ridges. Get in touch with Sercan for a free quote.','Light fixture installation in Richmond Hill is often part of an LED changeover. The executive houses in Bayview Hill and Westbrook were built with halogen pot lights, glass pendants and dimmers from the incandescent era, and swapping the bulbs alone is what makes them flicker.\n\nI change fixtures and trims and I change the dimmer to match, so the two work together. Chandeliers, vanity lights, kitchen pendants and ceiling fans all get the same treatment on the circuits you already have.','Half the calls I get in Richmond Hill start with buzzing or flickering after someone put LED bulbs into a house full of nineties halogen. Old rotary and slide dimmers were designed for a heavy incandescent load, and a few LED lamps do not draw enough for them to behave. Swapping the dimmer for an LED-rated model on the same circuit fixes it, and matching the trims across a room stops one pot light reading warmer than the rest.','Replacing old dimmers that buzz with LED bulbs\nSwapping halogen pot light trims for LED units\nHanging a new chandelier in a Bayview Hill foyer\nFitting ceiling fans in Oak Ridges bedrooms\nChanging vanity lights in a Mill Pond bathroom','My new LED bulbs buzz on the old dimmers in my Richmond Hill house. Can you fix that?','Yes, and it is nearly always the dimmer rather than the bulbs. The ones fitted in houses from the nineties expect a big incandescent load, and a handful of LEDs sits below what they can control smoothly. I replace the dimmer with an LED-rated one on the existing circuit and set its low end so the lights come on evenly instead of flickering or humming.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (94,7,47,'Light Fixture and Ceiling Fan Installation in Oakville','Light Fixture Installation in Oakville','Period fixture swaps, ceiling fans and vanity lights for Oakville homes in Bronte, Kerr Village and Glen Abbey. Contact Sercan to arrange a free quote.','Light fixture installation in Oakville covers two very different kinds of ceiling. South of Lakeshore in Old Oakville and Bronte the houses are old, plastered and often finished with a moulded medallion around the fixture; north in Glen Abbey and River Oaks the ceilings are drywall over standard framing.\n\nI work with both. Chandeliers, lanterns, vanity lights, ceiling fans, dimmers and smart switches, always on the circuit that is already there, and always with a look at how the old one was held up.','The medallions in Old Oakville and Kerr Village houses set the rules. A modern canopy is usually smaller than the plaster rosette it has to sit inside, which leaves a shadow gap, so I either choose a fixture with a wide canopy or fit a spacer that keeps the trim reading properly from below. Older plaster there also means the box is fixed to the side of a joist rather than centred, and a chandelier hanging a few centimetres off the table centre is very noticeable.','Fitting a chandelier inside an existing plaster medallion\nReplacing porch and entry lanterns on older houses\nHanging pendants over a Glen Abbey kitchen island\nSwapping ensuite vanity lights in a River Oaks home\nInstalling a bedroom ceiling fan with a longer downrod','There is a plaster medallion around my Oakville dining light. Will a new fixture suit it?','It can, but the canopy size decides it. Most current fixtures come with a small canopy that leaves a ring of bare plaster showing inside the medallion, which looks unfinished. I either pick a fixture with a canopy wide enough to cover it or add a matching plate. Send me a photo with a tape measure across the medallion and I will tell you what will look right.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (95,7,43,'Light Fixture and Ceiling Fan Installation in Pickering','Light Fixture Installation in Pickering','Stairwell fixtures, hugger ceiling fans and vanity light swaps across Pickering, from West Shore to Seaton. Talk to Sercan about a free written quote.','Light fixture installation in Pickering often means a split level. Bay Ridges, West Shore and Amberlea are full of seventies backsplits where the hall light sits above a half flight of stairs and the lower level has a ceiling barely over seven feet.\n\nBoth of those decide what can go up. I swap chandeliers, flush mounts, vanity lights and fans on existing wiring, and I will say plainly when a fixture you have picked is too long a drop for the room it is going in.','The backsplit landing is the awkward one in Pickering. The fixture hangs over a short run of stairs with a railing on one side, so there is nowhere flat to stand a ladder and the old glass usually has to come off before anything else moves. Lower levels in those houses run low as well, which rules out a fan on a downrod and points to a hugger or a flush LED panel. Newer Seaton builds are the opposite, with taller great rooms and trusses overhead.','Changing a stairwell light above a split level landing\nFitting hugger ceiling fans in low lower levels\nReplacing kitchen fluorescent fixtures in West Shore bungalows\nSwapping vanity lights in an Amberlea main bathroom\nHanging a dining fixture in a newer Seaton home','Can a ceiling fan go in the lower level of my Pickering backsplit?','Often yes, as long as it is the right shape. Those lower levels sit close to seven feet, and a standard fan on a downrod puts the blades in the way. A hugger or low profile model mounts tight to the ceiling and keeps the clearance you need. I check the height and the box before I fit anything, and I will tell you if the room suits a light better.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (96,7,44,'Light Fixture and Ceiling Fan Installation in Ajax','Light Fixture Installation in Ajax','Bedroom fans, hallway fixtures and vanity light swaps for Ajax houses and townhomes. Tell Sercan what you need and a free quote follows shortly after.','Light fixture installation in Ajax runs from Pickering Village up through the newer streets north of Rossland. The houses built in the nineties and after share one habit that catches people out: plenty of bedrooms have no ceiling box at all, just a wall switch that controls half a receptacle.\n\nI swap what is there, and I tell you honestly when what you want needs an electrician instead. Chandeliers, pendants, vanity bars, fans, dimmers and smart switches on existing circuits are all my end of it.','That switched receptacle setup is worth knowing about before you buy a fan for an Ajax bedroom. If there is no box in the ceiling, running a new one is licensed electrical work and not something I take on, so the honest answer is a lamp on the switched outlet, or an electrician first and me after. Where a box does exist, the newer Ajax framing is straightforward: standard joists at sixteen inches, drywall, and room above for a proper fan brace.','Swapping hallway and stair fixtures in Ajax townhouses\nFitting a ceiling fan where a box already exists\nReplacing vanity lights in a Nottingham ensuite\nChanging dated dining chandeliers in Pickering Village homes\nInstalling dimmers and smart switches through the main floor','There is no light in my Ajax bedroom ceiling, only a switched outlet. What can you do?','That wiring is normal in Ajax houses of a certain age and it is a real limit. Putting a new box and cable into that ceiling is licensed electrical work, so I do not do it. What I can do is fit fixtures anywhere a box already exists, change the switch, or help you plan the rooms so an electrician makes one visit rather than three.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (97,9,1,'Shelf, Mirror and Picture Hanging in Toronto','Picture Hanging and Shelves in Toronto','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in Toronto, from plaster and lath in old semis to concrete walls in downtown condos. Ask me for a free quote today.','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in Toronto usually means working with plaster and lath rather than tidy modern drywall. In a Cabbagetown row house or an Annex third floor, the surface is horsehair plaster over wood lath, and the studs behind it sit wherever the carpenter left them a century ago.\n\nSo I read the wall before I drill. A small pilot hole in a spot that will be hidden tells me more than any stud finder. Old plaster is brittle, but the lumber behind it is solid, and handled properly it will carry a heavy mirror for decades.','Two Toronto jobs, two different toolkits. In a CityPlace or Liberty Village unit the demising walls are poured concrete and the partitions are steel stud, so I bring a hammer drill and toggles rated for thin metal rather than wood. In Leslieville or Parkdale I am into plaster over lath with brick sometimes right behind it. Plenty of Annex and Roncesvalles flats still have their original picture rail near the ceiling, and hanging from that leaves the plaster untouched.','Heavy mirrors fixed to plaster and lath walls\nGallery walls up narrow row house staircases\nFloating shelves in downtown condo alcoves\nCurtain rods on tall bay windows in semis\nCloset organisers in narrow east end bedrooms','My Annex apartment still has a picture rail. Can you hang from that?','Yes, and I would rather use it than drill. A picture rail carries a surprising amount once the hooks match the moulding profile, and it keeps your plaster completely untouched, which suits renters and heritage owners alike. I can hang a full wall of frames from it on adjustable cord so you can move things later. If a piece is too heavy for the rail, I will say so before I start.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (98,9,2,'Shelf, Mirror and Picture Hanging in North York','Shelf and Mirror Hanging in North York','Picture hanging, shelves and mirrors across North York, from Willowdale bungalows to Yonge Street condo towers. Anchors matched to your wall. Free quote.','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in North York covers two very different kinds of home. The post-war bungalows around Bathurst Manor, Downsview and Willowdale are framed at a predictable sixteen inches, which makes solid shelf work straightforward. The towers along Yonge and Sheppard are nothing like that.\n\nI check what a wall is made of before anything goes into it. A stud finder tells part of the story and a small pilot hole tells the rest. Then I match the fixing to the wall and the weight, so you can load the shelf without watching it.','A lot of North York work happens in finished basements and second suites. Those lower walls are usually strapping and panelling in front of concrete block, so there is almost nothing to grip and the fastener has to reach past the furring into masonry. Upstairs in a 1955 Willowdale bungalow the ceiling sits near eight feet, which means a tall mirror has to be sized and placed with care. In the Yonge corridor towers everything solid is concrete and every delivery needs a booked service elevator.','Shelving fixed through panelling into block walls\nFull-length mirrors in low-ceilinged bungalow bedrooms\nBlinds and rods in Yonge corridor condo units\nCloset organisers for shallow post-war bedroom closets\nFamily photo walls along bungalow hallways','The basement walls in my North York house sound hollow. Is that a problem?','Usually that is wood strapping and panelling sitting in front of concrete block with an air gap behind it. It is only a problem if the wrong fixing goes in. I drill through the furring and set the anchor into the block itself, so the shelf is carried by masonry rather than by a thin sheet of panelling. I bring a hammer drill and the right bits for exactly that.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (99,9,3,'Shelf, Mirror and Picture Hanging in Scarborough','Mirror and Shelf Hanging in Scarborough','Shelves, mirrors and gallery walls in Scarborough, including split-level stairwells and concrete apartment walls. Level and solid. Ask me for a quote.','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in Scarborough often starts on a staircase. The back-splits and side-splits around Wexford, Bendale and Agincourt have those short runs of wall between half levels, and that is exactly where people want the family photographs.\n\nGetting it right is layout more than muscle. I measure the stair rise, set a line that follows it, and space the frames so the group climbs with the steps instead of fighting them. Everything lands in a stud or a proper anchor, and the wall is left clean when I go.','Split-level houses give Scarborough a shape of wall you rarely meet elsewhere: a half-flight stairwell with a sloping ceiling above it, where a frame group needs a rising baseline instead of a level one. The other half of my work here is in the 1970s apartment slabs along Kingston Road, Markham Road and Eglinton East. Those walls are poured concrete or block, so they take a hammer drill and sleeve anchors, and tenants understandably want the smallest number of holes possible.','Stairwell photo groupings in side-split houses\nDrilling concrete walls in rental apartment towers\nHeavy mirrors above bungalow living room sofas\nGarage and basement shelving for storage overflow\nCurtain rods across wide picture windows','I rent an apartment near Kingston Road. How many holes will this take?','Fewer than you expect. I lay the whole arrangement out on the floor first, mark it on the wall with tape, and only drill once you are happy with it. In a concrete building every hole is deliberate, and small ones can be filled and touched up when you move out. If your lease is strict about walls, tell me early and I will suggest lighter mounting options instead.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (100,9,4,'Shelf, Mirror and Picture Hanging in Etobicoke','Picture and Mirror Hanging in Etobicoke','Mirror, shelf and picture hanging in Etobicoke, from Humber Bay condo units to Kingsway plaster walls. Right anchor, no guesswork. Book a free quote.','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in Etobicoke swings between two extremes. The Humber Bay Shores towers are mostly glass, so there is very little wall to work with and every piece has to earn its place. The Kingsway and Alderwood houses have the opposite problem: plenty of wall, but 1930s and 1950s plaster behind the paint.\n\nI plan both the same way. Look at the wall, confirm what is inside it, then choose fixings that suit the weight. No guessing, no cracked plaster, and no shelf that sags a year later.','In a Humber Bay Shores or Mimico waterfront unit the outside face is window wall, and the only solid surfaces are concrete demising walls and steel-stud partitions, so shelving has to work around the short blank stretches near the entry and the bedroom. Anything large needs a service elevator booking and a time slot from the concierge, which I plan around. Up in the Kingsway it is plaster over lath with real brick behind the exterior walls, so heavy mirrors go in on masonry anchors.','Floating shelves on the few solid condo walls\nLarge mirrors on Kingsway plaster and brick walls\nBlinds and rods beside floor-to-ceiling condo windows\nCloset organisers in narrow Mimico apartment bedrooms\nEntry mirrors and coat hooks in bungalow hallways','There is barely any wall space in my Humber Bay condo. What can I do?','That is the normal starting point in those buildings. I usually place the mirror where it reflects the window rather than competing with it, keep shelving shallow so the walkway stays clear, and use the short wall beside the entry that most people overlook. Once I have seen the unit I can suggest placements that suit your layout and furniture. Looking and quoting costs you nothing.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (101,9,25,'Shelf, Mirror and Picture Hanging in Mississauga','Shelf and Picture Hanging in Mississauga','Shelf and picture hanging in Mississauga, sized for nine-foot ceilings, two-storey foyers and Square One condos. Send me your list for a free quote.','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in Mississauga is very often a question of height. Houses built in Churchill Meadows, Lisgar and Streetsville over the past twenty-five years have nine-foot main floors and open-to-above foyers, so a picture that looked large on the floor can vanish once it is on the wall.\n\nI size the piece to the wall before anything is drilled. That means measuring the drop from the ceiling, setting the centre line where your eye actually lands, and bringing a ladder tall enough to work safely on those entry walls.','The two-storey foyer wall is the classic Mississauga request. It climbs past the staircase to a ceiling two floors up, and a single small frame in the middle of it looks stranded. I usually build a vertical group or hang one large mirror, placed so it reads from the front door and from the upper landing. In the Square One towers the problem inverts: concrete walls, tighter rooms, and a booked service elevator, so oversized pieces have to fit the lift first.','Art on two-storey foyer and staircase walls\nBig mirrors above open-concept great room fireplaces\nShelving and rods in Square One condo units\nCloset organisers fitted into walk-in bedroom closets\nGallery walls along upper hallway landings','How high should art hang on a nine-foot wall in a newer Mississauga house?','Higher than most people guess, but still tied to the furniture. On a nine-foot wall I keep the centre of the piece near eye level when it sits above a sofa or console, then use a larger frame so it fills the extra height. On a two-storey foyer wall I go taller and bigger again so it reads from the landing. I hold everything up first so you can see it before I drill.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (102,9,26,'Shelf, Mirror and Picture Hanging in Brampton','Shelf and Mirror Hanging in Brampton','Picture hanging, shelving and mirrors in Brampton, with whole-house lists in new subdivision homes done in one visit. Send your list for a free quote.','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in Brampton is usually a whole-house list rather than a single job. New owners in Mount Pleasant, Springdale or Fletcher\'s Meadow move into a builder-finished house with bare walls in every room and want the lot sorted at once.\n\nThat suits how I work. I go room by room, mark everything out first, then drill in one pass so there is a single clean-up at the end. Mirrors, shelves, curtain rods, blinds and closet organisers can all go up in the same visit if the list is ready.','Brampton\'s newer subdivisions are framed at sixteen inches with no blocking behind the drywall, so a loaded floating shelf on a wide great-room wall lives or dies on hitting real studs. A lot of houses here run as multi-generational homes with a finished basement suite, which means two full sets of closets, mirrors and storage shelving rather than one. In the older Bramalea sections the picture changes again: 1960s builds, tighter rooms, and plaster instead of drywall in some of them.','Whole-house picture hanging after a subdivision move-in\nCloset organisers for shared multi-generational bedrooms\nCurtain rods and blinds across new-build windows\nShelving for finished basement suites and storage rooms\nLarge mirrors in formal dining and entry areas','I have just moved into a new build in Springdale. Can it all go up in one visit?','Usually yes, as long as the pieces are on site and you have a rough idea where they belong. I like to walk the whole house first, agree placements room by room, then hang it all in one continuous run. Batching the small jobs into a single visit saves you a second trip and a second booking. Send me a list with photos and I will tell you what fits in a day.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (103,9,31,'Shelf, Mirror and Picture Hanging in Vaughan','Mirror and Picture Hanging in Vaughan','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in Vaughan, including stone fireplace walls and two-storey great rooms in Woodbridge and Maple. Ask me for a free quote.','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in Vaughan often runs straight into stone. Houses in Woodbridge, Maple and Kleinburg frequently have a fireplace wall finished in stone veneer or full brick, and that is precisely where the large mirror or the family portrait is meant to go.\n\nMasonry is not a problem, it just needs the right method. Where I can, I drill into the mortar joint instead of the face of the stone, size sleeve anchors to the weight with a margin, and keep every fixing hidden behind the frame.','Vaughan houses are big and the walls match. A two-storey great room in Woodbridge or Maple leaves a huge blank stretch above the fireplace, and coffered or tray ceilings mean the top edge of a tall mirror has to line up with a moulding rather than the drywall. Stone and brick feature walls are common enough here that masonry bits are standard kit for me. In the Vaughan Metropolitan Centre towers it reverts to concrete, steel studs and a booked service elevator for anything oversized.','Mirrors mounted on stone veneer fireplace walls\nTall art on two-storey great room walls\nShelving and display units in finished basements\nCurtain rods aligned with coffered ceiling mouldings\nGallery walls along wide upstairs hallways','Can a heavy mirror go on the stone fireplace wall in my Woodbridge home?','Yes. Stone veneer and brick both take a proper anchor, and I aim for the mortar joints so the stone face stays unmarked. I weigh the mirror first, or check the maker\'s figure, and size the fixings for that with room to spare. The one thing I will not do is hang something heavy on adhesive alone over masonry. It lets go eventually, and it takes the finish with it.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (104,9,32,'Shelf, Mirror and Picture Hanging in Markham','Shelf and Picture Hanging in Markham','Shelves, mirrors and stairwell gallery walls in Markham, from Cornell townhouses to Unionville homes. Layout planned before I drill. Request a free quote.','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in Markham keeps meeting the same wall: the three-storey townhouse stairwell. Cornell and the Highway 7 area are full of them, and that stair wall is often the largest uninterrupted surface in the whole house.\n\nI treat it as one composition rather than a row of separate frames. Layout on paper first, tape on the wall next, drilling only once you have signed it off. Working above a staircase needs proper ladder footing, so nothing leans on the drywall and no tread gets scuffed on the way up.','The rear-lane townhouses in Cornell and the stacked towns near Downtown Markham are narrow and tall, so the usable wall space runs vertically instead of across. Shelving has to stay shallow or it steals from a room barely twelve feet wide, while the stair wall can carry frames across three floors. Older Unionville and Berczy houses are the reverse: wide 1980s and 1990s rooms, drywall over sixteen-inch framing, and enough clear wall for a long run of floating shelves.','Three-storey stairwell gallery walls in townhouses\nShallow floating shelves in narrow townhome rooms\nMirrors that open up small Cornell entryways\nCloset organisers for shared family bedrooms\nBlinds and rods on tall townhouse windows','My Cornell townhouse stairwell rises three floors. Can art be hung that high safely?','Yes, with the right ladder setup. A stairwell needs a levelling base or a proper platform so the feet sit square on the treads, and I will not work off anything improvised over a drop like that. Once the footing is solid, the layout follows the stair angle up through each landing. I usually keep the heaviest and largest pieces low and let the group get lighter as it climbs.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (105,9,33,'Shelf, Mirror and Picture Hanging in Richmond Hill','Mirror and Picture Hanging in Richmond Hill','Picture hanging, mirrors and shelving in Richmond Hill, including walkout basements and older Mill Pond homes. Fixings chosen for the wall. Get in touch.','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in Richmond Hill often ends up in the walkout basement. The land slopes here, so a large share of homes from Oak Ridges down through Bayview Hill have a finished lower level with full-height windows and proper living space.\n\nThose rooms deserve the same care as the main floor. I hang art to the sightlines people actually use down there, allow for exterior walls that may be framed tight against poured concrete, and pick fixings that suit a level where humidity swings more through the year.','Sloped lots give Richmond Hill an unusual number of walkout basements, and their walls are a mixture. Interior partitions are ordinary drywall on studs, while the exterior sides are framed and insulated in front of poured concrete, and in some builds the studs sit tight against it with no cavity at all. That changes the fixing completely, so I check first. Around the Mill Pond the houses are older and smaller, with plaster in some and rooms that a well-placed mirror genuinely improves.','Art and shelving in finished walkout basements\nMirrors that brighten older Mill Pond rooms\nCurtain rods on tall walkout basement windows\nCloset organisers in Oak Ridges bedroom suites\nGallery walls up open two-storey staircases','Parts of my Richmond Hill walkout basement wall feel solid. Why is that?','Because the exterior side is framed in front of the poured foundation, and in some builds the studs are pressed right against the concrete with no cavity behind them. The interior partitions are normal drywall with a hollow space. I sound the wall and drill a small pilot hole to find out which I am dealing with, then either fix into a stud or set a masonry anchor into the concrete itself.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (106,9,47,'Shelf, Mirror and Picture Hanging in Oakville','Picture and Mirror Hanging in Oakville','Careful picture hanging, mirrors and shelving in Oakville, including original plaster in Old Oakville homes. Small holes, level results. Free quote available.','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in Oakville has to respect the walls it goes into. South of the QEW, around Old Oakville and Lakeshore Road, the houses are pre-war with plaster over lath, original trim, and in some cases brick immediately behind the finish.\n\nThat calls for a slower approach. I drill small, drill once, and use fixings that spread the load instead of crumbling a hundred-year-old wall. North of the highway in Glen Abbey and River Oaks the framing is standard drywall, and the challenge becomes scale rather than fragility.','Old Oakville plaster is soft at the surface and hard underneath, and the trim beside it is usually original, so a mis-drilled hole is not a five-minute patch. I keep fixings clear of the lath edges and use plaster-rated anchors for anything with weight. Owners here also tend to have real framed art and older mirrors, which need their wire tension checked and their D-rings set square before they go anywhere near a hook. In Glen Abbey and Joshua Creek the pieces simply get bigger.','Framed art hung on Old Oakville plaster walls\nAntique mirrors rehung with proper D-rings and wire\nPicture rail hanging in pre-war Lakeshore homes\nLarge art on tall Glen Abbey family room walls\nShelving and rods before a house goes to market','My Old Oakville house has original plaster. Will hanging art crack it?','Not if it is done carefully. Plaster cracks when somebody drives a screw straight in or drills fast with a blunt bit. I use a sharp masonry bit, run the drill slowly, and choose anchors that spread the load across the lath rather than pulling on one weak point. For very heavy pieces I look for a stud first, or use the picture rail if your house still has the original in place.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (107,9,43,'Shelf, Mirror and Picture Hanging in Pickering','Shelf and Mirror Installation in Pickering','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in Pickering, from brand new Seaton builds to 1970s Bay Ridges houses. Whole lists welcome. Contact me for a free quote.','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in Pickering runs from brand new drywall to walls that are fifty years old. The Seaton homes north of the 407 are barely lived in, with nothing on the walls yet. Down in Bay Ridges and West Shore the houses date from the 1960s and 1970s and have been through several owners already.\n\nBoth get the same treatment. I find out what is behind the surface, choose the anchor for the weight it has to carry, and set everything level before it takes any load at all.','A new Seaton house arrives with clean drywall and nothing on it, so the whole plan is mine to set: shelf runs lined up with the studs, and every room mapped in one go before the furniture lands. Older south Pickering is a different exercise. The 1960s and 1970s houses near Liverpool Road and the West Shore have lower ceilings, smaller rooms and original trim, so a big mirror has to be scaled down and a shelf has to clear a window casing.','Full move-in hanging lists in new Seaton homes\nShelving over panelled basement walls in older houses\nMirrors in compact West Shore bungalow hallways\nBlinds and curtain rods on new-build windows\nCloset organisers added to builder-standard closets','My Seaton house is brand new. Should I wait before hanging anything?','There is no need to wait. A new house does settle a little in the first year, but that shows up as hairline cracks at corners and joints, not as pictures shifting on the wall. What I do allow for is that builder walls carry no extra blocking, so heavy shelves and mirrors get fixed into studs wherever I can reach them. A whole list in one visit is the efficient way to do it.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (108,9,44,'Shelf, Mirror and Picture Hanging in Ajax','Picture and Shelf Hanging in Ajax','Shelves, mirrors and gallery walls in Ajax townhomes and semis, planned around narrow rooms and stair walls. Price agreed up front. Message me for a quote.','Shelf, mirror and picture hanging in Ajax is mostly townhouse and semi work. The neighbourhoods built through the 1990s and 2000s north of Rossland Road and off Harwood Avenue are full of narrow-plan homes where the living room has one usable wall and the rest is door, window or stairs.\n\nThat makes placement the real job. I measure the free wall, size the mirror or the shelf run to it, and keep the depth down so the room still walks properly. Then it goes up level, solid and where you actually want it.','Ajax rooms tend to be narrow and long, so a wide horizontal grouping suits them far better than a tall stack, and shelving deeper than about twenty-five centimetres starts catching shoulders on the walk through. In the 1990s and 2000s semis the living and dining areas often run together with only a short return of wall between them, which limits where a mirror can sit. Down near Pickering Beach some original wartime-era houses remain, with smaller rooms and framing that does not always land at sixteen inches.','Gallery walls on townhouse entry stair walls\nShallow shelving for narrow living room walls\nMirrors placed to widen small semi-detached rooms\nCurtain rods and blinds on standard builder windows\nCloset organisers in tight upstairs bedrooms','My Ajax living room has only one clear wall. What works best on it?','One larger piece usually beats a scattered group when wall space is tight. A single wide mirror or one long horizontal frame above the sofa keeps the room feeling open and leaves the doorways and window trim alone. If you want shelving on that same wall, I keep it shallow and run it high so the walkway underneath stays clear. I can tape both options up so you can compare before I drill.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (109,2,1,'TV Mounting in Toronto Homes and Condos','TV Wall Mounting in Toronto Homes','TV mounting in Toronto, from plaster walls in east end semis to concrete in downtown condos. Level brackets, hidden cables. Ask me for a free quote.','TV mounting in Toronto runs from lath and plaster in a Riverdale semi to poured concrete in a King West tower, and the wall decides everything. I check what is behind the surface before I drill, then pick anchors and a bracket that suit it.\n\nYou choose fixed, tilting or full-motion, I get it level, hide the cable where the construction allows, and clean up. Parking is tight on the older downtown streets, so tell me what is possible and I will plan around it. If a second TV needs hanging, I can do both in one visit.','In an old Toronto semi the natural TV wall is often the party wall you share with next door, and that is double brick, so it takes masonry anchors and a few minutes of hammer drilling your neighbour will hear. I try to keep that to a weekday afternoon. The rest of the house is lath and plaster on studs that ignore the modern sixteen inch rule. Downtown in the King West and CityPlace towers it swings the other way: poured concrete, and a drilling window set by the building.','Full-motion mount on a Riverdale semi\'s plaster wall\nTV on the concrete wall of a King West condo\nBedroom TV mounted in a Parkdale rental apartment\nCables hidden in a Leslieville living room wall\nTilting bracket above a brick chimney breast','Do I need condo approval before you mount a TV in my downtown unit?','Most downtown Toronto buildings do not ask for approval to hang a TV bracket, but many limit drilling to weekday daytime hours and want the service elevator booked if a large box is coming up. Check with your concierge or property manager before you pick a time. I keep the hammer drilling short, vacuum the concrete dust as I go and work inside whatever window the building sets.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (110,2,2,'TV Mounting in North York Houses and Condos','TV Mounting in North York Homes','Wall mounting TVs in North York bungalows, side-splits and Yonge Street condos. Right anchors, comfortable viewing height, cables tucked away. Get a quote.','TV mounting in North York usually means one of two houses. A 1960s bungalow or side-split with plaster walls and solid framing, or a Yonge Street condo where the wall behind the drywall is concrete. Both hold a TV well once the right anchors go in.\n\nI look at where you sit before I mark anything, since height matters more than most people expect. Cables get tucked into the wall when the construction allows and into a channel when it does not. Free quote first, price agreed before I start, and the room left clean.','The bungalows and side-splits through Bathurst Manor, Downsview and Don Mills are framed on a dependable sixteen inch layout, but the finish is plaster skimmed over gypsum lath, so it chips if you drill without scoring it first. Side-split family rooms sit half a storey down with a beam overhead, which limits where a cable can run. Along Yonge in Willowdale the towers are concrete, and the rebuilt houses behind them have tall great rooms and stone surrounds instead.','TV mounted over a Don Mills bungalow fireplace\nFull-motion arm in a Willowdale condo living room\nSide-split family room mount with cables run inside\nSecond TV for a North York basement apartment\nLarger screen rehung on an existing Bayview Village bracket','Can the cables be hidden inside the plaster walls of my Willowdale bungalow?','Usually yes. Behind the plaster there is a normal stud bay, so I can cut two small openings, drop the cable down inside the wall and finish with recessed plates. Older plaster is brittle, so I cut it rather than punch it. If that wall turns out to be solid brick, or there is fire blocking in the way, I will suggest a slim channel painted to match instead.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (111,2,3,'TV Mounting in Scarborough Homes and Basements','TV Wall Mounting in Scarborough','TV mounting in Scarborough houses, panelled basement rec rooms and apartment towers. Solid masonry anchors and neat cable routing. Send me your details.','TV mounting in Scarborough often ends up in the basement. The rec rooms in Agincourt, Wexford and Bendale houses were finished decades ago, and the wall you want is panelling or drywall sitting against a block foundation. That is fine to mount on, it just needs the right anchors.\n\nUpstairs the fireplace wall is the usual choice, and the question is how high the screen should sit. I measure from the couch, get the bracket level and keep the cables out of sight. Ask for a quote first, and if a few other small jobs are waiting, I can take care of them in the same visit.','Scarborough basements are where this job gets interesting. Rec rooms in Wexford, Bendale and Agincourt houses are often panelled in thin wood over furring strips fixed to a block foundation, so a bracket has to reach past both and anchor into the block itself. A drop ceiling above gives me a tidy route for the cable. Upstairs the 1960s fireplaces sit on a solid chimney breast, and the apartment slabs along Kingston Road and Markham Road are concrete throughout.','Mount on a panelled Agincourt basement rec room wall\nFireplace wall install in a 1960s Bendale bungalow\nConcrete wall mount in a Kingston Road apartment\nBedroom TVs mounted for a Malvern family home\nOld bracket removed and bigger TV rehung in Wexford','Will a TV really hold on the wood panelling in my basement?','The panelling itself holds nothing, so I do not rely on it. In most Scarborough basements that panelling is fixed to furring strips over a block or poured foundation, and I anchor straight through into the masonry behind. That gives a very strong fixing, stronger than drywall and studs. If the basement wall is framed in wood instead, I mount to the framing the normal way.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (112,2,4,'TV Mounting in Etobicoke and Lakeshore Condos','TV Mounting in Etobicoke Homes and Condos','TV mounting in Etobicoke, including Humber Bay condo concrete and Mimico bungalow plaster. Tilting and full-motion brackets fitted level. Message me today.','TV mounting in Etobicoke changes a lot between the lakeshore towers and the streets behind them. A Humber Bay condo gives you glass on two sides and a short run of solid wall to work with. A Mimico or Alderwood bungalow gives you plaster on wood studs and plenty of choice.\n\nI work out the best wall with you first, then set the bracket at the height that suits your seating. Cables go inside the wall where I can and into a neat channel where I cannot. The quote is free and the price is settled before I open a toolbox.','In the Humber Bay Shores towers the walls are mostly window. A suite can have glass on two sides, so the TV usually lands on the demising wall or the short return beside the kitchen, both concrete or block, and both needing a hammer drill and masonry anchors. Sightlines in a narrow suite then call for a tilting or full-motion bracket. Inland, the Alderwood and Mimico bungalows are plaster on wood studs, easier to drill but softer to hold.','TV on the demising wall of a Humber Bay condo\nFull-motion mount for a narrow lakeshore condo living room\nTV above the fireplace in a Kingsway two-storey\nMimico bungalow living room mount with cables concealed\nBasement TV and soundbar mounted in Alderwood','My Humber Bay condo has windows on two walls. Where can the TV actually go?','In those suites the solid options are usually the demising wall you share with a neighbour, the wall beside the entry, or the short return next to the kitchen. All of them are concrete or block, so I drill with a hammer drill and use masonry anchors. If the only good wall sits off to one side, a full-motion arm brings the screen out to face the sofa.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (113,2,25,'TV Mounting in Mississauga Homes and Condos','TV Wall Mounting in Mississauga','TV mounting in Mississauga, from Square One condo walls to fireplace installs in Erin Mills. Cables concealed, price agreed first. Ask about a free quote.','TV mounting in Mississauga takes me from City Centre condos to family rooms out in Erin Mills. The tower suites are concrete behind the drywall and need masonry anchors. The houses west and north are ordinary framing, where the harder question is usually height above the fireplace.\n\nI check the wall, confirm the studs and mark the position against where you actually sit. Then the bracket goes up level, the cable disappears and the packaging leaves with me. Booking is simple: Monday to Saturday, and I usually reply within one business day.','Mississauga splits two ways for this job. In the City Centre towers off Hurontario the wall behind the drywall is concrete or block, drilling is kept to daytime hours and the service elevator has to be booked if a big box is coming up. West of there, the Erin Mills and Meadowvale two-storeys from the seventies and eighties are plain drywall on sixteen inch studs, though the fireplace often sits on an angled corner, which pushes people towards a full-motion arm.','TV mounted on concrete in a Square One condo\nCorner fireplace mount in an Erin Mills two-storey\nFull-motion arm for a Meadowvale open-plan family room\nTwo TVs hung in one Churchill Meadows visit\nBedroom TV and cable concealment in Port Credit','Can a TV go above the corner fireplace in my Erin Mills family room?','It can, and the corner is actually helpful. There is framing on both sides of the angle to anchor into, and a full-motion arm lets the screen swing out of the corner and square up with the seating. I would look at the mantel height first, because a corner install that ends up too high is uncomfortable to watch for a whole evening.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (114,2,26,'TV Mounting in Brampton Houses and Townhomes','TV Mounting Services in Brampton','TV mounting in Brampton homes with tall main floors and high fireplace niches. Tilting brackets set for comfortable viewing. Tell me what needs mounting.','TV mounting in Brampton is mostly newer houses with tall main floors. Springdale, Fletcher\'s Meadow and Mount Pleasant were built with nine foot ceilings and a fireplace niche that sits high, so bracket choice and screen height matter more than the drilling does.\n\nI set the TV where it is comfortable from the couch, not where the niche happens to be. Cables run inside the wall when there is a path for them. If you have screens waiting for the basement and the bedrooms too, list them all and I will quote the lot as one visit.','Most Brampton calls come from the newer subdivisions: Springdale, Fletcher\'s Meadow and Mount Pleasant. Main floors there run nine feet, so a TV in the fireplace niche sits higher than people expect and a tilting bracket is what keeps the picture aimed at the couch rather than the ceiling. Builders usually left a recessed box and a conduit above the niche, which makes the cable easy to hide. The narrow freehold towns are the other regular one, where a single long room serves kitchen and TV both.','Tilting mount above a Springdale great room fireplace\nTV mounted in a Mount Pleasant townhouse living room\nThree TVs hung during one Brampton house visit\nBasement apartment TV mounted in Fletcher\'s Meadow\nOld plasma taken down and new screen mounted','The fireplace niche in my Springdale house is high. Will the TV be comfortable?','That niche was framed for looks more than for viewing. With nine foot ceilings the screen often lands well above eye level, so I fit a tilting bracket and angle it back down towards the couch. If the niche is very high, I will hold the TV in place at that height so you can see it from your seat before I drill anything and commit to it.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (115,2,31,'TV Mounting in Vaughan Homes and Family Rooms','TV Wall Mounting in Vaughan Homes','TV mounting in Vaughan, including stone fireplace surrounds in Woodbridge and Kleinburg. Careful masonry anchoring and hidden cabling. Request a free quote.','TV mounting in Vaughan very often means a stone fireplace. Woodbridge, Patterson and Kleinburg family rooms tend to have a full height surround, and a screen over it needs masonry anchors set with care, because stone does not forgive a hole in the wrong place.\n\nI plan the layout, mark it on the wall and only then drill. Cables come off the stone and into the drywall beside it so nothing shows across the face. Free quote, price agreed before I start, and everything cleaned up before I leave.','A lot of Vaughan family rooms have a floor to ceiling stone or cultured stone surround, common through Woodbridge, Patterson and Kleinburg. Stone is unforgiving. I set anchors in the mortar joints wherever the bracket layout allows, use long masonry fixings that reach solid backing, and take my time, because a wrong hole in stone cannot be patched. Two storey great rooms raise the height question as well, and the cable normally has to leave the stone and enter drywall to one side.','TV mounted on a Woodbridge stone fireplace surround\nTwo-storey great room mount in a Patterson home\nConcrete wall install in a Vaughan Metropolitan Centre condo\nHome theatre screen mounted in a Maple basement\nThornhill family room TV with cables run inside','Is it safe to drill into the stone surround on my Woodbridge fireplace?','Yes, with the right approach. I set the anchors in the mortar joints wherever the bracket pattern lets me, since a joint is easier to drill and far easier to make good later than the face of a stone. The fixings are long enough to reach the solid backing behind. Stone cannot be patched invisibly, so I measure twice and drill once.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (116,2,32,'TV Mounting in Markham Homes and Condos','TV Mounting in Markham Houses and Condos','TV mounting in Markham houses and condos, with cable runs moved to the wall you actually want. Neat, level and hidden. Drop me a line about pricing.','TV mounting in Markham usually starts with a question about cables. Houses in Berczy, Unionville and Cornell were wired by the builder, but the outlet is rarely on the wall where you want the screen. Moving the picture is normally straightforward once I know what is inside the wall.\n\nI confirm the framing, set the height from your seating and run the cable inside the wall where a path exists. Fixed, tilting or full-motion, I will tell you which suits the room you have. Quotes are free and I usually reply within one business day.','Markham houses from the nineties and two thousands in Berczy and Unionville often came with a low voltage panel in the basement and coax or data drops in the family room, and that drop is almost never where you want the TV. Moving the screen means running a fresh cable inside the wall back towards the panel side. In Cornell the homes are narrow and deep with the living space stacked at the front, so a full-motion arm earns its keep in a room only a few metres wide.','TV moved to a different wall in Unionville\nFull-motion mount in a narrow Cornell living room\nMount plus cable run to the basement panel\nCondo TV mounted on concrete in Downtown Markham\nBedroom and office TVs mounted in one Berczy visit','The cable outlet in my Berczy house is on the wrong wall. Can the TV still move?','Normally yes. I can run new low voltage cable inside the wall from your equipment to the new spot and finish both ends with recessed plates, which is tidy in these houses because the basement panel makes a good starting point. Adding a new electrical receptacle is a licensed trade in Ontario, so if that wall needs power added I will point you to an electrician.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (117,2,33,'TV Mounting in Richmond Hill Homes','TV Wall Mounting in Richmond Hill','TV mounting in Richmond Hill, from bonus rooms over the garage to raised brick hearths. Height set from your seating. Get in touch for a free quote.','TV mounting in Richmond Hill covers everything from a bonus room over the garage in Jefferson to a raised brick hearth in Richvale. Sloped ceilings and awkward wall heights are common here, so where the screen goes is worth a short conversation before the drill comes out.\n\nI measure from the seating, find solid framing and set the bracket so the picture meets your eyes rather than the ceiling. Cables go inside the wall where I can route them, and into a slim channel where I cannot. Free quote first, agreed price, no mess left behind.','Two things come up again and again in Richmond Hill. The newer Jefferson and Oak Ridges houses often have a bonus room over the garage with a sloped ceiling and short knee walls, so the only full height stud bay can be off beside a window and the mount and the seating have to be planned together. Older Richvale and Mill Pond homes have raised brick hearths that push the screen up, unless it goes on the wall beside instead.','TV mounted on a Jefferson bonus room wall\nRaised hearth fireplace mount in a Richvale home\nFull-motion bracket for an Oak Ridges family room\nCondo TV mounted on concrete near Yonge Street\nMount repositioned after a Mill Pond living room repaint','The bonus room over my garage has a sloped ceiling. Can a TV go up there?','Usually it can. The trick is finding a full height section of wall, because knee walls are short and the sloped part is no use for a bracket. Often the best spot is the gable end or the wall beside the stairs. I check the framing there, then set the height for the seating you plan to put in the room.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (118,2,47,'TV Mounting in Oakville Homes, Old and New','TV Mounting in Oakville Homes','TV mounting in Oakville, careful work on old lath and plaster and quick work on newer drywall. Cables kept out of sight. Reach out for a free estimate.','TV mounting in Oakville can mean a century home near Lakeshore Road or a newer house out in Joshua Creek, and the walls are nothing alike. Old plaster over wood lath wants a light touch and careful piloting. Modern drywall on standard framing is quick by comparison.\n\nEither way I find the framing first, then set the height against your seating rather than the mantel. Cables are hidden inside the wall when the construction allows it. The quote is free, the price is agreed up front and the room goes back the way it was.','Old Oakville and Kerr Village hold houses well older than the post war stock, and they are plaster over wood lath with studs that wander off the modern layout. Plaster keys break when a lag is driven through dry, so I pilot every hole and let the bracket bear on framing rather than on the surface. The chimney breasts in those homes are solid brick. Out in Glen Abbey and Joshua Creek it is plain drywall, and the questions are about height above the mantel and where the cable comes out.','Careful mount on lath and plaster in Old Oakville\nTV above a brick chimney breast near Kerr Village\nGlen Abbey family room mount with concealed cables\nFull-motion arm in a Bronte condo living room\nTV and soundbar mounted in a Joshua Creek basement','Will mounting a TV crack the plaster in my Old Oakville house?','It should not. Plaster cracks when a screw is driven hard through it into nothing behind, so I locate the framing first, drill a clean pilot hole and let the bracket bear on solid wood. On lath and plaster I take the extra minute for each hole rather than driving fast. If a spot turns out to be hollow, I move the position rather than force it.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (119,2,43,'TV Mounting in Pickering Homes and Townhomes','TV Wall Mounting in Pickering','TV mounting in Pickering, from three-storey Duffin Heights towns to bungalows near the lake. Safe handling, level brackets, tidy cables. Ask for a quote.','TV mounting in Pickering runs from three storey towns in Duffin Heights to older bungalows down near the lake. In the towns the living space is a floor up, so getting a large screen there safely is half the job. In Bay Ridges the walls are simpler and the rooms are smaller.\n\nI check the route, the wall and the seating before anything is drilled. Brackets go up level, cables get hidden where the wall allows and the packaging leaves with me. Free quote, agreed price, and small extra jobs can be added to the same visit.','Duffin Heights is full of three storey towns where the living room sits on the middle floor, above the garage. A large TV has to go up two flights of narrow stairs before anything gets drilled, so I look at the turn on the landing before the box leaves the room it arrived in. The wall behind the sofa in those units is usually shared with the neighbour. South in Bay Ridges and West Shore the houses are older and lower, with brick fireplaces and wood framed walls that drill easily.','TV carried up and mounted in a Duffin Heights town\nFireplace wall mount in a Bay Ridges bungalow\nFull-motion arm for a West Shore living room\nTV mounted in an Amberlea finished basement\nBracket replaced for a larger screen in Pickering','I live in a three-storey town here. Is getting a big TV upstairs a problem?','It is rarely a problem, but it is worth planning. I look at the stairs and the turn on the landing before anything is lifted, and I would rather unbox the TV on the ground floor and carry the panel up than fight a wide carton around a corner. For the largest screens, a second pair of hands at home for two minutes makes it easier.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
INSERT INTO `seo_service_locations` (`id`, `service_id`, `location_id`, `h1`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `intro`, `local_angle`, `common_jobs`, `faq_q`, `faq_a`, `is_published`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) VALUES (120,2,44,'TV Mounting in Ajax Homes and Family Rooms','TV Mounting in Ajax Homes and Condos','TV mounting in Ajax family rooms where afternoon glare decides the layout. Full-motion arms, solid anchors, concealed cabling. Contact me for a free quote.','TV mounting in Ajax is often a fight with the afternoon sun. Open family rooms in the newer north end have a wide bank of rear windows, and the glare decides the bracket as much as the wall does. A full-motion arm solves most of it.\n\nI look at the room at the time of day you actually watch, then set the height from the couch. Cables get concealed inside the wall where there is a path for them. Free quote, price agreed first, and Monday to Saturday hours that suit most households.','In the newer Ajax subdivisions north of Kingston Road the family room opens straight onto the kitchen with a wide bank of rear windows, and afternoon sun on the screen decides the mount more than the wall does. A full-motion arm lets you pull the TV out and turn it off the glass. South towards Pickering Beach and the older streets off Harwood Avenue the rooms are smaller and the fireplace already takes the best wall, so the screen is sometimes better beside it than above it.','Full-motion mount to beat the afternoon glare\nTV mounted beside the fireplace in Pickering Beach\nOpen-plan family room mount visible from the kitchen\nBasement suite TV mounted off Harwood Avenue\nTwo bedroom TVs mounted in one Ajax visit','Afternoon sun pours into the back windows here. Where should the TV go?','Face the screen away from the glass wherever you can. In most open Ajax family rooms that means the TV ends up on the same wall as the windows, or on a full-motion arm you can angle off the glare late in the day and push flat again after dark. I will hold it in a couple of positions with you before I drill.',1,'2026-08-12 04:17:12','2026-08-12 04:17:12');
